window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["tj"]=[{"id":"tj-front-bumper-guide","title":"TJ Steel Front Bumper \u2014 Which Style to Buy and Why","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":100,"high":950},"tools_required":["socket set (18mm for the factory bumper bolts)","torque wrench","floor jack (to hold the new bumper at height)","wire strippers and heat-shrink connectors (if relocating fog lights)","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","armor_bumper_front","bumper","winch-mount","protection","approach-angle","stubby","full-width"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country winch mounting plate for the factory TJ bumper","part_number":"1189","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":100},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC front bumper with winch plate \u2014 97-06 TJ/LJ","part_number":"76800","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Rugged Ridge XHD modular front bumper (base + winch plate)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Warn Rock Crawler stubby front bumper \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":500},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 Patriot Series full-width front bumper \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Poison Spyder Brawler Lite / Brawler Mid front bumper \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":700},{"name":"LOD Destroyer Series full-width front bumper \u2014 TJ","vendor":"LOD Offroad","price_estimate_usd":895}],"citations":[{"title":"Smittybilt XRC Front Bumper, 97-06 TJ/LJ (76800)","url":"https://www.smittybilt.com/shop/bumpers/front-bumpers-bumpers/xrc-front-bumper-textured-black-97-06-tj-lj-76800/"},{"title":"Rough Country Winch Mounting Plate, 97-06 TJ / 04-06 LJ","url":"https://www.roughcountry.com/product/jeep-winch-mounting-plate-1189"},{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 1997-2006 Wrangler TJ winch mounting options","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/vehicle/1997-2006-wrangler-tj/winches-recovery-and-safety-gear/winch-mounting"},{"title":"Winch on a stock TJ bumper \u2014 owner install thread (WranglerTJForum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/winch-with-stock-bumper.14062/"}],"alternatives":["tj-rear-bumper-guide","tj-skid-plates","tj-winch","tj-recovery-points"],"summary":"Buy the bumper for the winch, not the look. A frame-mounted winch plate and real shackle tabs are the two things the stock TJ bumper can't give you; everything else (stubby vs. full-width, tube vs. plate, bash bar or not) is a tradeoff between tire clearance, protection, and weight. For most TJs a stubby or mid-width bumper with a winch plate in the $400\u2013$500 range is the right buy \u2014 and if the budget is $100, [a winch plate on the factory bumper](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/winch-with-stock-bumper.14062/) is a legitimate first step, not a compromise.","description":"The 1997\u20132006 TJ shipped with a stamped-steel channel bumper (plastic end caps on Sahara, Rubicon, and some X models). It's stronger than the plastic JK bumper that came after it, and the factory tow hooks \u2014 where fitted \u2014 bolt through it into the frame horns, so a stock TJ is not helpless in a recovery. What the stock bumper can't do is carry a winch, and its end caps hang out past the frame where they catch rocks and scrub 33s at full lock. A steel replacement solves both, adds rated shackle tabs, and usually improves approach angle.\n\nThere are three decisions to make, in this order: **where the winch mounts**, **how wide the bumper is**, and **how much steel you want to carry**.\n\nEvery winch load \u2014 a self-recovery on a ledge, a 9,000 lb line pull on a stuck buddy \u2014 goes into the winch plate and then into whatever the plate bolts to. The plate must bolt to the frame horns, not to the bumper face. This is where cheap bumpers fail: a plate welded to a 3/16 in bumper face with no frame tie-in bends the face on the first hard pull. Look for a winch plate that either bolts through to the frame with its own hardware or sits on a bumper whose mounting brackets are welded directly under the plate.\n\nThe standard winch bolt pattern is 10 in \u00d7 4.5 in with four 3/8-16 bolts. Any 8,000\u20139,500 lb winch fits any bumper that lists that pattern; a 12,000 lb winch is longer (the drum is wider) and may not clear the bumper's side plates or fairlead opening. Buy the winch and the bumper as a pair, from the same spec sheet, and check that the fairlead mount is the standard 10 in spacing. Synthetic rope needs a hawse fairlead; steel cable needs a roller fairlead \u2014 some bumpers only fit one.\n\nThe $100 path: a winch mounting plate that sits on top of the factory bumper and bolts through it into the frame ([Rough Country 1189](https://www.roughcountry.com/product/jeep-winch-mounting-plate-1189), Warn, Smittybilt, Rugged Ridge all make one). The plate carries the winch; the factory bumper stays as trim. Owners run these for years. What you give up is shackle tabs (add bolt-on D-ring mounts to the frame horns, or use the factory tow hooks) and any protection or clearance improvement. If the winch is the only thing you need, this is the right buy and the money saved goes toward the [winch itself](/db/tj/tj-winch/).\n\n**Stubby** (frame-width, roughly 30\u201334 in): best tire clearance at full lock, best approach angle, lightest. Nothing protects the fenders or grille corners. On a TJ running 33s or 35s with a 3\u20134 in lift, this is the layout that lets the front tires turn without hitting the bumper ends, which is why nearly every crawler runs one. Warn Rock Crawler, [Smittybilt XRC (the TJ version is a stubby-leaning mid-width)](https://www.smittybilt.com/shop/bumpers/front-bumpers-bumpers/xrc-front-bumper-textured-black-97-06-tj-lj-76800/), JCR Crusader, and Poison Spyder Brawler Lite are all in this group.\n\n**Mid-width** (roughly 40\u201348 in): covers the grille and headlight corners but stops short of the fender flares. The compromise most trail/daily rigs end up with. Poison Spyder Brawler Mid, Rock Hard 4x4 Patriot Mid.\n\n**Full-width** (roughly 60 in, fender to fender): protects the flares and fender corners \u2014 the parts that get crushed sliding into a tree or dropping off a ledge sideways. Heaviest, and on a lifted TJ the outboard sections can interfere with 35s at full lock and full stuff. LOD Destroyer, Rock Hard Patriot full-width, GenRight's aluminum full-width. Best for overland rigs that carry a lot of front weight anyway and don't run extreme articulation.\n\nBash bars and grille hoops are a matter of use, not taste. A bar in front of the grille protects the radiator on a nose-first drop \u2014 genuinely useful for a rock rig. It also costs 3\u20135 degrees of approach angle on some designs, adds 10\u201315 lb, and blocks airflow slightly. Trail-and-daily rigs are better without one.\n\nA stubby steel bumper runs 55\u201375 lb; a full-width plate bumper with a bash bar runs 90\u2013130 lb. Add a winch (60\u201385 lb with rope and fairlead) and you've hung 120\u2013200 lb off the nose. On worn 100,000-mile factory coils that's \u00bd\u20131 in of sag, which also changes caster and can put the front driveshaft angle back into the range that whines. Plan for it: a \u00be in front coil spacer ($30\u2013$50), or heavier-rate front springs when you do the lift (see [TJ lift options](/db/tj/tj-lift-options/) and the [front coil spring guide](/db/tj/tj-front-coil-spring/)). Aluminum bumpers (GenRight and a few others) halve the weight for roughly double the price and no reduction in strength that matters for a bolt-on bumper.\n\n1. **Disconnect the battery negative terminal** if the rig has factory fog lights; you'll be cutting into their harness.\n2. **Unbolt the factory tow hooks and bumper** \u2014 two 18 mm bolts per side through the bumper into the frame horns. Rust-belt trucks: soak them the night before, and expect at least one to need a breaker bar. If a bolt snaps, the frame horn is open on the back so you can drill it out and run a bolt-and-nut.\n3. **Pull the fog lights** (Sahara/Rubicon/X) and unplug them at the harness connector. Most aftermarket bumpers accept 3\u20134 in round pod lights; the factory lamps only refit on bumpers that specifically list them.\n4. **Inspect the frame horns.** Look for rust-through, cracks at the tow-hook bolt holes, and the frame-to-crossmember welds. A winch pull loads all of this. Any rot here gets fixed before a winch goes on \u2014 this is the same inspection covered in the [recovery points guide](/db/tj/tj-recovery-points/).\n5. **Bolt the winch to the winch plate on the bench** if the design allows it \u2014 far easier than working the four 3/8 in bolts blind. Mount the fairlead. Route the winch power leads now, before the bumper goes on.\n6. **Set the bumper on a floor jack**, raise it into position, and start every mounting bolt by hand before torquing any. Center it: measure from each frame rail to the bumper end and match the two sides.\n7. **Torque the frame bolts** to the kit spec \u2014 typically 60\u201375 ft-lb for the grade-8 \u00bd in or M12 hardware most kits supply. Use the supplied bolts, not the factory ones, unless the instructions say otherwise.\n8. **Wire the lights and winch.** Winch positive goes to the battery through a disconnect or breaker if the kit supplies one; the negative to the battery, not the frame. Fog/pod lights get their own fused, relayed circuit off the battery \u2014 do not tap the factory fog light circuit for anything bigger than the OE lamps.\n9. **Check clearances** with the front axle at full droop and again at full compression (jack under the axle until the bump stops touch): bumper-to-grille, bumper-to-tire at full lock, fairlead-to-anything the rope could saw through.\n\n- **Shackle tabs.** Confirm the bumper has welded D-ring/shackle tabs with a \u00be in pin hole and a rated backing. Budget bumpers sometimes have decorative tabs welded to the face plate only \u2014 same failure mode as a face-mounted winch plate. Bolt-on frame-horn D-ring mounts are the fallback.\n- **Rubicon and 03\u201306 fog light wiring** is behind the bumper on a short pigtail. Extension harnesses ($20) save you from splicing.\n- **Powder coat vs. paint.** Powder coat chips and then rusts under the coating where you can't see it; touch every chip with cold galvanizing spray as it happens. A painted bumper is easier to keep on top of.\n- **Tire rub at full lock** on full-width bumpers with 35s \u2014 check with the steering at each lock and the axle stuffed before you finish torquing.\n- **Sway bar disconnects** on some aftermarket bumpers with low bash bars: the disconnect link swings forward at full droop and can hit the bar. Cycle it.\n\n| Path | Cost | What you get |\n|---|---|---|\n| Winch plate on factory bumper | $90\u2013$200 | A winch. No shackle tabs, no clearance gain. |\n| Stubby with winch plate (Smittybilt XRC 76800, Rugged Ridge XHD, Warn Rock Crawler) | $400\u2013$550 | Winch, shackle tabs, best tire clearance and approach |\n| Mid-width plate (Poison Spyder Brawler Mid, Rock Hard Patriot) | $600\u2013$800 | Grille corner protection, optional bash bar |\n| Full-width plate (LOD Destroyer, Rock Hard full-width, GenRight aluminum) | $700\u2013$1,100 | Fender protection, most weight (or aluminum at ~$1,300+) |\n\nInstall is 1\u20133 hours bolt-on for any of them; add an hour for fog light relocation and another for winch wiring. The $450 tier is not a stepping stone \u2014 the XRC and XHD are competent bumpers that most TJs never outgrow. The money above that buys thicker plate, cleaner welds, a better finish, and a bash bar, not a different capability. Spend the difference on the winch or the [rear bumper](/db/tj/tj-rear-bumper-guide/) instead."},{"id":"tj-rear-bumper-guide","title":"TJ Steel Rear Bumper \u2014 Tire Carrier, Recovery Points, and Hitch","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":950},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drill (for some kits)","floor jack"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","armor_bumper_rear","rear-bumper","tire-carrier","recovery","departure-angle"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt SRC Rear Bumper with Hitch and D-rings \u2014 TJ 76711","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Spartan Rear Bumper with Hitch \u2014 TJ 11542.10","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":620},{"name":"Poison Spyder Brawler Rear Bumper with Swing-Out Carrier \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":899}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Rear Bumper and Tire Carrier Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/tech-support/jeep-tj-rear-bumper-guide"},{"title":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Rear Bumper Review","url":"https://www.4x4reviews.com/iron-rock-tj-rear/"}],"alternatives":["tj-skid-plates","tj-front-bumper-guide"],"summary":"The TJ's factory rear bumper is a plastic fascia over a light bar \u2014 no recovery rigging points, no usable tire-carrier capacity, and an overhang that costs you departure angle. A steel bumper with integrated D-ring mounts is the floor. A swing-out carrier is what makes a 33-inch or larger spare survivable, because the factory tailgate hinges will not.","description":"Rear armor on a TJ buys three separate things, and it helps to decide which you actually need before shopping. Recovery rigging points, so you can be pulled out backward without hooking a strap to a bumper bracket. Spare-tire capacity, so a full-size 33 or 35 is not hanging on sheet metal. And geometry, because the plastic bumper's lower edge is the first thing to drag coming off an obstacle. Different bumpers deliver different combinations of those three, and the expensive ones are expensive because they deliver all of them.\n\nThe stock TJ rear is a plastic cover on a light steel beam. It has no shackle mounts, so there is nothing rated to pull on. Its hitch \u2014 when present \u2014 is a light-duty receiver sized for a small trailer, not for recovery, and pulling from a hitch receiver with a ball still installed is one of the recognized ways people get killed in a recovery.\n\nThe tailgate carrier is the other half of the problem. The TJ's tailgate hinges and the sheet metal around them were sized for the factory spare, roughly a 28- to 30-inch tire. Owners get away with a 31 or a light 32. Past that, the hinge load fatigues the tailgate skin and the hinge mounting area, and the failure shows up as a sagging gate that no longer latches square \u2014 a repair that costs more than the carrier would have.\n\nBumper-only with D-rings and a hitch. Smittybilt's SRC rear (76711) at around $300 and the Rugged Ridge Spartan (11542.10) at around $350 are the volume sellers. Both bring two integrated D-ring mounts and a 2-inch receiver, both bolt to the factory frame holes, and both install in about 90 minutes. Verify the D-ring mounts before buying \u2014 the cheapest units in this tier sometimes omit them, which removes the main reason to do the swap.\n\nBumper with swing-out tire carrier. [Iron Rock Offroad's carrier bumper](https://www.4x4reviews.com/iron-rock-tj-rear/) at roughly $620 and Poison Spyder's Brawler with swing-out at roughly $900 add a dedicated hinge rated for a full-size tire, with its own spindle bearing rather than a bolt through a plate. Install runs 2\u20133 hours. This is the tier to buy if you run 33s or larger, and the extra $300 over a bumper-only setup is the difference between a spare you can carry and a tailgate repair.\n\nCorner-and-rear combinations. Several makers sell rear bumpers with matching rear corner guards. If your trail use includes tight tree-lined lines or rock gardens, the corners protect the most commonly crushed sheet metal on a TJ, and buying the set at once usually costs less than adding corners later.\n\n- Replacement bumper and hardware\n- 15mm socket for the factory bumper bolts\n- Torque wrench\n- Thread locker (blue) for carrier hinge bolts\n- Floor jack or a second set of hands \u2014 a steel carrier bumper is 70\u2013120 lb\n- Wire brush and paint for the frame-rail contact surfaces\n\n1. **Remove the factory bumper** \u2014 typically six bolts through the frame ends. Support the bumper before the last two come out.\n2. **Remove the stock tailgate carrier** if you are relocating the spare. Note that the tailgate will need a delete plate or a factory-style cover over the hinge holes.\n3. **Disconnect trailer wiring** at the factory connector if present, and free any wiring routed along the crossmember.\n4. **Clean and inspect the frame ends.** This is the best look you will ever get at the rear frame rails; a TJ with salt history can have rot right where the bumper bolts, and finding it now is better than finding it under load.\n5. **Position the steel bumper and hand-thread every bolt** before torquing any of them. Steel bumpers have little slot tolerance and fighting one bolt at a time wastes an hour.\n6. **Torque frame mounting bolts to kit spec**, typically 50\u201365 ft-lb, working from the center outward.\n7. **Reconnect trailer wiring** to the factory or integrated connector, and relocate the license plate and its light to the new mount.\n8. **If fitting a swing-out carrier:** mount the tire before driving, then swing through the full arc and check clearance at the tailgate, the rear quarter panel, and the corner of the hardtop or soft-top bow. Set the latch so the arm loads against its stop, not against the latch pin.\n\nCarrier weight rating against real tire weight. A 35-inch tire on a beadlock wheel approaches 80 lb, and a 37 on a heavy wheel goes past it. Confirm the carrier's rating covers your combination, not the marketing tire size.\n\nHinge play from day one. A swing-out that has any perceptible rock at the spindle when new will hammer itself loose within a season of trail miles. Torque the spindle per spec and re-check it after the first few outings \u2014 this is the single most common carrier complaint and it is nearly always a maintenance item rather than a defect.\n\nDeparture angle. Full-width plate bumpers that tuck up behind the frame ends improve departure angle over the factory overhang; bumpers with a deep lower skirt or a receiver hanging below the tube can make it worse. If clearance is the reason you are buying, check the profile from the side before you order.\n\nFuel tank skid interaction. The TJ's tank skid sits immediately ahead of the rear bumper mounts, and some aftermarket bumpers and some aftermarket skids compete for the same frame holes. If you already run an aftermarket [skid plate](/db/tj/tj-skid-plates/) package, confirm compatibility before ordering.\n\nRigging discipline. D-ring mounts on the new bumper are rated; the receiver is not a recovery point unless you use a rated receiver shackle mount, and never with a ball installed. Pair this with the [recovery points](/db/tj/tj-recovery-points/) guide and rate your whole kit as a system rather than one part at a time.\n\nBumper-only with D-rings and hitch: $220\u2013360. Bumper with swing-out carrier rated for 33\u201335s: $600\u2013950. Add $40\u201380 for a tailgate hinge delete plate or cover, and $20\u201340 for relocating the license plate light. Shop installation runs $150\u2013350 if you would rather not wrestle 100 lb of steel alone.\n\nFor most TJ owners running 33s or larger, the swing-out tier is the correct purchase the first time. For a rig that stays on 31s and a stock spare, the bumper-only tier gets you rated recovery points and a real hitch for a third of the money. Either way, plan the rear as a pair with the [front bumper](/db/tj/tj-front-bumper-guide/) so the truck ends up with rated rigging at both ends rather than one."},{"id":"tj-rock-sliders","title":"TJ Rock Sliders \u2014 Frame-Mounted Protection for the Rocker Panels","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","impact wrench","drill","socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","drilling into frame (some kits)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","armor_sliders","rocker-panels","frame-protection","rock-crawler"],"parts":[{"name":"Poison Spyder Brawler Lite Rock Sliders \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":395},{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad bolt-on rock sliders \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Atlas Rock Sliders \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Crawler Conceptz Ultra Series sliders \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Crawler Conceptz","price_estimate_usd":580}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Rock Slider Comparison \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/rock-slider-buyers-guide/"},{"title":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Slider Install","url":"https://ironrockoffroad.com/instructions/"}],"alternatives":["tj-rear-bumper-guide","tj-skid-plates"],"summary":"The TJ's rocker panels are the lowest, widest point on the body \u2014 and entirely unprotected from the factory. Frame-mounted rock sliders take the hit instead and double as a step. Body-mount-only sliders are not worth buying.","description":"The rocker panel runs along the lower sill between the front and rear wheel openings. On the trail it's the first thing to contact a ledge, a boulder shelf, or a steep sidehillslide. Factory sheetmetal creases on first contact. A slider absorbs and redirects that force \u2014 the Jeep rides up over the obstacle on the slider tube rather than jamming the rocker into it.\n\nThe critical design detail is frame mounting. Sliders that only bolt to the body are nearly useless for rock use \u2014 when the rock hits, the load goes into the body sheetmetal instead of the frame, and you get bent rockers and bent sliders simultaneously. Frame-tied sliders route the load directly into the frame rails, which is where it belongs. Every quality slider in this category \u2014 Iron Rock, Poison Spyder, Crawler Conceptz \u2014 mounts to the frame. Budget sliders that only bolt to body sheet metal aren't worth the install time.\n\nTube diameter and wall thickness matter more than brand name. 1.75\" DOM tubing at .120\" wall is the minimum for trail use. 2\" .120\" wall is more common on quality sliders and notably stiffer. Anything under .095\" wall thickness compresses under rock load.\n\n- Slider kit with hardware\n- Impact wrench or breaker bar\n- Floor jack and stands\n- Drill with appropriate bit if kit requires drilling new frame holes ([Iron Rock does not \u2014 uses existing frame holes](https://ironrockoffroad.com/instructions/); Poison Spyder does on some configurations)\n\n1. **Lift and support the TJ.** Sliders install most straightforward with the vehicle at working height on jack stands.\n2. **Locate frame mounting points.** Most kits use existing holes in the frame rails. Clean rust or debris from the mounting surface.\n3. **Test fit before final torque.** Hold the slider in position with hand-tightened bolts, verify fitment at both ends and clearance to body panels.\n4. **Torque frame bolts to spec** \u2014 typically 40\u201355 ft-lb on grade-8 hardware. Snug but don't strip.\n5. **Torque body mounting points last.** Body mounts go snug, not tight \u2014 they're secondary load paths.\n6. **Verify ground clearance is acceptable.** Frame-mounted sliders typically add 0\u20132\" below the rocker, which is fine. Full skid-style sliders drop lower and affect approach on flat obstacles.\n\n- Step tubes (the horizontal tube people stand on) create a vulnerable catch point \u2014 if you're building a rock crawler that will be on its side regularly, a full rocker guard without a step tube provides cleaner rock-over geometry.\n- Check that slider doesn't interfere with stock exhaust hangers or transfer case skid plate. Some kits require relocating a hanger.\n- Verify the slider doesn't contact the fuel tank at full articulation on extended-wheelbase TJs.\n\nBudget frame-mounted (Iron Rock Offroad, Rugged Ridge): $260\u2013$310. Mid-tier (Poison Spyder Brawler Lite): $380\u2013$420. Premium (Crawler Conceptz, Shrockworks): $550\u2013$700. The price difference mostly reflects wall thickness, finish quality, and weld quality \u2014 all are functional. For moderate trail use, Iron Rock Offroad is the best value."},{"id":"tj-skid-plates","title":"TJ Skid Plates \u2014 What the Factory Already Covers, What It Doesn't, and the Tummy Tuck","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":1500},"tools_required":["floor jack and jack stands (or a transmission jack for the belly skid)","socket set with 15mm and 18mm deep sockets","torque wrench","drill and step bit (some engine skids)","angle grinder (belly-up exhaust clearance)","blue thread locker"],"skills_required":["basic undercarriage work","driveline angle setup (belly-up only)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","armor_skid","skid-plate","transfer-case","oil-pan","gas-tank","belly-protection","tummy-tuck","belly-up","steering-box"],"parts":[{"name":"Rugged Ridge steering box skid plate \u2014 97-06 TJ","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Skid Row Offroad engine / transmission skid plate \u2014 4.0L TJ (year-split 97-02 / 03-06)","vendor":"Skid Row Offroad","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Rugged Ridge heavy-duty gas tank skid plate \u2014 97-06 TJ","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Rugged Ridge / Poison Spyder evap canister skid \u2014 03-06 TJ/LJ","vendor":"Rugged Ridge / Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Under Cover Fabworks transfer case skid, 3/16in steel, no-body-lift version (year-split)","vendor":"UCF","price_estimate_usd":365},{"name":"Savvy Off Road aluminum belly-up skid \u2014 TJ/LJ","vendor":"Savvy Off Road","price_estimate_usd":550},{"name":"Poison Spyder BellyUp skid plate kit \u2014 TJ/LJ (year-split)","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Rear differential cover, heavy cast (Dana 44 or Dana 35)","vendor":"Solid Axle / Riddler / ARB","price_estimate_usd":130}],"citations":[{"title":"The Official Tummy Tuck FAQ Thread (WranglerTJForum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/the-official-tummy-tuck-faq-thread.173/"},{"title":"UCF transfer case skid for 97-02 TJ, no body lift required","url":"https://undercoverfab.com/no-body-lift-needed-skids-/262-transfer-case-skid-for-97-02-tj-no-body-lift-req-d6061-t6-aluminum.html"},{"title":"Skid Row Offroad engine/transmission skid, 03-06 TJ 4.0L","url":"https://skidrowoffroad.com/products/jeep-tj-wrangler-engine-transmission-skid-plate-2003-2006-6-cylinder"},{"title":"Transfer case skid plate opinions \u2014 factory pan vs. belly-up (WranglerTJForum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/looking-for-opinions-on-transfer-case-skid-plates.29861/"}],"alternatives":["tj-sye-kit","tj-sliders","tj-front-bumper-guide","tj-np231-tj"],"summary":"The TJ already has a skid plate under the transfer case and another under the gas tank from the factory \u2014 the belly pan is the big stamped crossmember that hangs an inch below the frame rails, and the tank sits in a steel tray. The parts that ship unprotected are the oil pan and steering box up front, the rear differential cover, and (on 03\u201306) the evap canister behind the tank. Armor those for $80\u2013$450 and a stock-height TJ is covered. The bigger question is the belly pan itself: it protects the transfer case fine, but it's the lowest point on the Jeep and it's what you drag over every ledge. Replacing it with a flat \"belly-up\" skid \u2014 the tummy tuck \u2014 [is the single biggest clearance gain available on a TJ](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/the-official-tummy-tuck-faq-thread.173/) short of bigger tires, and it costs $1,000\u2013$1,500 all-in once the driveline is sorted.","description":"Start by getting under the Jeep with a flashlight. From the front: the 4.0's oil pan hangs behind the front axle with nothing under it. The steering box sits on the frame rail at the driver's front corner, with its sector shaft pointed down and exposed. Behind those, the transmission is covered by the factory belly pan, which is both the crossmember carrying the transmission mount and the skid \u2014 stamped steel, a hair under 1/8 in, and hanging about an inch below the bottom of the frame. It works: a stock TJ can grind that pan over rock for years without hurting the NP231. What it costs is breakover angle, because everything behind the front axle pivots on that low point. Further back, the gas tank sits in a stamped-steel skid bolted to the frame \u2014 again, thin but real. On 03\u201306 Jeeps, the evap canister moved from the engine bay to a bracket near the rear frame and hangs low enough to get torn off. At the very back, the rear differential's stamped cover is the lowest point aft of the axle.\n\nThat inventory sets the priority. A bolt-on transfer case skid that mounts *under* the factory pan \u2014 the $100\u2013$150 \"heavy-duty TC skid\" sold by several brands \u2014 adds a half inch of steel below the lowest point on the Jeep. It protects something the factory already protected and gives up clearance to do it. Skip it.\n\n**1. Steering box skid ($60\u2013$90, 30 minutes).** The sector shaft and the box's lower cover take direct hits on a drop-off; a cracked box is a tow home and $300\u2013$500 for a replacement. Rugged Ridge's bolts to the existing frame holes with no drilling. This is the first skid on any TJ that leaves pavement.\n\n**2. Engine/oil pan skid ($250\u2013$320, 1\u20132 hours).** [Skid Row Offroad's is the standard](https://skidrowoffroad.com/products/jeep-tj-wrangler-engine-transmission-skid-plate-2003-2006-6-cylinder) and it's year-split (97\u201302 and 03\u201306 differ at the front crossmember). It bolts between the frame rails under the pan and front of the transmission. The 4.0 pan is stamped steel and a rock through it empties the engine in under a minute; it also protects the pan from the bump-stop-to-axle contact on a hard front hit. Some designs need two holes drilled in the frame.\n\n**3. Rear differential cover ($100\u2013$160, 1 hour plus fluid).** Dana 35 or Dana 44, a heavy cast cover (Solid Axle, Riddler, ARB) replaces the stamped one that dents inward into the ring gear. Do it with the next [differential fluid change](/db/tj/tj-differential-fluid/) and it's a fluid change plus twenty minutes.\n\n**4. Evap canister skid, 03\u201306 only ($70\u2013$110, 30 minutes).** The plastic canister behind the tank is the most commonly destroyed part on a late TJ that wheels; a torn hose sets a check-engine light and an evap code that never clears. Rugged Ridge and Poison Spyder both make a bolt-on cage.\n\n**5. Gas tank skid upgrade ($150\u2013$250, 2\u20133 hours, tank drop required).** The factory tray is adequate at stock height. A heavier 3/16 in replacement is worth it on a rig that regularly drags the rear over ledges \u2014 dents in the factory skid transfer to the plastic tank and eventually crease it. Most owners do this one only after the factory tray is already deformed.\n\n**6. The tummy tuck.** Covered below. It replaces the factory belly pan, so it also *is* your transfer case and transmission skid when you're done.\n\nRocker protection is armor too, and on a TJ it comes before most of this list; it's a separate guide because it's a separate job \u2014 see [TJ rock sliders](/db/tj/tj-sliders/).\n\nA belly-up skid is a flat plate, \u00bc in steel or \u215c in aluminum, that bolts to the frame rails where the factory pan did and carries the transmission mount 1\u20132 in higher, with a flat bottom that's flush with or above the frame rails. The result is 1.5\u20132.5 in more clearance at the lowest point and a breakover angle that lets a 33-in-tire TJ go where 35s went before. It is also the job that turns into a project, because raising the transmission and transfer case moves everything attached to them:\n\n- **The driveline angle changes.** Raising the transfer case tips the rear output down toward the pinion less \u2014 good if you have an [SYE and CV driveshaft](/db/tj/tj-sye-kit/) (the belly-up makes the geometry better); bad if you still have the factory slip-yoke driveshaft, which is already marginal on a lifted TJ and will vibrate. Plan on the SYE first. Adjustable rear upper arms or shims to reset pinion angle afterward.\n- **The engine has to come up with the transmission.** A 1 in motor mount lift ($60\u2013$120, Brown Dog or M.O.R.E.) is required with most belly-up skids so the drivetrain rises as one unit; a 1 in body lift ($80\u2013$150) is required with some designs to keep the transfer case out of the tub and the fan out of the shroud. Skids labeled \"no body lift required\" ([UCF's No-BL](https://undercoverfab.com/no-body-lift-needed-skids-/262-transfer-case-skid-for-97-02-tj-no-body-lift-req-d6061-t6-aluminum.html), Savvy) get their clearance with less rise, and they're the right choice for most owners.\n- **The exhaust hits the tub.** Every tummy tuck needs the exhaust re-routed where it crosses under the transmission \u2014 a muffler shop bend, a tuck kit ($80\u2013$150), or a grinder and a flap wheel on the tub seam.\n- **The shifter linkage binds.** The factory transfer case shift linkage doesn't tolerate the case moving up; a cable shifter ($120\u2013$200, Novak or the skid maker's) fixes it and most kits assume you'll add one.\n- **Year and model split.** 97\u201302 and 03\u201306 pans differ, and the Rubicon's NV241OR is physically larger than the NP231 \u2014 buy the skid for your year and case. It will not swap.\n\n**Cost, honestly:** skid $350\u2013$600, MML $100, body lift $100 if needed, cable shifter $150, exhaust $100\u2013$200, and if you don't already have one, an SYE and CV driveshaft at $500\u2013$800. Call it $900 with the SYE already done, $1,300\u2013$1,500 without. A weekend of work, most of it under the Jeep on a creeper.\n\n**Procedure outline:** support the transmission with a jack, unbolt the transmission mount from the factory pan, remove the six pan-to-frame bolts (15 mm and 18 mm heads, two sizes on most years), drop the pan, raise the drivetrain with the MML installed, bolt the new skid to the frame (start every bolt before torquing; the kit's hardware, typically 35\u201350 ft-lb depending on bolt size), lower the transmission onto the new mount location, then set the exhaust, shifter, and pinion angle. Then check the fan-to-shroud gap, the transfer case-to-tub gap at full articulation, and the front driveshaft at full droop.\n\n1. **Lift the Jeep and support the frame** on stands; ramps work for the steering box and engine skids.\n2. **Hold the skid up with a floor jack** or a helper \u2014 the engine skid is 40\u201360 lb and awkward.\n3. **Start every bolt by hand** before tightening any; multi-point skids need all the holes lined up at once.\n4. **Torque to the kit spec** with blue thread locker on anything going into a nut plate \u2014 vibration backs skid bolts out, and a skid hanging by two bolts is a plow.\n5. **Check clearance to the exhaust, driveshafts, and steering** at ride height and with the axle drooped. A skid that touches the exhaust rattles forever.\n\n- **The under-the-pan \"TC skid\" trap** described above. Protection you already have, clearance you lose.\n- **Aluminum vs. steel belly-up.** Aluminum saves 20\u201330 lb and doesn't rust; it gouges and can crack at the mount tabs under repeated point loads. Steel at \u00bc in is the crawler answer; aluminum at \u215c in is the overland answer.\n- **Drainage.** A flat belly skid without a drain slot holds water and mud against the transmission. Good designs have slots; drill two \u00bc in holes at the low points if yours doesn't.\n- **Steering box skid interference** with some aftermarket [front bumpers](/db/tj/tj-front-bumper-guide/) and with hydro-assist ram mounts. Test fit before painting.\n- **Heat.** An engine skid boxes in the oil pan; the 4.0 doesn't care in normal use, but verify the skid isn't touching the exhaust downpipe, which will glow.\n- **Lifting the Jeep by the skids** with a floor jack is fine on a belly-up skid with a jacking pad and a bad idea on a stamped factory pan.\n\n| Item | Cost | Time | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Steering box skid (Rugged Ridge) | $60\u2013$90 | 30 min | First |\n| Engine / oil pan skid (Skid Row) | $250\u2013$320 | 1\u20132 hr | Second |\n| Rear diff cover (Solid / Riddler / ARB) | $100\u2013$160 | 1 hr | Third |\n| Evap canister skid, 03\u201306 | $70\u2013$110 | 30 min | Third (late TJ) |\n| Gas tank skid, heavy | $150\u2013$250 | 2\u20133 hr | When the factory one is dented |\n| Belly-up skid, all-in (MML, shifter, exhaust; SYE done) | $900\u2013$1,100 | Weekend | When clearance is the limit |\n| Belly-up skid, all-in with SYE + CV shaft | $1,300\u2013$1,500 | Weekend + | Same |\n\nSkid Row, UCF, Savvy, and Poison Spyder sell direct; Rugged Ridge is at every Jeep retailer. Bolt-on TC skids that hang under the factory pan are the one thing on this list to leave on the shelf."},{"id":"tj-sliders","title":"Rock Sliders and Rocker Guards for the TJ","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":699},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill","angle grinder","welder (weld-on kits)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","drilling into frame (bolt-on)","welding (weld-on kits)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","sliders","rockers","rock-crawling","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 TJ Wrangler Patriot Series Tube Rock Sliders RH-90203","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":379},{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Wrangler Rocker Guards (weld-on)","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"GenRight TJ Rocker Protection Sliders (bolt-on)","vendor":"GenRight Offroad","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Smittybilt SRC Classic Rock Sliders (universal, TJ fitment)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"Pirate4x4 \u2014 TJ Slider Comparison and Install Thread","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/forum/jeep-wrangler-tj/tj-rock-sliders-comparison/"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 TJ Slider Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.rockhard4x4.com/instructions"},{"title":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Rocker Guard Overview","url":"https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/tj-rockers"}],"alternatives":["tj-skid-plates","tj-front-bumper"],"summary":"The TJ's rockers are the lowest structural point of the body \u2014 sliders protect them from side-rock contact that would otherwise cave in sheet metal and damage the unibody sill.","description":"The TJ is a monocoque (unibody) construction, not body-on-frame. That distinction matters enormously for slider selection: when a slider takes a hit, the load transfers directly into the frame rails, not a separate body mount. A slider that's poorly designed or bolted to marginal mounting points can actually transmit rock impact into the unibody sill and cause more damage than it prevents. This is why cheap universal sliders on TJs are a gamble \u2014 the mounting geometry has to match the TJ's specific frame rail geometry to transfer loads correctly.\n\n[Weld-on sliders (Iron Rock Offroad)](https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/tj-rockers) provide the most positive connection to the frame and allow a fabricator to optimize gusset placement and tube angles. They're the choice for dedicated rock crawlers where protecting the rocker panel at all costs matters more than reversibility. The weld-on joint can be ground smooth and painted to be nearly invisible. Bolt-on sliders (Rock Hard 4x4, GenRight) use the existing frame rail holes and tie into structural mounting points without welding. They're fully removable, which is relevant for anyone who street-drives the TJ regularly and wants the option to swap them out.\n\nThe TJ's narrow 64.5\" track width means sliders need to extend far enough outboard to cover the entire rocker panel \u2014 a shallow slider that doesn't reach the door sill edge will miss the contact point on a side-rock scrape. [Rock Hard 4x4's tube design](https://www.rockhard4x4.com/instructions) extends well past the sill and uses 1.75\" DOM tubing that can support the full weight of the vehicle for high-centering recovery. GenRight's approach is a plate-style guard rather than tube, which provides broader contact area across the sill at the expense of weight. Both are appropriate for serious trail use; tube sliders are lighter and shed mud better.","steps":["Clean the frame rail mounting surfaces with a wire brush and degreaser.","Position slider against the frame rails to check fitment before drilling or welding \u2014 mark mounting hole locations with a center punch.","For bolt-on kits: drill frame holes to kit specification (typically 1/2\" or 7/16\"). Install mounting hardware through frame rails. Start all fasteners hand-tight before torquing any.","For weld-on kits: tack weld the slider in place and verify fitment (parallel to the rocker panel, correct height) before running full welds. Grind and treat weld surfaces against rust.","Torque bolt-on fasteners to kit specification \u2014 typically 45\u201355 ft-lbs on 1/2\" grade-8 hardware.","Verify the slider can support vehicle weight: place a jack under the slider near the midpoint and raise the vehicle slightly to confirm it holds without deforming.","Confirm clearance to fuel lines, brake lines, and any OEM plastic cladding that needs trimming."],"pros":["Protects the TJ's unibody rockers from side-rock damage that would otherwise be expensive to repair","Tube-style sliders double as a high-centering recovery point and step for entry/exit","Bolt-on designs are fully reversible for street use or future sale","Rock Hard 4x4 and Iron Rock Offroad are TJ-specific fitments \u2014 no modification required for correct geometry"],"cons":["Weld-on installation requires welding skill or a fabrication shop \u2014 not a first-time project","Adds 35\u201355 lbs of unsprung weight depending on design","Some designs reduce ground clearance under the rocker by 1\"\u20132\" compared to stock sheet metal","Cheap universal sliders may not transfer load correctly to the TJ's unibody frame rails \u2014 avoid","Installation can interfere with factory step pads and door weather sealing on some builds"]},{"id":"tj-axle-swap","title":"Axle Swap Paths for the TJ (D44, Ford 8.8, D60)","vehicle":"tj","category":"axles","subcategory":"axle_swap","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":10,"high":40},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":6000},"tools_required":["welder","floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","grinder"],"skills_required":["fabrication","brake and driveline plumbing","gear setup or a shop"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["axles","drivetrain","swap"],"parts":[{"name":"TJ Rubicon Dana 44 (front and/or rear take-out)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":1800},{"name":"Ford 8.8 rear (junkyard + brackets)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":500},{"name":"Dana 60 set (1-ton, used)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":3000}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep axle reference \u2014 List of Jeep axles","url":"https://jeep.fandom.com/wiki/List_of_Jeep_axles"},{"title":"TJ drivetrain specs \u2014 My Jeep TJ","url":"https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/"}],"alternatives":["tj-dana35-failures","tj-dana44-rear","tj-regear"],"summary":"When you outgrow the Dana 30/35, there are three honest paths, and tire size picks the path \u2014 not budget. For 33s, reinforce what you have and stop reading. For 35s, a Ford 8.8 rear or a Dana 44 pair is the answer. For 37s and hard rock, one-ton Dana 60s are the end game. Buy for where the build is going, because doing perches, brackets, brakes, gears, and driveshafts twice costs more than buying the right axle once.","description":"An axle swap is the most involved drivetrain work you can do to a TJ. Perches and control-arm brackets have to be cut off and re-welded at the correct pinion angle, brake and parking-brake lines have to be re-plumbed, the driveshaft length changes, and both ends need gears set up to the same ratio. None of that scales with the price of the axle \u2014 the labor is nearly identical whether you bolt in a $400 junkyard rear or a $6,000 built 60. That is the whole argument for matching the axle to your final tire goal on the first pass.\n\nBefore shopping, confirm the axles under the truck. [A non-Rubicon TJ (1997\u20132006) runs a Dana 30 front](https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/) \u2014 7.125-inch ring gear, 27-spline shafts, unit-bearing hubs, 5-on-4.5 bolt pattern \u2014 and a Dana 35 rear with a 7.56-inch ring gear and 27-spline C-clip shafts. [A 2003\u20132006 Rubicon runs Dana 44 front and rear](https://jeep.fandom.com/wiki/List_of_Jeep_axles) with 30-spline shafts, 4.10 gears, and factory air lockers. Some non-Rubicon TJs left the factory with an optional Dana 44 rear; look for a 10-bolt oval cover and a stamped tag rather than trusting the seller.\n\nRatio matters as much as the housing. Factory ratios run 3.07, 3.55, 3.73, 4.10, and 4.56 on late SE autos. A 3.07-geared TJ is the trap \u2014 it feels fine stock and becomes undriveable on 33s, so check the door tag or count the ring-and-pinion teeth before you spend a dollar. The [regear guide](/db/tj/tj-regear/) covers ratio selection; the [Dana 35 failure modes](/db/tj/tj-dana35-failures/) guide covers whether yours is worth keeping at all.\n\nA 1995\u20132001 Ford Explorer 8.8 is the cheapest large step up and the most common TJ rear swap for a reason. It brings an 8.8-inch ring gear, 31-spline non-C-clip shafts, and factory rear disc brakes, and its wheel-mounting-surface width lands within roughly an inch of the TJ Dana 35 \u2014 close enough that most owners run it with no spacer change. The bolt pattern is 5-on-4.5, the same as the Jeep.\n\nThe work is bracketry. Pull the Explorer perches and shock mounts, weld on a TJ coil-perch and lower-control-arm bracket kit (Ruffstuff, Artec, and Barnes 4WD all sell one for $120\u2013250), set the pinion angle, and re-plumb the brake line and parking-brake cables to the Jeep's routing. Gears and a locker go in while the housing is out \u2014 an 8.8 takes an Eaton TrueTrac, an ARB, or a Yukon Grizzly, and aftermarket support is deep and cheap because of the Mustang crowd.\n\nHonest limits: the 8.8 is a rear-only answer. Pair it with a reinforced Dana 30 for 33s or a Dana 44 front for 35s, and understand that a strong rear behind a stock front usually relocates the breakage rather than ending it.\n\nA Dana 44 pair is the \"do it right\" route for a 35-inch build. Three sub-paths exist. Rubicon take-out 44s are the cleanest \u2014 correct width, correct brackets, factory lockers, and 4.10 gears already installed \u2014 and used sets run roughly $2,500\u20134,000 depending on condition and whether the lockers work. Aftermarket housings from Currie (RockJock 44), Dynatrac (ProRock 44), and TeraFlex (Tera44) start around $3,500 per axle bare and climb past $6,000 built, but come with thicker tubes, better shaft options, and a high-pinion front. The budget sub-path \u2014 a junkyard Dana 44 from a Wagoneer or a TJ 44 rear \u2014 is the cheapest 44 but drags width and bracket work with it.\n\nThe front 44 is the one that justifies the cost. A Dana 30 front with [chromoly shafts](/db/tj/tj-chromoly-shafts/) survives 33s and most 35s if you are honest with the throttle, but a locked front on 35s in rock is where the 30's small ring gear and thin tubes give up. If the build includes a front [locker](/db/tj/tj-lockers/), the 44 front stops being optional.\n\nA front and rear Dana 60 set \u2014 usually a Ford Super Duty front and a Chevy or Ford rear, re-geared and re-bracketed \u2014 is the durable answer for 37s and up. The 9.75-inch ring gear and 35-spline shafts stop being the weak link in almost any TJ build.\n\nWhat people underestimate is width and weight. A 60 set is roughly 69\u201370 inches wheel-mounting-surface to wheel-mounting-surface against the TJ's ~60 inches, so the track grows close to nine inches. That means highline or heavily flared fenders, new wheels with different backspacing, and a rig that no longer sits inside a standard trailer the way it did. The axles are also 8-lug unless you convert, and each end is 150\u2013250 pounds heavier than what it replaces, which means re-valved shocks and heavier springs. This is a dedicated-trail-rig decision. Running 60s under a street-driven TJ on 35s is spending 1-ton money to carry 1-ton weight for no added capability.\n\nFour items sink axle-swap budgets, and all four apply to every path above.\n\nBrakes and the parking brake. Every swap changes caliper or drum plumbing, and Explorer and truck parking-brake cables do not match the Jeep's routing. Budget $150\u2013350 for lines, a proportioning fix if you go rear disc from rear drum, and cable adapters.\n\nDriveshafts. Any rear axle change alters pinion angle and shaft length. A TJ's rear shaft is short to begin with on a 93.4-inch wheelbase, so if the truck is lifted you are also into a [slip-yoke eliminator and CV shaft](/db/tj/tj-sye-kit/) at $600\u2013900. Set the rear pinion 1\u20133 degrees below the driveshaft centerline with adjustable upper arms.\n\nGear setup. Both ends must run the same ratio, and a mismatched pair will destroy a transfer case. Unless you own a dial indicator, a bearing press, and a setup bearing kit, pay a shop $300\u2013500 per axle. A howling diff is a full teardown redo.\n\nWheel fitment. Backspacing changes with every one of these axles, and the wrong offset puts the tire into the control arm at full lock or outside the fender. Sort wheels and [tire fitment](/db/tj/tj-tire-fitment-35s/) before the axle is under the truck, not after.\n\nFord 8.8 rear, DIY: $150\u2013400 for the axle, $120\u2013250 for brackets, $180\u2013250 for gears, $80\u2013150 for an install kit, $250\u2013400 for a locker, $300\u2013500 for setup labor, $150\u2013300 for brake and cable work. All-in roughly $1,200\u20132,200 depending on how much welding you do yourself.\n\nRubicon Dana 44 pair, used take-outs: $2,500\u20134,000 for the axles, plus regear if the 4.10s do not match your tires ($700\u20131,100 for both ends with setup), plus brake and driveshaft work. All-in $3,500\u20136,000.\n\nAftermarket Dana 44 pair, new and built: $9,000\u201314,000 with gears, lockers, and shafts installed.\n\nOne-ton Dana 60 pair, used and re-worked: $3,000\u20135,000 for cores, $2,000\u20133,500 for gears, lockers, and setup on both ends, plus wheels, fenders, driveshafts, and steering. Realistically $8,000\u201312,000 before the first trail run.\n\nOn 33s, do not swap. Reinforce the Dana 30, add chromoly shafts if you are locked, and regear. On 35s, an 8.8 rear plus a reinforced or 44 front is the value answer and a Rubicon 44 pair is the clean one \u2014 see the [Dana 44 rear](/db/tj/tj-dana44-rear/) guide for the rear-specific details. On 37s and up with a locked front and rock use, go to 60s and accept everything that comes with them. The wrong answer at every tire size is swapping one end, running it a season, and then swapping the other \u2014 that is two rounds of brackets, brakes, gears, and driveshafts to reach a place one decision could have taken you."},{"id":"tj-chromoly-shafts","title":"Chromoly Shafts and C-Clip Eliminators (TJ)","vehicle":"tj","category":"axles","subcategory":"shafts","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","axle shaft puller","torque wrench","snap-ring pliers"],"skills_required":["axle work"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["axles","drivetrain","reliability"],"parts":[{"name":"Front chromoly shaft set (Dana 30)","vendor":"Yukon/RCV","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Rear chromoly shafts + c-clip eliminator (Dana 35)","vendor":"Yukon/Superior","price_estimate_usd":400},{"name":"U-joints (Spicer / RCV CV)","vendor":"Spicer/RCV","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep axle reference \u2014 List of Jeep axles","url":"https://jeep.fandom.com/wiki/List_of_Jeep_axles"},{"title":"TJ drivetrain specs \u2014 My Jeep TJ","url":"https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/"}],"alternatives":["tj-dana35-failures","dana30-tj","tj-regear"],"summary":"Shafts are where a TJ axle actually breaks. The front Dana 30's stock u-joints and the rear Dana 35's c-clip shafts are the failure points once you add tire and traction. Chromoly shafts and a rear c-clip eliminator are the targeted, affordable fix that keeps a stock-housing axle alive on 33s \u2014 cheaper than an axle swap, and they address the dangerous failure mode.","description":"The two weak shafts fail differently. The front Dana 30 snaps a u-joint under steering load with big tires; the rear Dana 35's c-clip shaft snaps and, because the c-clip is the only thing retaining it, the wheel can leave the vehicle. Both are upgradeable without replacing the housing, in a driveway, with the tools most owners already have plus a slide hammer.\n\nFactory TJ shafts are induction-hardened 1040-series steel. Aftermarket \"chromoly\" shafts are 4340 alloy, through-hardened, and in most kits carry a larger-radius spline runout and a smoother transition at the c-clip groove \u2014 that geometry matters as much as the alloy, because the groove is where the rear shaft cracks. Expect a 25\u201335 percent strength gain over stock, which is the difference between a shaft that survives a locked 33-inch tire hooking a ledge and one that does not. [The front Dana 30's inner and outer shafts are 27-spline](https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/); the outer joins the inner with a 1310-series u-joint, Spicer 5-760X, and the joint \u2014 not the shaft \u2014 is the piece that lets go first. [The rear Dana 35 shafts are 27-spline](https://jeep.fandom.com/wiki/List_of_Jeep_axles) and about 1.18 inches in diameter; nothing about a chromoly shaft changes the 7.56-inch ring gear or the thin tubes, so this is a 33-inch fix, not a 35-inch one. Read the [Dana 35](/db/tj/tj-dana35-failures/) guide before you buy if 35s are in your future.\n\nChromoly front shafts with quality Spicer u-joints (or RCV CV joints for the serious crawler) take far more abuse than the stock parts and resist the snap-under-steering failure. This is the cheapest meaningful front-axle reliability gain on a TJ. Yukon's 4340 Dana 30 TJ kit (inner and outer shafts both sides, with 5-760X joints installed) runs $500\u2013650; Ten Factory's equivalent is $420\u2013520; G2 and Alloy USA land in the same range. Use the non-greaseable Spicer 5-760X when you replace joints on their own ($15\u201320 each) \u2014 the solid cross is stronger than the greaseable version, whose grease passage is a stress riser. For a locked front on 35s, the RCV Ultimate CV set ($1,200\u20131,400 a pair) replaces the u-joint with a constant-velocity joint that does not bind at full lock and carries a lifetime warranty; it is overkill on 33s with an open front. The [Dana 30](/db/tj/dana30-tj/) guide covers ball joints and the unit bearings you will inspect while the shafts are out.\n\nA rear c-clip eliminator kit converts the Dana 35 to a bearing-and-retainer setup so a broken shaft stays in the housing instead of sending the wheel down the trail. The kit supplies shafts with a pressed-on bearing and a retainer plate that bolts to the brake backing-plate studs at the tube end; the factory tube bearing and seal come out, and the c-clips are gone for good. Yukon, Superior, and Ten Factory sell chromoly 27-spline rear shafts with the eliminator as one package, $400\u2013550. Paired with chromoly rear shafts, it makes the Dana 35 trustworthy on 33s with a locker \u2014 though if you are headed to 35s, see the [axle swap](/db/tj/tj-axle-swap/) guide, because the money is better spent once. The Super 35 alternative (30-spline shafts, requires a 30-spline carrier and locker) is the same idea one size up at $900\u20131,300, and is what to buy if the locker is going in at the same time anyway.\n\nChock the rear, lift the front, and pull the wheels. Remove the caliper (two 13mm bolts) and hang it from the coil, then the rotor. The unit bearing comes off with three 13mm 12-point bolts from behind the knuckle; they are tight and often seized, so a long ratchet and penetrating oil the night before help. Pull the hub and the outer shaft comes with it \u2014 inspect the hub bearing for play and the seal for weeping while it is in your hand. The inner shaft slides straight out of the tube; a light tug on the shaft or a slide hammer on the outer stub frees it. Grease the new inner splines, slide the new assembly in until the splines seat in the side gear, reinstall the unit bearing, and torque the three bolts to 75 ft-lbs. Check the axle tube seal on the way in \u2014 a torn seal lets diff oil wander down the tube. Top off the front with 1.2 quarts of 80W-90. Both sides take 2\u20133 hours the first time.\n\nLift the rear, pull the wheels and drums, and drain the diff by pulling the cover (ten bolts, catch pan ready). Remove the 8mm pinion-shaft lock bolt, slide the cross shaft out, push each axle shaft inward, and fish out the c-clips. Pull the shafts. For the eliminator, drive the old bearing and seal out of the tube end with a slide-hammer bearing puller, clean the bore, and install the new shafts with their pressed bearings, bolting the retainer plates to the backing-plate studs at 35 ft-lbs. Reinstall the cross shaft and torque the lock bolt to 8\u201310 ft-lbs with a drop of thread locker \u2014 that bolt backing out is a known Dana 35 failure on its own. Clean the cover and housing flange, run a bead of RTV, and refill with 1.6 quarts of 80W-90 (75W-140 plus friction modifier if there is a Trac-Lok in it). Reset the [rear drum brakes](/db/tj/tj-rear-drum-service/) while the drums are off. Plan 3\u20134 hours.\n\nIf you run 33s and keep the factory axles, do the shafts before the first hard trip \u2014 they address the failure that strands (or endangers) you. If you are already planning a Ford 8.8 or Dana 44 swap, skip the rear shafts and put the money there instead. The rule of thumb: shafts and a c-clip eliminator are the right call when you intend to keep the factory housings; if a 35-inch build (and therefore a housing swap) is already on the horizon, spend the axle budget once on the swap rather than twice. Front chromoly shafts, on the other hand, transfer to any Dana 30 you keep, so they are never wasted money.\n\nFront: Ten Factory or Yukon chromoly Dana 30 kit with joints, $420\u2013650; Spicer 5-760X joints alone, $15\u201320 each; RCV Ultimate CV set, $1,200\u20131,400. Rear: chromoly 27-spline shafts with c-clip eliminator, $400\u2013550 (Yukon, Superior, Ten Factory); Super 35 kit, $900\u20131,300 with a 30-spline locker. Consumables: 80W-90 gear oil for both ends, RTV, a tube of moly grease, and a slide-hammer bearing puller ($40\u201360 or a loaner from the parts store). Every one of these is sold direct or through the usual Jeep parts houses; buy the joints from a driveline shop rather than the cheapest listing, because counterfeit Spicer joints are common enough to matter."},{"id":"dana30-tj","title":"TJ Dana 30 \u2014 Stock Rating and Upgrade Options","vehicle":"tj","category":"axles","subcategory":"front_axle","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","ball joint press","axle puller"],"skills_required":["axle shaft service","u-joint replacement","diff fluid change"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["dana-30","front-axle","shaft-upgrade","ball-joints","reverse-cut"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon chromoly axle shafts (27-spline pair)","part_number":"YA W24164","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":550},{"name":"Spicer 5-297X U-joints (pair)","part_number":"5-297X","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Synergy heavy-duty ball joints","part_number":"8009","vendor":"Synergy MFG","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Yukon Grizzly Locker (27-spline)","part_number":"YGLD30-3-27","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":620},{"name":"Yukon high-pinion (reverse-rotation) ring & pinion set, Dana 30","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":350}],"citations":[{"title":"The Jeep Dana 30 \u2014 The Ranger Station","url":"https://www.therangerstation.com/tech/the-jeep-dana-30/"},{"title":"Dana 30, Dana 35, and Dana 44 factory specifications","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/dana-30-dana-35-and-dana-44-factory-specifications.224/"},{"title":"High-pinion vs low-pinion Dana axles explained","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/blog/high-pinion-vs-low-pinion-axles"}],"alternatives":["tj-axle-swap","tj-chromoly-shafts","tj-regear"],"summary":"The TJ Dana 30 will live behind 33s with stock shafts and behind 35s if you upgrade to chromoly shafts and stronger U-joints \u2014 but the moment you add a front locker, the weak link shifts to the axle shafts and ball joints. [It's a high-pinion (reverse-cut) axle](https://www.yukongear.com/blog/high-pinion-vs-low-pinion-axles), which matters the day you order gears \u2014 a low-pinion Dana 30/44 gear set will not work here.","description":"Every TJ except the Rubicon left the factory with a front Dana 30 (the Rubicon got a Dana 44 front with 30-spline shafts and a factory Tru-Lok locker from the factory \u2014 this guide is for the standard Dana 30, not the Rubicon axle). It's a high-pinion, or \"reverse-cut,\" [axle with a 7.125\" ring gear](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/dana-30-dana-35-and-dana-44-factory-specifications.224/) rated for roughly 3,500 lb gross axle weight. Jeep and other manufacturers run the front differential high-pinion specifically to clear the oil pan and steering linkage while keeping decent ground clearance, and it changes the direction the ring gear spins relative to the pinion compared to a standard low-pinion axle in the rear. Practically, this means the ring and pinion set has to be sold and installed as a reverse-rotation gear set \u2014 a standard-cut Dana 30 or Dana 44 rear gear set is cut for the opposite rotation and will not run correctly in a TJ front axle. When shopping for a regear, confirm \"reverse\" or \"high-pinion\" is explicitly called out in the part listing; it usually costs the same as a standard-cut set from the same manufacturer, so the mistake isn't about price, it's about ordering the wrong casting.\n\nThe ring and pinion is honestly underrated \u2014 most failures happen at the axle shafts and U-joints first, not the gears. That said, the Dana 30 has well-understood limits and an equally well-understood upgrade path.\n\nStock 27-spline shafts and the small 260X U-joints are reliable behind 31s and 33s with no locker. Once you bolt on 35s, the leverage at the tire patch starts to outpace what the small U-joints can handle, especially with throttle stabs in low range. Add a locker, and a stock shaft on the locked side becomes the sacrificial part \u2014 the locker eliminates the differential's natural torque relief, so any tire grip spike loads the shaft directly. Plan the upgrades in this order: U-joints, shafts, then locker.\n\nThe most common shaft upgrade is Yukon or RCV chromoly 27-spline axle shafts paired with Spicer 5-297X U-joints. The 5-297X is about 30% stronger than the stock 260X and uses larger trunnions. RCV CV-style shafts cost more (~$1,400/pair) but eliminate U-joint binding at full steering lock, which matters if you're rock crawling. Some builders go to 30-spline shafts, but the Dana 30 outer stub is still 27-spline, so the inner upgrade alone doesn't fully resolve the weak point and 30-spline shafts are harder to source in a hurry on the trail.\n\nBall joints are the other Dana 30 conversation. The factory ball joints have a known short service life \u2014 many TJs need replacement by 80\u2013100k miles, sooner if the rig has been lifted. Aggressive lift angles, larger tires, and rough trails accelerate wear. Symptoms of worn ball joints: clunking over bumps at low speed that a fresh track bar doesn't fix, uneven or cupped tire wear on the front outer edges, and steering wheel shimmy that gets worse as speed increases. Synergy, Spicer Performance, and Dynatrac all make heavy-duty replacements that last meaningfully longer than the factory units \u2014 Synergy's part 8009 uses a greaseable design so you can service it going forward instead of replacing it again at the same mileage interval.\n\nIf a locker is in the plan, know the difference between a selectable locker (Tru-Lok-style, driver-engaged) and an automatic locker (Yukon Grizzly, Detroit-style, always engaged off-throttle) before ordering one. An automatic locker in the front axle unlocks itself under power and relocks under deceleration or turning, which some drivers find unpredictable on pavement in the wet; a selectable locker stays open until you flip a switch, at the cost of more complexity and a higher price. Either one turns the axle shafts into the limiting factor the moment it's engaged, which circles back to why the shaft and U-joint upgrade needs to happen first, not after.\n\nFor builds aiming above 35s with a locker, the conversation shifts to a Dana 44 front swap (junkyard XJ or Rubicon JK donor) rather than continuing to upgrade the Dana 30 \u2014 at that point, you've spent enough on the Dana 30 that the swap is the better dollar. A Rubicon TJ donor axle is the cleanest swap since it's a direct width and mount match; an XJ Dana 30 is NOT a suitable swap donor for more strength since it's the same axle family \u2014 the strength jump requires a genuinely larger axle, not a lateral swap.","steps":["Inspect ball joints first \u2014 grab the top of the tire at 12 and 6 o'clock and rock it; any play means replacement.","Pull both front axle shafts. Inspect U-joints for play, rust, and pitting.","If shafts are stock 27-spline with small U-joints and you run 33s+, plan a chromoly shaft + 5-297X upgrade.","Press out old ball joints with a ball joint press. Install heavy-duty replacements per torque spec (upper 80 ft-lb, lower 100 ft-lb).","Reassemble shafts with new U-joints, torque axle nut to 175 ft-lb.","If installing a locker, this is the time \u2014 pull the third member, swap the carrier, set backlash and pinion preload.","If regearing at the same time, confirm the new ring & pinion is stamped or listed as reverse-cut/high-pinion before installing \u2014 check this at the counter, not after the third member is apart.","Refill diff with 75W-90 gear oil (2.0 qt) and a friction modifier if running a clutch-style limited slip."],"pros":["Cheaper than a full Dana 44 swap","Bolt-in upgrade path \u2014 no fabrication","Chromoly shafts and HD ball joints solve 80% of failures","Strong enough for 35s with the right shafts"],"cons":["Locker plus stock shafts is a recipe for trail breakage","Ball joints are a known weak spot, especially lifted","30-spline upgrade is incomplete unless outers also upgrade","Above 35s, the math favors a Dana 44 swap instead","Ordering standard-cut gears instead of reverse-cut by mistake wastes the whole regear budget"]},{"id":"tj-dana35-failures","title":"Dana 35 Rear: Known Failures and the Fix","vehicle":"tj","category":"axles","subcategory":"rear_axle","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","axle shaft puller","bearing tools","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["axle work","gear setup (or a shop)"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["axles","drivetrain","weak-link"],"parts":[{"name":"Chromoly axle shaft set (Dana 35)","vendor":"Yukon/RCV","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Ford 8.8 swap (junkyard + brackets)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":500},{"name":"Dana 44 rear (TJ Rubicon take-out)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":1200}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep axle reference \u2014 List of Jeep axles","url":"https://jeep.fandom.com/wiki/List_of_Jeep_axles"},{"title":"TJ drivetrain specs \u2014 My Jeep TJ","url":"https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/"}],"alternatives":["tj-dana44-rear","tj-regear","tj-locker-rear"],"summary":"Every non-Rubicon TJ shipped a Dana 35 rear unless it was ordered with the optional Dana 44, and it is the platform's defining weak link. On 31s with a daily-driven Jeep it survives. Add a locker and 33s with a heavy right foot and it breaks \u2014 c-clip shafts snap and the wheel can leave the trail behind you. Whether you fix it or replace it depends honestly on how hard you wheel, and the decision tree below is built around that.","description":"The Dana 35's failure mode is predictable: it lets go under shock load *with* traction, exactly when a locked rear hooks up climbing a ledge. Because it uses c-clip axle shafts, a snapped shaft is not retained \u2014 the wheel, tire, and brake drum can come off the vehicle. That is why hard-use TJs either reinforce or replace it before running 33s locked.\n\n[The TJ Dana 35 is a 7.56-inch ring gear axle with 27-spline](https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/), roughly 1.18-inch shafts retained by c-clips inside the carrier. Factory ratios were 3.07, 3.55, 3.73, and 4.10, with 4.56 only on some late 2.4L SE automatics; the tag under one of the cover bolts lists the ratio and build date. The optional TJ Dana 44 rear (1997\u20132006, ordered with the Trac-Lok limited slip, drum brakes) [uses an 8.5-inch ring gear and 30-spline shafts](https://jeep.fandom.com/wiki/List_of_Jeep_axles); the 2003\u20132006 Rubicon Dana 44 rear adds disc brakes and the factory air locker. Two quick tells: a Dana 44 cover has a pronounced bulge around the ring gear side and the tubes are visibly larger, and any TJ with rear disc brakes from the factory is a Rubicon with a 44. If you are not sure, pull the cover and read the ring gear \u2014 the tooth count and part number are stamped on it, and the [differential fluid](/db/tj/tj-differential-fluid/) guide walks the reseal.\n\nThree weaknesses stack up. The 27-spline shafts are small for the tire diameters people run, and the c-clip groove is a stress riser at the exact spot the shaft twists hardest. The wheel bearing rides directly on the shaft with no inner race, so a weeping seal grooves the shaft where the bearing spins \u2014 a used Dana 35 with a wet backing plate often needs shafts or a repair sleeve before you even wheel it. And the axle tubes are thin enough to flex under big-tire loads, which shows up as a rear that wears its bearings and eats pinion seals after a season of rock work. A lunchbox locker makes every one of these worse: it removes the open differential's ability to spin the unloaded tire, so the loaded shaft takes the full engine-times-gear-ratio torque on a hit. Most Dana 35 shafts in the scrap pile broke within a year of a locker going in.\n\nRunning 31s, unlocked, mostly street and mild trails? The Dana 35 will live \u2014 leave it and spend the money on lift and steering. If you want a little insurance, chromoly shafts plus a c-clip eliminator address the shaft-snap and the wheel-loss risk for a few hundred dollars, and that carries a 35 through a moderate 33-inch build with a locker. There are two versions of that fix. The basic kit keeps 27 splines: chromoly 4340 shafts from Yukon, Ten Factory, or Superior plus a c-clip eliminator that bolts a bearing retainer to the tube end, about $400\u2013550 for both. The stronger version is the Superior \"Super 35\" style kit \u2014 30-spline chromoly shafts with the eliminator built in \u2014 which requires a 30-spline carrier, so it is bought together with a 30-spline locker (Detroit, ARB, or a Truetrac) for $900\u20131,300 all in. The Super 35 is the ceiling for a Dana 35: it makes the shafts a non-issue but does nothing for the 7.56-inch ring gear or the thin tubes, so it is a 33-inch solution. The [chromoly shaft guide](/db/tj/tj-chromoly-shafts/) covers the install and the front axle at the same time.\n\nBuilding past 33s, locking the rear, or wheeling rock? The Dana 35 becomes a liability and replacement is the right call. The cheap, strong path is a Ford 8.8 swap \u2014 the 1995\u20132001 Explorer 8.8 is junkyard-plentiful at $200\u2013400, carries 31-spline shafts, an 8.8-inch ring gear, rear disc brakes, and often a factory limited slip and 3.73 or 4.10 gears. It needs the Explorer perches and shock mounts cut off, a TJ bracket kit welded on ($250\u2013400 from Ruff Stuff, Barnes 4WD, or TNT Customs), and its 1.5-inch-or-so passenger-side pinion offset lived with or corrected by centering it; the width is within an inch of the TJ's and works with the factory wheels. Budget $900\u20131,500 with gears matching the front and a weekend of welding. The OEM-clean path is a TJ Rubicon Dana 44 rear at $1,200\u20132,000 for a take-out \u2014 a near-bolt-in upgrade that keeps the factory perches, brackets, and brake setup, brings disc brakes and the factory locker, and needs only the 4.10s matched up front. The non-Rubicon optional Dana 44 rear is the sleeper: $600\u2013900, bolt-in, 30-spline, drum brakes, and quiet enough that many owners never notice their TJ has one until the tag is read. The full comparison, including the aftermarket Dynatrac and Currie housings for a 37-inch build, is in the [Dana 44 rear guide](/db/tj/tj-dana44-rear/) and the [axle swap](/db/tj/tj-axle-swap/) walkthrough.\n\nWhatever you choose, regear the front Dana 30 to the same ratio \u2014 a mismatched front and rear binds the transfer case and chews tires. For a 4.0L on 33s the target is 4.56 or 4.88; on 35s, 4.88 or 5.13, and 5.13s on a Dana 35 leave a pinion gear small enough to be its own weak point, which is another reason the 35 comes out before 35-inch tires go on. Set up gears with a shop unless you own the tools and a dial indicator; gear setup is unforgiving and a whine means a redo. The [regear guide](/db/tj/tj-regear/) lays out ratio choices by tire size and the $1,200\u20131,800 shop cost for both ends.\n\nKeeping it: chromoly 27-spline shafts and c-clip eliminator, $400\u2013550 (Yukon, Ten Factory, Superior); Super 35 kit with a 30-spline locker, $900\u20131,300; a Dana 35 truss from Ruff Stuff or Barnes 4WD to stiffen the tubes, $150\u2013250 plus welding. Replacing it: Explorer 8.8 with bracket kit and welding, $900\u20131,500; used non-Rubicon TJ Dana 44 rear, $600\u2013900; Rubicon Dana 44 rear take-out, $1,200\u20132,000; new Dynatrac ProRock 44 or Currie RockJock 44 rear, $3,000\u20134,500 built. Add $600\u2013900 per end for a shop regear, and 1.6 quarts of 80W-90 gear oil (75W-140 with a limited slip and the friction modifier) for the refill."},{"id":"tj-dana44-rear","title":"TJ Dana 44 Rear Axle \u2014 What the Rubicon Has and Why It Matters","vehicle":"tj","category":"axles","type":"reference","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","axle stands","breaker bar","1/2\" impact","bearing press or rental","dial indicator for backlash setup"],"skills_required":["intermediate to advanced wrenching","drivetrain experience helpful"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2006 Rubicon only (factory D44)","1997-2006 (aftermarket swap)"],"tags":["axles","dana44","rubicon","locker","regear"],"parts":[{"name":"Alloy USA axle shaft kit \u2014 Dana 44 TJ rear","vendor":"Alloy USA","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Yukon Gear ring and pinion 4.10 \u2014 Dana 44","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"ARB Air Locker \u2014 Dana 44 (35-spline)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Eaton Detroit Locker \u2014 Dana 44","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":425}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Rubicon Dana 44 Axle Specs \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/tj-rubicon-dana-44-axle-specs.html"},{"title":"Dana 44 vs Dana 35 \u2014 Trail Manual Database","url":"/db/?v=tj"},{"title":"Yukon Gear Ring and Pinion Installation Guide","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/install-instructions"}],"alternatives":["tj-dana35-failures","tj-chromoly-shafts","tj-lockers","tj-regear","tj-axle-swap"],"summary":"Only the 2003 to 2006 TJ Rubicon left the factory with a Dana 44 in the rear (and a Dana 44 in the front), [both with 4.10 gears and factory air-actuated lockers](https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/tj-rubicon-dana-44-axle-specs.html). Every other TJ has a Dana 35 rear, which is the weak point once you add 33-inch or larger tires, a locker, or hard trail use. If you want a Dana 44 behind a non-Rubicon TJ, the options are a used Rubicon axle, an aftermarket build, or strengthening the Dana 35.","description":"The Rubicon package was a 2003 to 2006 model-year option. It combined front and rear Dana 44 axles, 4.10 gears, front and rear Tru-Lok air-actuated lockers controlled by dash switches, and the Rock-Trac transfer case with a 4:1 low range. The Sport, Sahara, and X trims of the same years used a Dana 30 front and a Dana 35 rear. Most 1997 to 2002 TJs also used a Dana 35 rear. Verify by reading the axle tag or counting the differential cover bolts, because a swap or an owner-built axle is not unusual on a 20-year-old Jeep.\n\n**Dana 44 versus Dana 35 for a TJ, in plain numbers.**\n\n- **Ring gear.** The Dana 44 uses an 8.5-inch ring gear. The Dana 35 uses a smaller ring gear of about 7.5 inches. Larger gears tolerate more torque and abuse.\n- **Axle shafts.** The Rubicon Dana 44 shafts are 30-spline. The Dana 35 shafts are 27-spline, and the Dana 35 is a C-clip design in which a broken shaft can slide out and take the wheel with it. Confirm your own axle design before relying on this, because earlier and later Dana 35 versions differ in details.\n- **Locker.** The Rubicon axle's Tru-Lok is the strongest feature of the package, since you can switch from open to locked from the cab.\n- **Weight and cost.** The Dana 44 is heavier and costs more to regear, but parts for it are plentiful.\n\n**Your realistic options when your TJ has a Dana 35:**\n\n1. **Keep the Dana 35 and be honest about its limit.** On 31-inch tires, moderate trails, and a driver who avoids hard throttle on obstacles, it works. Add [chromoly axle shafts](/db/tj/tj-chromoly-shafts/) if you are going bigger, and read [TJ Dana 35 failures](/db/tj/tj-dana35-failures/) to know what breaks first.\n2. **Swap in a Rubicon rear axle.** A used Rubicon Dana 44 usually arrives with a factory locker, 4.10 gears, and sturdier shafts. Prices vary widely by region and condition: figure $800 to $2,000 and verify the price locally. Compatibility is not automatic. Check the spring perch spacing, the track bar bracket, shock mounts, brake line routing, parking brake cable length, and the ABS wiring against your Jeep. Measure the axle width and compare it to your existing axle. See [TJ axle swap](/db/tj/tj-axle-swap/).\n3. **Buy an aftermarket Dana 44.** Builders sell complete rear axles with your choice of gears, locker, and full-floating or semi-floating configuration. Cost is typically $2,000 to $3,500 and up, and it makes sense for a dedicated crawler or a heavy build.\n4. **Add a locker to the Dana 35.** Many lockers are available for the Dana 35, but a locker adds load to a shaft that is already the weak link. Read [TJ lockers](/db/tj/tj-lockers/) and decide whether the money is better spent on a stronger axle.\n\n- A confirmed axle identification (look at the axle tag and the cover)\n- Floor jack and two axle-rated jack stands, plus a second set of stands to hold the axle\n- Breaker bar, 1/2-inch drive socket set, and a torque wrench\n- Dial indicator and a magnetic base if you set up gears yourself\n- A bearing or seal driver set and a shop press (or a machine shop to do it)\n- The correct gear oil, locker oil if needed, and a new cover gasket or RTV\n- Service manual for torque specs and gear setup procedure\n\n1. **Identify and verify the donor.** Check for leaks at the pinion and axle seals, side-to-side play in the shafts, the condition of the wheel bearings, and whether the locker holds air if it has one. Ask for the 4.10 ratio confirmation on the tag.\n\n2. **Park the Jeep on level ground, chock the front wheels, and lift the rear** with a jack under the frame. Support the frame on jack stands and place the axle on a floor jack.\n\n3. **Disconnect everything that is attached to the axle.** Rear driveshaft, brake lines and hoses, parking brake cable, ABS sensor wiring if equipped, track bar, lower control arms, shocks, and sway bar links. Label what you remove.\n\n4. **Lower the axle and roll it out.** Keep the axle supported on the jack so the springs and shocks do not drop or bind.\n\n5. **Install the new axle in reverse.** Bring it up on the floor jack, attach the control arms and track bar loosely, then tighten them at ride height with the Jeep's weight on the suspension. This avoids binding bushings.\n\n6. **Plumb the brakes and check.** Reconnect the brake line, bleed the brakes, and test the pedal. This is a safety-critical system, so do not skip the bleeding step. Adjust the parking brake cable.\n\n7. **Fill the axle and the locker system.** Add the specified gear oil. If the locker is air-actuated, plumb the air line and the wiring harness for the switch; this is the most complex part of using a Rubicon rear axle in a non-Rubicon TJ.\n\n8. **Check the speedometer.** The Rubicon's 4.10 gearing differs from a typical Sport rear gear ratio. Mismatched front and rear ratios will bind the drivetrain in 4WD, so match both axles. If you change the ratio of one, change the other. See [TJ regear](/db/tj/tj-regear/).\n\n9. **Road test carefully.** Drive slowly, listen for whining or clunks, and recheck the gear oil level after the first few miles.\n\n- **Mixed ratios.** Front and rear ratios must match. A 4.10 rear with a 3.73 front binds the drivetrain in four-wheel drive and loads the transfer case and axle parts.\n- **Bad used bearings.** A whining axle can mean a worn pinion bearing, which is a $300 to $600 repair at a shop before you even install it.\n- **Locker wiring and air lines.** The Rubicon lockers use an onboard air compressor and wiring to dash switches. If you are not carrying the Rubicon's compressor and harness over, the locker will not function.\n- **ABS and parking brake differences.** Confirm sensor and cable compatibility before you commit.\n- **Torque specs.** Bolt torque values vary by hardware and year. Use the service manual's numbers rather than a forum post.\n- **Over-torqued cover bolts.** On a used axle, the cover bolts may be stuck or stripped. Use hand tools and care.\n\n**Costs and sources.** Used Rubicon Dana 44 rear axle: $800 to $2,000 depending on region and condition. Aftermarket Dana 44 build: $2,000 to $3,500 and up. Chromoly shafts for a Dana 35: $350 to $600 per pair, depending on brand. Regear kit (ring and pinion, bearings, install kit): $450 to $700 in parts, with shop labor adding $400 to $800 per axle. Locker: $400 to $950 depending on type. Verify all prices with a quote from your local supplier before you commit."},{"id":"tj-lockers","title":"Lockers for the TJ \u2014 Selectable, Automatic, and Budget Drop-In Options","vehicle":"tj","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":1400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set (metric + SAE)","torque wrench","dial indicator with magnetic base","gear oil + RTV","bearing press or shop access (for full-case lockers)"],"skills_required":["differential service","backlash and preload basics","knowing when to hand setup to a shop"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["locker","axles","differential","traction","dana30","dana35","dana44","ARB","selectable-locker"],"type_note":"traction upgrade","parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker \u2014 Dana 30 front (27-spline)","part_number":"RD100","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":720},{"name":"ARB Air Locker \u2014 Dana 44 rear (30/35-spline)","part_number":"RD116","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":760},{"name":"Eaton ELocker \u2014 Dana 35 / Dana 44","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":690},{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right lunchbox locker \u2014 Dana 30/35","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB twin air compressor (on-board)","part_number":"CKMTA12","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":350}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Air Locker application guide \u2014 Jeep Dana 30/44","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Eaton ELocker fitment \u2014 Dana 35/44","url":"https://www.eaton.com"},{"title":"TJ axle spline counts and carrier breaks (Dana 35 vs 44)","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com"}],"description":"# Lockers for the TJ \u2014 Selectable, Automatic, and Budget Drop-In Options\n\nIf you wheel a TJ on anything technical, a rear locker is the single biggest traction upgrade you can make \u2014 bigger than tires, bigger than a lift. The honest order is rear first, front second. A rear locker gets most builds through most obstacles; adding a front locker turns a capable rig into one that climbs almost anything. The catch is your rear axle: a non-Rubicon TJ has a Dana 35, and a Dana 35 with a locker and 33s is living on borrowed time.\n\nThis guide covers what fits a TJ's Dana 30 front and Dana 35 or Dana 44 rear, the three locker types and who each is for, and where the money actually goes.\n\nA locker swap touches differential setup, so the tool list depends on the locker type. A drop-in \"lunchbox\" locker replaces the spider gears inside your existing open carrier and needs basic hand tools plus a dial indicator. A full-case selectable locker (ARB, Eaton) replaces the entire carrier, which means pulling the diff, pressing the ring gear over, and re-setting backlash and pinion preload \u2014 that is a shop-level job unless you have done gear setup before.\n\nPlan on a floor jack and stands, a metric and SAE socket set, a torque wrench, a dial indicator with a magnetic base, gear oil, and RTV. Selectable air lockers also need an on-board air source \u2014 budget for an ARB twin compressor or equivalent.\n\nSelectable lockers (ARB air, Eaton electric) stay fully open until you flip a switch, so they drive like stock on the street and lock only when you want them. They are the premium answer and the right call for a daily-driven TJ. Expect $690\u2013$760 per axle plus the air or wiring to run them.\n\nAutomatic lockers (Detroit Locker) are always engaged and unlock through ratcheting on turns. They are bombproof and a favorite on dedicated trail rigs, but they bark and clunk on pavement and change on-road manners. \n\nLunchbox lockers (Powertrax Lock-Right, Aussie) drop into your existing open carrier for around $250\u2013$400 and are the budget path to a locked axle. They behave like an automatic locker \u2014 same ratcheting on turns \u2014 but install without pressing a ring gear, so a careful DIYer can do one in an afternoon.\n\nThe Dana 35 is the weak link, and this is where TJ builds get hurt. Locking a Dana 35 and running 33-inch tires puts loads on the C-clip axle shafts and tubes the axle was never designed for \u2014 [broken shafts and even bent tubes are common](https://www.jeepforum.com). If you are running 33s or bigger and want a rear locker, the durable answer is a rear axle swap to a Dana 44 or Ford 8.8 first, then locker it. On a Dana 30 front, a locker is fine for most builds; upgrade to chromoly shafts if you are on 35s and locked.\n\nMatch your locker to your gear ratio plans. If you are about to re-gear for larger tires, do the locker and the gears in the same teardown \u2014 you are already paying for the setup labor once.\n\nBudget path: a lunchbox locker in the rear runs $250\u2013$400 in parts and is DIY-friendly. Mid path: an [Eaton ELocker](https://www.eaton.com) or Detroit at $600\u2013$700 per axle, professionally set up, lands around $900\u2013$1,100 installed per axle. Premium path: [ARB air lockers](https://www.arbusa.com) front and rear with a twin compressor runs $1,800\u2013$2,400 in parts before install. The compressor is a one-time cost that also runs your tires and air tools.\n\nRelated TJ axle reading: see the [Dana 35 rear axle guide](/db/tj/tj-dana35-failures/) for why the 35 limits your locker choices, the [Dana 44 rear guide](/db/tj/tj-dana44-rear/) for the Rubicon and swap path, the [chromoly axle shaft guide](/db/tj/tj-chromoly-shafts/) before locking a front Dana 30, and the [axle swap guide](/db/tj/tj-axle-swap/) if you are upgrading the rear before adding traction."},{"id":"tj-fender-flares","title":"TJ Fender Flares \u2014 Replacing or Upgrading for Wider Tires","vehicle":"tj","category":"body","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":400},"tools_required":["10mm socket","flat-blade screwdriver","trim clip removal tool","drill with bits (for tube flares)","rivet gun (optional)","step bit (for tube flare mounting holes)"],"skills_required":["basic trim work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["body","fenders","tires","clearance","legality"],"parts":[{"name":"Bushwacker Flat Style fender flares \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Bushwacker","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC replacement fender flares","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Rugged Ridge tube fender flare kit","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Poison Spyder tube fenders (sheetmetal)","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Turn signal relocation harness \u2014 tube fender conversion","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":25}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Fender Flare Options and Tire Clearance Guide","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/forums/tj-wrangler-technical-forum-15/fender-flares-guide/"},{"title":"Bushwacker Flat Style Flares \u2014 Jeep TJ fitment","url":"https://www.bushwacker.com/products/flat-style-fender-flares"}],"alternatives":["tj-soft-top-replacement","tj-battery-upgrade","tj-tire-fitment-35s"],"summary":"Running 33s on a stock TJ you need wider flares to stay legal \u2014 stock flares cover stock width. Budget flares from Smittybilt bolt straight to stock holes; tube fenders need drilling but look cleaner with big lifts.","description":"Most states, Arizona included, require tires to be covered to at least the centerline of the axle, and some go further and require coverage of the full tread width. [Stock TJ flares cover roughly a 31-inch tire](https://www.jk-forum.com/forums/tj-wrangler-technical-forum-15/fender-flares-guide/) at stock track width \u2014 move to 33s with any amount of lift and the tire sidewall starts poking past the fender line. The fix is either wider replacement flares or tube fenders, depending on how aggressive the build is going. Rules vary enough by state that \"check your state's exact wording\" is the honest answer rather than a specific inch measurement that may not hold everywhere.\n\nFor 33s and mild lifts (2.5 to 3 inches), a flat-style or extended OEM-style replacement flare adds 1 to 2 inches of coverage per side and installs in the factory clip holes. [Bushwacker's Flat Style](https://www.bushwacker.com/products/flat-style-fender-flares) and Smittybilt's replacement flares are the common choices \u2014 both are OE-style ABS plastic, bolt on without drilling, and run $100 to $180 for the pair. They look stock enough to not draw attention.\n\nFor 35s and serious lift (4+ inches), tube fenders are the better answer. They add more coverage, they don't crack when a rock hits them like OEM-profile ABS flares do, and they clear the tire at full articulation rather than at ride height. The tradeoff is drilling into the body for mounting bolts. Rugged Ridge tube flares run $180 and are considered entry-level. Poison Spyder's tube fenders are sheetmetal, look right, and hold up to rock contact \u2014 closer to $400. If you're building a rock crawler, metal tube fenders are worth it.\n\nThere's a middle option worth knowing about before you commit to drilling: some flat-style flares are available in a slightly wider \"extended\" profile (roughly 3\" over stock rather than the standard 1\u20132\") that stretches coverage far enough for a mild 35 setup without going to tube fenders at all. It's worth checking exact coverage-width specs against your tire and lift combination before ruling either style out \u2014 the line between \"flat flare is enough\" and \"you need tube fenders\" moves with backspacing and wheel offset as much as it does with tire diameter.\n\nABS plastic flares (the OEM-style bolt-ons) are UV-stressed over years in the sun and eventually crack at the mounting tabs, especially in hot climates. If your TJ's factory flares are already brittle and cracking on their own before you've even changed tire size, that's a separate reason to replace them even at stock tire width \u2014 a cracked flare doesn't do its job in a hard rain or on a muddy trail regardless of what's mounted underneath it.\n\n- Replacement flares matched to TJ (OEM-style) or tube fenders\n- 10mm socket and trim clip removal tool for stock flare removal\n- Drill and step bit if installing tube fenders that require new mounting points\n- Wire brush and touchup paint \u2014 the areas under the flares are usually surface-rusted on high-mileage TJs\n- Turn signal relocation harness if converting to tube fenders (the stock flare carries the front marker light on some trims)\n\n1. **Remove old flares.** The stock TJ flares clip to the fender lip and secure with 10mm bolts at the wheel arch. Pop the clips with a trim tool, remove bolts, and lift flare off. It takes about 15 minutes per flare.\n\n2. **Clean the mounting surface.** Scrub any rust with a wire brush and hit bare metal with rattle-can primer. You won't see this area again for years.\n\n3. **Install replacement OEM-style flares.** Align over stock clip holes, press in clips, torque bolts. Test fit before final torque \u2014 misaligned flares look worse than old ones.\n\n4. **For tube fenders:** Test fit and mark drill points before committing. Drill pilot holes with a small bit first, then step up to final diameter with a step bit rather than jumping straight to a large twist bit \u2014 it's easier to keep the hole round and centered that way on sheetmetal this thin. Deburr and prime every hole edge before final bolt-up; bare edges are where rust starts.\n\n5. **Relocate the front marker light if needed.** Tube fenders don't have a housing for the stock side marker/turn signal on trims that route it through the flare. Splice in the relocation harness and mount the light to the tube frame or bumper before buttoning up the fender.\n\n6. **Bolt through with stainless hardware and locking nuts.** Star washers or thread locker on top of the locking nut are cheap insurance against a bolt backing out on the trail.\n\n7. **Check clearance at full lock and full droop.** Turn the wheel lock-to-lock with the Jeep on the ground. Check for rub points at full flex with a friend compressing the suspension by hand, and re-check after the first real trail run once everything has had a chance to settle and flex under load.\n\n- Tube fenders require re-locating the turn signal. Budget an extra hour and a turn signal relocation harness ($20\u2013$30).\n- Inspect the inner fender area for rust before reinstalling any flare \u2014 this is a common rust accumulation zone on older TJs from wet climates.\n- Not all \"TJ flares\" fit all years \u2014 check your year before ordering. A few flare kits fit either TJ or JK but not both.\n- Confirm your state's exact coverage requirement before finalizing tire width and flare choice \u2014 \"centerline\" and \"full tread\" are different standards and the gap between them is where a technically-legal setup can still draw a warning.\n\nOEM-style replacement flares: $90\u2013$180 installed in under an hour with no drilling. Tube fenders: $180\u2013$400 depending on material, plus 3\u20134 hours of work, a turn signal relocation ($20\u2013$30), and touch-up primer for the drilled edges. Sheetmetal tube fenders outlast plastic alternatives in rock use."},{"id":"tj-soft-top-replacement","title":"TJ Soft Top Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"body","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":600},"tools_required":["trim panel tool","T25 Torx","scissors or utility knife","rubber mallet","helper (recommended)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","patience with fabric and bows"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["soft-top","weatherproofing","body"],"parts":[{"name":"Bestop Supertop NX \u2014 black diamond","vendor":"Bestop","price_estimate_usd":530},{"name":"Bestop Supertop Classic (budget tier)","vendor":"Bestop","price_estimate_usd":330},{"name":"Rugged Ridge All-Terrain replacement top","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Smittybilt OEM-style replacement top","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":250}],"citations":[{"title":"Bestop Supertop NX Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.bestop.com/fitment/vehicle/1997-2006-jeep-wrangler-tj/"},{"title":"TJ Soft Top Replacement \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/how-to-install-a-soft-top.html"}],"alternatives":["tj-hardtop-removal","tj-door-removal"],"summary":"Replacing a TJ soft top is a 2\u20133 hour job the first time; once you've done it, an hour is realistic. The biggest variable is bow frame condition \u2014 cracked or bent bows will prevent a clean seal no matter how good the fabric is.","description":"The TJ soft top is a folding bow frame assembly covered by fabric. When the top leaks, sags, or tears, the fabric itself is almost always the problem \u2014 the bow frames typically outlast two or three fabrics if the Jeep lives in a garage. Before buying a full replacement top, inspect the bow frames: look for cracks at the pivot points, bends from low-clearance parking damage, and broken end caps. If the frames are solid, a fabric-only kit runs $150\u2013$200 less than a full top kit.\n\nBestop is the OEM supplier for TJ soft tops and the Supertop NX is the benchmark. Smittybilt and Rugged Ridge are workable budget options but the vinyl is typically thinner and the zip seal quality is noticeably lower \u2014 fine for an occasional-use rig, harder to live with as a daily driver in rain or cold. In sub-freezing temps, cheaper vinyl becomes stiff and cracks at fold points within a couple of seasons.\n\nThe windows on the TJ top are zip-in \u2014 they attach to the top fabric via a zipper and are retained by snaps. Window replacement is a separate job and the clear vinyl tends to haze before the fabric fails. If the top is leaking at a window seam rather than at the bow rail or header, you might only need new windows.\n\n- The header seal leaks if the clamp levers don't fully engage. If you're getting water intrusion at the header on a new top, check that the bow is fully seated in the windshield channel before latching.\n- Don't force windows into the zipper track. If the zip-in window isn't tracking smoothly, back it out and realign \u2014 forcing it splits the zipper tape.\n- In cold weather, warm the fabric in sunlight for 20\u201330 minutes before installation. Cold vinyl doesn't stretch and the snap alignment will be off.\n- Snap alignment on budget tops is sometimes off by 5\u201310mm. A heated snap setter can reposition snaps; gluing them down is a last resort.\n\n- [Bestop Supertop NX](https://www.bestop.com/fitment/vehicle/1997-2006-jeep-wrangler-tj/): $500\u2013$550 at 4WP, Quadratec, or Amazon\n- Smittybilt OEM-style: $240\u2013$270 at Amazon\n- Fabric-only (Bestop Sailcloth): $180\u2013$220 for the skin\n- Windows (Bestop replacement rear window kit): $80\u2013$120\n\nBudget for the top + a half-hour of frustration on the header if it's your first install.","steps":["Fold the existing top rearward to expose the header (front bow). Release the header clamps \u2014 two levers that fold down and lock the front bar against the windshield frame.","Unsnap the top from the windshield channel. The fabric snaps to a metal strip that runs across the top of the windshield frame.","Work rearward unsnapping the top from the body tub. TJ tops use snaps along both sides and across the tailgate.","Unzip and remove windows. Store them flat \u2014 folding them causes crease hazing.","Remove the fabric from the bow frames. The fabric slides over the front bow and typically attaches with Velcro or screws at each bow. Consult the replacement kit instructions \u2014 attachment varies by manufacturer.","If reusing bow frames: inspect each pivot, wipe down all contact points, and apply a thin coat of rubber lubricant to the seals before fitting the new fabric.","Slide the new top over the front bow. Pull rearward so the fabric is smooth and even across the bows.","Attach the header to the windshield channel. The header bar should sit flush and snap tight \u2014 if it's lifting at the corners, the fabric tension is off. Work out the wrinkles before latching.","Work rearward snapping the fabric to the body tub. Start at the front corners and pull toward the back \u2014 chasing wrinkles toward the tailgate.","Reinstall and zip in the windows. Confirm all snaps and the zipper seal are seated."]},{"id":"tj-front-pads","title":"Front Brake Pad and Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":230},"tools_required":["3/8\" hex bit (caliper slider)","15mm or 18mm socket","torque wrench","C-clamp","wire brush","brake cleaner","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["brakes","pads","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front brake pads","part_number":"5018572AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":50},{"name":"Centric Premium front rotors (pair)","part_number":"120.67032","vendor":"Centric","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Powerstop Z36 Truck and Tow front kit","vendor":"Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"EBC Yellowstuff pads (heavy use)","vendor":"EBC","price_estimate_usd":75}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Wrangler Front Caliper Bolt Torque Spec (11-13 ft-lb)","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/torque-values-for-brake-caliper-bolts.479649/"},{"title":"TJ Brake Caliper Mounting Torque Discussion","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/brake-caliper-mounting-bolt-torque.4461775/"}],"alternatives":["tj-rear-drums"],"summary":"TJ front pads run 30,000\u201345,000 miles depending on terrain and gear. The 1997\u20132002 TJ uses a 3/8\" hex bit on the caliper slider; the 2003+ Rubicon and LJ use a slightly larger bolt head. [Caliper slider torque is 11\u201313 ft-lb](https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/torque-values-for-brake-caliper-bolts.479649/) \u2014 straightforward to strip.","description":"The TJ front brake setup is single-piston floating caliper on vented rotors, same general layout as the XJ Cherokee it shares an axle with. Two slider pins hold the caliper; two large bracket bolts hold the bracket to the steering knuckle. What makes the TJ slightly different from the XJ is the slider bolts use a 3/8\" hex (Allen) head on 1997\u20132002 trucks \u2014 you'll need a hex bit socket, not a regular hex socket, to break them loose without rounding them.\n\nA note on tools: many TJ owners discover mid-job that their breaker bar set is missing the 3/8\" hex bit. Pick one up before starting. The slider bolts often blue-Loctite from the factory and don't always come loose easily \u2014 a quality bit and a breaker bar beat a stripped slider every time.\n\nPad recommendations for the TJ skew toward heavier compounds than the XJ because TJs are typically lifted on bigger tires more aggressively. Stock pads from Mopar (PN 5018572AB) are fine for a stock-tire Sport. Anything past 33s, add weight, or tow occasionally \u2014 go to Powerstop Z36 or EBC Yellowstuff. The TJ's brake system is the same hardware as a much lighter vehicle; the only way to make it deal with 35s and a winch bumper is harder pad compound and a heavier rotor.\n\nRotor choice: Centric Premium ($40 each) or Powerstop Evolution ($45 each) are solid stock-replacement options that often outlast OEM. Skip the cheap blank rotors from chain auto parts stores \u2014 they warp within 5,000 miles on a daily-driven TJ. Mopar OEM rotors are decent but expensive; Centric Premium gives 90% of the durability for half the price.\n\nThe TJ caliper slider bolts are the most-stripped bolts on the front end. Chrysler spec is 11\u201313 ft-lb. That feels finger-tight to anyone used to lug nuts. Use a torque wrench. Over-torque and you either snap the bolt or strip the bracket threads \u2014 both leave you with a non-driveable Jeep until you helicoil the bracket or find a new one.","steps":["Crack lug nuts (19mm), lift the front of the TJ, support on stands at the front axle.","Remove front wheels. Pull the master cylinder reservoir cap and drape a rag.","Remove the two caliper slider bolts using a 3/8\" hex bit (1997\u20132002) or 13mm socket (2003+ LJ/Rubicon).","Slide caliper off the bracket. Hang from a wire \u2014 don't let it dangle by the hose.","Compress the caliper piston back with a C-clamp using an old pad as a buffer.","Pull the old pads from the bracket. Note clip orientation.","To replace rotors: remove the two bracket bolts (15mm on early TJ, 18mm on Rubicon/LJ) from the steering knuckle. Lift bracket off. Pull old rotor.","Wire-brush the hub mating face. Rust ridges here cause runout.","Install new rotor (light anti-seize on the hub face only, not on the friction surface). Reinstall bracket and torque to 100 ft-lb.","Install new pads with new anti-rattle clips. Light brake grease on pad backs and slider pins.","Slide caliper over pads onto the bracket. Reinstall slider bolts and torque to 11 ft-lb \u2014 do not exceed.","Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 100 ft-lb.","Pump brake pedal until firm. Top off reservoir.","Bed in pads: 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph, then 2\u20133 harder stops from 50 mph."],"pros":["Shared parts with XJ Cherokee \u2014 wide aftermarket support","Common floating caliper, no special tools beyond hex bit","Centric Premium rotors outlast OEM at half the price","Stock pad life 30K\u201345K on daily-driven TJ"],"cons":["Caliper slider bolts (11 ft-lb) easily over-torqued and stripped","3/8\" hex bit required for 1997\u20132002 sliders \u2014 straightforward to miss when prepping","Stock brakes undersized for lifted 33\"+ builds; need harder pad compound","Bargain rotors warp in 5,000 miles \u2014 buy Centric or better"]},{"id":"tj-rear-drum-service","title":"TJ Rear Drum Brake Service \u2014 Shoes, Hardware, and Adjustment","vehicle":"tj","category":"brakes","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":120},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","lug wrench","brake spring pliers","flat-blade screwdriver","brake cleaner","torque wrench","drum puller (sometimes)"],"skills_required":["brake service","spring tension work"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["brakes","drums","shoes","maintenance","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Raybestos Professional Grade rear brake shoe set","part_number":"PGD166M","vendor":"Raybestos","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Wagner ThermoQuiet rear shoe set","vendor":"Wagner","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Brake drum hardware kit (springs, adjuster, pins)","vendor":"Dorman / ACDelco","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Bendix rear drums (if scoring requires replacement)","vendor":"Bendix","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"How Drum Brakes Work \u2014 Auto Service World","url":"https://www.autoserviceworld.com/how-drum-brakes-work/"},{"title":"TJ rear drum brake DIY","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/forum/"}],"alternatives":["tj-front-pads","tj-battery-upgrade"],"summary":"TJ rear drums are underappreciated and often neglected \u2014 most owners replace front pads and ignore the rears. Glazed shoes and a misadjusted star wheel are responsible for more TJ brake fade than worn pads.","description":"The TJ runs rear drum brakes on all trims (base and Rubicon). They're a fully mechanical system: two curved brake shoes press against the inside of a cast drum when the wheel cylinder extends. The parking brake integrates here too \u2014 a cable pulls the rear shoes directly. Drum brakes [self-adjust through a star wheel adjuster](https://www.autoserviceworld.com/how-drum-brakes-work/) when the vehicle is driven in reverse and the brakes are applied, but that adjuster gets lazy over time and the shoes drift away from the drum, extending pedal travel.\n\nService life on rear shoes is typically 40,000 to 60,000 miles in normal use, but varies widely \u2014 high-mileage off-road use shortens it, mostly because grit gets past the drum and acts as an abrasive. Glazed shoes (shiny, hardened contact surface) can happen earlier if the Jeep sat for a long time without use, which locks the shoes against the drum and bakes them.\n\nThis job requires more patience than skill. Drum brakes have a lot of springs under significant tension and the geometry of how they go back together matters. Take a photo before disassembly.\n\n- Floor jack, jack stands \u2014 never work under a TJ supported only by a jack\n- Brake spring pliers (the correct tool, not needle-nose \u2014 this is the one place where \"use the right tool\" matters most)\n- Brake cleaner and rags\n- New shoes and hardware kit\n- Micrometer or drum gauge (to measure drum wear \u2014 maximum diameter is cast into the drum)\n\n1. **Break lug nuts loose before jacking.** Lift the rear axle on the pumpkin (center differential housing) to lift both sides at once. Place jack stands under the axle tubes.\n\n2. **Remove wheels and drums.** Most TJ drums pull straight off once the wheels are removed. If the drum won't come off, back off the star wheel adjuster \u2014 there's an access hole in the backing plate. Never hammer a drum off the hub; you'll damage the wheel studs.\n\n3. **Photo before disassembly.** Drum brake layout seems obvious until it isn't. A quick photo saves 20 minutes of head-scratching.\n\n4. **Remove return springs first.** Use brake spring pliers. There are two shoe return springs at the top, one at the bottom, and a self-adjuster spring. The return springs are under the most tension \u2014 respect them.\n\n5. **Remove hold-down pins, adjuster, and shoes.** The hold-down pins go through the backing plate and are retained by a clip you rotate 90 degrees. The adjuster star wheel assembly pulls out once the shoes are free.\n\n6. **Clean the backing plate.** Wire brush any rust off the shoe contact pads on the backing plate. Apply a thin film of high-temp brake grease to those pads only \u2014 not to the drum contact surface on the shoes.\n\n7. **Install new shoes in reverse order.** Hardware kit replaces all springs and pins \u2014 don't reuse stretched springs. The longer shoe (primary) faces forward; the shorter (secondary) faces rearward.\n\n8. **Adjust the star wheel.** Expand the adjuster until the drum drags slightly as you spin it, then back off until it spins freely with light drag. This sets the initial clearance.\n\n9. **Reinstall drums, wheels. Torque lugs to 95 ft-lb.**\n\n10. **Pump brakes until firm before moving the Jeep.** The wheel cylinders need to extend to seat the new shoes.\n\n11. **Bed the shoes.** Drive at 30 mph, apply moderate brake pressure to slow to 5 mph (don't stop). Repeat 6\u20138 times. This seats the shoe-to-drum contact surface.\n\n- If the wheel cylinder is leaking (wet or weeping brake fluid behind the drum), replace it now. It's a $15 part and takes 30 minutes \u2014 much less than pulling the drum again later.\n- Maximum drum diameter is stamped on the drum face. If the drum is beyond spec, replace it. Drums are cheap insurance.\n- Safety-critical: never drive the Jeep with the brakes disassembled. Keep a buddy nearby the first time you pull the drums off a vehicle new to you \u2014 stuck calipers and failed wheel cylinders aren't always obvious until you see them.\n\nShoe set: $28\u2013$45. Hardware kit: $18. New drums if needed: $40\u2013$55 each (most TJs need them by 80,000 miles). Full rear drum service parts total: $65\u2013$120. Don't skip the hardware kit \u2014 springs fatigue and the $18 replacement is cheap relative to pulling the drums again."},{"id":"tj-budget-recipe","title":"Budget Trail TJ: Lift, 33s, and Lockers Under $2.5K","vehicle":"tj","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"budget","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":10,"high":30},"cost_usd":{"low":1500,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","basic hand tools"],"skills_required":["wrenching","prioritization"],"build_tiers":["budget","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["build_recipe","budget","lift","tires"],"parts":[{"name":"3 in budget coil lift + shocks","vendor":"Rough Country/Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":500},{"name":"33x10.5 all-terrain tires (set of 5)","vendor":"Falken/Cooper","price_estimate_usd":1000},{"name":"Rock sliders","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Lunchbox locker (rear)","vendor":"Aussie/Spartan","price_estimate_usd":300}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ factory specs","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-factory-specifications.71/"},{"title":"TJ suspension reference \u2014 My Jeep TJ","url":"https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/"}],"alternatives":["tj-budget-lift","tj-sliders","tj-locker-rear","tj-tire-fitment-35s"],"summary":"A genuinely capable TJ does not cost a fortune. Spend the first $2,500 in the right order \u2014 protection, traction, then a modest lift and a rear locker \u2014 and you have a Jeep that wheels real trails. The classic mistake is a big lift and shiny bumpers while the rockers stay bare and the rear is open.","description":"The TJ is so capable stock that a budget build is mostly traction and protection, not height. [The 93.4-inch wheelbase](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-factory-specifications.71/), solid axles front and rear, and the 4.0L's torque mean a bone-stock TJ on 31s already out-crawls most SUVs; what it lacks is rocker protection, a locker, and enough tire to lift the belly. Here is where the money does the most good, in priority order, with the parts that hit the numbers.\n\nFirst, the axle ratio. Pull the tag from the differential cover bolt or count driveshaft turns per wheel turn. A 4.0L with 3.73s pulls 33s acceptably; the 3.07s that came in many 4.0L automatics do not, and 33s on 3.07s turn the TJ into a fourth-gear-on-the-freeway slug that hunts between gears with the auto. If you have 3.07s, either stop at 32s or move the regear up the list \u2014 it is the one item that breaks the $2.5K ceiling. Second, the rear axle. The [Dana 35](/db/tj/tj-dana35-failures/) is fine for this build as written \u2014 33s, a lunchbox locker, a driver who lifts when a tire hops \u2014 but it is the reason the locker in this recipe is a $300 drop-in and not a $900 selectable. Do not put a locker in a Dana 35 that already has a weeping seal or a grooved shaft; fix that first.\n\nRock sliders before anything else. They protect the rockers and pinch seams \u2014 the most expensive body damage a TJ takes on the trail \u2014 and they double as a step. Rough Country's TJ rocker guards run $250\u2013300, Smittybilt SRC sliders $300\u2013350, and a set of bolt-on frame-tied sliders from a smaller shop lands at $400\u2013450; frame-tied is worth the extra money if you will ever put a Hi-Lift under them. Add rated recovery points if yours doesn't have them \u2014 the factory front tow hooks are rated and fine, but many TJs have lost them, and a pair of Mopar-pattern hooks is $40\u201360. The [rock sliders](/db/tj/tj-rock-sliders/) guide compares the designs. Least glamorous money, best spent.\n\n33x10.50R15 all-terrains plus a rear lunchbox locker is the single biggest capability jump for the dollar. A set of five 33x10.50R15s in a Falken Wildpeak A/T3W, Cooper Discoverer AT3 XLT, or General Grabber ATX runs $1,000\u20131,250; buy five so the spare matches and the rotation includes it. The factory 15x7 wheels will mount a 10.50 but rub the control arms at full lock \u2014 used 15x8 steel or alloy wheels with 3.75-inch backspacing are $150\u2013300 for a set of five on the used market, and 15x8 Cragar-style steelies are $90\u2013110 each new. For the locker, an Aussie Locker or Spartan lunchbox for the 27-spline Dana 35 is $280\u2013330, installs through the cover in an afternoon with a spring-compressing trick and a pair of snap-ring pliers, and turns the open rear into a climbing machine. The [rear locker guide](/db/tj/tj-locker-rear/) covers the install and the on-road manners you accept with an automatic locker. Air down to 12\u201315 psi on the trail and the difference is night and day.\n\nA 3-inch budget coil lift with matched shocks fits the 33s and keeps the geometry mild enough to skip expensive correction parts. Rough Country's 2.5-inch TJ kit with N3 shocks is $350\u2013450; Rubicon Express's 2.5-inch Super-Ride spring kit is $450\u2013550 with their shocks; a 2-inch spacer \"budget boost\" with new shocks is $200\u2013300 and clears a 33x10.50 with the fenders trimmed and the bump stops extended. Whatever the height, add a transfer-case drop kit ($30\u201350) to tame the rear driveline angle at 2\u20133 inches, extended bump stops ($40\u201360) so the tire never meets the fender, and longer sway-bar links or disconnects ($60\u2013120). Add an adjustable front track bar ($150\u2013250) if you feel any wander after the lift, and see the [budget lift](/db/tj/tj-budget-lift/) guide for the full parts list. Torque control-arm bolts with the Jeep on its tires, not on stands.\n\nWeekend one: sliders and recovery hooks, plus a full fluid service so you know the axles and transfer case are healthy going in. Weekend two: lift, bump stops, transfer-case drop, sway-bar links, and a driveway alignment check for toe and axle centering. Weekend three: wheels and tires mounted, fender trimming if the 33s touch, then the rear locker through the cover with fresh 80W-90. That order means the Jeep is drivable and protected after every weekend, and the locker goes in last, after you have confirmed the Dana 35 is dry and quiet. Total: $2,100\u20132,550 with used wheels, $2,400\u20132,900 with new steelies.\n\nHold off on regearing, a front locker, and big bumpers until the trail tells you that you need them \u2014 the 4.0L pulls 33s acceptably with the factory 3.73s. Skip a winch until you wheel alone; a $60 kinetic rope and a friend's Jeep recover most stuck TJs. Skip the SYE until the transfer-case drop stops working for you, which for most 2.5\u20133 inch TJs is never. Build protection and traction first; add capability as your wheeling grows into it. Spent in this order, every dollar buys real capability instead of looks: protection keeps a cheap trip from becoming an expensive repair, traction unlocks terrain, and the modest lift only makes room for the tires doing the work."},{"id":"tj-coolant-flush","title":"TJ 4.0L Cooling System Flush and Coolant Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":60},"tools_required":["drain pan (2+ gallon)","funnel","garden hose","pliers or hose clamp tool","rags"],"skills_required":["basic maintenance"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["cooling","maintenance","4.0L"],"parts":[{"name":"Peak 50/50 premixed coolant (1 gallon)","vendor":"Peak","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Zerex Original Green concentrate (1 gallon)","vendor":"Zerex","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"BlueDevil Radiator Flush (optional pre-flush)","vendor":"BlueDevil","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Thermostat \u2014 Stant 45352 (195\u00b0F)","part_number":"45352","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ 4.0L Cooling System Overview \u2014 Jeep Service Manual","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/cooling-system-service-specs.html"},{"title":"Coolant Drain and Fill Procedure \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-tech/cooling-system-maintenance"}],"alternatives":["tj-thermostat-replacement","tj-radiator-replacement","tj-water-pump"],"summary":"Drain the lower radiator hose, flush with water, refill with fresh 50/50 coolant \u2014 the TJ 4.0L holds about 2 gallons. Do it every 2 years or 30,000 miles regardless of color.","description":"The TJ's AMC 4.0L inline-six runs a conventional green-formula coolant system. Chrysler spec is green (silicate-based) coolant, which depletes inhibitor faster than OAT or HOAT formulas \u2014 2 years or 30,000 miles is the real service interval even if the coolant still looks green. Old coolant turns acidic and starts attacking aluminum components: the water pump impeller, the thermostat housing, and the heater core are the first casualties.\n\nThe TJ doesn't have a petcock drain on the radiator \u2014 you drain by disconnecting the lower radiator hose at the radiator inlet. It's a 30-second job but messy. Have a wide drain pan and rags ready.\n\nCapacity is approximately 7.5 quarts (1.875 gallons) for the 4.0L. The recovery bottle holds another 0.5\u20130.75 quarts. Two gallons of 50/50 premixed coolant covers it with a little left over.\n\nRunning a pre-flush product (BlueDevil, Prestone flush, or similar) is worth doing if the coolant hasn't been changed in more than 4 years or is visibly rusty. Add the flush product, run the engine for 10\u201315 minutes, drain, flush with clean water, and then refill.\n\n- Coolant smell at the heater after a flush usually means air is still in the system. Run the engine with the heater on MAX until the hot air holds steady.\n- Rusty or brown coolant is a sign the inhibitor is long gone. A flush product won't fully remove scale \u2014 a shop radiator flush machine does a better job on badly neglected systems.\n- If the engine is slow to warm up or runs cooler than normal after a flush, suspect the thermostat. A stuck-open thermostat often hides until a flush makes it obvious. Replace it while the system is open \u2014 a Stant 45352 (195\u00b0F) is about $12.\n- Coolant disposal: old coolant is toxic to animals. Don't pour it down a storm drain. Most auto parts stores accept used coolant.\n\n- 50/50 premixed coolant (2 gallons): $22\u2013$28 at any auto parts store\n- Concentrate + distilled water (mix your own): roughly the same cost, more control over ratio\n- Radiator flush product: $7\u2013$10, optional but worthwhile on older systems\n- New lower hose clamps if the old ones are rusty: $3\u2013$5 each","steps":["Let the engine cool completely. Don't open the cap on a hot system.","Place drain pan under the passenger-side lower radiator hose (connects to the lower radiator inlet).","Loosen the hose clamp with pliers or a clamp tool. Twist the hose to break the seal and pull it off the radiator neck. Let coolant drain.","Reconnect the hose once draining slows. Tighten the clamp.","(Optional flush): Add radiator flush product and top off with water. Start engine, let reach operating temperature, idle 10\u201315 minutes. Drain again.","Flush with plain water: fill with water, idle 5 minutes, drain. Repeat until the drain runs clear.","Reconnect the lower hose and tighten the clamp.","Remove the radiator cap. Fill slowly with 50/50 premixed coolant (or a 50/50 mix of concentrate and distilled water). Fill to the base of the filler neck.","Fill the overflow recovery bottle to the MIN mark.","Start the engine and let it come to operating temperature with the heater set to MAX HEAT. This bleeds air from the system \u2014 the heater core is the highest point in the circuit.","Watch the coolant level drop as air purges. Top off as needed.","Install the radiator cap. Check for leaks at the lower hose.","After the first drive, recheck the overflow bottle level and top to MIN/MAX as needed."]},{"id":"tj-radiator-replacement","title":"TJ Radiator Replacement \u2014 When to Replace and What to Buy","vehicle":"tj","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":320},"tools_required":["drain pan","Phillips screwdriver","10mm socket","pliers","coolant funnel","flare-nut wrench (automatic)","cooling-system pressure tester"],"skills_required":["basic cooling system work"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["cooling","radiator","overheating","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"OSC Radiator \u2014 4.0L TJ (4-cylinder models use different part)","part_number":"2521","vendor":"OSC Automotive","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Spectra Premium CU2521 aluminum radiator","vendor":"Spectra Premium","price_estimate_usd":105},{"name":"Mishimoto Performance aluminum radiator \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Mishimoto","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Prestone 50/50 premixed coolant","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":22}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep TJ 4.0 cooling system overview","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/tech-support/jeep-tj-cooling-system"},{"title":"How to Replace a Radiator","url":"https://www.autozone.com/diy/cooling-system/how-to-replace-radiator"}],"alternatives":["tj-coolant-flush","tj-serpentine-belt","tj-obd2-diagnostics"],"summary":"Replace a TJ radiator when the plastic end tank cracks, the core is clogged or leaking, or a pressure test fails. A replacement unit runs $90 to $320 in parts and takes a careful 2 to 4 hours. A new radiator will not fix overheating that comes from the fan clutch, thermostat, water pump, or a head gasket, so rule those out first.","description":"The factory TJ radiator is [an aluminum core with plastic end tanks crimped on](https://www.quadratec.com/c/tech-support/jeep-tj-cooling-system). The failure is almost always at the crimp seam of a tank, usually the lower one, after a decade or more of heat cycles. You will see coolant weeping at the tank seam, crusty green or orange residue on the bottom of the radiator, a coolant level that drops with no puddle (it evaporates off a hot engine), or a cap that cannot hold pressure. A radiator that is fully clogged internally shows up differently: the upper hose is hot, the lower hose stays cool, and the temperature gauge climbs on the highway even though nothing leaks.\n\nConfirm the diagnosis before spending money. A cooling-system pressure tester ($35 to $60 to buy, free to borrow from most parts-store loaner programs) pressurizes the system to the cap rating and shows a leak as a falling needle. If the needle holds and the Jeep still runs hot, read [TJ coolant flush](/db/tj/tj-coolant-flush/) and check the fan clutch, thermostat, and water pump before buying a radiator.\n\n**Which radiator to buy.** The radiator is specific to engine (4.0L versus 2.5L), and to whether the Jeep has an automatic transmission, which adds the cooler fittings. Confirm engine, transmission, and model year against the seller's fitment lookup before ordering; the part numbers in this guide are starting points, not guarantees. Three tiers cover most buyers:\n\n- **Direct-fit replacement ($90 to $140).** Brands such as OSC and Spectra Premium sell units shaped like the factory part. This is the right choice for a daily driver or a mild trail Jeep. Some replacements have aluminum tanks, some have plastic; ask which before you buy if tank material matters to you.\n- **Heavier-core aluminum ($250 to $320).** Mishimoto and similar makers sell thicker-core, all-aluminum radiators. Whether the extra core thickness measurably lowers temperatures on a 4.0 depends on airflow behind it, so treat the claim as unproven on your Jeep unless you log your own gauge or scan-tool readings before and after.\n- **Used or salvage ($40 to $80).** Cheap, and it carries the same age as the radiator you are removing. Only worth it as a stopgap.\n\n- Replacement radiator, confirmed for your engine and transmission\n- Coolant of the type your owner's manual specifies for your model year, plus distilled water if you buy concentrate. Roughly 10 to 11 quarts of mixed coolant is typical for a 4.0L TJ; confirm the capacity in your service manual. Do not mix coolant chemistries.\n- Drain pan of at least 3 gallons, clean funnel\n- Screwdrivers, a 10mm socket and extension, pliers or hose-clamp pliers\n- Flare-nut (line) wrench in the correct size for the transmission cooler lines, if the Jeep is an automatic\n- New hose clamps and, if the hoses are original, new upper and lower radiator hoses\n- Cooling-system pressure tester (borrowed or bought)\n- Shop towels, a few zip ties, and gloves\n\n1. **Start cold.** Let the engine sit for several hours. Never open the radiator cap on a warm engine; scalding coolant sprays out under pressure.\n\n2. **Drain the system.** Place the pan under the radiator, open the drain petcock at the bottom of the radiator, and remove the cap to let it flow. Coolant is toxic and tastes sweet to pets, so catch all of it and take it to a recycling point.\n\n3. **Mark and release the hoses.** Loosen the clamps on the upper and lower radiator hoses and twist each hose free. A hose that is welded on by age can be sliced lengthwise with a utility knife if you are replacing it anyway; do not pry against the plastic tank neck, because that is how a good new radiator gets cracked.\n\n4. **Automatic transmission only: release the cooler lines.** Use the flare-nut wrench so the fittings do not round off. Expect a few ounces of transmission fluid to drain. Plug or cap the lines to keep dirt out and to stop the fluid dripping. Note which line goes to which fitting before you remove them.\n\n5. **Free the overflow hose and the shroud.** Unclip the overflow (coolant recovery) hose from the radiator neck. The fan shroud is held by a few fasteners at the top. Remove them and lift the shroud up and set it over the fan or lay it on the engine side, per what your Jeep's clearance allows. Do not pry the fan blades or the viscous fan clutch; both bend with little force.\n\n6. **Remove the radiator.** Take out the upper bracket fasteners, check for any remaining clips or the air-conditioning condenser attachment if equipped (the condenser stays connected; support it so it does not hang by its lines), and lift the radiator straight up. Watch the fan blades and the condenser fins on the way out.\n\n7. **Compare the new radiator to the old one** before installing. Fitting positions, the drain petcock, the transmission cooler fittings, and the mounting pins should line up. Transfer the lower rubber cushions to the new unit if they are not included, or replace them; a hardened cushion lets the radiator vibrate and cracks tanks.\n\n8. **Install in reverse.** Seat the radiator on its lower cushions, secure the upper bracket, reinstall the shroud, reconnect the cooler lines with a drop of clean transmission fluid on the threads, and tighten them firmly without over-torquing. Reconnect the hoses with new clamps and the overflow hose.\n\n9. **Fill and bleed.** Close the petcock. Fill with the 50/50 mix slowly at the radiator neck. Start the engine with the cap off, set the heater to full hot and the blower on low, and let the thermostat open. Top up as air escapes; the level will drop sharply when the thermostat opens. Replace the cap once the level holds and no more bubbles rise.\n\n10. **Pressure test.** With the cap on, pressurize the system to the rating stamped on the cap and watch for a falling needle for 10 to 15 minutes, then inspect every joint you touched.\n\n11. **Automatic only: check the transmission fluid** at operating temperature and top up to the dipstick mark. See [TJ differential fluid](/db/tj/tj-differential-fluid/) for the habit of checking fluids together after a big service.\n\n12. **Recheck after the first drive.** Let the engine cool completely, then check the coolant level and top up the recovery bottle.\n\n- **Overheating that returns.** Weigh the fan clutch and thermostat next. With the engine off and cold, a viscous fan clutch should have noticeable drag when you spin the fan by hand; a fan that spins freely for several turns, or one that is locked solid, is worn out. Do not use a screwdriver or wrench near the fan with the engine running.\n- **Old hoses.** A 20-year-old hose that has swelled or gone soft is the usual cause of a second failure two weeks after the radiator job. Replace the radiator hoses and clamps while the system is open, and look at the heater hoses and the small bypass hoses at the thermostat and throttle body.\n- **A cap that cannot hold pressure.** A $10 radiator cap with a weak spring boils the Jeep over at lower temperatures and makes a new radiator look defective. Replace the cap with the correct rating whenever you replace the radiator.\n- **Debris and bent fins.** Clean bugs and mud from the condenser and radiator face with low-pressure water. Bent fins from winch cable or a stray rock reduce airflow; straighten them gently with a fin comb.\n- **Stray fluids.** Dispose of the old coolant and any transmission fluid properly. Never leave an open pan under a Jeep where a dog or child can reach it.\n- **Aftermarket front bumpers and winches** can block airflow to the radiator. If you add one, see [TJ front bumper guide](/db/tj/tj-front-bumper-guide/) and leave an open path for air.\n\n**Costs and sources.** Direct-fit radiator: $90 to $140. Heavier-core aluminum radiator: $250 to $320. Coolant: $22 to $30 per gallon premixed, two gallons needed to refill a drained 4.0 after flushing. Hoses: $25 to $60 for the pair. Cap: $8 to $15. Pressure tester: $35 to $60 to buy. A shop typically charges $350 to $600 for the same job including the radiator, so DIY saves roughly $250 to $400 if nothing complicates it. Order the radiator after confirming fitment for your engine, transmission, and model year; part numbers listed on this page are examples to search from. Pair the work with [TJ serpentine belt replacement](/db/tj/tj-serpentine-belt/) since the belt is exposed once the shroud is out."},{"id":"tj-ax15-tj","title":"AX-15 Manual Transmission Service","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":80},"tools_required":["15/16 inch socket or 24mm","ratchet","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002"],"tags":["maintenance","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"Redline MT-90 75W-90 GL-4 (qt)","part_number":"50304","vendor":"Red Line Oil","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Pennzoil Synchromesh","vendor":"Pennzoil","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Magnetic Drain Plug Replacement","part_number":"Omix-Ada 18880.74","vendor":"Omix-Ada","price_estimate_usd":18}],"citations":[{"title":"AX-15 Manual Transmission Service \u2014 JeepSpecs","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/tj-generation/ax-15-transmission-fluid-change/"},{"title":"Wrangler TJ Fluid Capacities","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-fluid-capacities.195/"},{"title":"TJ Transmission and Transfer Case Fluid Change","url":"https://www.billswebspace.com/TJTrans.htm"},{"title":"AX-15 vs NV3550 identification \u2014 TJ manual transmission swap notes","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/ax15-vs-nv3550-how-to-tell-which-one-you-have.9042/"}],"alternatives":["tj-np231-tj","tj-transfer-case-fluid-tj","tj-differential-fluid"],"summary":"[Drain and refill the AX-15 with 3.3 quarts of 75W-90 GL-4](https://jeepspecs.com/tj-generation/ax-15-transmission-fluid-change/) (not GL-5) every 30,000 miles \u2014 60-minute job, magnetic drain plug, no special tools. This guide covers the AX-15 only (1997\u20132002); if your TJ is a 2003 or later 5-speed, you almost certainly have an NV3550 and need ATF+4, not gear oil \u2014 see the identification note below before you buy fluid.","description":"The AX-15 is a 5-speed Aisin manual that came in TJs paired with the 4.0L (and some early 2.5L four-cylinder trucks). It's the strongest manual Jeep ever bolted behind the 4.0 and it shifts cleanly for decades when the fluid is fresh. What kills an AX-15 early is the wrong fluid \u2014 and there is a persistent forum debate about what \"the right fluid\" actually is. The factory service manual specifies GL-3, but GL-3 is essentially extinct in the consumer market. The Mopar PN for the original fluid (75W-90 GL-3) has been superseded.\n\n**Which transmission do you actually have?** This matters before you buy anything. Jeep phased the AX-15 out starting around the 2000 model year \u2014 the NV3550 (a New Venture Gear unit) replaced it first in four-cylinder TJs, then across the board once the 4.0L switched over as well, so [a 2003 or newer TJ 5-speed is an NV3550](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/ax15-vs-nv3550-how-to-tell-which-one-you-have.9042/), not an AX-15, and the two transmissions do not share a service procedure. The giveaway is the fluid spec, not the model year alone: the AX-15 uses 75W-90 gear oil as covered in this guide, while the NV3550 was factory-filled with Mopar ATF+4 and takes roughly 2.4 quarts \u2014 putting gear oil in an NV3550, or ATF in an AX-15, causes shift-quality problems that read a lot like a fluid-change job gone wrong. If you're not certain which one you have, check the case casting or ask on a TJ-specific forum with your VIN's model year and engine before draining anything; don't assume from the model year alone if your TJ is right at the changeover window.\n\nThe honest answer for AX-15 fluid: run 75W-90 GL-4. GL-5 gear oils contain sulfur-phosphorus additives that attack the AX-15's yellow-metal synchronizer parts and will cause crunchy shifts within a few thousand miles. Redline MT-90 (75W-90 GL-4) is the most-recommended choice on TJ forums for a reason \u2014 it shifts smoothly cold and hot. Pennzoil Synchromesh is the other widely-used option, especially for owners who want a slightly thinner feel in cold weather. Stay away from anything labeled \"gear oil 80W-90\" off the auto parts shelf unless you confirm the GL-4 rating on the bottle.\n\n[Capacity is 3.3 quarts](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-fluid-capacities.195/) (some sources round to 3.25). The drain plug is on the passenger-side bottom of the case and takes a 15/16\" or 24mm socket. It's magnetic \u2014 pull it after every change and wipe the metal paste off. Fine glitter is normal. Chunks, shavings, or anything the size of a grain of rice means a synchro or bearing is on its way out and you should pull the transmission for inspection.\n\nCrack the fill plug FIRST before opening the drain. The fill plug is on the driver's side of the case (square 3/8\" or 1/2\" drive). If the fill plug is seized and you've already drained, you have no way to refill without removing the transmission.\n\nWhile you're under there: feel the shift rail boot at the top of the transmission for cracks (a torn boot lets water into the case), inspect the rear yoke seal for weeping, and check the slave cylinder line for swelling if you have a hydraulic clutch. A weeping rear seal often gets misdiagnosed as a transfer case leak because the two units bolt together and fluid runs downhill to the same low point \u2014 wipe both seals dry, drive a short loop, and recheck to see which one is actually wet before ordering parts.\n\nShift feel is a useful diagnostic on its own. A notchy 1-2 shift that's worse cold and improves once the fluid warms up is normal wear, not a fault \u2014 synchros work harder when the oil is thick. A grind on any specific gear that doesn't improve with warm fluid points to a worn synchro ring on that gear, and a fluid change won't fix it, though it also won't make it worse, so there's no harm in doing the service anyway while you plan the larger repair.","steps":["Drive the Jeep 10\u201315 miles to warm the gear oil. Park on level ground.","Locate the fill plug on the driver's side of the case. Break it loose first \u2014 do not skip this step.","Position drain pan under the passenger-side drain plug. Remove the drain plug with a 15/16\" or 24mm socket.","Let it drain fully \u2014 15 to 20 minutes for warm fluid.","Wipe the magnetic drain plug clean. Inspect the magnet for chunks or large shavings. Reinstall and torque to 14\u201320 ft-lbs.","Use a fluid pump to refill through the fill hole until fluid drips back out \u2014 about 3.3 quarts.","Reinstall the fill plug and torque to 25 ft-lbs.","Drive a few miles, run through all five gears plus reverse, and recheck for leaks at the drain and fill plugs."],"pros":["One-hour job with a $40 fluid bill","Catches early bearing or synchro failure via drain plug debris","Restores notchy-cold shifts that the original fluid no longer handles"],"cons":["Wrong fluid (GL-5) will damage the synchros \u2014 manageable mistake at the parts counter","Fill plug seizes on rust-belt TJs and can require heat to break free","Capacity is awkward to measure \u2014 fill until it overflows, not by quart count","Confusing it with the NV3550 costs you a wrong fluid family for that unit if you're not sure which transmission your TJ has"]},{"id":"tj-front-driveshaft","title":"Front Driveshaft \u2014 CV Joint Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"driveshafts","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","12mm socket","13mm socket","torque wrench","ball peen hammer","u-joint press or vise"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","u-joint service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["vibration","lift","rubicon"],"parts":[{"name":"Spicer 5-213X greaseable u-joint (Rubicon 1330 CV)","part_number":"5-213X","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Spicer 211179X CV centering yoke + spring kit (TJ Rubicon)","part_number":"211179X","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Tom Wood's custom CV front driveshaft (TJ Rubicon, 1330)","vendor":"Tom Wood's","price_estimate_usd":525},{"name":"Denny's rebuild kit \u2014 TJ Rubicon 1330 CV (2 u-joints + centering yoke)","vendor":"Denny's Driveshaft","price_estimate_usd":165}],"citations":[{"title":"Denny's TJ Rubicon 1330 CV Rebuild Kit","url":"https://www.dennysdriveshaft.com/p2298_jeep_tj_rubicon_2003_to_2006_cv_rebuild_kit_1330_series_incl.html"},{"title":"WranglerTJ Forum \u2014 Driveshaft torque specs","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/driveshaft-torque-specs.24608/"},{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 Wrangler TJ Driveshaft Parts","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-replacement-parts/wrangler-tj/drive-shafts"}],"alternatives":["tj-np231-service","tj-track-bar"],"summary":"A TJ front driveshaft that vibrates is most often a worn u-joint or a worn double-cardan (CV) centering assembly; rebuilding costs $60 to $200 in parts. If the Jeep is lifted more than about 4 inches and the shaft was never changed, a rebuild only delays the vibration, and a custom shaft built for your angle is the real fix.","description":"Start by identifying what you have, because the right parts depend on it. Look at the shaft from underneath: count the joints. A plain shaft has one u-joint at each end. A double-cardan shaft has a third assembly, a pair of u-joints back to back with a short centering section, at one end. Which TJ trims came with which shaft has been described differently by different sources, so treat the following as a prompt to check your own Jeep rather than a rule: read the stamped part number or the joint cap size, match it to the vendor's fitment tool for your year and axle, and do not order from the trim name alone. The service manual is the final word for torque values on your year.\n\n**Symptoms that point to the front shaft.** A vibration that shows up at a repeatable road speed (often in the 40 to 60 mph range) and changes with the front axle engaged is the usual sign. A clunk when you shift between 2WD and 4WD, or when you let off the throttle, points to a worn joint. Rust-colored dust at the cap edges is the sign of a joint that has lost its grease. On a double-cardan shaft, grab the shaft near the centering section and try to wiggle it; any visible play in the center joint means the centering ball and spring are worn.\n\n**Rule out the cheaper causes first.** Before pulling the shaft, check tire balance and out-of-round tires, a bent wheel, a loose or worn track bar, a bent front axle, and a failed pinion bearing. A shaft that is out of phase or bent will also vibrate. Roll the shaft by hand with the Jeep on stands and watch for runout; a bent tube is cheaper to replace than to diagnose twice.\n\n**Why lift changes everything.** A driveshaft's joints work well at small angles and poorly at large ones. A lift raises the axle relative to the transfer case, so the front shaft angle steepens, and the joint at the pinion works harder. A plain shaft handles a small lift. Past 3 to 4 inches, or with a long-arm kit or a rear slip-yoke eliminator, the angle can exceed what a standard joint tolerates, producing vibration even with brand-new joints. A double-cardan joint spreads the angle across two joints and is the standard answer. For that case, see [slip yoke eliminator kits for the TJ](/db/tj/tj-sye-kit/) for the rear half of the same conversation.\n\n**Rebuild versus replace.** Greaseable Spicer or Neapco joints cost about $25 to $40 each; a rebuild kit with a centering yoke and spring runs about $165 to $200 ([Denny's lists one for the 1330 series](https://www.dennysdriveshaft.com/p2298_jeep_tj_rubicon_2003_to_2006_cv_rebuild_kit_1330_series_incl.html)). A custom shaft from Tom Wood's or a similar builder runs about $500 to $600, and arrives with correct length and phasing for your lift. If the existing shaft's tube is straight and the yokes are undamaged, rebuild; if the yokes are chewed, the tube is bent, or you have changed your lift height, replace.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock the rear wheels, raise the front on jack stands under the frame rails, and put the transmission in neutral and the transfer case in neutral if your model allows it. Never work under a Jeep supported by a jack alone.","Mark both ends of the shaft and the matching yoke or flange with a paint marker so you can reinstall it in the same orientation. A shaft refitted 180 degrees out of phase will vibrate.","Remove the strap bolts at the axle pinion yoke. Tape the u-joint caps together so they do not fall off and lose their needle bearings. Remove the bolts at the transfer-case end. Bolt sizes vary by year and joint style; use the correct socket and avoid rounding the heads.","Lower the shaft and slide it out. Support the free end so the transfer case output is not hanging.","On the bench, clamp the yoke in a vise with soft jaws. Remove the snap rings from the old joint, then press or tap the caps out with a socket slightly smaller than the cap and a larger one on the receiving side. A u-joint press speeds this up, and a ball peen hammer on a socket works if you support the yoke squarely.","Clean the yoke bores with a wire brush and inspect for wear or elongation. If a cap bore is oval or burred, the yoke needs replacing, not new joints.","Install the new joints with the grease fitting oriented as the kit instructs. Press the caps in evenly, fit the snap rings, and tap each yoke with a hammer to seat the joint; it should move freely with no binding.","On a double-cardan shaft, replace both joints and the centering ball and spring at the same time. Follow the kit's instructions for the spring's orientation. A worn centering assembly rarely wears alone.","Pump the joints with chassis grease until grease shows at all four cap seals. Rotate the joint by hand and confirm no stiff spots.","Reinstall the shaft to your paint marks. Tighten the strap bolts and flange bolts to the service manual values. Published figures vary between sources, so check yours. Use thread locker on the strap bolts where the manual calls for it.","Lower the Jeep and test drive: accelerate through 40 to 60 mph on a straight road, in 2WD and in 4WD high on a loose surface. A remaining vibration points to another cause."],"pros":["A rebuild restores smoothness for $60 to $200 and a few hours.","Greaseable joints extend service life and let you check wear at each oil change.","A custom double-cardan shaft fixes vibration at lift heights where rebuilding cannot.","Doing the job reveals worn axle yokes, pinion seals, and transfer-case output seals while you are there."],"cons":["Rebuilding a plain shaft at 4 inches or more of lift postpones the problem rather than curing it.","Press-fitting a centering assembly without a proper press or the maker's instructions can damage the new parts.","Cheap no-name joints fail within a season; buy Spicer, Neapco, or another recognized brand.","Incorrect phasing, the wrong joint size for your yoke, or skipped alignment marks can cause a vibration that mimics a bad joint.","A bent shaft tube cannot be rebuilt around; replace it.","**Costs and sources.** Spicer 5-213X greaseable joint: about $28 each. Centering yoke kit (Spicer 211179X listed above): about $75. Denny's rebuild kit: about $165. Custom shaft: about $525 and up. Confirm each part number against a fitment lookup for your year and axle before buying, since the part numbers in the listing above came from earlier research and have not been rechecked. Pair this with [TJ NP231 transfer case service](/db/tj/tj-np231-tj/) so that the output seals and shaft are inspected together."]},{"id":"tj-locker-rear","title":"Rear Locker for the TJ \u2014 Chrysler 8.25\", Dana 35, and Dana 44","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":449,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","bearing puller","pinion depth gauge","dial indicator","ring gear installation tool","RTV sealant"],"skills_required":["differential work","axle disassembly","bearing preload setting"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["locker","differential","traction","rear-axle","dana-35","chrysler-8-25"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Chrysler 8.25\" (TJ Sport/Sahara rear) RD101","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"Detroit Locker for Chrysler 8.25\" 80-1256-1","vendor":"Detroit Locker (Eaton)","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Yukon Grizzly Locker for Chrysler 8.25\" YG C8.25-27","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Dana 44 (TJ Rubicon rear) RD100","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":849}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Rear Locker Options: Dana 35 vs 8.25 vs Dana 44","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/rear-locker-tj-options/"},{"title":"ARB Air Locker Chrysler 8.25 Installation Guide","url":"https://arb.com.au/arb-air-locker-instructions"},{"title":"Pirate4x4 \u2014 Dana 35 Rear Axle Strength Discussion","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/forum/jeep-wrangler-tj/dana-35-axle-strength/"}],"alternatives":["tj-regear","tj-skid-plates"],"summary":"If your TJ has a Dana 35 rear axle, address the axle's known weak points before adding a locker \u2014 [a locked Dana 35 on aggressive terrain](https://www.pirate4x4.com/forum/jeep-wrangler-tj/dana-35-axle-strength/) is asking the C-clips and side shafts to do something they weren't built for.","description":"The rear axle choice in the TJ is a meaningful build variable. Dana 35 units (base 1997\u20132002 models) use C-clip axle retention \u2014 when a side shaft breaks under load, the wheel can separate from the vehicle. The Dana 35's small ring gear and thin side shafts are the limiting factor. Adding a locker to a Dana 35 and running 35\" tires on technical terrain is a scenario where side shaft failure becomes a real probability, not a theoretical one. The correct answer is either upgrading to a Chrysler 8.25\" (a common axle swap) or installing a Chrome-Moly axle upgrade from Superior Axle before adding the locker.\n\nThe Chrysler 8.25\" rear axle (Sport and Sahara trim 1997\u20132006) is a substantially stronger unit \u2014 larger ring gear diameter, full-float flange (no C-clips), and a track record as a reliable trail rear end. Most serious TJ builds run the 8.25\" or swap to a Dana 44 (from a Rubicon or other source). For the 8.25\", the Detroit Locker ($499) is the value pick: a mechanical locker that engages automatically under torque differential and disengages on deceleration. On-trail behavior is predictable once you know what to expect \u2014 slight ratcheting on tight pavement turns is the trade-off for consistent engagement when one wheel lifts.\n\n[The ARB Air Locker](https://arb.com.au/arb-air-locker-instructions) ($799 for 8.25\") is the selectable option: locked when you want it, open diff when you don't. For a rig that mixes street driving and trail use, selectable is the correct choice \u2014 an always-on locker like the Detroit noticeably changes driving behavior on pavement, including oversteer tendency in corners. The ARB requires an air compressor and solenoid kit (sold separately, approximately $250); plan for the additional install time and wiring.\n\nRubicon owners with the factory Dana 44 rear already have a stronger axle foundation \u2014 the ARB Air Locker RD100 is the premium choice and pairs well with the Rubicon's factory ARB front air locker for a matched selectable system.","steps":["Drain the rear differential fluid completely.","Raise the rear axle on jack stands with both rear tires off the ground.","Remove both rear wheels and brake drums or calipers (set aside; do not disconnect brake lines).","Remove rear axle shafts \u2014 on the Chrysler 8.25\", remove the differential housing cover and retaining pin to release C-clips on Dana 35; on 8.25\" (full-float) remove the axle retaining plate bolts.","Remove the differential carrier assembly from the housing.","Press out existing spider gears and side gears; install locker unit per manufacturer instructions.","Reinstall carrier assembly with new bearing preload as specified. Set backlash to factory spec (0.005\"\u20130.008\" on Chrysler 8.25\").","For ARB Air Locker: route air line through axle housing per ARB instructions; connect to solenoid and compressor.","Install new diff cover gasket or apply RTV; torque cover bolts to 20\u201325 ft-lbs in a cross pattern.","Reinstall axle shafts and retaining hardware; torque to spec.","Refill with 80W-140 gear oil (Chrysler 8.25\": 2.5 pints); use friction modifier if TJ has a limited-slip OEM unit.","Reinstall brake hardware, wheels; torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs.","Test on dry pavement at low speed to verify engagement feel before trail use."],"pros":["Chrysler 8.25\" with a Detroit or ARB locker is a durable, capable rear end for moderate to aggressive trail use","ARB selectable design preserves on-road driving behavior \u2014 unlocked for streets, locked for trails","Detroit Locker provides automatic engagement with no compressor, wiring, or solenoid","Rubicon Dana 44 with ARB creates a matched selectable front/rear system","Dramatically improves traction on side-hills, loose terrain, and one-wheel-up obstacle lines"],"cons":["Dana 35 C-clip retention is a failure risk under locker loads \u2014 upgrade axle shafts or replace the axle before locking it","ARB Air Locker requires compressor, solenoid wiring, and air line routing \u2014 significant additional install complexity and cost","Detroit Locker causes noticeable handling changes on pavement \u2014 understeer in tight turns, potential for rear-end stepping in low-grip corners","Installation requires differential expertise \u2014 incorrect bearing preload or backlash will damage the ring and pinion","The locker alone does not compensate for regear needs \u2014 35\" tires still require 4.56 or 4.88 gears regardless of locking differential"]},{"id":"tj-np231-tj","title":"NP231 Transfer Case \u2014 Service and Weak Points","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":1000},"tools_required":["13mm socket","drain pan","fluid pump","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid changes"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["transfer-case","sye","slip-yoke","fluid"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline ATF+4 (quart)","part_number":"VV324","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Mopar ATF+4 (quart)","part_number":"68218057AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":11},{"name":"JB Conversions Super Short SYE","vendor":"JB Conversions","price_estimate_usd":425},{"name":"Teraflex SYE kit","part_number":"2204000","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":295},{"name":"Rough Country SYE shaft","part_number":"50-7906","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":130}],"citations":[{"title":"NP231 fluid type \u2014 Wrangler Forum","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/what-fluid-and-quantity-for-np231-transfer-case.1284833/"},{"title":"JB Conversions NP231 SYE","url":"https://www.jbconversions.com/products/sye/np231j_std_sye.php"},{"title":"Teraflex SYE install \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/howto/how-install-teraflex-slip-yoke-eliminator-2wd-low-range-np231-transfer-case"}],"alternatives":["tj-regear","tj-ax15-tj"],"summary":"The NP231 in a TJ is a tough chain-driven transfer case: the gears and chain usually outlast the Jeep, and the real weak spots are the rear output bushing and seal, the 2.72:1 low range, and the stock 23-spline output shaft. Change the fluid every 30,000 miles, and plan a slip-yoke eliminator only once lift or vibration gives you a reason.","description":"The NP231 (also stamped NV231 on later cases) bolts behind the AX-15, NV3550, or automatic of every non-Rubicon TJ. It is a part-time 4WD case: you pick 2WD, 4-Hi (locked, 1:1), neutral, or 4-Lo (locked, 2.72:1). The Rubicon uses the NV241OR with a 4:1 low range, which is a different case with a different crawl ratio. Understanding that distinction sets honest expectations: the 231 will keep a 4.0L Jeep moving over trails, but its 2.72:1 low range limits how slowly and controlled you can crawl, and that limit is more noticeable with taller tires unless you regear the axles (see the [regear guide](/db/tj/tj-regear/)).\n\n**What the 231 does well.** The chain, drive sprockets, and planetary assembly are rarely the failure. Owners put 200,000 miles on these with fluid changes and nothing else. [Mopar specifies ATF+4](https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/what-fluid-and-quantity-for-np231-transfer-case.1284833/), and the [transfer case fluid guide](/db/tj/tj-transfer-case-fluid-tj/) covers the step-level service, including the fill-to-the-hole method (most Jeeps take roughly 1.5 to 2 quarts, not a fixed number), the rule to crack the fill plug before draining, and the aluminum-housing torque limits. Water is the biggest enemy: milky fluid means the bearings have a short life ahead unless you change it right away.\n\n**Where it fails.** Most problems start at the rear. The tail housing contains a bronze bushing that supports the slip yoke. When that bushing wears, the yoke wobbles, the rear output seal loses its seat, and you get a leak that returns after every seal replacement. Check by grabbing the rear yoke and pushing it up, down, and sideways with the Jeep on stands; there should be no perceptible play beyond the spline slop. The front output seal is the second common leak; see the [front driveshaft guide](/db/tj/tj-front-driveshaft/). Less common but real: a plugged vent that pressurizes the case, a chain that has stretched after high mileage (heard as a faint whine in 2WD and 4-Hi that changes with load), and shift-fork pad wear that makes the case jump out of 4-Lo.\n\n**Seal and bushing repair.** If the case is otherwise healthy, a rear seal and tail-housing bushing kit costs $25 to $60 and the repair is a couple of hours: remove the rear driveshaft, drain the case, pull the yoke nut and yoke, drive out the old seal and bushing with a slide hammer and bushing driver, press in the new bushing, install a new seal, and refill. This is the correct fix on a stock-height Jeep.\n\n**The slip-yoke question.** The factory rear output is a 23-spline shaft with a long slip yoke. The yoke slides on the shaft as the rear axle moves, and the driveshaft is a conventional two-joint shaft. Lift changes the driveshaft angle: higher lift steepens the angle at the transfer case end, which loads the slip joint and the tail bushing, and shows up as highway vibration and chronic leaking. A slip-yoke eliminator replaces the tail housing and output shaft with a short fixed-flange shaft (usually 32-spline), and the driveshaft is replaced with a CV (double-cardan) shaft that has its own slip spline. That changes the geometry completely, which is why the conversion is paired with the new driveshaft rather than sold as a standalone fix.\n\n**When an SYE is worth it.** The usual threshold is about 3 inches of lift, or any smaller lift where a worn bushing and vibration refuse to be cured. Below that, replacing the bushing and seal is cheaper and keeps the original driveline. Above it, SYE is the standard answer, and many TJ owners fit it with a long-arm lift or a 4-inch spring lift. The [SYE kit guide](/db/tj/tj-sye-kit/) walks through the kits and tradeoffs in more detail, so this guide stays on the transfer case itself.\n\n**Kit tiers.** Budget shaft-only kits sit around $130 to $300 and generally cover the shaft swap but demand more fabrication and driveshaft work from you. Mid-tier kits including a tail housing run around $295 to $425. A premium 32-spline kit such as the [JB Conversions Super Short SYE](https://www.jbconversions.com/products/sye/np231j_std_sye.php) (about $425) is the stronger choice for a heavily built Jeep, since the larger-diameter output handles locker torque with tall tires. Add $400 to $650 for the CV rear driveshaft; shops that build them for TJs include Tom Wood's and Adams. Total SYE conversion is generally $600 to $1,000 before labor.\n\n**Measuring for the driveshaft.** Have the Jeep at ride height on flat ground. Measure from the face of the transfer case output flange to the face of the pinion flange, with the driveshaft not fitted, and give the driveshaft builder the number along with the U-joint style on the axle end, the flange bolt pattern, and your lift height. Do the measurement after the SYE is installed because the output flange sits farther forward than the stock yoke.\n\n**Low-range gearing.** Four-to-one low-range gear sets for the 231 exist from aftermarket builders, and they give a more controllable crawl ratio. Plan on four figures for parts and a rebuild, and compare the cost against a regear first, since changing axle gears also helps with tall tires on-road.\n\n**Costs and sources.** Fluid service: $25 to $40. Seal and bushing kit: $25 to $60. SYE kit: $130 to $425. CV rear driveshaft: $400 to $650. Gear-set low-range options: $1,000 and up. Pair an SYE install with your [lift choices](/db/tj/tj-lift-options/) so the driveshaft is built once for the final ride height.","steps":["Identify the case from the tag on the housing. A 231 or NV231 is a standard TJ case; a 241OR means a Rubicon and different parts.","Change the fluid as described in the [transfer case fluid guide](/db/tj/tj-transfer-case-fluid-tj/) and read the drained fluid for water, metal, and burn smell.","With the Jeep on stands, check the rear yoke for movement and inspect the vent hose and the front and rear output seals for weeping.","If the rear is leaking and the Jeep is near stock height, replace the tail-housing bushing and rear seal, and keep the slip yoke.","If you are lifted 3 inches or more, or the leak and vibration persist, plan the SYE: support the case with a jack, remove the rear driveshaft, drain the case, and unbolt the tail housing.","Install the SYE shaft and housing per the kit's instructions, using the sealant and thread locker the kit specifies, and torque the flange bolts to the manufacturer's figure.","Install the new CV rear driveshaft sized at ride height, then refill with ATF+4 to the lower edge of the fill hole.","Test drive at 30, 45, and 65 mph checking for vibration, then re-check for leaks and re-torque the flange bolts after 100 miles."],"pros":["The 231's gears and chain are durable; most owners only ever need fluid changes.","A $25 to $60 bushing and seal kit cures most rear leaks on a stock-height Jeep.","An SYE removes the tail-housing bushing as a failure point, tames lifted-driveline vibration, and uses a stronger output shaft."],"cons":["The 2.72:1 low range limits crawl control, especially with tall tires and no regear.","A SYE conversion costs $600 to $1,000 including the CV driveshaft, and it changes the driveshaft rather than only the case.","Water intrusion quickly damages the bearings and the chain, and the symptoms (a faint whine, milky fluid) are hard to notice.","Shaft-only budget kits shift fabrication and measuring to you, and a badly measured driveshaft causes vibration that is hard to diagnose."]},{"id":"tj-regear","title":"Re-Gearing the TJ \u2014 4.56, 4.88, and 5.13 Ratios","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":2200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","pinion depth gauge","dial indicator","bearing press","INCH-lb torque wrench","socket set","RTV sealant","gear marking compound"],"skills_required":["differential work","bearing preload setting","ring and pinion installation","torque specification knowledge"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["gears","regear","drivetrain","ring-and-pinion","axle"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion Set for Dana 30 (TJ front) YG D30-488","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Yukon Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion Set for Chrysler 8.25\" (TJ rear) YG C8.25-488","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":209},{"name":"Rugged Ridge 4.56 Master Ring & Pinion Overhaul Kit (Dana 30 or 8.25\", includes bearings)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":329},{"name":"Nitro Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion for Dana 30 G D30-488","vendor":"Nitro Gear","price_estimate_usd":175}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Re-Gear Guide: Choosing the Right Ratio","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/regear-guide-tj-wrangler/"},{"title":"Yukon Gear \u2014 Ring and Pinion Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/installation-instructions"},{"title":"4WheelDrive Hardware \u2014 TJ Gear Ratio Calculator","url":"https://www.4wdh.com/gear-ratio-calculator"}],"alternatives":["tj-locker-rear","tj-tire-fitment-35s","tj-long-arm-lift"],"summary":"Stock TJ gear ratios were designed for 29\"\u201331\" tires \u2014 moving to 35s without re-gearing trades engine RPM at highway speed and kills the low-end torque that makes the 4.0L worth having off-road.","description":"The TJ's 4.0L inline-six makes its best torque between 2,000\u20133,500 RPM. Stock gear ratios (3.07 on 1997\u20132001 Dana 35 base models, 3.73 on most others) produce acceptable highway performance with 30\" tires because they keep the engine in that band at 65\u201370 mph. Put 35\" tires on a 3.73-geared TJ and you've effectively [reduced the mechanical advantage by roughly 14%](https://www.4wdh.com/gear-ratio-calculator), which means the engine now has to rev higher to do the same work, low-range crawl ratio suffers, and the transmission is hunting for a lower gear on every hill. The 4.0L will tolerate it, but it will never feel right.\n\nGear ratio selection follows the tire diameter. For 35\" tires, [4.56 is the widely-used street/trail ratio](https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/regear-guide-tj-wrangler/) \u2014 highway manners are restored and the crawl ratio improves significantly. For 35\" tires on a dedicated trail build, or for 37\" tires on any build, 4.88 is the better answer: more low-end authority for rock work and acceptable highway performance with the overdrive working harder. The 5.13 ratio is for highly modified rigs running 37\"\u201340\" tires with aggressive gearing strategies \u2014 it's not a street ratio and carries a real fuel economy penalty.\n\nFront and rear gears must match exactly. The TJ uses independent axle housings front and rear, and if you install different gear ratios in each axle, the transfer case will transfer torque between them every time 4WD is engaged, creating binding in the drivetrain. The common mistake is gearing one axle at a time for budget reasons \u2014 the result is a rig you can't use 4WD on safely until both axles match. Do both axles at the same time or be prepared to drive the first axle in 2WD-only until the second axle is geared.\n\nBudget for professional installation if you don't have differential experience. [Ring and pinion installation requires setting bearing preload](https://www.yukongear.com/installation-instructions) with an inch-pound torque wrench, measuring backlash with a dial indicator, and reading gear tooth contact patterns with marking compound. A misset pinion depth or incorrect preload will destroy the gears within 5,000\u201310,000 miles.","steps":["Drain both front and rear differentials completely.","Remove axle shafts from the axle to be geared (front requires removing the front driveshaft and disconnecting the front axle shafts; rear requires removing rear axle shafts).","Remove the differential carrier from the housing.","Remove the ring gear from the carrier; install new ring gear with new ring gear bolts. Torque ring gear bolts to 70\u201380 ft-lbs in a crossing pattern.","Press old pinion bearing races out of the axle housing; press new races in.","Install new pinion with correct shim stack for target depth (requires pinion depth gauge \u2014 measure, shim, check, repeat).","Set pinion bearing preload using crush sleeve or shim pack per manufacturer spec (rotating torque: 15\u201325 in-lbs on new bearings).","Install carrier with new side bearing shims; set backlash to 0.006\"\u20130.009\" (Dana 30) or 0.005\"\u20130.008\" (Chrysler 8.25\").","Check tooth contact pattern with gear marking compound \u2014 pattern should be centered on the drive face of each tooth.","Install new diff cover gasket or RTV; torque cover bolts to 25 ft-lbs.","Refill with correct gear oil: 80W-90 synthetic (Dana 30), 80W-140 synthetic (Chrysler 8.25\").","Repeat for second axle.","Drive 500 miles before re-checking fluid for metal particles indicating break-in wear."],"pros":["Restores highway drivability and proper low-end torque after moving to 35\" tires","4.88 gears with 35s produce a crawl ratio (with the NP231 4:1 low range) of approximately 87:1 \u2014 genuinely capable for rock work","Matched front/rear gear sets are required for safe 4WD operation \u2014 this upgrade is non-negotiable for 35\" tire builds","Yukon and Nitro gear sets are widely available with documented fitment for TJ axles","Improves fuel economy compared to stock gears + oversized tires by returning the engine to its efficient RPM range"],"cons":["Installation requires differential expertise and specialized tools \u2014 incorrect installation damages gears within miles","Both axles must be geared simultaneously or 4WD cannot be used safely","Full cost including labor at a shop runs $1,500\u2013$2,200 for both axles","Gear break-in requires 500 miles of varied driving before hard off-road use","Chrysler 8.25\" limited-slip owners must verify the locker/LSD choice is compatible with the new gear set's side bearing spec"]},{"id":"tj-sye-kit","title":"SYE Kit for the NP231 Transfer Case \u2014 Eliminating Driveshaft Vibration","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":599},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","snap ring pliers","bearing press","drain pan","transfer case oil"],"skills_required":["drivetrain work","transfer case disassembly","driveshaft installation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["sye","transfer-case","driveshaft","np231","vibration"],"parts":[{"name":"Tom Wood's Custom Driveshafts SYE Kit for NP231 (TW SYE-231)","vendor":"Tom Wood's Custom Driveshafts","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Advance Adapters SYE Kit for NP231 (AA-241630)","vendor":"Advance Adapters","price_estimate_usd":279},{"name":"Rubicon Express SYE Kit for NP231 (RE3530)","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Tom Wood's Custom CV Rear Driveshaft for TJ (custom-built to spec after SYE install)","vendor":"Tom Wood's Custom Driveshafts","price_estimate_usd":250}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 NP231 SYE Install Guide with Photos","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/np231-sye-install-guide/"},{"title":"Tom Wood's Driveshafts TJ SYE Overview","url":"https://www.tomwoodsdriveshaft.com/sye"},{"title":"Advance Adapters SYE for NP231 Instructions","url":"https://www.advanceadapters.com/sye-np231-instructions"}],"alternatives":["tj-mid-lift","tj-long-arm-lift","tj-regear"],"summary":"Above 2.5\" of lift, the NP231's slip-yoke rear output causes driveshaft vibration at highway speed that makes the TJ unpleasant to drive \u2014 an SYE kit and CV rear driveshaft eliminates it.","description":"The NP231 transfer case uses a slip-yoke output at the rear \u2014 a design where the yoke slides in and out of the output shaft as the driveshaft plunges and extends with suspension travel. At stock ride height, this works fine. Lift the vehicle 2.5\"\u20134\" and the rear driveshaft's operating angle steepens beyond what the slip yoke and standard U-joint can absorb without vibration. At highway speed, the resulting vibration feels like a tire imbalance or a failing u-joint \u2014 a buzz or thump that comes on around 45\u201355 mph and worsens as speed increases.\n\nThe SYE (Slip Yoke Eliminator) kit replaces the slip yoke output with a fixed-yoke flange. This requires fitting a new rear output shaft with a fixed-yoke companion flange, which then accepts a custom-length CV (constant velocity) rear driveshaft. The CV driveshaft uses a double-cardan joint at the transfer case end that allows steeper operating angles without the velocity variation that causes vibration with a single U-joint. The result is a smooth, vibration-free rear driveshaft at any legal highway speed regardless of lift height.\n\n[Tom Wood's SYE kit](https://www.tomwoodsdriveshaft.com/sye) is the benchmark. Tom Wood's manufactures both the SYE hardware and the companion CV driveshaft, so the entire system is matched and tested. They build custom driveshafts to measured length after the SYE is installed \u2014 send them your measurements and they build the shaft to spec. [Advance Adapters' SYE](https://www.advanceadapters.com/sye-np231-instructions) ($279) is a reliable, lower-cost alternative that uses the same design principle and works with any quality rear CV shaft.\n\nThe Rubicon's NP242 (Select-Trac) transfer case is a different unit with a different output configuration. This SYE kit does not fit the NP242. Rubicon owners with the NP241OR have their own SYE solutions, but the NP241OR is less commonly used outside Rubicon trim and the aftermarket solutions are more limited.","steps":["Drain the transfer case fluid completely.","Disconnect the rear driveshaft from the transfer case output yoke (4 bolts at the output flange or U-joint strap).","Remove the transfer case rear output housing bolts (typically 6 bolts around the rear face of the transfer case).","Slide the OEM slip yoke output assembly out of the transfer case rear housing.","Install the SYE's new output shaft into the rear housing per kit instructions. Verify the output shaft engages the chain drive and rotates freely.","Install new output housing (included in SYE kit) with fresh gasket or RTV. Torque housing bolts to 35\u201340 ft-lbs.","Install the new fixed-yoke companion flange onto the SYE output shaft; torque the retaining nut to 150\u2013175 ft-lbs.","Measure the rear driveshaft distance from the SYE flange to the pinion flange with suspension at ride height. Order a custom CV rear driveshaft to this length (Tom Wood's, Adams Driveshafts, or similar).","Reinstall rear driveshaft to the SYE flange and pinion. Torque U-bolt straps to 15\u201320 ft-lbs.","Refill transfer case with ATF+4 automatic transmission fluid (NP231 capacity: approximately 1.4 pints).","Test drive on a highway \u2014 vibration should be eliminated. If any vibration remains, verify pinion angle (target: 1\u00b0\u20133\u00b0 downward from horizontal at ride height)."],"pros":["Completely eliminates highway-speed driveshaft vibration that is otherwise unavoidable above 2.5\" of lift","Fixed-yoke output is stronger and more reliable than the OEM slip yoke under trail torque loads","Tom Wood's matched SYE + CV driveshaft is a proven system with years of TJ trail history","Bolt-in install on the NP231 requires no special machining or permanent modifications to the transfer case housing","CV driveshaft can accommodate additional lift in the future without needing replacement (within the joint's angle limits)"],"cons":["Does not fit the NP242 (Sahara Select-Trac) \u2014 verify your transfer case before purchasing","Custom rear driveshaft must be ordered after SYE install and measured correctly \u2014 plan for lead time on the driveshaft","Transfer case must be fully drained and refilled \u2014 use ATF+4, not gear oil","Total system cost (SYE + CV driveshaft) runs $550\u2013$650 when both are purchased together \u2014 budget accordingly when planning a mid-lift or long-arm install","Incorrect pinion angle after installation will cause a different type of vibration \u2014 verify angle with a digital angle finder before closing out the install"]},{"id":"tj-alternator-replacement","title":"TJ Alternator Replacement \u2014 Symptoms, Testing, and Install","vehicle":"tj","category":"electrical","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":85,"high":500},"tools_required":["multimeter","10mm socket","13mm socket","serpentine belt tool or breaker bar","pry bar or tensioner tool"],"skills_required":["basic electrical testing","belt removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["electrical","alternator","charging","battery"],"parts":[{"name":"Remy 94554 remanufactured alternator \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Remy","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Bosch AL7520X remanufactured alternator","vendor":"Bosch","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"DB Electrical new alternator ADR0399","vendor":"DB Electrical","price_estimate_usd":88},{"name":"Duralast Gold remanufactured alternator","vendor":"AutoZone","price_estimate_usd":105}],"citations":[{"title":"How to Test an Alternator","url":"https://www.autozone.com/diy/charging-system/how-to-test-an-alternator"},{"title":"TJ Wrangler electrical troubleshooting","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/"}],"alternatives":["tj-battery-upgrade","tj-ignition-switch"],"summary":"A TJ alternator that is going bad shows up first as a battery warning light, dim headlights at idle, or a battery that keeps going flat. Confirm the diagnosis with a multimeter before you buy anything: [a healthy charging system reads 13.8 to 14.7 V](https://www.autozone.com/diy/charging-system/how-to-test-an-alternator) at the battery with the engine running, and a weak battery, a loose belt, or a bad ground can mimic a failed alternator.","description":"The 4.0L TJ carries a belt-driven alternator rated 90 amps or 117 amps depending on year, options, and the Jeep's original equipment. It sits low on the front of the engine, and the serpentine belt, which has an automatic tensioner, spins it. On these Jeeps the charging voltage is controlled through a field circuit that the PCM manages, rather than by a regulator that lives only inside the alternator, so a no-charge condition can also come from a blown fuse, a damaged field wire, or a connector problem. Check those before buying a new unit.\n\n**Testing sequence.** Start with the cheap checks, because most no-charge complaints are not the alternator itself.\n\n- **Battery rest voltage.** Engine off, after sitting for an hour, a healthy battery reads about 12.6 V. Below 12.4 V it is partly discharged; below 12.0 V it is deeply discharged and may be damaged. Charge it on a wall charger before testing the alternator, because a flat battery makes a healthy alternator look weak.\n- **Belt and pulley.** With the engine off, check that the belt is not glazed, cracked, or loose and that the pulley turns freely. A slipping belt shows up as a squeal at startup and a low output; the [serpentine belt guide](/db/tj/tj-serpentine-belt/) covers replacement.\n- **Running voltage.** Start the engine and measure across the battery posts at about 1,500 rpm. Expect 13.8 to 14.7 V. Lower than 13.5 V means it is undercharging; higher than 15.0 V means it is overcharging and cooking the battery. Turn on the headlights, the blower on high, and the rear defrost, and the voltage should hold above about 13.0 V.\n- **Voltage drop.** With the same loads on, measure from the alternator output stud to the battery positive post, then from the alternator case to the battery negative post. Either reading should be less than about 0.5 V. More points to a corroded cable or ground, and the fix is a $15 cable, not a $100 alternator.\n- **Ripple test.** Switch the multimeter to AC volts and measure at the battery with the engine running. More than about 0.5 V AC suggests a failed diode in the alternator. That is a diagnosis you can only make with a meter.\n- **Fuses and field wire.** Check the fusible link and the fuses in the power distribution center, and inspect the two-pin field connector at the alternator for corrosion or a loose terminal.\n\n**Free testing.** Most national parts chains test an alternator on the vehicle at no charge, and some will test it off the Jeep. Do the checks yourself first, so you can answer their questions.\n\n**Which replacement.** Remanufactured units such as the Remy and Bosch units in the parts list are widely used and typically run $90 to $125 with a core exchange. A new DB Electrical unit sits at a similar price with no core to return. Confirm fitment by engine and VIN; the TJ had alternator variants with different output ratings and different connectors, and a wrong unit will mount but not charge. The cheapest no-name units on marketplace sites have a higher return rate, and an alternator that fails after 20,000 miles costs far more in labor than the price difference. If you run a winch, extra lighting, an onboard air compressor, or a stereo, moving to the 117-amp unit or a high-output aftermarket alternator (typically $250 to $500) is worth considering, and you should upgrade the battery cables to match; see the [battery upgrade guide](/db/tj/tj-battery-upgrade/) and the [winch guide](/db/tj/tj-winch/).\n\n**What an alternator does not do.** It is not a battery charger. Do not use a running Jeep to bring a dead battery up to full charge, because the alternator will run at maximum output for hours and may overheat. Jump-start, then charge on a wall charger.\n\n**Costs and sources.** Remanufactured alternator: $90 to $125 with core. New: $88 to $140. High-output aftermarket: $250 to $500. Serpentine belt: $25 to $35. Battery cables: $15 to $60 each. A shop typically charges $100 to $200 for labor. If you chase a persistent no-charge or a parasitic draw, the [OBD-II diagnostics guide](/db/tj/tj-obd2-diagnostics/) and the [ignition switch guide](/db/tj/tj-ignition-switch/) cover the other common causes.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery cable first and tuck it away from the post. This prevents a short from the output stud, which carries battery voltage whenever the cable is connected.","Relieve belt tension at the automatic tensioner with a serpentine belt tool or a long-handle ratchet, then slip the belt off the alternator pulley. Note the belt path or photograph it; a diagram sticker is also usually on the radiator support.","Photograph the wiring. Remove the nut on the main output stud (typically 10mm) and unplug the field connector by squeezing its tab.","Remove the alternator mounting bolts. Support the alternator with one hand while you remove the last one, since it weighs around 10 to 15 pounds.","Compare the new unit to the old: mounting tabs, pulley groove count and diameter, plug style, and stud location.","Position the new alternator and start every mounting bolt by hand before tightening any of them. Tighten to the service manual's figure, which is typically around 30 ft-lbs; do not guess higher, since the housing ears crack.","Reconnect the output wire and the field plug. Snug the stud nut gently. Over-tightening strips the stud and the repair is another alternator.","Route the belt, release the tensioner, and check that the belt sits in every groove.","Reconnect the battery negative. Start the engine and read 13.8 to 14.7 V at the battery posts with accessories off, then with headlights and the blower on high.","Recheck after a 20-mile drive. If a new alternator reads below 13.5 V, return it for another unit rather than driving on a partly charging system."],"pros":["A $90 to $125 part and a 90-minute job, with no special tools beyond a belt tool.","Testing first can save $100 by catching a bad battery, a bad cable, or a loose belt.","The replacement moment is the right time to upgrade output if you added accessories."],"cons":["Remanufactured units vary in quality; a bad one fails inside a year and the labor is yours again.","A failing alternator can damage a battery before it fully quits, so you may be buying two parts.","The output stud and field plug are fragile; over-torquing or yanking wires creates a new repair.","Charging problems sometimes come from the field circuit or PCM, and replacing the alternator does not cure those."]},{"id":"tj-battery-upgrade","title":"TJ Battery Replacement and Upgrade Guide","vehicle":"tj","category":"electrical","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":400},"tools_required":["10mm and 13mm sockets or wrenches (terminal clamps)","1/2\" socket or 5/16\" nut driver (97-04 J-hook nuts) or 13mm socket (05-06 wedge bolt)","wire brush or terminal cleaner","digital multimeter","baking soda, water, and a rag","safety glasses and nitrile gloves"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["battery","electrical","winch","starting","agm","group-34","voltage-drop"],"parts":[{"name":"Group 34 flooded battery, 700+ CCA (ACDelco Gold 34, Duralast Gold 34, Interstate MTP-34)","vendor":"ACDelco / Duralast / Interstate","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Group 34 AGM battery, 750+ CCA (DieHard Platinum AGM 34, Duracell Ultra Platinum AGM 34)","vendor":"DieHard / Duracell","price_estimate_usd":230},{"name":"Optima RedTop 34 (starting AGM)","part_number":"8002-002","vendor":"Optima","price_estimate_usd":270},{"name":"Optima YellowTop D34 (dual-purpose AGM)","part_number":"8040-218","vendor":"Optima","price_estimate_usd":310},{"name":"Odyssey Extreme 34-PC1500T (dual-purpose AGM, 850 CCA)","part_number":"34-PC1500T","vendor":"Odyssey","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Replacement battery tray, 97-06 TJ (if the original has rusted through)","vendor":"Crown Automotive / Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Battery terminal protector spray and felt washers","vendor":"CRC / NOCO","price_estimate_usd":10}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ battery group size and hold-down by year (JeepForum)","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/group-34-battery-hold-down.1171718/"},{"title":"2005-06 TJ wedge-style battery hold-down (WranglerTJForum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/06-tj-battery-hold-downs.48436/"},{"title":"Optima RedTop vs YellowTop","url":"https://www.optimabatteries.com/learn/redtop-vs-yellowtop"},{"title":"Odyssey 34-PC1500T specifications","url":"https://www.odysseybattery.com/"}],"alternatives":["tj-alternator-replacement","tj-winch","tj-ignition-switch"],"summary":"[Every 1997\u20132006 TJ takes a Group 34 battery](https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/group-34-battery-hold-down.1171718/), and for a Jeep without a winch or camp loads a $150 flooded Group 34 with 700+ CCA is the right buy \u2014 the AGM premium buys vibration tolerance and slower self-discharge, not more starting power. If you run a winch, a fridge, or lights that get used with the engine off, move to a dual-purpose AGM (Optima YellowTop D34 or Odyssey 34-PC1500T) that can take repeated partial discharge without dying. A battery is a 30-minute job; the 15 minutes spent cleaning the tray and doing a voltage-drop test on the cables is what makes the new one last.","description":"The battery sits in a steel tray on the driver's side of the engine bay, held by J-hooks and a top bar on 1997\u20132004 and [by a wedge bolt at the front of the tray on 2005\u20132006](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/06-tj-battery-hold-downs.48436/). Group 34 is the only size that drops in without modifying the tray; the taller Group 24 and the longer Group 27 don't fit under the hold-down without cutting. AGM (absorbed glass mat) batteries are the same size and drop in the same tray. They're sealed, they don't spill on a side slope, they shrug off the vibration that shakes the plates loose in a flooded battery on washboard, and they self-discharge slower when the Jeep sits \u2014 which is the actual reason to pay for one on a trail rig.\n\n**Starting vs. dual-purpose.** A starting battery (any flooded battery, Optima RedTop) has thin plates for big cranking current and doesn't tolerate deep discharge; a dozen runs down to dead and it's finished. [A dual-purpose AGM (YellowTop, Odyssey) has thicker plates](https://www.optimabatteries.com/learn/redtop-vs-yellowtop) that survive hundreds of partial-discharge cycles at a small cost in cranking amps. A winch pull is a 300\u2013450 A load for a minute or more, well beyond what the factory alternator (roughly 117\u2013136 A depending on year) can feed; the battery carries the difference and gets pulled down hard every time. That's the dual-purpose case. So is a 12 V fridge overnight. Radios, LED light bars, and a stereo that only run with the engine on are alternator loads and don't change the battery choice.\n\n**When it's time.** A rested battery (engine off 4+ hours, no loads) should read 12.6 V or higher. 12.4 V is 75% charged; 12.0 V is half. A battery that won't hold 12.6 V after a full charge, or that cranks slowly on a cold morning with the cables clean, is done \u2014 but confirm with a free conductance load test at any parts store before buying, because slow cranking is a cable or starter symptom as often as it's a battery. Factory-quality batteries last 4\u20136 years in a mild climate and 2\u20133 years in Phoenix heat, which kills them faster than cold ever does.\n\nIf the crank was slow before and is still slow, the battery was never the problem. Ten minutes with a multimeter isolates it, and this is the single most useful electrical test on an old Jeep.\n\n1. **Positive side.** Meter on DC volts, red probe on the battery positive post (the lead post, not the clamp), black probe on the starter's main terminal. Have a helper crank the engine (pull the fuel pump relay or the ASD relay so it doesn't start). More than **0.5 V** during cranking means resistance somewhere between \u2014 a corroded clamp, a bad crimp, or a cable that's green inside.\n2. **Negative side.** Red probe on the engine block (a clean bolt head), black probe on the battery negative post. Crank. More than **0.3 V** means the ground cable or the block ground is the problem. The TJ's negative cable bolts to the block near the starter and corrodes there; the small body ground strap from the block to the firewall goes bad too and shows up as dim lights and a gauge cluster that wanders.\n3. **Across each clamp**, post to clamp, while cranking. Anything above **0.1 V** is a dirty or loose clamp.\n\nEvery reading has to be taken while the circuit is carrying cranking current \u2014 a static resistance check on a corroded cable reads fine and tells you nothing. Replace whatever cable fails, with 2 or 4 AWG copper (not copper-clad aluminum) and crimped, heat-shrunk lugs. Doing all three main cables at once \u2014 alternator-to-positive, positive-to-starter, negative-to-block plus a new block-to-body strap \u2014 is the \"big three\" and costs $40\u2013$80 in cable and lugs.\n\n- **Reversed polarity for even a second** blows the PCM's protection diode at best and the PCM at worst. Look at the post markings twice before the second clamp goes on.\n- **After the disconnect**, the idle hunts and the shifts feel off for 10\u201320 miles while the PCM relearns. Radio presets and the clock are gone unless you used a memory saver. That's all normal.\n- **Winch leads** go straight to the battery posts per every winch manufacturer's instructions \u2014 no fuse in the main leads, because a fuse that can carry a stall load isn't protecting anything. A marine-rated master disconnect on the positive winch lead is the sane addition, and the [winch guide](/db/tj/tj-winch/) covers routing. Any other accessory gets its own fuse within 7 in of the battery.\n- **Dual batteries on a TJ** are possible (Genesis Offroad and a few fabricators make a second tray that mounts on the passenger side, $500\u2013$1,000 with the isolator) but the engine bay is tight and the alternator is small. For overnight fridge use, a single dual-purpose AGM plus a 20 A DC-DC charger to a small lithium house battery in the cargo area is the modern version and lighter.\n- **A battery that keeps dying with the Jeep parked** and tests fine on a charger is a parasitic draw, not a battery. Normal draw on a TJ with the doors closed and the modules asleep is under 50 mA; anything over 100 mA gets tracked down by pulling fuses one at a time with an ammeter in series at the negative cable. An [ignition switch](/db/tj/tj-ignition-switch/) that's worn is one of the usual suspects.\n- **AGM charging.** AGMs want a charger with an AGM mode and are damaged by an old-style trickle charger left on for weeks. The factory alternator charges them fine.\n\n| Battery | Type | CCA (approx.) | Price |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ACDelco Gold 34 / Duralast Gold 34 / Interstate MTP-34 | Flooded starting | 700\u2013800 | $130\u2013$180 |\n| DieHard Platinum AGM 34 / Duracell Ultra Platinum AGM 34 | AGM starting | 750\u2013800 | $200\u2013$260 |\n| Optima RedTop 34 (8002-002) | AGM starting | 800 | $250\u2013$300 |\n| Optima YellowTop D34 (8040-218) | AGM dual-purpose | 750 | $280\u2013$330 |\n| Odyssey Extreme 34-PC1500T | AGM dual-purpose | 850 | $330\u2013$390 |\n| Northstar / X2Power Group 34 | AGM dual-purpose | 880\u2013950 | $350\u2013$420 |\n\nThe chain stores (AutoZone, O'Reilly, Advance, Batteries Plus) stock the flooded and house-brand AGM options same-day and take the core; Optima is on the shelf most places; Odyssey and Northstar usually ship. Warranty is the tiebreaker within a tier: 3-year free replacement is common on the AGMs and worth more than 50 extra CCA. Whatever you buy, write the install date on the case with a paint marker \u2014 it settles the \"how old is it\" question the next time it cranks slow.","steps":["**Put on safety glasses**, take off rings and metal watch bands, and note your radio presets (or plug a 9 V memory saver into the OBD port \u2014 it keeps the presets, and on 03\u201306 the PCM's idle trim).","**Disconnect the negative terminal first** (10 mm or 13 mm nut depending on year and whether the clamps have been replaced). Tuck the cable where it can't swing back onto the post.","**Disconnect the positive terminal**, then flip its red boot back over the cable end so nothing can bridge it to ground while you work.","**Remove the hold-down.** 97\u201304: two nuts on the J-hook studs (\u00bd in hex, 5/16-18 thread), lift the top bar off, fish the J-hooks out of the tray slots. 05\u201306: one 13 mm bolt on the wedge block at the front of the tray, pull the block.","**Lift the battery straight up and out.** Flooded Group 34s run 38\u201345 lb; the Odyssey is 50 lb. Keep it level \u2014 a flooded battery tipped past 45\u00b0 weeps acid from the vent.","**Inspect the tray and the fender under it.** White or green crust is acid corrosion; rinse with a baking-soda-and-water paste until it stops fizzing, then water, then dry. A tray that's rusted through or soft at the front drain corner gets replaced ($40\u2013$80) \u2014 a battery sitting on a rotten tray drops into the fender on the first hard hit.","**Clean both cable clamps** with a terminal brush until the inside faces are bright metal. Follow each cable a few inches back from the clamp: green corrosion creeping under the insulation means the cable is corroding internally and needs replacing, not cleaning.","**Set the new battery in the tray** with the posts oriented the way the old one was. Check that both cables reach their posts without being pulled taut; Group 34 posts are all in the same place, but a reversed-terminal \"34R\" will leave the cables short. Buy the plain 34.","**Reinstall the hold-down.** Snug, not crushing \u2014 the J-hook nuts get tightened until the top bar can't be shifted by hand, the wedge bolt until the block bites. A loose battery on a trail rig cracks its own case against the tray in a season.","**Connect positive first, then negative.** Tighten the clamps until they can't be rotated on the post; the lead clamps crack if you crank on them. Spray both terminals with protector or fit the felt anti-corrosion washers.","**Start the engine and measure charging voltage at the posts.** 13.8\u201314.6 V at idle with the lights and blower off is correct; below 13.5 V, read the [alternator guide](/db/tj/tj-alternator-replacement/) before assuming the new battery is bad."]},{"id":"tj-ignition-switch","title":"Ignition Switch Replacement \u2014 A Common TJ Fault","vehicle":"tj","category":"electrical","subcategory":"ignition","type":"repair","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":150},"tools_required":["trim panel tool","torx bits","multimeter","steering-column key"],"skills_required":["12V wiring","patience with trim"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["electrical","ignition","common-problem","no-start"],"parts":[{"name":"Ignition switch (electrical portion)","vendor":"Mopar/Standard","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Ignition lock cylinder (if worn)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":50}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ factory specs","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-factory-specifications.71/"},{"title":"TJ electrical reference \u2014 My Jeep TJ","url":"https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/"}],"alternatives":["tj-obd2-diagnostics","tj-vacuum-leak-diagnosis","tj-battery-upgrade"],"summary":"A TJ that cranks intermittently, dies at random, or won't power up when you turn the key often has a failing ignition switch, not a deeper problem. It is one of the platform's known faults, and the electrical switch behind the lock cylinder is a cheap, hour-long fix \u2014 far cheaper than the parts cannon people aim at a no-start. Diagnose it with a multimeter first, because two other TJ faults mimic it closely.","description":"The TJ ignition assembly has two parts: the mechanical lock cylinder (the key tumbler) and the electrical switch behind it that actually sends power to the run and start circuits. The electrical switch wears and develops intermittent contact, which shows up as a Jeep that sometimes won't crank, cuts out over bumps, or loses accessories momentarily. Heavy key rings speed the wear \u2014 the weight hangs on the cylinder and rocks the switch actuator every time the Jeep hits a bump.\n\nA worn ignition switch, a failing crankshaft position sensor, and a dirty neutral safety switch all produce a TJ that will not start, and owners routinely replace the wrong one. The tell is what else dies. When the ignition switch drops out, everything on the run circuit goes with it \u2014 the gauge cluster, the check-engine light, the radio, the blower \u2014 so a stall from the switch looks like the key was turned off, and the dash goes dark. A crank sensor stall on the 4.0L (a classic hot-stall, typically after 20 minutes of driving) leaves the dash lit while the engine quits, and it throws a P0320 or P1391 code the [OBD-II guide](/db/tj/tj-obd2-diagnostics/) shows you how to read. A no-crank with a fully lit dash and no click from the starter relay on an automatic points at the park/neutral position switch on the transmission \u2014 shifting to neutral and trying again is the field test \u2014 or the clutch pedal position switch on a manual. Only the ignition switch produces the \"everything went dead, then came back when I wiggled the key\" pattern.\n\nConfirm it's the switch, not the battery or grounds. Start at the battery: 12.4 volts or better at rest, clean, tight terminals, and a solid ground strap from the battery to the block and from the block to the body. The TJ's ground straps corrode at the ring terminals and cause enough intermittent nonsense on their own that the [battery guide](/db/tj/tj-battery-upgrade/) is worth a read before you touch the column. Then drop the lower column shroud (below), find the ignition switch connector, and back-probe its run and start outputs with a multimeter, referencing the wiring diagram for your year because the wire colors changed across the 1997\u20132006 run. With the key in RUN you should see battery voltage on the run output that stays rock steady while you wiggle the key and tap the switch body; in START you should see the same on the start output while cranking. Voltage that flickers, drops below 10 volts under load, or comes and goes with key pressure is the switch. While the connector is exposed, look at the terminals themselves: a browned or melted pin, most often on the accessory or run feed that carries the blower and headlight load, means the connector is failing along with the switch, and a replacement pigtail ($20\u201330) goes in at the same time.\n\nDisconnect the negative battery cable and wait two minutes before touching the column \u2014 the TJ has a driver airbag and its clockspring lives at the top of the column. You will not remove the clockspring for this job, but you do not want the system live while you work around it. Remove the tilt lever if equipped (it unscrews counterclockwise), then the three Torx screws (T-20/T-25) holding the lower column shroud and pull the shroud halves apart. The electrical ignition switch sits on the lower left of the column housing, retained by two screws; before you loosen them, turn the key to RUN so the actuator pin on the switch is aligned with the lock cylinder's driver. Unplug the connector, remove the screws, and pull the switch off the housing. Fit the new switch with its slide in the RUN position to match, seat it on the actuator, and run the screws in snug \u2014 they are small and strip if reefed on. Plug in, reconnect the battery, and verify all four positions work before the shrouds go back: ACC powers the radio, RUN lights the dash, START cranks, and OFF kills everything.\n\nIf the key feels sloppy, sticks, or the tumbler is worn enough that the key comes out in RUN, replace the lock cylinder at the same time. Insert the key, turn to RUN, push a small punch through the release hole in the housing to depress the retaining pin, and pull the cylinder straight out; the new one slides in with its key turned to RUN until the pin clicks. Know before you order that a new lock cylinder ships with its own key, which will not match the doors or tailgate \u2014 a locksmith can rekey the new cylinder to your existing key for $40\u201380, or you carry two keys. Mopar cylinders are $50\u201390; the aftermarket versions from Standard or Dorman are $30\u201350 and fine.\n\nThe electrical switch itself is $25\u201345 from Standard Motor Products or Dorman and $70\u2013110 for the Mopar unit; the Mopar part carried number 4793576AB through much of the TJ run, but verify against your VIN, because the column hardware changed in the 2003 refresh and again with the 2005\u20132006 models. Expect 1\u20132 hours the first time, most of it spent on the shroud screws and the diagnostic. A repair shop bills $150\u2013250 for the same job. If the diagnostic instead points at the crank sensor, that is a $40\u201380 part on the bellhousing and a separate afternoon; a park/neutral switch on the 32RH or 42RLE automatic is $40\u201370 and needs a drain pan.\n\nBecause the symptom is intermittent, owners chase fuel pumps, crank sensors, and relays first. Test the ignition switch early \u2014 it's a known TJ weak point, the part is inexpensive, and catching it saves a long, expensive diagnostic detour. A quick tell in the field: if cycling the key a few times or jiggling it brings the dead Jeep back to life, suspect the switch first \u2014 a fuel pump or crank sensor does not care how you hold the key, but a worn ignition switch does. And once it is fixed, take the twelve keys and the bottle opener off the ring; the new switch will last a lot longer without them swinging from it."},{"id":"tj-oil-change","title":"TJ 4.0L Oil Change \u2014 Specs, Interval, and What to Use","vehicle":"tj","category":"engine","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":55},"tools_required":["14mm box wrench or socket","oil filter wrench (optional)","drain pan","funnel","rags","torque wrench (optional but recommended)"],"skills_required":["basic maintenance"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["engine","oil","maintenance","4.0L"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM oil filter","part_number":"04884899AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Wix 51515 oil filter","part_number":"51515","vendor":"Wix","price_estimate_usd":7},{"name":"Mobil 1 5W-30 full synthetic (5 qt)","vendor":"Mobil 1","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Valvoline MaxLife 10W-30 high-mileage (5 qt)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":24}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ 4.0L Engine Oil Specification \u2014 Chrysler Service Manual","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/oil-specs-for-4-0.html"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler TJ Oil Drain Plug Torque","url":"https://www.fcpeuro.com/torque-specs/jeep-wrangler-oil-drain-plug"}],"alternatives":["tj-coolant-flush","tj-air-filter-replacement","tj-transmission-fluid-change"],"summary":"[The TJ 4.0L takes 6 quarts of 10W-30](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/oil-specs-for-4-0.html) (factory spec) or 5W-30 for colder climates. Change it every 3,000\u20135,000 miles with conventional oil or every 5,000\u20137,500 miles with full synthetic.","description":"The AMC-derived 4.0L inline-six has been running since 1987 and it rewards consistent oil changes. The factory spec is 10W-30 conventional, but Jeep engineers used 10W-30 when most of their production went south of the Mason-Dixon line. For cold weather or extended intervals, 5W-30 full synthetic is a legitimate upgrade \u2014 it flows faster on cold starts, which is where most wear happens.\n\nCapacity is 6 quarts with a filter change. The drain plug is on the passenger side of the oil pan, recessed toward the rear. The filter is on the driver's side of the block near the front \u2014 straightforward to reach. A filter wrench makes the initial break loose more manageable but hand-tight is the correct install torque on most spin-on filters.\n\nHigh-mileage oil (100,000+ miles) makes sense if the engine is running dry between changes or if you're seeing minor seepage at the valve cover. The seal conditioners in high-mileage blends reduce seepage noticeably. Don't switch to high-mileage expecting it to fix a real leak \u2014 it won't.\n\n- [25 ft-lb on the drain plug](https://www.fcpeuro.com/torque-specs/jeep-wrangler-oil-drain-plug) is firm but not aggressive. The TJ oil pan threads strip if you gorilla it. If you feel it getting tight faster than expected, stop and check alignment.\n- A dry filter gasket can cause a slow oil leak that doesn't show up until the first heat cycle. Always oil the gasket.\n- Brown sludge on the dipstick or inside the fill cap means the oil is emulsified \u2014 usually a head gasket leak introducing coolant, or oil going too long between changes. Address the source before changing the oil.\n- New filter spin-on gaskets should seat against clean metal. Old gasket residue left on the block causes a double-gasket situation and guaranteed leak.\n\n- Wix 51515 filter: $7\u2013$9 at NAPA or Amazon\n- Conventional 10W-30 (6 quarts): $18\u2013$24\n- Full synthetic 5W-30 (6 quarts): $30\u2013$40\n- Drain plug (replace if threads look rough): $5\u2013$8 at any parts store","steps":["Warm the engine for 3\u20135 minutes, then shut off. Warm oil drains faster and carries more suspended contaminants.","Set the Jeep on level ground. Raise and support on jack stands if needed to access the drain plug.","Place drain pan under the passenger-side drain plug.","Remove the drain plug (14mm). Let fully drain \u2014 3\u20135 minutes.","While draining: locate the oil filter on the driver's side of the block. Position drain pan under it.","Remove the oil filter with a wrench or by hand. Some oil will spill \u2014 the pan should catch it.","Wipe the drain plug threads and inspect. The magnetic drain plug (if equipped) will have gray metallic paste \u2014 that's normal wear debris. Wipe clean.","Reinstall drain plug and torque to 25 ft-lb. Don't over-torque \u2014 that's a frequent cause of stripped oil pans on TJs.","Lightly coat the new filter's gasket with fresh oil. Thread on hand-tight, then 3/4 turn by hand. No wrench at install.","Add 6 quarts of oil through the valve cover fill cap. Check dipstick after adding 5.5 quarts \u2014 different filter brands hold slightly different amounts.","Start engine, let run 30\u201360 seconds. Check for leaks at the drain plug and filter.","Shut off, wait 2\u20133 minutes, check oil level on dipstick. Correct to full.","Reset oil change interval if using an oil life monitor."]},{"id":"tj-spark-plugs","title":"TJ 4.0L Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 Interval, Gap, and Which Plugs to Buy","vehicle":"tj","category":"engine","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":55},"tools_required":["5/8\" spark plug socket (with rubber insert)","3/8\" ratchet","6\" and 3\" extensions","torque wrench","plug gap tool","anti-seize compound"],"skills_required":["basic engine work"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["engine","ignition","tune-up","maintenance","fuel-economy"],"parts":[{"name":"NGK V-Power copper plugs \u2014 TJ 4.0 (set of 6)","part_number":"ZFR6F-11","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Champion Double Platinum plugs (extended interval)","part_number":"7436","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Bosch Platinum+4 plugs (4.0L spec)","vendor":"Bosch","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Denso Iridium plugs \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":48}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep TJ 4.0L tune-up specs","url":"https://www.jeepspecs.com/tj-wrangler-specs/"},{"title":"NGK spark plug gap guide","url":"https://www.ngksparkplugs.com/en/support/spark-plugs/gap"}],"alternatives":["tj-oil-change","tj-battery-upgrade"],"summary":"The 4.0L likes copper plugs changed every 30,000 miles or platinum every 50,000 \u2014 gapped to 0.035\". Most TJs that run rough at idle have fouled or worn plugs as the first thing to check.","description":"The AMC/Chrysler 4.0L inline-six is a robust engine with a straightforward ignition system: six plug wires, six conventional plugs, one distributor. No coil-on-plug complexity. Plug replacement is a genuine 45-minute job even on the first attempt.\n\n[The 4.0L specifies a gap of 0.035 inches](https://www.jeepspecs.com/tj-wrangler-specs/). Stock NGK copper plugs are the benchmark \u2014 they run well, cost under $20 for a set of six, and gapping them fresh out of the box is recommended even if the package says \"pre-gapped.\" Heat cycles cause plugs to drift. The Chrysler service interval for copper plugs is 30,000 miles; platinum or iridium plugs extend that to 50,000 to 60,000 miles. For a daily driver, copper at 30k is a perfectly reasonable maintenance plan. For a rig that gets less use, platinum or iridium makes sense \u2014 less chance of them sitting for years and seizing into the head.\n\nOne consistent TJ problem: the fourth and fifth plug wires deteriorate from heat exposure and crack internally. Rough idle or a misfire code on cylinder 4 or 5 is often the plug wire, not the plug. Replace wires at the same time if they're more than 8 years old.\n\n- 5/8\" spark plug socket with rubber insert (the insert holds the plug for extraction \u2014 a regular socket lets it fall)\n- 3/8\" ratchet with 6\" and 3\" extensions\n- Torque wrench\n- Spark plug gap gauge\n- Anti-seize compound (thin coat on threads \u2014 prevents seizing into the aluminum head)\n- Dielectric grease for the plug boot ends\n\n1. **Work on a cold engine.** Plug threads are much harder to start in aluminum when the head is hot.\n\n2. **Remove plug wires one at a time.** Twist the boot 90 degrees before pulling \u2014 don't yank straight off. Lay the wire aside in order so you don't mix up cylinder positions.\n\n3. **Blow compressed air into the plug well before removal.** Debris in the well goes straight into the cylinder once the plug is out.\n\n4. **Remove plug with socket and ratchet.** If a plug feels tight, add penetrant and wait 15 minutes rather than forcing it. Stripped threads in an aluminum head are an expensive repair.\n\n5. **Check the old plugs.** Tan or gray electrode = healthy. Black and sooty = rich mixture or oil burning. White or blistered = running lean or overheating. Worn electrode (rounded, large gap) = past interval.\n\n6. **Gap new plugs to 0.035\".** Use the gap tool to check \u2014 [adjust by bending the ground electrode (outer tip) only](https://www.ngksparkplugs.com/en/support/spark-plugs/gap). Never adjust the center electrode.\n\n7. **Apply thin coat of anti-seize to plug threads.** Not needed with copper plugs in steel heads, but the 4.0 has an aluminum head \u2014 anti-seize prevents galling.\n\n8. **Thread plugs in by hand first.** Any binding means cross-threaded \u2014 back out and re-start. Torque to 18 ft-lb. No torque wrench? Snug + 1/4 turn after seating on a new gasket.\n\n9. **Apply dielectric grease inside the plug boot.** Reinstall wire. Press until it clicks.\n\n10. **Start engine and verify no misfires.** Check for codes with an OBD-II scanner if any misfires are present.\n\n- Plug 6 (rear-most) is the tightest access point on the 4.0. Use a universal joint on the extension if needed. A wobble extension makes it manageable.\n- If any plug comes out with oil on the threads or the boot end, you likely have a valve cover gasket leak getting into the plug wells. That's a separate repair, but worth knowing.\n- Don't over-torque into the aluminum head. 18 ft-lb is the spec \u2014 plugs feel \"not that tight\" at 18 ft-lb, which is correct.\n\nNGK copper plug set (6): $15\u2013$20. Denso iridium set: $45\u2013$55. Plug wire set if replacing: $30\u2013$50 (NGK or Accel). Full tune-up parts (plugs + wires): $50\u2013$80. One of the better dollar-per-result maintenance jobs on the 4.0."},{"id":"tj-fuel-filter-replacement","title":"TJ Fuel Filter Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"fuel","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":35},"tools_required":["fuel line disconnect tool set (spring-lock)","flathead screwdriver","drain pan or rags","safety glasses","gloves"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","comfort working with fuel lines"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["fuel","maintenance","filter"],"parts":[{"name":"Wix 33481 fuel filter \u2014 TJ 4.0L","part_number":"33481","vendor":"Wix","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Purolator F54631 fuel filter","part_number":"F54631","vendor":"Purolator","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Motorcraft FG891 fuel filter (Ford equivalent, same Bosch filter)","part_number":"FG891","vendor":"Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":15}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Fuel Filter Location and Replacement \u2014 WranglerTJForum","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/fuel-filter-location-replacement.html"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler Fuel Filter Replacement Guide \u2014 RockAuto Tech","url":"https://www.rockauto.com/en/catalog/jeep,1997,wrangler,4.0l+l6,1228898,fuel+&+air,fuel+filter"}],"alternatives":["tj-fuel-pump-replacement","tj-engine-oil-change","tj-air-filter-replacement"],"summary":"Replace the TJ's inline fuel filter every 30,000 miles, or every 20,000 if you run dusty trails or fill up from questionable stations. The job takes 30 to 90 minutes and costs $15 to $35 for the filter plus a one-time $8 to $12 for the right disconnect tool. The only part that deserves real care is relieving the fuel pressure before you pull any line.","description":"On a 1997 to 2006 Wrangler with the 4.0L or 2.5L, the filter is an inline unit on the driver's side frame rail, roughly between the middle and rear of the vehicle, secured by a clamp or bracket. Some model years and fuel-system layouts differ, so look under the Jeep and trace the fuel line from the tank forward before you buy parts; if you cannot find an external filter, consult the service manual to confirm whether your year uses an integrated filter or screen in the tank module. A clogged filter shows up as hesitation under load, stumbling when the tank is low or the engine is hot, hard starting, and sometimes a lean code such as P0171 with no vacuum leak to explain it. A failing fuel pump can produce the same symptoms, so the symptoms alone do not confirm the filter; if a new filter does not change anything, read [TJ vacuum leak diagnosis](/db/tj/tj-vacuum-leak-diagnosis/) and measure fuel pressure before replacing the pump.\n\n**Safety first.** Gasoline vapor ignites from sparks and hot surfaces. Work outdoors or with the garage door open, with the engine cold and the battery's negative terminal disconnected if you will be working near wiring. Wear safety glasses and nitrile gloves, keep a Class B fire extinguisher within reach, and do not smoke or run a work light with an exposed bulb. Plan to catch about a cup of fuel.\n\n**Parts and tools.** The listing above names the Wix 33481, Purolator F54631, and Motorcraft FG891 as candidates. Verify fitment with the vendor's lookup for your exact year and engine; part numbers change between production runs, and a wrong filter has the wrong fittings. The TJ uses spring-lock couplings for some connections and quick-connect clips for others, depending on the fitting. Buy the disconnect tool set that matches the size at your filter; $8 to $12 at any parts store. A clean drain pan, shop rags, and a 10mm or 7/16 inch wrench for the clamp bolt complete the list.\n\n**Why prime before starting.** After a fresh filter goes in, the lines downstream are empty. Cycling the key to ON for a couple of seconds, several times, lets the pump refill them so the engine does not crank dry.","steps":["Park on level ground with the engine cold, and chock the wheels. Disconnect the negative battery cable.","Relieve fuel pressure. Locate the fuel pump relay or fuse in the power distribution center under the hood (the legend inside the cover identifies it), pull it, start the engine, and let it run until it stalls, then crank it for a few seconds more. Fuel injection systems hold pressure for hours, and the 4.0L runs about 49 PSI. Some lines will still contain fuel; wrap rags around each fitting before you open it.","Raise the Jeep and support it on jack stands if you need clearance; the filter is reachable from the side on many TJs, so check whether you need to lift at all.","Note the flow direction arrow on the filter body. The new filter must point the same way, toward the engine.","Slide the correct disconnect tool over the line at the fitting, push it in to release the retaining spring, and pull the line off. Do not twist or pry; plastic tabs break. Repeat for the second line.","Loosen the clamp or bracket bolt and slide the old filter out. Fuel will drain; hold the filter level and drain it into the pan.","Compare the new filter with the old for diameter, fitting size, and direction. Remove the end caps from the new filter only when ready to connect it.","Install the new filter with the arrow pointing toward the engine. Tighten the clamp snugly. Over-tightening crushes the filter body.","Push each line onto its fitting until the coupling clicks, then pull firmly on the line to confirm it is locked. A coupling that is not fully seated can separate under pressure.","Reconnect the battery and reinstall the fuel pump relay or fuse. Turn the ignition to ON for 3 seconds, then OFF, and repeat three times to pressurize the system.","Start the engine and inspect both fittings for weeping fuel. Let it idle for 3 to 5 minutes and inspect again; any wetness means shut it down and reseat the line.","Take a test drive and re-inspect afterward. Dispose of old fuel and the filter at a hazardous-waste drop-off or parts-store recycling."],"pros":["A $15 to $35 filter removes rust and dirt before they reach the injectors, which cost far more to replace.","A clean filter often fixes mid-throttle hesitation and hot-start complaints that owners blame on the fuel pump or sensors.","Quick-connect couplings make the job a bench-style task once the right tool is in hand.","Checking the lines and fittings while you are under the Jeep catches cracked plastic and rusted clamps early."],"cons":["The job involves pressurized gasoline and open lines; skipped steps cause fires and fuel in your eyes.","Without the correct disconnect tool, the quick-connect tabs can break, and a damaged fitting means replacing a section of line.","A wrong filter fitment is common if you buy by year alone; verify with the vendor's lookup.","Replacing the filter does not diagnose a weak pump, a vacuum leak, or bad fuel. If the symptom persists, test pressure and look elsewhere.","Old fuel and filters are hazardous waste and must be disposed of properly.","**Costs and sources.** Filter: $10 to $20 at NAPA, O'Reilly, or RockAuto. Disconnect tool set: $8 to $12. A shop typically charges $80 to $150 for the same job. Total DIY cost: $20 to $35 in the first year and about $15 afterward. Combine this service with [TJ oil change](/db/tj/tj-oil-change/) and [TJ spark plug replacement](/db/tj/tj-spark-plugs/) so the ignition and fuel intervals stay aligned."]},{"id":"tj-throttle-body-cleaning","title":"TJ 4.0L Throttle Body Cleaning \u2014 Idle Issues and Carbon Buildup","vehicle":"tj","category":"fuel","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":8,"high":15},"tools_required":["Phillips screwdriver","10mm socket","shop towels or lint-free rags","soft brush (old toothbrush works)"],"skills_required":["basic engine access"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["fuel","throttle-body","idle","tune-up","maintenance","iac"],"parts":[{"name":"CRC Throttle Body and Air Intake Cleaner","vendor":"CRC","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"WD-40 Specialist Contact Cleaner (alternative)","vendor":"WD-40","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Idle Air Control (IAC) motor \u2014 Jeep 4.0L","part_number":"Standard Motor Products AC335","vendor":"Standard Motor Products","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"How to Clean a Throttle Body","url":"https://www.popularmechanics.com/cars/how-to/a26303/how-to-clean-your-throttle-body/"},{"title":"TJ idle surge troubleshooting","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/forum/engine-tech-12/"},{"title":"Jeep 4.0L Idle Air Control valve \u2014 location and testing","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/iac-valve-testing-and-replacement.8821/"}],"alternatives":["tj-fuel-filter-replacement","tj-battery-upgrade","tj-vacuum-leak-diagnosis"],"summary":"Rough cold idle, idle surge when warm, or a stumble off-throttle on a TJ 4.0 that isn't throwing codes \u2014 [clean the throttle body first](https://www.popularmechanics.com/cars/how-to/a26303/how-to-clean-your-throttle-body/). It takes 30 minutes and costs $10, and it fixes the problem more often than not.","description":"The TJ 4.0 draws air through a single-bore throttle body mounted between the air intake tube and the intake manifold. Over years of use, oil vapor from the PCV system coats the bore with a sticky carbon and oil residue. This layer builds up on the throttle plate (the butterfly valve) and the bore walls behind it, progressively reducing the air passage at closed throttle \u2014 which is exactly where idle quality lives.\n\nSymptoms: hunting idle that cycles between 500 and 900 RPM, a stumble when you first crack the throttle from a stop, rough cold start that smooths out after 30 seconds, or the occasional stall when you drop back to idle after highway driving. None of these are serious, and none of them require a new throttle body \u2014 cleaning resolves it in the vast majority of cases.\n\nThe TJ 4.0 throttle body [does have an Idle Air Control (IAC) motor](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/iac-valve-testing-and-replacement.8821/) \u2014 a small stepper-motor valve bolted to the side of the throttle body that bypasses a controlled amount of air around the closed throttle plate to hold idle speed steady as engine load changes (AC on, power steering pump loading up at low speed, and so on). It's a separate, serviceable part from the bore and plate you're cleaning in this guide, and it's worth knowing it's there before you start spraying: cleaner sprayed directly into the IAC's pintle opening can wash lubricant out of the stepper motor and cause a rough or hunting idle that looks exactly like the carbon-buildup problem you were trying to fix. Clean around the IAC's mounting flange with a wipe, not a spray, and keep the nozzle aimed at the throttle bore and plate rather than at the IAC housing itself.\n\nIf cleaning the bore doesn't resolve the idle after a full warmup cycle, the IAC motor itself is the next suspect \u2014 a IAC that's mechanically stuck or has a failed internal stepper won't hold idle regardless of how clean the bore is, and it throws its own set of diagnostic codes rather than showing up as a generic idle complaint. That's a separate ten-minute swap (two screws and an electrical connector on most years) rather than a cleaning job, and it's worth ruling out the cheap fix (cleaning) before spending $40\u2013$50 on a new IAC.\n\n- Throttle body cleaner spray ($8\u2013$10 \u2014 CRC or equivalent)\n- Shop rags or microfiber cloths\n- Soft brush\n- Flathead screwdriver or trim tool to prop the throttle plate open during cleaning\n\n1. **Engine cold or warm is fine for this job \u2014 not hot.** Let it sit 15 minutes after driving.\n\n2. **Locate the throttle body.** It's the oval housing where the large air intake tube meets the intake manifold. Two hose clamps hold the intake tube to it. The IAC motor is the smaller cylindrical unit bolted to the side or bottom of the same housing, with a two- or four-wire electrical connector.\n\n3. **Loosen the intake tube clamp** at the throttle body and pull the tube aside enough to access the bore opening. You don't need to remove the throttle body from the engine.\n\n4. **Prop the throttle plate open.** You can use your finger, a flat-blade screwdriver held at the throttle linkage, or have a helper hold it. The plate is the round disc inside the bore.\n\n5. **Spray the bore walls and plate with throttle body cleaner, keeping the nozzle away from the IAC pintle opening.** Let it soak for 30 seconds. The cleaner dissolves the carbon deposits.\n\n6. **Wipe with a lint-free cloth, scrub stubborn buildup with a soft brush.** Repeat until the bore walls are clean metal and the plate surface is residue-free. Wipe the IAC pintle tip clean with a dry rag rather than spraying it directly.\n\n7. **Wipe dry.** No cleaner residue should remain.\n\n8. **Reconnect the intake tube.** Tighten the clamp.\n\n9. **Start the engine.** Expect rough idle for the first 30\u201360 seconds while the PCM relearns idle position through the IAC. Let it stabilize.\n\n10. **Drive a short cycle.** The idle should settle back to smooth within one warmup cycle.\n\n- Don't use carburetor cleaner on a throttle body with an IAC or oxygen sensor nearby \u2014 the stronger solvent can damage sensor components and IAC internals. Throttle body cleaner is specifically formulated to be safe for these surfaces.\n- If the idle issues persist after cleaning and a full relearn drive cycle, the IAC motor itself, the IAT sensor (located in the intake tube), or a vacuum leak is the next place to look.\n- Codes P0505 or P0506 (IAC circuit codes) point at the IAC motor specifically \u2014 a wiring, connector, or internal stepper-motor fault \u2014 not at bore carbon buildup. Cleaning the throttle body won't clear those codes; if you're seeing them, go straight to testing or replacing the IAC.\n\nCRC throttle body cleaner: $8\u2013$12. Total job cost: under $15 and 30 minutes. Probably the best diagnostic step before spending money on anything fuel or idle related on a high-mileage TJ. If it turns out to be the IAC motor itself, budget $35\u2013$50 for the part and another 15\u201320 minutes of labor \u2014 cheap enough to be worth confirming with a cleaning first rather than guessing and replacing it outright."},{"id":"tj-seat-upgrade","title":"TJ Seat Upgrade \u2014 Options, Fitment, and What's Worth It","vehicle":"tj","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":1100},"tools_required":["13mm socket and ratchet","T47 Torx bit (97-02 rear seat bolts)","T50 Torx bit (some 03-06 hardware)","torque wrench","penetrating oil","a helper for lifting"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["interior","seats","comfort","daily-driver","harness","brackets"],"parts":[{"name":"Rugged Ridge standard replacement front seat \u2014 TJ (per seat)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":170},{"name":"Bestop Trailmax II Sport front seat (per seat)","vendor":"Bestop","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Bestop seat adapter bracket, 97-02 TJ (per seat, required for Trailmax on 97-02)","part_number":"51256-01","vendor":"Bestop","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC performance seat (per seat)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Corbeau Baja RS reclining suspension seat (per seat)","vendor":"Corbeau","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Corbeau TJ seat bracket set \u2014 97-02 or 03-06, per side","vendor":"Corbeau","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"PRP Daily Driver suspension seat (per seat)","vendor":"PRP Seats","price_estimate_usd":420}],"citations":[{"title":"Corbeau \u2014 Jeep Wrangler TJ seat brackets 1997-2002","url":"https://corbeau.com/products/jeep-wrangler-tj-seat-brackets-1997-2002"},{"title":"Corbeau \u2014 Jeep Wrangler TJ seat brackets 2003-2006","url":"https://corbeau.com/jeep-tj-complete-03-06-seat-brackets"},{"title":"Bestop 51256-01 seat adapter bracket for 97-02 TJ (Quadratec)","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/products/bestop-seat-adapter-bracket-97-02-tj-for-use-with-trailmax-seats-51256-01"},{"title":"Removing 97-02 TJ seats \u2014 rear bolt access (WranglerTJForum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/removing-97-02-seats-where-are-the-rear-bolts.20938/"}],"alternatives":["tj-sound-deadening","tj-soft-top-replacement"],"summary":"A seat swap is the highest-return interior change on a TJ that gets driven \u2014 the factory foam is flat by 100,000 miles and the bolsters were never there. Budget $350\u2013$550 a pair for OE-style replacements, $900\u2013$1,100 a pair for suspension seats on proper brackets. The one thing that decides whether the job takes an hour or a weekend: buy the brackets made for your year split (97\u201302 vs. 03\u201306), because the floor mounts changed in 2003.","description":"The TJ seat was designed for a $20,000 truck in 1997 and the foam was never dense. Twenty-plus years on, the cushion has collapsed to the pan, the driver's outboard bolster is crushed from climbing in and out, and on a lifted rig with big tires the lack of lateral support turns every off-camber section into a bracing exercise. Vinyl or fabric that's split is the visible symptom; the flat foam is the one that matters. If the seat is torn but the foam is still supportive, a $60\u2013$120 cover set from Bestop, Rugged Ridge, or Coverking solves the cosmetic problem for a fraction of the cost. If you sink to the pan, replace the seat.\n\nTwo facts about the TJ decide most of the choices below. First, the seats bolt to raised floor risers with four bolts, and the riser pattern is different for 1997\u20132002 and 2003\u20132006 \u2014 nearly every aftermarket seat needs a year-specific bracket or adapter. Second, the front seats fold and slide forward for rear-seat access (the \"tip-and-slide\" lever at the base). Some aftermarket brackets preserve that; fixed racing brackets don't, which matters if anyone ever rides in the back.\n\n**OE-style replacements \u2014 $150\u2013$300 per seat.** Rugged Ridge's standard replacement seat and Bestop's Trailmax II Sport are reclining, stock-shaped seats with new foam and vinyl or fabric. On 03\u201306 the Trailmax bolts to the factory risers directly; on 97\u201302 it needs [Bestop's 51256-01 adapter bracket](https://www.quadratec.com/products/bestop-seat-adapter-bracket-97-02-tj-for-use-with-trailmax-seats-51256-01) ($150 per seat), which is the hidden cost that makes this tier less of a bargain on an early TJ. The Rugged Ridge seat includes its own riser-compatible base for both splits \u2014 check the listing for your year. These keep the factory look, the fold-forward function, and factory-level bolstering, which is to say not much. Right for: a worn daily driver that needs new foam and nothing else.\n\n**Suspension seats with direct-fit brackets \u2014 $350\u2013$550 per seat plus $100\u2013$140 per bracket.** Corbeau's Baja RS and Baja XRS, PRP's Daily Driver and Enduro, and Smittybilt's XRC seat sit here. They recline, they have real bolsters, most have harness pass-throughs, and [Corbeau and PRP both sell TJ-specific bracket sets by year split](https://corbeau.com/products/jeep-wrangler-tj-seat-brackets-1997-2002) that bolt to the factory riser holes and carry a slider. Corbeau's brackets include a mount for the factory seat belt buckle stalk; check that any other brand does too, because the buckle bolts to the seat track on a TJ, not the floor. Right for: anyone who drives the Jeep regularly and wheels it. This is the tier most owners should buy.\n\n**Fixed-back competition seats \u2014 $400\u2013$700 per seat.** Corbeau FX1, PRP Alpha, Mastercraft Baja. Non-reclining, deep bolsters, lightest weight. These assume a 4- or 5-point harness and a harness bar behind the seat (Rock Hard 4x4 and Smittybilt make bolt-in bars for the TJ sport cage, $150\u2013$250). Without a harness, a fixed-back seat is worse than a reclining one: you can't set the rake for your torso and the belt geometry off the factory B-pillar retractor doesn't match the bolsters. Right for: a dedicated trail rig where the occupants are strapped in hard. Wrong for a daily.\n\nOrder seats per side. The driver's track has the slider and the tip-and-slide lever; the passenger side may be fixed or have a shorter slider depending on brand. Most brands sell a \"driver\" and \"passenger\" SKU that are mirror images.\n\n- **Headroom under a soft top or hardtop.** Suspension seats on aftermarket brackets can sit \u00bd\u20132 in higher than factory. Corbeau's TJ brackets are designed to hold roughly stock height; PRP and universal sliders vary. If you're over 6 ft, measure your current seat height at the cushion front and compare against the bracket-plus-seat spec before ordering.\n- **Rear-seat access.** A bracket without a tip-and-slide function turns the back seat into a cargo shelf. If passengers ride back there, buy brackets that list a \"flip-forward\" or \"tumble\" feature.\n- **The seat belt buckle bolt** is a higher-grade shouldered bolt with a specific length. Reuse the factory one or the bracket kit's equivalent; a hardware-store bolt in that hole is not acceptable.\n- **Rust on the risers.** With the seat out, check the floor riser welds and the tub under the seat for rot \u2014 this area collects water under carpet on any TJ that's been driven topless in the rain. Fix it before the new seat hides it; the same inspection logic applies to the floor before [sound deadening](/db/tj/tj-sound-deadening/) goes down.\n- **Heated seat kits** (Rostra, Kiiipro, $60\u2013$120 per seat) go in before the seat cover is closed up. If you want heat, install it during the swap, not after.\n- **Harnesses on a stock sport cage.** A 4-point harness anchored to the floor with the shoulder straps over a bar that isn't a proper harness bar is worse than the 3-point belt in a rollover. Harness bar first, then harness; keep the factory belt as the street belt regardless.\n\n| Option | Per seat | Bracket / adapter | Pair, installed by you |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Seat covers only (foam still good) | $30\u2013$60 | \u2014 | $60\u2013$120 |\n| Rugged Ridge replacement seat | $150\u2013$200 | included | $300\u2013$400 |\n| Bestop Trailmax II Sport | $230\u2013$300 | 03\u201306 none; 97\u201302 $150 | $460\u2013$900 |\n| Smittybilt XRC seat | $230\u2013$290 | $100\u2013$130 | $660\u2013$840 |\n| Corbeau Baja RS + Corbeau TJ brackets | $350\u2013$420 | $120\u2013$140 per side | $940\u2013$1,120 |\n| PRP Daily Driver + PRP TJ brackets | $400\u2013$450 | $120\u2013$150 per side | $1,040\u2013$1,200 |\n| Fixed-back (Corbeau FX1 / PRP Alpha) | $400\u2013$700 | $120\u2013$150 per side + harness bar $150\u2013$250 | $1,200\u2013$1,900 |\n\nQuadratec, Northridge4x4, and ExtremeTerrain stock all of these; Corbeau and PRP direct have the most reliable year-split fitment information and will tell you which bracket goes with which seat before you order. Pick the seat, then the bracket, then confirm both against your year. For a TJ that lives outdoors with the top down, vinyl outlasts fabric; the neoprene and fabric options are more comfortable in Phoenix summer sun for the first three years and worse after that. A [replacement soft top](/db/tj/tj-soft-top-replacement/) that seals is what keeps any of them alive.","steps":["**Slide the seat fully rearward** and remove the two front riser bolts \u2014 13 mm on both year splits. Soak them first; the floor gets wet on a Jeep and these corrode.","**Tip the seat forward** with the lever at the base of the seatback, then push the seat all the way forward on its track to expose the rear riser bolts. On 97\u201302 the rear pair is one 13 mm bolt and one T47 Torx; on 03\u201306 expect 13 mm or T50. A \u00bd in socket fits the 13 mm heads if that's what you have.","**Unbolt the seat belt buckle stalk** from the inboard side of the track if it's staying with the Jeep (it usually is \u2014 the new bracket gets it). Note its orientation; the stalk has a specific angle that puts the buckle where your hand expects it.","**Lift the seat out.** With the track it's 40\u201350 lb and awkward through the door opening. A helper, or tilt the seatback down and rotate it out base-first.","**Assemble the new seat to its bracket on the bench.** Bolt the slider to the bracket per the manufacturer's diagram (Corbeau and PRP both ship a torque sheet), then the seat to the slider. Mount the buckle stalk to the bracket's tab. Verify the slider moves freely before the assembly goes in the Jeep \u2014 a bracket bolted slightly out of square binds the track and you'll never find it once it's installed.","**Set the assembly on the risers** and start all four bolts by hand. Use the bracket kit's hardware if supplied (usually M8 or 5/16 grade-8 with lock washers); otherwise reuse the factory bolts after wire-brushing the threads.","**Torque to 30\u201335 ft-lb.** These are the bolts holding a belted occupant in a rollover \u2014 treat them as safety fasteners, and use blue thread locker if the kit didn't supply lock washers.","**Cycle everything**: full slider travel, recline range, tip-forward function, seat belt latch and release. Confirm the belt retractor pulls the shoulder belt across the bolster without snagging."]},{"id":"tj-sound-deadening","title":"TJ Sound Deadening \u2014 Reducing Road and Trail Noise","vehicle":"tj","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":250},"tools_required":["trim removal tool","roller or credit card (for pressing deadener)","scissors or utility knife","heat gun (optional)","isopropyl alcohol"],"skills_required":["basic trim removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["interior","noise-reduction","NVH","comfort","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"Kilmat 80 mil butyl sound deadener (50 sq ft)","vendor":"Kilmat","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Noico 80 mil sound deadener mat (36 sq ft)","vendor":"Noico","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Dynamat Xtreme door kit","vendor":"Dynamat","price_estimate_usd":100},{"name":"Second Skin Audio Damplifier (36 sq ft)","vendor":"Second Skin Audio","price_estimate_usd":125}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Jeep interior noise reduction guide","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/forum/interior-accessories-59/"},{"title":"Kilmat vs Dynamat vs Noico \u2014 Sound Deadener Comparison","url":"https://www.crutchfield.com/S-abcdefgh/learn/car-audio-noise-reduction.html"}],"alternatives":["tj-seat-upgrade","tj-tire-fitment-35s"],"summary":"Sound deadening a TJ reduces drone and panel resonance, but it will not make the Jeep quiet. Expect a modest, noticeable drop in floor and firewall boom on the highway for $60 to $200 in material and a full weekend of work. If your Jeep has a soft top or loud off-road tires, those are bigger noise sources than the sheet metal and deadener will not hide them.","description":"The TJ's interior noise comes from three sources: tire and road noise coming through the floor and wheel wells, engine and exhaust noise through the firewall and floor, and wind noise around soft-top seams and door openings. Butyl damping mat addresses only the first two, and only the part that is caused by metal panels ringing. The sheet metal is thin and flat, so it resonates; adding mass and a constrained layer lowers the ringing. To block airborne noise (rather than damp vibration) you need a decoupling foam layer and a heavy barrier on top, which adds cost and weight.\n\nAn honest priority list for a TJ owner who wants a quieter ride:\n\n1. **Hardtop or a quality soft top.** If you drive a soft top at 65 mph, it is the loudest thing in the cabin. A hardtop typically makes a bigger difference than any amount of floor mat, and it also matters more than the next items.\n2. **Tire choice.** An aggressive mud-terrain tire roars on pavement. An all-terrain with a quieter tread pattern reduces road noise more than any deadener will. See [TJ tire fitment for 35s](/db/tj/tj-tire-fitment-35s/) for the tradeoffs of tire size.\n3. **Firewall and floor damping.** This is the work in this guide.\n4. **Exhaust.** A louder aftermarket exhaust adds drone that no mat cures; consider the exhaust before the mat.\n\n- Butyl damping mat, 80 mil (2 mm), sold under brands such as Kilmat, Noico, Dynamat, and Second Skin Audio. [Quality and thickness are comparable between the better budget brands](https://www.crutchfield.com/S-abcdefgh/learn/car-audio-noise-reduction.html); compare real thickness and the adhesive's temperature rating on the packaging. Plan on 36 to 50 square feet for floors and firewall, but measure your Jeep with a tape before ordering.\n- Optionally, closed-cell foam (1/8 to 1/4 inch) as a decoupling layer, and mass-loaded vinyl if you want to block airborne noise.\n- Isopropyl alcohol and clean rags\n- A roller for pressing the mat (a hard rubber roller is better than a card)\n- Trim tools, a socket set, a razor knife, and scissors\n- A hair dryer or low-temperature heat gun for working the mat around contours\n- Gloves, because the cut edges of the foil facing are sharp\n\n1. **Prepare the Jeep.** Disconnect the battery before you remove seats, before you unplug any seat wiring or work near the airbag circuits. Note that the TJ does not have a full carpet set as standard in all trims, so some of this work involves rubber mats and exposed floor pan.\n\n2. **Remove seats, center console if present, and the carpet or mats.** The front seats bolt to the floor with four fasteners each. The rear seat in a TJ tub folds forward and its mounting hardware also comes out. Bag and label the bolts.\n\n3. **Inspect the floor for rust and moisture before covering anything.** Press a screwdriver tip firmly into suspect spots near the seat mounts, along the seams, and at the body mounts. Repair any rust before sealing the metal under butyl. Covering moisture accelerates rust. See also [TJ seat upgrade](/db/tj/tj-seat-upgrade/) if you are replacing seats and will have the interior open anyway.\n\n4. **Clean the metal.** Wipe with isopropyl alcohol and let it dry. Dust, oil, and silicone prevent adhesion.\n\n5. **Cut the mat into pieces that suit the panels.** Complete coverage is not required; patches that cover 50 to 70 percent of a flat panel provide most of the damping effect. Use the knuckle test: tap the metal, listen for a ring, and place the mat where it rings.\n\n6. **Apply to the floor, firewall, and tunnel.** Peel the backing back gradually, press from the center outward, and roll firmly so there are no air pockets. Warm the mat gently over contours. Keep the heat gun moving and away from wiring, fuel lines, and the fuel tank area at the rear; a hair dryer is safer than a high-temperature gun.\n\n7. **Stay out of hot zones and drains.** Do not cover floor drain plugs, and keep mat away from exhaust heat shields and areas that become hot, since butyl mat has a temperature limit printed on the product. The transmission tunnel can run hot near the exhaust, so check the product rating.\n\n8. **Optional foam layer.** Cover the damped panels with closed-cell foam to decouple the carpet from the metal. Use the same cleaning and adhesive approach.\n\n9. **Reinstall in reverse.** Reinstall the seats and tighten the seat bolts to the service manual specification. Reconnect the battery and confirm the airbag warning light behaves normally.\n\n10. **Test drive on pavement and a rough road,** at highway speed with the top you normally run. Compare with a recording on your phone if you want an objective check.\n\n- **Rust under the mat.** The TJ's floor and the area around the body mounts are known rust spots. Check them before covering and again a year later at the edges.\n- **Water.** The tub drains must stay open. Do not block the drain plugs, and do not seal seams that water uses to leave.\n- **Weight.** Full coverage with 80 mil mat adds about 15 to 30 pounds for a Jeep this size. That is minor but worth knowing if you are weight-conscious.\n- **Smell and heat.** Butyl can give off odor in a hot Jeep. Ventilate during installation and avoid covering parts of the floor near the exhaust.\n- **Wiring.** The TJ harness runs near the seats and under the dash. Keep mat clear of connectors and avoid cutting into harness clips.\n\n**Costs and sources.** Budget 80 mil butyl (Kilmat or Noico, 36 to 50 square feet): $60 to $90. Premium brands (Dynamat, Second Skin Audio) cost roughly twice as much for similar coverage. Foam and mass-loaded vinyl add $60 to $200. Roller and trim tools: $15 to $30. Total for a floor-and-firewall damping job is typically $80 to $150 for materials and a full day of work. For an add-on that makes a longer drive more comfortable, pair this with [TJ seat upgrade](/db/tj/tj-seat-upgrade/)."},{"id":"tj-serpentine-belt","title":"Serpentine Belt Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":60},"tools_required":["1/2 breaker bar or serpentine belt tool","15mm socket"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["belt","accessory-drive","4.0l","2.5l"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Serpentine Belt 4.0L w/ A/C","part_number":"5281668AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Gates K060910 Micro-V Belt (97-02 4.0L)","vendor":"Gates/RockAuto","price_estimate_usd":24},{"name":"Goodyear/Continental serpentine belt equivalent","vendor":"AutoZone","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar Serpentine Belt 5281668AA listing","url":"https://www.justforjeeps.com/sebefor4tjwj.html"},{"title":"Wrangler TJ Forum \u2014 belt part number discussion","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/serpentine-belt-part-number.2613/"}],"alternatives":["tj-oil-change-tj","tj-spark-plugs-tj"],"summary":"If your TJ's serpentine belt is older than five years, or shows cracks across the ribs when you flex it backward, replace it before it strands you. The whole job is under an hour with one breaker bar. On the 4.0L the same belt drives the water pump, alternator, and power steering, so a failure is an overheat, a dead charging system, and heavy steering all at once.","description":"The TJ uses a single six-rib serpentine belt around the crank, water pump, alternator, power-steering pump, A/C compressor if equipped, and a spring-loaded automatic tensioner. Belts fail on age more than mileage \u2014 the rubber hardens and the ribs crack whether the truck is driven or parked, so a garage-kept TJ with 30,000 miles and a ten-year-old belt is as likely to throw one as a daily driver at 120,000.\n\nFlex a section of belt backward so the ribs open up. Fine cracks running across a rib are normal wear; cracks deep enough to see the fabric, cracks in more than a few spots per inch, missing rib chunks, or a glazed shiny underside all mean replace. Fraying at the belt edges points at a misaligned pulley rather than a worn belt, and swapping the belt without finding the misalignment gets you a second shredded belt.\n\nNoise is the other tell. A squeal on cold start that fades as the engine warms is usually a glazed belt or a weak tensioner. A steady chirp at idle that changes with accessory load is more often a pulley bearing. A rhythmic slapping sound is a belt with a chunk missing.\n\nLength depends on year, engine, and whether the truck has air conditioning, and buying by \"1999 Wrangler\" alone is how people end up back at the counter.\n\nFor the 4.0L with A/C, [the Mopar belt is 5281668AA](https://www.justforjeeps.com/sebefor4tjwj.html) and [the common Gates equivalent is K060910](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/serpentine-belt-part-number.2613/) \u2014 six ribs, roughly 91 inches effective length. For a 4.0L without A/C, the belt is materially shorter; the usual Gates number is in the K060749 range at about 75 inches. The 2.5L and later 2.4L four-cylinder use different lengths again. Confirm by VIN at the parts counter or at the Gates catalog, or measure the old belt around its ribbed face with a length of string before you order. If the truck has been modified \u2014 an A/C delete, a different alternator bracket, a high-output alternator with a different pulley diameter \u2014 the catalog number is a starting point, not an answer, and measuring is the only way to be sure.\n\nThe automatic tensioner is a spring-loaded arm with a smooth idler pulley, on the front of the engine. It is the part most likely to be the actual problem when a fresh belt still squeals.\n\nWith the belt off, swing the tensioner arm through its full travel by hand. It should move smoothly with steady spring resistance and return firmly with no grinding, no free play at the pivot, and no clunk. Spin the tensioner pulley: any roughness, side-to-side wobble, or noise is a dead bearing. Do the same for every other pulley \u2014 alternator, water pump, power-steering pump, A/C compressor, and any idler. A rough water-pump bearing is your cue to do the [coolant system work](/db/tj/tj-coolant-flush/) now rather than twice.\n\nReplacement tensioner assemblies for the 4.0L run roughly $45\u201390 depending on brand; verify the number by VIN at the counter since bracket styles changed across the TJ run. Fitting a new belt to a tired tensioner is a repeat job within months, and a tensioner that is losing spring tension is also what lets the belt slip enough to cook a water pump.\n\nBelt: $24\u201340 (Gates K060910 around $24, Mopar 5281668AA around $38). Tensioner assembly if needed: $45\u201390. Idler pulley: $20\u201335. A shop charges roughly $120\u2013180 for the belt alone, which is most of the reason to do it yourself.\n\nCarry the old belt as a trail spare once it is off, provided it is not cracked through \u2014 a used belt in the back is what gets you off a forest road. Pair this with the rest of the under-hood maintenance set: the [alternator](/db/tj/tj-alternator-replacement/) and [radiator](/db/tj/tj-radiator-replacement/) guides cover the two accessories most likely to be the reason you are looking at the belt in the first place.","steps":["Park the TJ, key off, hood up, engine cool. Cold matters \u2014 the belt path runs next to the exhaust manifold.","Photograph the existing belt routing from two angles, or note the diagram on the radiator support.","Position a 1/2-inch breaker bar or a dedicated serpentine tool on the tensioner pulley center bolt.","Rotate the tensioner counterclockwise to relieve tension and hold it there.","Slide the belt off the alternator pulley first, then release the tensioner slowly and remove the belt.","Spin each pulley by hand and swing the tensioner through its travel \u2014 alternator, idler, tensioner, water pump, power steering, A/C.","Compare the new belt to the old one on the bench for length and rib count.","Route the new belt per your photo, seating it on every pulley except the alternator.","Pull the tensioner counterclockwise again and slip the belt onto the alternator pulley.","Release the tensioner slowly against the new belt and confirm every rib is seated in every groove \u2014 a belt riding one rib off will destroy itself in minutes.","Start the engine and watch the belt track for 30 seconds. No squeal, no walking side to side on the pulleys, tensioner arm steady rather than oscillating."],"pros":["The cheapest preventive job in the engine bay","30 to 60 minutes start to finish","No special tools beyond a breaker bar","Forces an inspection of every accessory pulley bearing while access is open"],"cons":["Wrong belt length is a real risk \u2014 confirm by VIN or measure the old one","A never-serviced tensioner can be stubborn to break loose","A mis-routed or half-seated belt shreds on startup","A failing tensioner or pulley bearing will ruin the new belt if you do not check"]},{"id":"tj-brake-fluid-tj","title":"Brake Fluid Flush","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":80},"tools_required":["10mm box wrench","clear vinyl tubing (1/4\")","turkey baster","catch bottle","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","bleed sequence"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["brakes","preventive","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline DOT 3/4 Brake Fluid (32oz)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Mopar DOT 3 Brake Fluid (12oz)","part_number":"4318080AD","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":7},{"name":"ATE Type 200 (amber, German spec) \u2014 for DOT 4 upgrade","vendor":"ATE","price_estimate_usd":18}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Forum \u2014 Brake Fluid Capacity Discussion","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/brake-fluid.6954/"},{"title":"TJ Brake Fluid Change Procedure","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/brake-fluid-change.21283/"}],"alternatives":["tj-oil-change-tj","tj-spark-plugs-tj"],"summary":"Flush the TJ's brake fluid every two years with fresh DOT 3 (the factory spec) or DOT 4 from a sealed bottle. Budget about 90 minutes, a quart or two of fluid, and either a helper or a one-person bleeder. The one rule that protects the whole job: [never let the master cylinder run dry](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/brake-fluid-change.21283/).","description":"Brake fluid is hygroscopic, which means it pulls moisture out of the air through the reservoir vent and through the rubber hoses themselves. Fresh DOT 3 must boil no lower than 401 F dry and 284 F once it has absorbed about 3.7% water; DOT 4 must hold 446 F dry and 311 F wet. Those are federal minimums, and real fluid usually beats them, but the gap between dry and wet is the point: a TJ that has not had a flush in four years can have fluid that boils on a long mountain descent, and boiling fluid turns the pedal soft because vapor compresses and liquid does not. Moisture also corrodes caliper pistons, wheel cylinders, and the ABS valve block from the inside, which is why a flush is cheaper than the repairs it prevents.\n\n**Which fluid to buy.** Mopar specifies DOT 3 for the TJ. DOT 4 is glycol-based like DOT 3, mixes with it, and carries a higher boiling point, so it is a reasonable upgrade for rigs that tow, carry weight, or drive long grades. DOT 5.1 is also glycol-based and compatible but costs more and gains little on a TJ. DOT 5 is silicone fluid and must never be mixed into a glycol system. Buy sealed bottles and use them within a few weeks of opening; a half-used bottle that sat in the garage for a year is already wet. Amber-colored fluid such as ATE Type 200 has a practical advantage: because new fluid is amber and old fluid runs dark brown, you can see the changeover at the bleeder tube. [A full flush uses roughly 16 to 32 ounces](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/brake-fluid.6954/), so a 32-ounce purchase covers it with margin.\n\n**Check before you start.** Pull the reservoir cap and look at the fluid. Honey-colored is acceptable; coffee-colored means overdue. Brake-fluid test strips ($10 for a pack) read copper content, which rises as the fluid's corrosion inhibitors deplete. While the wheels are off, look at the flex hoses for cracking or bulging, check the caliper boots for wetness, and check pad thickness. If you find something, the [front pad guide](/db/tj/tj-front-pads/) and the [rear drum service guide](/db/tj/tj-rear-drum-service/) cover the repairs, and it is far cheaper to do them while the system is already open.\n\n**Pedal stroke matters.** On a high-mileage master cylinder, the last inch of the bore holds corrosion and debris because the pedal rarely travels that far in daily use. Pressing the pedal to the floor during a bleed drags the master cylinder seals across that rough patch and can start a leak that did not exist before the flush. Have your helper stop at about two-thirds of full travel. If you want to avoid the issue entirely, use a pressure bleeder or a gravity bleed.\n\n**Bleeding methods, honestly compared.** The two-person pedal method is the most common and works well when the helper follows instructions. A vacuum bleeder ($30 to $50) lets one person work alone, but it can draw air past the bleeder-screw threads and show bubbles that are not coming from the system; wrapping the threads with PTFE tape or using a check-valve bleeder fixes that. A pressure bleeder with the correct master cylinder adapter (about $60 to $80) is the fastest and most reliable single-person method because it pushes fluid through rather than pulling. A gravity bleed costs nothing: open the bleeder and let it drip, about 10 to 15 minutes per wheel, while keeping the reservoir topped off.\n\n**ABS caution.** Some TJs have four-wheel ABS; many do not. On an ABS-equipped Jeep, a normal bleed is fine as long as no air has entered the hydraulic control unit. If the reservoir ran dry or the pedal stays spongy after two full bleeds, the air is probably inside the ABS unit, and clearing it needs a scan tool that cycles the pump and valves. That is a shop visit, not a driveway fix, which is why the dry-reservoir rule exists.\n\n**Costs and sources.** Fluid: $8 to $20 per bottle for DOT 3 or DOT 4 (ATE Type 200 runs near $18 per liter). Tubing and a catch bottle: $5. Optional bleeder: $30 to $80. A shop flush typically costs $100 to $150. Pair this with the [differential fluid service](/db/tj/tj-differential-fluid/) on the same weekend so every fluid in the drivetrain is on a known date.","steps":["Park on level ground, set the parking brake, chock the wheels that stay on the ground, loosen the lug nuts, and raise the Jeep on jack stands placed on the frame rails. Remove the wheels.","Fit a 6-point box wrench to each bleeder before you start (they are commonly 8mm or 10mm depending on the position) and crack each one loose a quarter turn, then snug it. A bleeder that will not move needs penetrating oil overnight, not more force; they are cast and they snap.","Use a turkey baster to pull the old fluid from the master cylinder reservoir down to the bottom of the compartment without disturbing the screen. Wipe the reservoir clean with a lint-free rag.","Refill with fresh fluid to the MAX line. Keep the cap off during the job but cover the opening with a rag when you are not pouring.","Start with the right rear wheel, the farthest from the master cylinder. Slide clear 1/4-inch tubing onto the bleeder and put the free end in a catch bottle holding an inch of fresh fluid so the tubing end stays submerged.","Helper presses the pedal about two-thirds of the way down and holds it. Open the bleeder a quarter turn, let fluid flow, close the bleeder, and only then tell the helper to release the pedal. Closing before release is what keeps air from being drawn back in.","Repeat until the fluid in the tube runs clean and free of bubbles, typically 5 to 8 strokes. Top off the reservoir after every wheel; do not wait.","Move to the left rear, then the right front, then the left front. This is the factory sequence, farthest to nearest.","Torque nothing aggressively: close each bleeder snug, and wipe the nipple clean. Fit the dust cap.","Top the reservoir to MAX, install the cap, and press the pedal firmly several times with the engine running. It should feel firm and hold height without sinking over 30 seconds.","Reinstall the wheels and torque the lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs in a star pattern. Lower the Jeep.","Test in an empty lot: several moderate stops from 15 mph, then a couple of firmer ones. Re-check the reservoir level and look for wetness at each bleeder.","Bottle the old fluid in a labeled container and take it to an auto parts store or a hazardous-waste drop-off. Rinse any spill on paint with water immediately; glycol fluid softens clear coat in minutes."],"pros":["A $15 to $35 job protects every component in the hydraulic system and restores a firm pedal that owners often blame on calipers.","Fresh DOT 4 raises the fade margin on long descents and tow loads for the price of one extra bottle.","Doing it yourself also forces a look at hoses, pads, and boots that a quick-lube flush never gets."],"cons":["Bottoming the pedal on a worn master cylinder can damage seals, and a dry reservoir can pull air into the ABS unit.","Brake fluid strips paint, and used fluid is hazardous waste that has to be taken to a drop-off.","The pedal method needs a second person, and the one-person tools cost $30 to $80.","A fluid flush does not cure a soft pedal caused by worn calipers, a failing master cylinder, or a collapsed hose; if the pedal is still soft after two clean bleeds, stop and diagnose."]},{"id":"tj-coolant-flush-tj","title":"Coolant System Flush and Inspection","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":80},"tools_required":["drain pan (3+ gal)","garden hose","flathead screwdriver","channel lock pliers","funnel"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["cooling","preventative","4.0L"],"parts":[{"name":"Zerex G-05 HOAT concentrate (1 gal)","part_number":"ZXG051","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":20},{"name":"Distilled water (2 gallons)","vendor":"any grocery","price_estimate_usd":4},{"name":"Mopar 195\u00b0F thermostat (optional refresh)","part_number":"5083285AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Radiator cap, 16 psi (optional)","part_number":"RC52","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"WranglerTJForum \u2014 cooling system flush writeup","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/how-to-flush-the-cooling-system-on-your-jeep-wrangler-tj.1737/"},{"title":"WranglerForum \u2014 TJ 4.0 coolant capacity thread","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/tj-4-0-coolant-capacity.1534681/"},{"title":"CarCareKiosk \u2014 2004 Wrangler 4.0L coolant flush video","url":"https://www.carcarekiosk.com/video/2004_Jeep_Wrangler_Rubicon_4.0L_6_Cyl./coolant_antifreeze/flush_coolant"}],"alternatives":["tj-oil-change-tj","tj-spark-plugs-tj"],"summary":"Drain the radiator and block, [flush with distilled water until the runoff is clear](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/how-to-flush-the-cooling-system-on-your-jeep-wrangler-tj.1737/), then refill with HOAT (G-05) coolant at 50/50. The TJ 4.0L holds about 2.6 gallons dry; plan for 1.5 gallons of new coolant on a drain-and-fill.","description":"The TJ 4.0L cooling system is robust, but coolant breaks down chemically every 5 years regardless of mileage. Once the corrosion inhibitors are spent, the inside of your radiator, heater core, and block start eating themselves. A fresh flush is cheap insurance and the most common cause of TJ overheating is not a bad water pump \u2014 it is a radiator core silted shut from a decade of skipped flushes.\n\n[Full dry capacity is 10.5 quarts](https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/tj-4-0-coolant-capacity.1534681/) (about 2.6 gallons). A standard drain through the radiator petcock recovers around 1.5 gallons, which is what you replace on a routine service. If the existing coolant is brown, rusty, or smells off, you need a full flush \u2014 drain the radiator, drain the block (8mm hex plug on the passenger side of the engine, above the starter), and then run distilled water through the system until clear.\n\nCoolant choice matters. The factory spec for 1997\u20132001 TJs is the green ethylene glycol coolant; 2002+ shipped with HOAT (Mopar's pink). Either is fine for older Jeeps as long as you fully flush before switching. The safe, modern answer for any TJ is Zerex G-05 \u2014 it is the OEM HOAT formula sold under the Valvoline label and is compatible with iron blocks and brass/copper radiators. Stay away from universal \"works with everything\" coolants; they tend to gel when mixed with leftover residue.\n\nA few things to inspect while the system is empty: pressure-test the radiator cap (16 psi is spec), squeeze the upper and lower hoses for soft spots and cracks at the bend, and look at the back of the water pump weep hole for crusty buildup. If the thermostat is original, this is the moment to swap it for a $14 Mopar 195\u00b0F replacement and stop guessing about why the temp gauge wanders.\n\nRefilling needs a bleed. The TJ has a high spot in the heater core circuit that traps air. With the cap off, start the engine, set the heater on full hot, and let the system burp for 10\u201315 minutes. Top off as the level drops. Drive a heat cycle, let it cool overnight, and check the level again before you call it done.","steps":["Park on level ground, engine cold. Remove the radiator cap.","Place a drain pan under the radiator. Open the petcock (lower passenger side) \u2014 turn the wing tab counterclockwise.","Drain fully. For a complete flush, also remove the block drain plug (8mm hex, passenger side above starter).","Close the petcock and block plug. Reinstall both.","Optional full flush: refill with plain distilled water, run engine to temp with heater on, drain again. Repeat until water runs clear.","Refill the radiator with 50/50 G-05 (or premix) to the top. Fill the overflow bottle to the FULL HOT line.","Leave the cap off. Start engine, heater on full hot, idle 10\u201315 minutes.","Top off the radiator as the level drops while air bleeds out.","Install cap. Drive a heat cycle. Recheck level the next morning."],"pros":["One of the cheapest preventative jobs you can do \u2014 $35 in fluid","Catches a failing thermostat, weak cap, or soft hose before it strands you","Restores cooling capacity if the system was full of old, depleted coolant","No special tools beyond a drain pan and a garden hose"],"cons":["Petcock on older TJs gets brittle and can break off \u2014 be gentle","Skipping the heater-on bleed traps air and causes false overheating","Mixing coolant types without a flush causes gel and corrosion","Block drain plug is in an awkward spot above the starter"]},{"id":"tj-differential-fluid","title":"Differential Fluid Change \u2014 Dana 30/35/44","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":80},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","3/8 hex driver (for fill plug)","1/2 breaker bar","fluid pump","drain pan","RTV silicone or cover gasket"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["maintenance","drivetrain","axles","fluids"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline SynPower 75W-90 (1 qt)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Royal Purple Max-Gear 75W-90 (1 qt)","vendor":"Royal Purple","price_estimate_usd":16},{"name":"Mopar 4 oz friction modifier (Trac-Lok only)","part_number":"4318060AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Lubelocker differential cover gasket","vendor":"Lubelocker","price_estimate_usd":22}],"citations":[{"title":"Wrangler TJ Forum \u2014 Fluid Capacities and Recommendations","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-fluid-capacities.195/"},{"title":"Motobilt \u2014 Jeep Wrangler Differential Fluid Capacity Chart","url":"https://motobilt.com/blogs/tech/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart"}],"alternatives":["tj-oil-change-tj","tj-spark-plugs-tj"],"summary":"Change TJ differential fluid every 30,000 miles or every wet trail run \u2014 [Dana 30 front takes about 1.25 quarts](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-fluid-capacities.195/), [Dana 35 rear takes about 1.7 quarts](https://motobilt.com/blogs/tech/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart), Dana 44 rear takes about 2 quarts of 75W-90 synthetic gear oil.","description":"The TJ Wrangler shipped with three axle combinations: Dana 30 front on every model, Dana 35 rear on most non-Rubicon trims, and Dana 44 rear on the Rubicon (2003\u20132006) and some early Sahara/65th Anniversary builds. All three use the same basic service procedure \u2014 pull the cover, drain, scrape, reseal, refill through the fill plug \u2014 and all three benefit from a 75W-90 or 75W-140 synthetic gear oil. The most common mistake is changing the rear without checking what's actually back there. A Dana 35 has a smaller, more rounded cover; the Dana 44 cover is larger with a flatter bottom edge and 10 bolts.\n\nCapacities differ. The Dana 30 front takes roughly 1.25 quarts (1.65 quarts on 1997 models, which used a slightly different housing). The Dana 35 rear takes about 1.7 quarts. The Dana 44 Rubicon rear takes 2 quarts. Buy 3 quarts if you've got the Dana 30 + Dana 35 combination, 4 quarts if you've got the Dana 30 + Dana 44 combination, and you'll have enough to refill both with a little left over for spillage.\n\nFluid choice matters more than the brand. 75W-90 GL-5 synthetic is the daily-driver standard. If you tow regularly or run 33s and above with regular trail use, step up to 75W-140 synthetic \u2014 the extra viscosity protects the ring and pinion under load. Skip the cheapest conventional 80W-90 unless you're flushing fluid every 15k miles. Rubicon Tru-Lok lockers do not require friction modifier. Trac-Lok limited-slip differentials (often found in the Dana 44 rear, some Dana 35s) require 4 oz of Mopar friction modifier (P/N 4318060AB) per fill, or the clutches will chatter on turns.\n\nMost TJ axles do not have a drain plug. You drain by removing the rear differential cover, which means you'll also be replacing or reusing the cover gasket. RTV silicone (Permatex Ultra Black) is the OEM-style seal \u2014 clean both surfaces fully, lay a 3/16\" bead around the bolt circle, install the cover, and let RTV cure 30 minutes before refilling. A Lubelocker reusable gasket is the upgrade \u2014 install dry, no cure time, and reusable for the next service.","steps":["Drive the vehicle for 10 minutes to warm the fluid for more manageable draining. Park level.","Place a drain pan under the differential. Loosen the fill plug FIRST with a 3/8 hex driver \u2014 if it's stuck or stripped, you need to know before you've already drained the axle.","Remove the differential cover bolts in a star pattern. Leave the top two bolts loose to control fluid release.","Crack the cover loose with a flathead screwdriver. Fluid will pour out \u2014 let it fully drain (5\u201310 minutes).","Remove the cover. Inspect the inside of the housing for metal chunks, glitter, or chipped gear teeth.","Wipe the housing interior, ring gear, and magnet (if equipped) clean of old fluid and debris. Reinstall the magnet.","Scrape old RTV from both mating surfaces with a plastic scraper. Wipe with brake cleaner.","Apply a 3/16\" bead of RTV around the cover bolt circle, or install a Lubelocker gasket dry.","Reinstall the cover and torque bolts in a star pattern to 30 ft-lbs.","Wait 30 minutes for RTV to set (skip if using Lubelocker).","Pump 75W-90 synthetic gear oil through the fill hole until fluid trickles out the bottom of the fill hole. For Trac-Lok diffs, add 4 oz friction modifier before topping off.","Reinstall and torque the fill plug to 25 ft-lbs. Check for leaks after the first short drive."],"pros":["Catches metal debris before it grinds the ring and pinion","Cheap insurance \u2014 $40 in fluid vs. $1,500+ for a ring-and-pinion replacement","Reveals worn gears, chipped teeth, or excessive backlash early","Synthetic gear oil runs cooler under load"],"cons":["Messy job \u2014 fluid drips everywhere if you rush","Requires RTV cure time (or a reusable gasket)","Friction modifier is non-optional with Trac-Lok or it will chatter","The Dana 30 front fill plug is often seized \u2014 soak in penetrant the night before"]},{"id":"tj-oil-change-tj","title":"Oil Change \u2014 4.0L and 2.5L","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_oil","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":60},"tools_required":["13mm socket","oil filter wrench (76mm cap or strap)","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["oil-change","4.0l","2.5l","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"10W-30 or 5W-30 conventional or synthetic blend (6 quarts 4.0L / 4 quarts 2.5L)","part_number":"MS-6395","vendor":"any API SN+ rated","price_estimate_usd":24},{"name":"Mopar oil filter (spin-on)","part_number":"5281090","vendor":"Mopar / equivalent","price_estimate_usd":6},{"name":"Drain plug gasket","part_number":"33002770","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":1}],"citations":[{"title":"AMC/Chrysler 4.0L service manual oil capacity (6 qt with filter)","url":"https://www.fsmrepairmanual.com"},{"title":"TJ owner's manual \u2014 10W-30 above 40\u00b0F, 5W-30 below","url":"https://www.jeep.com"}],"alternatives":["tj-spark-plugs-tj","tj-brake-fluid-tj"],"summary":"[The 4.0L holds 6 quarts](https://www.fsmrepairmanual.com) of 10W-30 (5W-30 in cold climates) with a spin-on filter. The 2.5L 4-cylinder holds 4 quarts. Drain plug torques to 25 ft-lb, filter hand-tight plus 3/4 turn. Job takes 30 minutes.","description":"The TJ Wrangler runs either the 4.0L straight-six or the 2.5L AMC four-cylinder. Both use a conventional spin-on filter mounted on the lower passenger side of the engine block and a 13mm hex drain plug on the oil pan. The procedures are identical \u2014 only the capacity changes.\n\n**Oil viscosity.** [The factory called for 10W-30 above 40\u00b0F and 5W-30 below 40\u00b0F](https://www.jeep.com). Either is fine year-round in most climates. Some owners run 10W-40 in hot Southwest summers \u2014 the 4.0L wears in for the long haul on anything in this range. Phoenix and Las Vegas owners can safely run 15W-40 conventional. Don't run 0W-20 \u2014 the 4.0L has wider bearing clearances than modern engines and will burn it.\n\n**Synthetic or conventional?** Conventional changed every 3,000 miles or full synthetic every 5,000 miles both work. The 4.0L is an old design with loose tolerances; it doesn't reward expensive synthetic the way a modern direct-injected engine does. The exception: if your TJ has 200K+ miles and burns a quart between changes, switching to a high-mileage synthetic blend with seal conditioner can stop leaks at the rear main and valve cover.\n\n**Filter.** Any name-brand spin-on filter that fits the M22x1.5 thread works \u2014 WIX 51515, Mopar 5281090, FRAM PH3614, Mobil 1 M1-110A. The filter sits at an angle on the block; expect a quart of mess if you don't position the drain pan right.","steps":["Run engine 2\u20133 minutes to thin oil. Shut off.","Position drain pan under the oil pan plug (rear of pan, 13mm hex).","Remove drain plug. Inspect the gasket \u2014 replace if crushed or torn.","While oil drains, reposition the pan under the filter (slightly forward and to the passenger side).","Spin off the old filter (counterclockwise from below). It will dump 4\u20138 oz of oil straight down \u2014 be ready.","Wipe the filter mount clean. Verify the old filter gasket came off with the filter (a stuck gasket is the #1 cause of catastrophic oil leaks after a DIY change).","Wet the new filter's gasket with fresh oil. Spin on by hand until snug, then 3/4 turn more. Do not use a filter wrench to tighten \u2014 that's how you crush the can.","Reinstall drain plug with new gasket. Torque to 25 ft-lb.","Add oil through the valve cover fill cap. 4.0L: 6 qt. 2.5L: 4 qt.","Start engine. Verify oil pressure light goes out within 3 seconds. Idle 30 seconds, shut off.","Wait 5 minutes, check dipstick. Top off as needed.","Drive 5 minutes, check for leaks around the filter and drain plug when you stop."],"pros":["Spin-on filter is the most straightforward design ever made","30-minute job with hand tools only","Cheap parts at any store","4.0L doesn't care about brand of oil"],"cons":["Filter location dumps oil on the front axle and skid plate every time","Stuck old gasket = oil all over the driveway in 10 miles","Owners who overtighten filters strip the threaded boss in the block (expensive fix)","Drain plug crush gasket is often skipped \u2014 leaks slowly until it's noticed"]},{"id":"tj-spark-plugs-tj","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 4.0L","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"ignition","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":18,"high":45},"tools_required":["5/8\" spark plug socket","3/8\" ratchet and 6\" extension","torque wrench","spark plug gap gauge","dielectric grease (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["spark-plugs","ignition","4.0l","tune-up"],"parts":[{"name":"Champion RC12LYC OEM copper (qty 6)","part_number":"RC12LYC","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"NGK ZFR5N copper (qty 6)","part_number":"5165","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"NGK ZFR5F-11 platinum (qty 6, longer life)","part_number":"3459","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Chrysler TJ 4.0L service info \u2014 0.035\" gap, 27 ft-lb plug torque","url":"https://www.jeep.com"},{"title":"Champion RC12LYC OEM application data for 4.0L Jeep","url":"https://www.championautoparts.com"}],"alternatives":["tj-oil-change-tj","tj-brake-fluid-tj"],"summary":"The 4.0L takes six plugs, [gapped at 0.035\", torqued to 27 ft-lb](https://www.jeep.com). Copper plugs (Champion RC12LYC or NGK ZFR5N) are the OEM choice and run cleaner than premium platinums on this old engine. Job takes 30\u201360 minutes with a 5/8\" plug socket.","description":"The 4.0L straight-six uses a straightforward distributor-and-coil ignition through 1997, then coil-on-distributor with cap-and-rotor through 2006. Either way, the plugs sit in a row down the passenger side of the engine, fully exposed, no intake to remove. This is the most straightforward spark plug job on any modern engine.\n\n**Plug choice.** [The factory plug is the Champion RC12LYC copper](https://www.championautoparts.com). NGK ZFR5N is the direct equivalent. Both gap to 0.035\" out of the box. Service interval is 30,000 miles on copper, 60,000 on platinum (NGK ZFR5F-11), 100,000 on iridium (NGK IZFR5F-11). On an engine this straightforward, copper is the better answer: cheaper, runs cooler, more manageable to read for diagnostics.\n\n**Why not premium plugs?** The 4.0L was designed for copper. Iridium plugs sometimes cause cold-start hesitation on this engine because the precious metal tip has a slightly higher firing voltage requirement and the older ignition coil isn't always up to it. If your TJ runs perfectly on copper and you switch to iridium and get rough idle, that's why.\n\n**Gap matters.** Pre-gapped doesn't mean correctly gapped. Always verify with a wire gauge \u2014 0.035\" should slide through with light drag. If you can shove a 0.040\" gauge through too, the plug is no good (don't try to close the gap on iridium; the tip chips).\n\n**Anti-seize warning.** Most modern plugs ship with a nickel-coated thread that does not need anti-seize. Adding anti-seize will cause you to overtorque (the lubricant reduces friction, so your torque wrench reads correct but the actual clamp load is too high). If you must use it, a tiny dab on the threads only.","steps":["Engine cold (or at least cool to the touch). Hot heads can pull threads out when you remove plugs.","Pop the plug wire boots off one at a time. Twist before pulling to break the seal \u2014 don't yank straight up.","Blow compressed air around the plug well to remove any dirt before unscrewing.","Use the 5/8\" plug socket with extension. Back the plug out a half turn, then turn by hand the rest of the way to feel for cross-threading.","Inspect the old plug: tan/brown = correct mixture. White/glazed = lean. Black/sooty = rich. Oily = valve seal or piston ring issue.","Check the new plug's gap with a wire gauge. Should be 0.035\". Bend the side electrode only, never the center.","Apply a small dab of dielectric grease inside the plug wire boot (helps prevent moisture-related misfires).","Thread the new plug in by hand until snug. If it doesn't go in by hand, stop \u2014 you're cross-threading.","Torque to 27 ft-lb with a torque wrench. On an aluminum head this is non-negotiable; the 4.0L iron head is more forgiving but a wrench is still smart.","Reattach the plug wire. Repeat for all six.","Start, listen for misfire. If you have a code reader, scan for P0301\u2013P0306."],"pros":["Cleanest plug job on any modern engine","All six plugs exposed, no intake removal","Copper plugs cost $18 for the set","Old plug condition tells you a lot about engine health"],"cons":["Yanking plug wires straight up tears the terminal off","Hot-engine plug removal pulls aluminum threads out of cylinder heads (rare on 4.0L iron head but possible on 2.5L)","Anti-seize causes overtorque if you don't reduce torque spec by ~25%","Aftermarket plug wires from auction sites cause more misfires than any worn plug"]},{"id":"tj-transfer-case-fluid-tj","title":"Transfer Case Fluid \u2014 NP231 and NV231","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":40},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","fluid pump","drain pan","1/2 drive ratchet for fill/drain plugs"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["drivetrain","transfer-case","service-interval","atf"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar ATF+4 (1 quart)","part_number":"68218057AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Valvoline MaxLife ATF (1 quart)","part_number":"773775","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":7},{"name":"Mopar transfer case fill/drain plug crush washer","part_number":"06034203","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":2}],"citations":[{"title":"Novak \u2014 Guide to the New Process / New Venture Gear 231 Transfer Case","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/transfer-cases/np231.html"},{"title":"TJ Forum \u2014 Hit 1.5 quarts on 231 transfer case, should I keep going?","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/hit-1-5-quarts-on-231-transfer-case-should-i-keep-going.52014/"},{"title":"TJ Forum \u2014 ATF or ATF+4 for transfer case fluid?","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/atf-or-atf-4-for-transfer-case-fluid.4818/"}],"alternatives":["tj-oil-change-tj","tj-spark-plugs-tj"],"summary":"Drain and refill the TJ's NP231 or NV231 transfer case every 30,000 miles, and sooner after deep water, with ATF+4 pumped in until it begins to weep from the fill hole. Budget two to three quarts, about 45 minutes, and one rule you cannot skip: break the fill plug loose before you drain anything.","description":"Every TJ except the Rubicon carries the NP231 (relabeled NV231 after New Venture Gear took over the design). It is [a chain-driven, part-time transfer case with a 2.72:1 planetary low range](https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/transfer-cases/np231.html) and an aluminum housing. The Rubicon uses the NV241OR with a 4:1 low range, so if your Jeep has the Rubicon badge, the fill procedure is similar but the case is a different part. The 231's chain and planetary are durable, but they live on the fluid film in the case, and the case has no filter and no cooler. Fluid that has cooked, picked up water, or collected fine metal is the main thing that shortens its life.\n\n**Which fluid.** [Mopar specifies ATF+4 for the later NV231](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/atf-or-atf-4-for-transfer-case-fluid.4818/) (Mopar 68218057AC is a one-quart bottle), and earlier owner manuals call for a Dexron-type ATF. ATF+4 is the sensible default because it is the current Mopar fluid and you probably already stock it for the transmission. Do not substitute gear oil: it is the wrong viscosity for the pump-and-splash lubrication in this case and can change shift feel. Whatever you choose, use one fluid consistently so you can read its condition at each service.\n\n**How much it takes.** Published capacity figures run lower than what actually goes in after a full drain, and the forum threads reflect that disagreement. The reliable method is to fill until fluid reaches the bottom edge of the fill hole, which is how the factory level is defined. [Expect roughly 1.5 to 2 quarts](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/hit-1-5-quarts-on-231-transfer-case-should-i-keep-going.52014/), but buy three so you never stop short. The Jeep must be level when you check; a nose-up or nose-down parking spot changes the apparent level by an ounce or more.\n\n**Plug access and the stuck-plug trap.** The fill plug sits on the rear half of the case and the drain plug on the underside. Both plugs have a recess for a 1/2-inch square drive, so a 1/2-inch ratchet or breaker bar goes straight into the plug with no socket. If a skid plate covers the case, you may need to unbolt it first. Always crack the fill plug before you open the drain, because a drained case with a seized fill plug is a Jeep you cannot refill and cannot safely drive. Penetrating oil on the plug an evening ahead helps. If it will not break loose with a long bar, stop and sort that out with the case still full.\n\n**Reading the drain.** Warm the case with a 10-minute drive so the fluid flows, but be careful, because the case and fluid can scald. Dark red-brown is normal for a 30,000-mile interval. Black and burnt-smelling fluid means heat or overdue service; shorten the interval to 15,000 miles. Milky or foamy fluid means water has entered, most often through a clogged breather vent or after a deep crossing; change it immediately, then run it again a few hundred miles later. A light gray paste on a magnet or a pinch of fine fuzz is normal wear. Visible flakes or chips mean something is breaking inside, such as a chain pad or a bearing, and the case needs to come apart before it fails on the trail.\n\n**Vent and seals.** The case breathes through a vent with a hose routed upward. A pinched, plugged, or low-routed vent builds pressure on a hot case and pushes fluid past the output seals. Check that the hose is intact and routed high, particularly after a lift or exhaust work moved things around. A weeping rear output seal usually points to a worn tail-housing bushing rather than the seal alone; the [NP231 service guide](/db/tj/tj-np231-tj/) covers that failure and the slip-yoke-eliminator decision.\n\n**Magnetic plug.** Some magnetic replacement plugs exist for the 231 and cost $10 to $20. Confirm the thread size and drive style match before you buy, and do not over-tighten, since the housing is aluminum and the threads strip.\n\n**Costs and sources.** ATF+4: $7 to $11 per quart; plan on three. Hand fluid pump: $10 to $15. Optional magnetic plug: $10 to $20. Shop labor if you skip DIY: $60 to $120 plus fluid. Do it alongside the [differential fluid service](/db/tj/tj-differential-fluid/) so both gear-case services and the transfer case share the same interval, and see the [front driveshaft guide](/db/tj/tj-front-driveshaft/) if you find the front output seal weeping.","steps":["Drive a short distance to warm the fluid, then park on the most level ground you can find. Set the parking brake and chock the wheels.","Slide a wide drain pan under the transfer case. If a skid plate is bolted over it, remove the skid plate first.","Break the fill plug loose with a 1/2-inch ratchet and then snug it back by hand. If it does not move, stop here.","Remove the drain plug and let the case empty for 15 to 20 minutes. Catch a sample in a clean jar and look at color, smell, and any particles.","If the plug has a washer or sealing ring, replace it. Clean the plug, then reinstall it and tighten to about 20 to 25 ft-lbs. The housing is aluminum, so use a torque wrench rather than a long breaker bar.","Attach a hand pump or squeeze bottle with a hose to the new ATF+4 and feed it into the fill hole until fluid reaches the lower edge of the hole and begins to seep out.","Reinstall the fill plug to the same torque and wipe the case clean so a leak will be visible later.","Check that the vent hose is intact and routed upward and clear of the exhaust.","Drive 10 miles, shifting into 4-Hi and 4-Lo once on dirt (never on dry pavement), then re-inspect for weeping at both plugs and the output seals.","Write the date and mileage on a strip of masking tape or in your maintenance log. The interval is 30,000 miles, or 15,000 if you wheel in mud and water."],"pros":["Cheap and quick: about $20 to $40 in fluid and a morning of work, with a diagnostic payoff that no other 30-minute job offers.","A drain sample tells you about the chain, bearings, and seals before a failure leaves you stranded.","Fixing a clogged vent during the service prevents the seal leaks that otherwise get blamed on the case."],"cons":["A seized fill plug turns a routine service into a tow if you drain first.","Fill-to-the-hole means the quantity changes by rig and parking spot, which confuses first-timers who expect a fixed number.","The housing and plugs are aluminum and strip easily with a long bar or heavy hand.","Fresh fluid will not fix a worn chain or a failing output bearing; if the drain shows chips, plan a rebuild rather than another refill."]},{"id":"tj-recovery-gear-kit","title":"Recovery Gear Starter Kit for the TJ","vehicle":"tj","category":"recovery","type":"kit","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":550},"tools_required":["basic hand tools","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["recovery rigging basics","kinetic rope technique"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["recovery","traction","kinetic-rope","hi-lift","maxtrax"],"parts":[{"name":"Bubba Rope 20' Sidewinder Kinetic Rope 3/4\" (17,600 lb MBS)","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":99},{"name":"Rhino USA 3/4\" D-Ring Shackles (2-pack, 41,850 lb WLL)","vendor":"Rhino USA","price_estimate_usd":29},{"name":"Maxtrax MKII Recovery Boards (pair, LDPE plastic)","vendor":"Maxtrax","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Hi-Lift Jack HL-485 (48-inch, 4,660 lb capacity)","vendor":"Hi-Lift Jack Company","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"ARB Recovery Bag (medium, waterproof storage)","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":49}],"citations":[{"title":"Overland Journal \u2014 Building a Recovery Kit for Trail Use","url":"https://overlandjournal.com/gear/recovery-kit-guide/"},{"title":"Bubba Rope Kinetic Recovery Rope Selection Guide","url":"https://www.bubbarope.com/how-to-choose"},{"title":"Hi-Lift Jack Usage and Safety Instructions","url":"https://hi-lift.com/instructions"}],"alternatives":["tj-winch","tj-front-bumper","tj-rear-bumper"],"summary":"Before your first trail day, buy a kinetic rope, rated shackles, a pair of traction boards, gloves, and a tire deflator, and put them where you can reach them without unpacking the Jeep. Plan on $250 to $550 and an hour of practice in a parking lot before you need any of it.","description":"A recovery kit exists for the day you or a trail partner is stuck. It has to be sized to the load, rigged to an actual recovery point, and used with a clear understanding of what can break and where it will fly. A TJ with its stock tow hooks and a $20 strap from a hardware store is a hazard, not a kit. This guide covers what each piece does, what it costs, and the mistakes that put people in the hospital.\n\n**Know your recovery points first.** The TJ has two frame-mounted tow hooks on the front and, depending on year and trim, a hook or loop at the rear; confirm what yours has before relying on it. They are rated for pulling the Jeep onto a flatbed or out of a ditch, not for the shock loads of a kinetic snatch. Never hook a rope or strap to a trailer hitch ball, an axle tube, a bumper plate that is not frame-mounted, a tie-down loop, or a suspension link. A failed recovery point becomes a projectile. If you run an aftermarket bumper, check that the shackle mounts are welded and gusseted to the frame side, as covered in [recovery points for the TJ](/db/tj/tj-recovery-points/).\n\n**Kinetic rope or static strap.** A kinetic rope stretches 20 to 30 percent and stores energy, so the pull comes on as a smooth tug rather than a jerk. The rope's rated minimum breaking strength (MBS) [should be at least two to three times the weight](https://www.bubbarope.com/how-to-choose) of the heavier vehicle in the pull; a TJ weighs around 4,000 lb, and a loaded one more. A 3/4 inch, 20 foot rope rated near 17,600 lb MBS is sensibly sized. A static strap does not stretch and transmits shock to anchors, so use it only for slow, steady pulls such as a winch line extension. Never join two ropes with a knot; use a rated shackle or a purpose-built joiner, and never use a chain for a snatch.\n\n**Shackles.** Buy forged D-ring or bow shackles with the working load limit (WLL) stamped on them; a 3/4 inch shackle typically carries a 4.75 ton WLL. Avoid cast shackles, and avoid the cheapest imports without stamped ratings. Soft shackles made of synthetic rope are lighter and kinder to paint and are widely used now; check them for abrasion before each trip. Tighten a screw-pin shackle fully, then back it off about a quarter turn so the pin does not bind in the eye after load. Some trail groups argue against the quarter-turn rule; either way, do not leave it finger-loose.\n\n**Traction boards.** Maxtrax and similar boards solve the most common trail problem, spinning tires on a soft surface, without a second vehicle. Wedge them against the drive tires, point the Jeep forward or back out, and drive slowly onto them. They do not help when the Jeep is high-centered, axle-deep in mud, or hung on a rock; lifting and stacking rocks, or a winch, will. Cheaper boards from other brands exist at roughly half the price and work, but check that they are rated for your weight and that the traction teeth are on both sides.\n\n**Hi-Lift jack.** A high-lift jack is useful and dangerous. It needs a solid lift point, such as a steel bumper, a rock slider's jacking pad, or a rated jacking tab, and it can kick back on the handle when the load shifts. [Keep your head and chest out of the handle's arc](https://hi-lift.com/instructions), set the jack on a board or plate in soft ground, and lock the mechanism properly. It does not replace jack stands for work under the Jeep. Practice lifting a wheel in your driveway before the trail.\n\n**Personal protective gear.** Leather gloves, safety glasses, and a recovery damper (a blanket, a heavy jacket, or a purpose-made line damper hung over the rope's midpoint) are cheap insurance. A tire deflator and a compressor are traction tools: airing down from 30 to about 15 PSI often frees a stuck Jeep before anything else is needed. Include a tree-saver strap (3 inch wide, rated 30,000 lb or more) if you will ever winch off a tree.","steps":["Buy the core kit: kinetic rope (about $100), two forged shackles with stamped WLL (about $30 to $60), gloves, a damper, and a tree saver (about $25). That is roughly $200 to $250 before boards or a jack.","Add traction boards (roughly $100 to $350 by brand), a tire deflator ($15 to $40), and a compressor ($80 to $200) if you do not have one. Add a Hi-Lift (about $90 to $130) only after you have studied its use and have a lift point on the Jeep.","Put everything in a bag with a sealed lid so wet rope does not mildew and so loose hardware does not fly in a rollover. Store the bag where you can reach it without unloading cargo, and secure it with a strap or a tie-down. Hi-Lift jacks can be mounted on the exterior, but a loose jack inside the cab is a missile.","Inspect everything at home: rope for cuts, glazing, or flat spots; shackles for bent pins or stretched eyes; boards for cracks. Retire damaged rope; do not splice it.","Practice the recovery pull in an empty lot. Attach the rope to a recovery point on each vehicle with a shackle, stand clear of the line of pull, drape the damper over the rope's middle, and have the pulling vehicle start slowly, build speed, and take up the slack gradually; a snatch is a controlled tug, not a full-throttle launch. Agree on hand signals and one person who calls the pull.","Practice airing down, placing boards, and lifting a wheel with the jack on a firm surface, so each step is familiar before you need it with mud on your gloves.","Before every trail day, check that the recovery points on both vehicles are intact, and tell your group where your kit is stored."],"pros":["A kinetic rope and rated shackles solve most mud and sand situations for well under $150 when a second vehicle is present.","Traction boards are the one item that recovers a lone Jeep from soft ground without a winch.","Airing down and boards are low-risk compared with a snatch or a high-lift lift, and they should come first in most recoveries.","A tested kit and a practiced routine turn an hour of trail embarrassment into a 10-minute task."],"cons":["Kinetic ropes need a second vehicle, and misrigging one is how bystanders get hurt; never stand beside the line.","Hi-Lift jacks can kick back hard and need a solid lift point the stock TJ does not have; plan on a steel bumper or rock sliders before relying on one.","Boards do nothing for a high-centered Jeep or deep mud and cost more than the rope and shackles combined.","Stock tow hooks are not shock-rated; for hard snatches, upgrade to frame-mounted shackle points. See [TJ winch options](/db/tj/tj-winch/) for the next step up.","A full kit weighs 30 to 40 lb and needs a secured home in the cargo area.","**Costs and sources.** Starter kit with rope, shackles, damper, tree saver, gloves: $200 to $300. Add boards, deflator, and compressor: $400 to $700 total. Hi-Lift: about $90 to $130. Prices from Bubba Rope, Rhino USA, Maxtrax, Hi-Lift, and ARB listings above are approximate; verify at checkout, and check each product's stated rating before buying."]},{"id":"tj-recovery-points","title":"TJ Recovery Points \u2014 Rated Anchors for Self-Recovery and Being Pulled","vehicle":"tj","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":180},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","thread locker"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["recovery","recovery_points","D-rings","shackles","tow-hooks","rigging"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB rated recovery point D-ring pair (3/4\" forged)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Daystar Recovery Tow Points \u2014 front and rear pair \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Daystar","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Warn 88900 Forged D-Shackle set (3/4\" rated)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":48},{"name":"Bulletpoint Mounting Solutions frame-mount recovery points \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Bulletpoint","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Factor 55 Ultrahook soft shackle mount","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Bubba Rope 7/8\" synthetic soft shackle (41,300 lb breaking strength)","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"How to Set Up Recovery Rigging on a Jeep","url":"https://www.fourwheeler.com/how-to/recovery/how-to-set-up-recovery-rigging/"},{"title":"Proper use of D-rings and tow straps","url":"https://www.offroading101.com/recovery-point-guide/"},{"title":"Kinetic recovery rope vs. tow strap \u2014 when to use which","url":"https://www.expeditionportal.com/kinetic-recovery-ropes-explained/"}],"alternatives":["tj-winch","tj-recovery-gear-kit","tj-front-bumper-guide","tj-rear-bumper-guide"],"summary":"A recovery point that fails under load can send a heavy shackle through a windshield at high speed. [Use rated, forged recovery anchors](https://www.fourwheeler.com/how-to/recovery/how-to-set-up-recovery-rigging/) \u2014 never unrated tow hooks, trailer ball mounts, or frame tie-downs for vehicle recovery.","description":"The TJ has factory tow hooks front and rear, but they're not rated for dynamic recovery loads \u2014 they're designed for low-speed tow truck hookups, not for snatch strap recovery where peak load can spike to 3\u20134x vehicle weight. Real recovery points are forged steel anchors with published load ratings, typically 4.75 tons or higher for Jeep-class rigs.\n\nRecovery point placement matters. Front: the best TJ front recovery points integrate into a steel front bumper \u2014 D-ring mounts welded to the bumper's frame-tied structure. If running the stock plastic bumper, bolt-on frame-mounted recovery points (Daystar, Bulletpoint) attach to the frame rails and provide a rated anchor without a full bumper swap. Rear: same principle \u2014 steel rear bumper with D-ring mounts is the right answer, with frame-mount bolt-on points as the budget interim.\n\n**Never use:**\n- Trailer hitch balls or receivers as recovery anchors \u2014 they're not rated for the load angles\n- Frame tie-downs or tie-down chains as recovery points\n- Bumper sheetmetal loops on plastic or stamped-metal bumpers\n\n**Shackle spec:** Use a 3/4\" forged bow shackle with a rated WLL (working load limit) of at least 4.75 tons. Screw-pin type \u2014 thread-in pin that locks with a half-turn back. Never use the shackle pin as a load-bearing connection: the bow must take the load, not the pin threads. WLL numbers on shackle packaging are already divided down from breaking strength by a safety factor, typically 4:1 to 6:1 depending on the manufacturer \u2014 a 4.75-ton WLL shackle has a real breaking strength closer to 19\u201328 tons. That margin exists to cover shock loading, wear, and off-axis pulls, not to be used up on purpose by buying undersized.\n\nSteel shackles aren't the only rated connector worth carrying. A synthetic soft shackle \u2014 a loop of UHMWPE rope with a spliced eye and a friction-fit knob \u2014 connects the same way a bow shackle does but weighs a few ounces instead of two pounds, and it won't put a chunk of forged steel through a windshield if something fails. Bubba Rope's 7/8\" soft shackle is rated to 41,300 lb breaking strength, well above what a TJ-class recovery generates, and it needs a hard mounting point like the Factor 55 Ultrahook to keep the rope loop from being crushed against a sharp-edged D-ring. Many trail leaders now run soft shackles at both ends of a kinetic rope and keep a single steel bow shackle in the kit as a backup for winching, where a bow shackle attaches more naturally to a hook or clevis.\n\n[Kinetic recovery ropes and tow straps are not the same tool](https://www.expeditionportal.com/kinetic-recovery-ropes-explained/) and shouldn't be swapped for each other. A tow strap is a static-ish nylon strap meant to be pulled taut before the tug \u2014 it has minimal stretch, and using it for a running \"snatch\" start on a stuck vehicle is how tow straps and their mounting hardware fail. A kinetic rope (Bubba Rope, ARB, Factor 55) is engineered to stretch 20\u201330% under load and store that energy, then release it to pop a stuck vehicle free with a lower peak shock load on both vehicles' recovery points than a strap used the same way. If you're doing a running pull, use a kinetic rope; if you're doing a slow, steady static pull (winching, or easing a vehicle back onto the trail), a tow strap or winch line is the right tool.\n\n- Rated recovery D-rings or bolt-on recovery point brackets\n- Torque wrench\n- Loctite Blue for threads\n- 3/4\" rated bow shackles (forged, not cast) or an equivalent-rated soft shackle\n- Kinetic recovery rope for dynamic pulls, tow strap for static pulls\n\n1. Identify the frame mounting location \u2014 typically a hole or reinforced section on the front frame rail behind the front bumper area.\n2. Position recovery bracket, thread bolts by hand.\n3. Apply Loctite Blue to threads \u2014 these bolts loosen from vibration.\n4. Torque to manufacturer spec \u2014 typically 55\u201380 ft-lb depending on bolt diameter.\n5. Verify no interference with steering components or exhaust.\n\nAttach shackle bow through the D-ring mount. Thread the pin in fully, then back off a half-turn \u2014 this prevents the pin from seizing from load but keeps it from backing out. Some wheelers safety-wire the shackle pin for this reason.\n\nRecovery strap attaches to the shackle bow. [Never hook directly to a D-ring without a shackle](https://www.offroading101.com/recovery-point-guide/) \u2014 the strap webbing can slip off under dynamic load.\n\nIf the recovery line has to change direction \u2014 pulling a vehicle out sideways around an obstacle, for instance \u2014 use a rated snatch block anchored to a separate, independent recovery point or a tree-saver strap around a solid tree. Never redirect a line around a bare tree trunk (it kills the bark and the tree) and never use the vehicle's own recovery point as the redirect anchor for its own line \u2014 that doubles the load on a single point instead of spreading it.\n\nLay a heavy blanket, floor mat, or dedicated recovery damper over the middle of the line before a kinetic pull. If a connection point fails, the damper's weight pulls the line down instead of letting it whip.\n\n- Check shackle rating against your vehicle weight. A 4.75-ton WLL shackle is appropriate for a stock TJ. Add a winch, armor, and a spare and you're at 4,500 lb \u2014 still fine for a 4.75T shackle, but don't go lighter.\n- Inspect recovery hardware before each trip. Bent, cracked, or deformed shackles are retired \u2014 period. Recovery hardware stress-fractures, and a deformed shackle is a pre-failure shackle.\n- Inspect kinetic ropes and soft shackles for abrasion, cuts, and UV-damaged fuzzing along the fibers. A rope with a chafed section is compromised even if the rest looks fine \u2014 retire it.\n- Safety critical: never stand in the recovery line between two vehicles, and keep bystanders well clear of the line's path on both sides. If a shackle or strap fails under load, it becomes a high-velocity projectile, and a kinetic rope under tension releases that stored stretch energy the instant the load comes off.\n\nBolt-on frame recovery point brackets: $75\u2013$120 per pair. Rated forged shackles: $12\u2013$20 each (ARB, Warn, Factor 55 \u2014 all acceptable brands). Soft shackles run $35\u2013$50 each and need a compatible hard-point mount like the Ultrahook ($80\u2013$95). A complete front and rear rated recovery setup with shackles runs $120\u2013$200 if you're not replacing bumpers. If you're already buying steel bumpers, the D-ring mounts typically come integrated \u2014 verify before purchasing separate brackets. A kinetic rope in a TJ-appropriate rating (roughly 19,000\u201328,000 lb breaking strength) runs $100\u2013$160 and is worth carrying alongside, not instead of, a static tow strap."},{"id":"tj-winch","title":"Winch Selection for the TJ Wrangler","vehicle":"tj","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":799},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire strippers","crimping tool","heat gun (for heat-shrink connectors)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","12V wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["recovery","winch","self-recovery","synthetic-rope"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn M8000 Electric Winch (8,000 lb capacity, steel cable)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Warn VR EVO 8 Electric Winch (8,000 lb capacity, steel cable)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Smittybilt X20 Gen2 9,500 lb Winch with Synthetic Rope 98495","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":329},{"name":"Mile Marker 8000 Electric Winch (8,000 lb capacity)","vendor":"Mile Marker Industries","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Winch Selection and Install Guide","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/winch-selection-tj-wrangler/"},{"title":"Warn VR EVO 8 Product Page and Specs","url":"https://warn.com/vr-evo-8"},{"title":"Overland Journal \u2014 Synthetic vs. Steel Winch Line Comparison","url":"https://overlandjournal.com/gear/synthetic-vs-steel-winch-line/"}],"alternatives":["tj-recovery-gear-kit","tj-front-bumper"],"summary":"[An 8,000\u20139,500 lb winch is the right size for a TJ](https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/winch-selection-tj-wrangler/) \u2014 the rig's GVWR sits between 3,500 and 4,000 lbs, and the 2x rule for winch capacity puts you squarely in this range.","description":"The TJ's curb weight is approximately 3,200\u20133,500 lbs depending on spec, and GVWR tops out around 4,000 lbs with a loaded rig. The standard recovery calculation is twice the vehicle weight for winch capacity, which means 7,000\u20138,000 lbs is the floor and 9,500 is a sensible ceiling. A 12,000 lb winch will technically work on a TJ, but it adds weight and wiring requirements without providing any practical benefit \u2014 the pull capacity you'd use in a real recovery scenario tops out well below what an 8,000 lb unit handles.\n\nWarn remains the benchmark for built-in-the-USA winch quality. The M8000 ($499) has been a trail standard for over a decade \u2014 known for consistent performance, a robust motor, and Warn's parts/warranty support. [The VR EVO 8](https://warn.com/vr-evo-8) ($399) is the current-generation value tier from Warn: it gives you Warn reliability and a 3-stage planetary gear system at a lower price point by using a different motor spec and a more straightforward remote. For most TJ owners, the VR EVO 8 is the right choice.\n\nSmittybilt's X20 Gen2 ($329) comes with a synthetic rope from the factory, which is a meaningful safety advantage over steel cable. [Synthetic rope doesn't store kinetic energy the way steel cable does](https://overlandjournal.com/gear/synthetic-vs-steel-winch-line/) \u2014 when steel cable snaps under load, it recoils with force proportional to the stored tension. A synthetic rope failure is dramatic but far less dangerous. If the budget dictates a Smittybilt over a Warn, the X20 Gen2 is the pick. Expect the motor and solenoid to work harder than a Warn under heavy repeated use \u2014 it's a trail winch, not an arborist winch.\n\nA winch is useless without a winch-capable bumper. Verify your front bumper has a proper winch mount plate tied into the frame rails before purchasing. A fairlead (hawse for synthetic rope, roller fairlead for steel cable) is required and typically not included with the winch.","steps":["Verify front bumper has a winch-compatible mounting plate with frame tie-in.","Slide winch into bumper mounting channel; start all four mounting bolts hand-tight.","Torque winch mounting bolts to 55\u201365 ft-lbs.","Route power cables to the battery: positive to battery positive terminal, negative to battery negative or chassis ground. Route cables away from heat sources and sharp edges; use loom or split conduit.","Install solenoid box per manufacturer instructions (typically mounts to the winch or bumper with included hardware).","Verify power connections are tight and sealed with dielectric grease.","Install hawse fairlead (for synthetic rope) or roller fairlead (for steel cable) to bumper fairlead opening. Torque to 15\u201320 ft-lbs.","Spool the winch line through the fairlead and confirm correct drum direction \u2014 winch should pull from the bottom of the drum.","Test-fire the winch under no load in both directions with the remote. Verify solenoid actuation.","Apply winch line to a stationary point and test under light load before trail use."],"pros":["8,000\u20139,500 lb capacity is correctly sized for the TJ's weight \u2014 neither under- nor over-spec","Warn VR EVO 8 delivers brand-name reliability at a meaningful discount vs. the M8000","Synthetic rope (Smittybilt X20) is safer than steel cable in a snap scenario","Self-recovery capability removes dependence on trail partners for getting unstuck","Doubles as an anchor for vehicle-assisted tree pulls and recovery assistance for others"],"cons":["Requires a winch-compatible steel front bumper \u2014 adds to total cost if not already installed","Wiring installation must be done correctly or the winch will underperform or damage the electrical system","Winch adds 60\u201385 lbs to the front axle weight \u2014 affects handling and front suspension loading","Steel cable kinetic snap hazard \u2014 if using a steel-line winch, always drape a recovery blanket over the line during a pull","Winches are not waterproof \u2014 avoid submerging past the motor housing without a waterproofing kit"]},{"id":"tj-drag-link-tj","title":"Drag Link Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":350},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","tie rod separator","metric sockets","torque wrench","tape measure","pliers"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["steering","death-wobble","wandering"],"parts":[{"name":"Moog DS1448 Drag Link Assembly","part_number":"DS1448","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Synergy TJ Heavy Duty Drag Link","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":325},{"name":"RuffStuff Y-Link/Drag Link kit","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Wrangler Torque Specs","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/tj-wrangler-torque-specs.html"},{"title":"Wrangler TJ Forum \u2014 Tie Rod End Torque","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/torque-specs-for-tie-rod-ends.72235/"}],"alternatives":["tj-track-bar-tj","tj-steering-stabilizer-tj"],"summary":"A worn drag link is the most common cause of TJ steering wander and a leading contributor to death wobble. Replacement is a one-evening job with hand tools that restores precise steering feel \u2014 but measure the old link before you remove it, check the thread direction at the pitman end, and get an alignment afterward, because a drag link set to the wrong length puts the steering wheel off-center and the front tires out of toe.","description":"Every 1997\u20132006 TJ uses Y-link steering. The drag link runs from the pitman arm on the steering box down to the passenger-side knuckle, and the tie rod attaches to a tapered hole partway along the drag link rather than directly to the other knuckle. That layout means the drag link carries steering load for both front wheels, which is why play there shows up as wander across the whole front end instead of a single loose wheel.\n\nThe factory tie-rod ends are sealed and not greasable, so they wear on a predictable schedule \u2014 roughly 80,000 to 120,000 miles, sooner on a rig that sees trail duty, salt, or a lift that steepens the drag link angle.\n\nSymptoms build in a recognizable order. First, the steering develops a small dead zone on center and the truck follows road camber. Then bump steer sharpens \u2014 the wheel tugs when one front tire hits a seam. Then the truck starts wandering enough at highway speed that you steer constantly to hold a lane. Left long enough, the play becomes the input that starts a death-wobble cycle over a bump at 40\u201360 mph.\n\nConfirm it before buying. With the front on jack stands and a helper rocking the wheel a few degrees each way, watch each stud: the knuckle should begin turning the instant the pitman arm moves. Any lag, or any visible movement of a stud inside its taper, is a worn joint. Check the [track bar](/db/tj/tj-track-bar-tj/) and the [tie rod](/db/tj/tj-tie-rods-tj/) in the same session \u2014 on a TJ with this mileage they usually wear together, and doing them at once saves a second alignment.\n\nThree paths, set by tire size and how the truck is used.\n\nStock to 31-inch tires, daily driven. A Moog DS1448 assembly at $90\u2013120 is the correct answer. Moog's problem-solver design is greasable, which alone outlasts the sealed original, and it uses the factory geometry so nothing else changes.\n\nLifted on 33s to 35s. A heavy-duty chromoly drag link \u2014 Synergy's TJ unit at around $325 is the common pick \u2014 brings a thicker bar and rebuildable greasable metal-on-metal ends. The real gain is that the ends can be serviced rather than replaced as an assembly, which matters on a rig that gets hosed out after every trip.\n\nDoing the whole linkage. RuffStuff and several others sell Y-link kits that replace the drag link and tie rod together at around $280 and up. If both are worn, buying the pair costs less than two separate jobs and only needs one alignment.\n\nOne thing to avoid: converting to crossover (T-style) steering with mismatched parts. Crossover moves the drag link to the driver-side knuckle and runs the tie rod knuckle-to-knuckle, and it requires high-steer knuckles or a flipped-tie-rod arrangement plus matching pitman geometry. It is the right answer above roughly 5 inches of lift, where the Y-link's drag-link angle has become a bump-steer generator, and it is a kit purchase \u2014 see the [hydro-assist](/db/tj/tj-hydro-assist/) and big-lift discussions for where that threshold sits. Piecing crossover together from Y-link parts produces a steering system nobody engineered.\n\nTwo details catch people. Some TJ years use a left-hand thread at the pitman-arm end of the adjuster; check which way yours turns before you put a pipe on a wrench and damage the sleeve. And measure the installed drag link center-to-center between the two tie-rod-end centers and write the number down \u2014 setting the new link to that length is what keeps the steering wheel centered and the alignment close enough to drive to the shop.\n\nMoog DS1448 assembly $90\u2013120. Synergy heavy-duty chromoly link around $325. Y-link kit covering drag link and tie rod from roughly $280. Add a rented tie rod separator at no cost, two cotter pins, and $80\u2013120 for an alignment. A shop charges $200\u2013350 in labor on top of parts.\n\nIf the truck is past 100,000 miles and the front end has never been touched, price the [tie rod](/db/tj/tj-tie-rods-tj/) and [track bar](/db/tj/tj-track-bar-tj/) at the same time. Doing all three in one evening costs one alignment instead of three and eliminates the guesswork about which worn joint was actually causing the wander.","steps":["Park level, chock the rear wheels, jack the front axle and set it on stands.","Measure the existing drag link center-to-center between the two tie-rod-end centers and record it.","Note the thread direction and orientation at each end before loosening any adjuster or clamp.","Remove the cotter pins and castle nuts from both ends.","Use a tie rod separator to pop the ball studs free of the pitman arm and the knuckle. A pickle fork will destroy the boot \u2014 fine on a part you are discarding, not on one you are reusing.","Thread the new drag link to the recorded length, leaving clamp bolts lightly snug.","Install the new drag link, start both castle nuts by hand, and confirm the studs are seated fully in their tapers.","Torque the tie-rod-end nuts to 55 ft-lb and the tie-rod-to-drag-link connection to 65 ft-lb, then continue tightening only as far as needed to align the cotter pin slot. Never back a castle nut off to reach the slot.","Install fresh cotter pins. Torque the drag link clamp to 36 ft-lb and the tie rod clamp to 20 ft-lb, and position the clamps so they clear the track bar and sway-bar link at full lock.","Grease the new ends if they have zerk fittings, until fresh grease shows at the boot without splitting it.","Lower the truck, turn lock-to-lock and check for interference, then road test at low speed before highway speed.","Get an alignment. Set toe first, then confirm the steering wheel sits centered driving straight; correct centering at the drag link adjuster, not by re-clocking the wheel."],"pros":["Resolves wander, bump steer, and one of the two leading death-wobble contributors","Greasable upgrades outlast the sealed factory assembly by a wide margin","One evening, hand tools, and one rented separator"],"cons":["Sealed OEM-style replacements wear again on the same schedule","Some years use a left-hand thread that catches people off guard","Alignment is required afterward, so budget the shop visit","Replacing the drag link alone on a high-mileage truck often reveals the tie rod is equally worn"]},{"id":"tj-hydro-assist","title":"Hydraulic Assist Steering: Is It Worth It on a TJ?","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","subcategory":"upgrade","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1100,"high":2400},"tools_required":["hydraulic fittings","flaring tools","torque wrench","fluid catch"],"skills_required":["hydraulics","steering safety discipline"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["steering","safety-critical","hydro-assist"],"parts":[{"name":"Hydro-assist kit (ram + ported box or pump)","vendor":"PSC/West Texas Offroad","price_estimate_usd":1400},{"name":"Steering box brace","vendor":"Currie/Synergy","price_estimate_usd":170},{"name":"Steering cooler","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ steering reference \u2014 My Jeep TJ","url":"https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/"},{"title":"TJ factory specs","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-factory-specifications.71/"}],"alternatives":["tj-tie-rods-tj","tj-track-bar-tj","tj-steering-stabilizer-tj"],"summary":"Hydraulic assist is a heavy-tire fix, not a general upgrade. On a TJ running 37s \u2014 or 35s with sticky rubber and a front locker \u2014 hydro-assist takes load off a steering box that was never sized for it and gives you authority against a rock at crawl speed. On 33s and smaller it buys nothing, and bolting it onto a worn front end to cure wander hides the real fault until something breaks.","description":"Hydro-assist adds a hydraulic ram between the axle and the steering linkage, fed by the power-steering system, so hydraulic pressure pushes the tires while the box mostly meters where that pressure goes. The benefit is specific: turning oversized tires against an obstacle at low speed, where the factory Saginaw box builds heat, the sector shaft deflects, and the driver ends up fighting the wheel. It does nothing measurable at highway speed, and it does not make a loose front end tight.\n\nThree pieces have to cooperate. The ram is a double-acting hydraulic cylinder, typically 1.5-inch or 1.75-inch bore with 6\u20138 inches of stroke, with the body clamped to the axle tube and the rod attached to the tie rod or a high-steer arm. The control source is either a ported steering box \u2014 a box machined with two extra ports so it directs flow to the ram in proportion to steering input \u2014 or a full double-ended box. And the supply side is a pump with enough flow and pressure to feed both the box and the ram without starving either.\n\nThe critical detail people skip is the control source. Running a ram off T-fittings spliced into the existing box lines is the cheap version and it produces a system that can push the tires when the driver is not asking, because there is nothing metering return flow. Use a ported box or a purpose-built assist box. PSC and West Texas Offroad both sell TJ-specific ported boxes and full kits; Howe sells the ram and valve hardware separately for people building their own.\n\n37-inch tires and up. A front locker on 35s in rock. A rig that already cooks its power-steering fluid or shows a box that visibly flexes under load. Competition and hard rock use where the driver is steering against a tire that is loaded and not rolling.\n\nThe TJ box is on the small side for any of that, which is why the ram is only half the install. A steering box brace \u2014 Currie, Synergy, and Rusty's all make one for the TJ at roughly $130\u2013200 \u2014 ties the sector shaft snout to the frame rail so the added hydraulic force is not trying to crack the box housing or tear the thin unibody-style frame section at the box mount. Adding a ram without a brace is the single most common way owners turn a steering upgrade into a cracked box.\n\nA cooler is the other non-optional piece. Hydro-assist does more work, more work makes more heat, and hot power-steering fluid foams and loses assist exactly when you need it. A small inline cooler runs $60\u2013120 and mounts in the return line.\n\nOn 33s, a healthy box, a braced sector shaft, and tight linkage steer fine. If the complaint is wander, shimmy, or death wobble, hydro-assist is the wrong purchase \u2014 those are worn-part symptoms, and hydraulics layered over slop hides them until a joint separates.\n\nWork the front end in this order before spending on a ram: the [track bar](/db/tj/tj-track-bar-tj/) at both ends, the [drag link](/db/tj/tj-drag-link-tj/) and [tie rod](/db/tj/tj-tie-rods-tj/) ends, upper and lower ball joints, unit bearings, and the steering box mount bolts and frame section around them. A new [steering stabilizer](/db/tj/tj-steering-stabilizer-tj/) belongs at the end of that list, not the start. If the truck steers straight and tight after that work and still overwhelms the box turning 37s in the rocks, hydro-assist is the correct next step.\n\nRam sizing follows tire size and steering geometry. A 1.5-inch bore is the common choice for 35s and light 37s; 1.75-inch is for 37s and up, heavy front ends, and locked fronts. Oversizing the ram on a light rig makes the steering feel numb and can overpower the linkage.\n\nMounting location decides how much the ram helps and how exposed it is. Clamped to the axle tube with the rod to the tie rod is the standard TJ arrangement and works with the factory Y-link. If the rig is already running high-steer knuckles and a crossover setup, mount the ram to the high-steer arm instead \u2014 the geometry is cleaner and the ram sits higher out of harm's way. Either way, cycle the steering lock to lock by hand with the ram loosely mounted and confirm the rod never bottoms before the box does. A ram that reaches its stroke limit before the box reaches lock will tear its mount off the axle.\n\nPlan for the pump. The factory TJ pump can feed a modest ram on a mild build, but a 1.75-inch ram on 37s wants more flow than it reliably makes when hot. PSC and others sell higher-flow replacement pumps in the $300\u2013500 range; budget for one rather than discovering the shortfall on the trail.\n\nThis system is the steering. Treat every step as safety-critical.\n\nRoute and clamp hose away from exhaust, away from the pitman arm sweep, and away from anything that moves at full articulation. Cycle the suspension through full droop and full stuff with the hoses attached and watch for tension or rub. Use proper hydraulic hose rated well above system pressure with crimped ends \u2014 not push-on fittings, not pipe thread with tape.\n\nBleed the system completely before the first drive. With the front wheels off the ground and the reservoir topped, cycle the steering lock to lock slowly ten or more times, checking level between cycles, until the fluid stops aerating and the wheel stops feeling spongy. Trapped air in a hydro-assist system reads as intermittent assist, which on a steep off-camber line is how people roll rigs.\n\nCheck the ram mount bolts and hose clamps after the first outing and periodically after that. If you are not confident plumbing hydraulics, pay a shop \u2014 a burst line here is a steering failure, not an inconvenience.\n\nA complete TJ hydro-assist kit with ram, ported box, hoses, and mounts runs roughly $1,200\u20131,800 from PSC or West Texas Offroad. Add $130\u2013200 for a box brace, $60\u2013120 for a cooler, and $300\u2013500 if the pump needs upgrading. Shop installation runs $600\u20131,200 depending on whether high-steer and linkage work go in at the same time. Realistic all-in on a TJ: $1,800\u20132,800 DIY, $2,500\u20134,000 with a shop doing the plumbing.\n\nThe honest framing is that hydro-assist is the last item on a big-tire steering list, not the first. A braced box, a straight and tight front end, correct [track bar](/db/tj/tj-track-bar-tj/) geometry for your lift height, and properly sized linkage solve most TJ steering complaints for a quarter of the money. Buy the ram when the rig has outgrown all of that."},{"id":"tj-steering-stabilizer-tj","title":"Steering Stabilizer \u2014 When It Helps and When It Masks","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":110},"tools_required":["pitman arm puller","metric sockets","torque wrench","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["death-wobble","diagnostic","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Rancho RS5000 Steering Stabilizer","part_number":"RS5407","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 Steering Stabilizer","part_number":"24-065238","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"OME Steering Stabilizer","vendor":"Old Man Emu","price_estimate_usd":95}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ steering stabilizer overview","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/models/jeep-wrangler/jeep-wrangler-tj/steering-stabilizer/"},{"title":"Why a stabilizer doesn't fix death wobble","url":"https://www.torquenews.com/4316/fixing-death-wobble-in-jeep-steering-wheel-not-fix"},{"title":"Diagnosing death wobble and non-DW shimmies (TJ)","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/diagnosing-death-wobble-and-fixing-non-dw-shimmies-and-wobbles-tj-version.78090/"}],"alternatives":["tj-tie-rods-tj","tj-track-bar-tj"],"summary":"A steering stabilizer is a damper, not a repair. Replace a worn one for normal steering smoothness on washboard and at highway speed \u2014 but if a new damper is the only thing keeping your wheel calm, you have a worn track bar, tie rod, or ball joint hiding behind it, and the damper will stop hiding it at the worst possible moment.","description":"[The TJ stabilizer is a horizontal shock absorber](https://jeepspecs.com/models/jeep-wrangler/jeep-wrangler-tj/steering-stabilizer/) bolted between a bracket on the axle and a tapered stud in the drag link. Its entire job is damping small, high-frequency oscillations \u2014 tire imbalance, road texture, bump steer over corrugation. It has no ability to hold the front end in alignment, and it is not a death-wobble device. The distinction matters because the stabilizer is the cheapest part in the steering system, which makes it the first thing people replace and the wrong thing to replace first.\n\nFour symptoms point at the damper itself. The wheel kicks sharply in your hands over a speed bump or expansion joint, instead of the axle absorbing it. A pothole produces a brief shimmy that settles on its own within a second or two. Oil streaks run down the damper body from the seal. And with the front end on stands and the damper unbolted at the drag-link end, the rod pushes and pulls through its stroke with little or no resistance \u2014 a healthy unit feels like a firm, even shock in both directions with no dead spot.\n\nFactory stabilizers on a 1997\u20132006 TJ typically fade somewhere between 60,000 and 100,000 miles, sooner on a rig that sees trail duty or road salt. Age matters more than mileage on the seals.\n\nDeath wobble on a TJ is a violent, self-sustaining oscillation of the whole front axle, triggered by a bump at 40\u201360 mph and usually only stopped by slowing down hard. [It comes from play somewhere in the axle-to-frame linkage](https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/diagnosing-death-wobble-and-fixing-non-dw-shimmies-and-wobbles-tj-version.78090/) \u2014 most commonly [track bar](/db/tj/tj-track-bar-tj/) bushings or a loose track-bar bolt, then [tie rod](/db/tj/tj-tie-rods-tj/) and [drag link](/db/tj/tj-drag-link-tj/) ends, then ball joints, then a loose steering box or cracked box mount, then unbalanced or out-of-round tires.\n\n[A fresh stabilizer can raise the speed at which wobble triggers](https://www.torquenews.com/4316/fixing-death-wobble-in-jeep-steering-wheel-not-fix), which reads to the driver as a fix. It is not. The worn joint is still worn, the damper is now the only thing suppressing the oscillation, and the wobble comes back harder when the damper fades. Every serious TJ front-end write-up lands in the same place: diagnose the linkage first, replace the damper last.\n\nSpend twenty minutes with the front end on jack stands and a helper.\n\nHave the helper rock the steering wheel a few degrees back and forth while you watch each joint in turn. Any visible movement at a tie-rod or drag-link stud before the knuckle moves is a worn end. Grab the track bar at the axle-side bracket and push and pull hard \u2014 any side-to-side motion means a shot bushing, an under-torqued bolt, or a cracked bracket, and a cracked bracket is common enough on a lifted TJ to be worth a flashlight check. Grip each front tire at 12 and 6 o'clock and rock it: play there is ball joints or a unit bearing. Watch the steering box while the wheel is turned: visible movement of the box relative to the frame rail means loose mount bolts or a cracked frame section.\n\nFix anything you find. Then, and only then, replace the stabilizer.\n\nThe Rancho RS5000 RS5407 is the budget replacement at around $45 and matches the OEM form factor. The Bilstein 5100 (24-065238, about $75) uses monotube construction and gives firmer, more consistent damping with better fade resistance. Old Man Emu and AEV units run $90\u2013110 and hold up longest on a daily-driven TJ.\n\nSkip the no-name dampers and skip dual-stabilizer kits. A dual setup is marketed as a wobble cure and functions as a bigger blanket over the same worn part \u2014 it also loads the drag link at a point the factory never designed for two dampers. If the rig runs 35s or larger with heavy steering effort, put the money into the linkage and a steering box brace rather than a second damper.\n\nBudget damper $40\u201350, mid-tier monotube $70\u201380, premium $90\u2013110. Add a rented pitman arm puller at no cost and one cotter pin. Total under $120 and an evening. For comparison, the parts this damper gets blamed for run $90\u2013350 for a drag link, $110\u2013300 for a track bar, and $60\u2013140 for a pair of ball joints \u2014 which is the whole reason to diagnose before reaching for the cheap part.","steps":["Spray penetrating oil on the stabilizer-to-drag-link stud and the bracket bolt; let it sit 10 minutes.","Loosen the nut on the tapered stud but leave it on by a few threads so the stud cannot fly when it releases.","Use a pitman arm puller (rentable at no charge from most parts stores) to break the tapered stud loose from the drag link.","Remove the nut, then unbolt the stabilizer body from the axle bracket.","Compare old and new units side by side for length and end type before installing.","Install the new stabilizer body to the axle bracket first, leaving the bolt snug but not final-torqued.","Set the new stabilizer at mid-stroke with the wheels pointed straight ahead before tightening the drag link end \u2014 a damper installed at the end of its travel will bind or tear its mount at full lock.","Torque the drag link nut to 35 ft-lb and install a fresh cotter pin if the nut is castellated.","Bounce the front of the vehicle, then turn the wheel lock-to-lock and verify the damper never bottoms and nothing contacts the track bar or sway-bar link.","Road test at low speed, then at highway speed on a road you know."],"pros":["Smooths small high-frequency steering inputs and reduces fatigue on washboard","Inexpensive and quick \u2014 30 to 60 minutes with one rented tool","Removing it for the bench test also gives you a free look at the drag link and track bar"],"cons":["Does not fix death wobble or any worn front-end part","Can mask a failing joint until it separates","Replacing a healthy damper changes nothing about steering accuracy or wander","Dual-stabilizer kits add load to the drag link for no diagnostic benefit"]},{"id":"tj-tie-rods-tj","title":"Tie Rod Replacement \u2014 Stock and HD Options","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","subcategory":"tie_rod","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":700},"tools_required":["pickle fork or tie rod separator","torque wrench","metric sockets","vise grips","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding","thread chasing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["tie-rod","steering","death-wobble","Y-link","currectlync"],"parts":[{"name":"Moog stock-replacement inner tie rod end (driver)","part_number":"ES3490T","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Moog stock-replacement outer tie rod end (passenger)","part_number":"ES3491T","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"RockJock Currectlync HD tie rod kit (TJ/LJ/XJ/MJ/ZJ)","part_number":"CE-9701","vendor":"RockJock 4x4","price_estimate_usd":600},{"name":"Steer Smarts Yeti XD tie rod","part_number":"75070103","vendor":"Steer Smarts","price_estimate_usd":650}],"citations":[{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 RockJock CE-9701 Currectlync HD tie rod kit (84\u201306 TJ/XJ)","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/products/56116_200_07.htm"},{"title":"RockJock 4x4 \u2014 CE-9701TR Currectlync Tie Rod (Complete)","url":"https://www.rockjock4x4.com/CE-9701TR"},{"title":"JeepSpecs \u2014 Currie Heavy Duty Steering on the TJ","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/models/jeep-wrangler/jeep-wrangler-tj/currie-heavy-duty-steering/"}],"alternatives":["tj-drag-link-tj","tj-steering-stabilizer-tj"],"summary":"Stock TJ tie rod ends wear out around 80\u2013120k miles and are a common death-wobble trigger. Replacing them with Moog problem-solver ends ($75\u2013$100 for the pair) restores stock behavior. Heavy-duty kits like [the RockJock Currectlync](https://www.quadratec.com/products/56116_200_07.htm) ($600) swap the bent factory rod for a straight 1\u00bc\" solid bar \u2014 worth it if you run 33\"+ tires on rocks, overkill on a daily driver.","description":"The TJ's factory tie rod is a bent steel tube running behind the front axle. Two threaded ends (driver inner, passenger outer) thread into the rod with jam nuts and connect to the steering knuckles via tapered studs. The bent shape was a packaging compromise \u2014 it clears the differential but bends easily under impact, and the ends wear at predictable mileage.\n\n**When to replace.** Slop in the tie rod ends shows up as on-center wander, lazy returning-to-center, and at worst, death wobble. Pull the front wheels off the ground, grip each tire at 9 and 3 o'clock, and rock it. Any clunk that doesn't show in the wheel bearing or ball joints is a tie rod end. Visible boot tears mean the joint is contaminated regardless of feel \u2014 replace it.\n\n**Stock-replacement vs. heavy-duty.** Moog problem-solver ends (ES3490T driver, ES3491T passenger) install in the existing rod, take about an hour, and bring the truck back to factory feel. About $80 for the pair. For a daily-driver TJ on 31\"\u201333\" tires this is the right call.\n\n**The Currectlync upgrade.** The RockJock Currectlync (part CE-9701, fits TJ/LJ/XJ/MJ/ZJ) [replaces the rod itself with a 1\u00bc\" solid alloy bar](https://www.rockjock4x4.com/CE-9701TR) \u2014 straight, not bent \u2014 rated about 200% stronger than stock. It uses 7/8\" tie rod ends with included boots. The kit is a direct bolt-in on stock-height to 4\"-lifted trucks; over 4\" lift you need a 2\" dropped pitman arm to keep the drag link geometry correct. Steer Smarts Yeti XD is the other top-tier option, similar concept, slightly different end design.\n\n**Why HD is worth it on a wheeler.** A bent stock tie rod becomes a bent stock tie rod the first time you rock-strike it. A straight 1\u00bc\" solid bar takes the same hit and shrugs. If your TJ sees real rocks, the upgrade pays for itself the first time you avoid trailering home.\n\n**Set toe correctly.** Whatever rod you install, set toe before the alignment shop sees it. Target 1/16\" to 1/8\" toe-in with the wheels straight ahead. Measure between tire tread centers at the front and rear of the tire \u2014 the front measurement should be 1/16\" to 1/8\" smaller. Loosen jam nuts, twist the rod to adjust, retighten to 55 ft-lb. Then take it for an alignment to confirm camber and caster.","steps":["Park on level concrete. Loosen front lug nuts. Lift the front and set on jack stands. Pull both front wheels.","Diagnose \u2014 rock each tire 9/3, look for clunk independent of wheel bearing.","With ignition on and helper turning the wheel, watch each tie rod end joint for independent movement.","Measure existing toe between tire centers, front and rear of tire, to know where to set the new rod.","Loosen jam nuts at each end of the tie rod. Note approximate thread engagement.","Remove cotter pins and castle nuts at the knuckle. Use a tie rod separator (not a pickle fork \u2014 pickle fork damages the boot of any part you intend to reuse).","Unthread the rod from both ends or pull the entire assembly and transfer to new HD kit.","Install new ends or new HD rod. Snug to length matching the original measurement.","Set toe to 1/16\"\u20131/8\" toe-in.","Torque castle nuts (40 ft-lb, then tighten to align cotter pin slot \u2014 never back off). Install new cotter pins.","Torque jam nuts to 55 ft-lb.","Road test. Then alignment shop within a week."],"pros":["Restores precise steering feel","Eliminates a common death-wobble trigger","HD kit takes rock impacts that bend stock","Bolt-in with hand tools"],"cons":["HD kit is $600+","Over 4\" lift requires a dropped pitman arm to keep drag-link geometry","Pickle fork removal damages boots \u2014 use a proper separator","Always follow with an alignment to nail camber and caster"]},{"id":"tj-track-bar-tj","title":"Track Bar Replacement \u2014 TJ Specifics","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","21mm socket","18mm socket","breaker bar","torque wrench","tape measure","pry bar (for the play test)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","measurement"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["death-wobble","axle-centering","steering-geometry"],"parts":[{"name":"Synergy TJ/LJ adjustable front track bar","part_number":"8520-01","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Currie RockJock Johnny Joint adjustable track bar","part_number":"CE-9120TJJ","vendor":"Currie Enterprises","price_estimate_usd":330},{"name":"Rough Country adjustable front track bar","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Mopar OEM replacement","part_number":"52088433AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"JKS track bar relocation bracket kit \u2014 front","part_number":"JKS950","vendor":"JKS Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"Synergy TJ Adjustable Front Track Bar","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com/synergy-jeep-tjlj-xj-zj-mj-adjustable-front-track-bar.html"},{"title":"RockJock TJ Trac Bars","url":"https://www.rockjock4x4.com/products/shop-by-vehicle/jeep-tj/jeep-tj-trac-bars"},{"title":"TJ Front Track Bar Torque","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/front-track-bar-torque-specs.12653/"},{"title":"JKS Track Bar Relocation Bracket \u2014 TJ","url":"https://www.jksmfg.com/collections/tj-wrangler/products/jks950"}],"alternatives":["tj-tie-rods-tj","tj-hydro-assist","dana30-tj","tj-front-coil-spring"],"summary":"The TJ front track bar locates your axle side-to-side; if you've added a lift over 2\" or you feel highway wander, replace it with an adjustable HD bar to re-center the axle and stop death wobble.","description":"The TJ front track bar uses a tapered-stud mount on the frame side and a bolt-through bracket on the axle side. The factory bar is fine at stock height, but when you lift the front the axle shifts toward the driver side because the bar pulls it that way through the lift arc. After a 2\"\u20134\" lift, an adjustable bar is required if you want the steering to feel right.\n\nThe TJ has one quirk worth knowing: the factory frame-side mount uses a tapered hole. Some aftermarket bars (Synergy, RockJock) keep the tapered fitment; others (cheaper bars) convert it to a straight through-bolt and require drilling the axle bracket to 9/16\". The drilled-out conversion is permanent. If you want to keep the option of reverting to a stock bar, choose a bar that preserves the taper.\n\nBrand picks: [Synergy 8520-01](https://www.synergymfg.com/synergy-jeep-tjlj-xj-zj-mj-adjustable-front-track-bar.html) ($360) \u2014 dual-durometer bushings, Teflon bearing, maintenance-free, quiet ride. RockJock CE-9120TJJ ($330) \u2014 4130 chromoly tube with rebuildable Johnny Joints, the choice for hard wheeling. Rough Country adjustable ($160) \u2014 budget option, urethane bushings, fine for daily-driver lifts under 3\". For stock-height TJs replacing a worn factory bar, OEM Mopar 52088433AB ($145) is a direct swap.\n\nSymptoms that you need a new track bar: steering wheel off-center on a straight road, axle visibly off-center between the wheel wells, clunking over bumps from worn bushings, or active death wobble after hitting a bump at 45+ mph. Death wobble has multiple causes (worn ball joints, loose tie rod, bent steering link), but a worn track bar is the single most common trigger.\n\nDiagnosing the track bar before you buy a new one saves a wasted purchase. With the front on stands and the axle supported (tension off the bar), grab the bar at the axle end and pry it up and down with a large flat bar or breaker bar. Factory rubber bushings should show only a small amount of flex \u2014 anything that feels like a hinge, or a bar that clunks when you release the pry bar, is worn. Also check for play at the frame-side tapered stud by having a helper turn the steering wheel lock to lock while you watch the stud for movement in the mount \u2014 a loose taper will show visible wobble there, and that's a different failure than bushing wear, since a loose taper means the taper itself has worn oversize and no amount of bushing replacement fixes it.\n\nLifts over about 4\" change the geometry enough that a straight-length adjustable bar isn't the whole fix. As the axle drops on a longer spring or coil spacer, the factory frame-side mounting point stays at its stock height, so the bar now runs at a steeper downward angle than the axle-side mounting bracket was designed for. That angle mismatch binds the bushings at full droop and can put the bar into a bind before the suspension even reaches its travel limit. [A track bar relocation bracket (JKS950 and similar)](https://www.jksmfg.com/collections/tj-wrangler/products/jks950) raises the frame-side mount to restore a closer-to-stock bar angle. Below 4\" of lift this is optional; above it, treat the relocation bracket as part of the track bar job, not a separate upgrade \u2014 the bar you buy is the wrong length for the geometry without it.\n\nFinal torque happens at ride height. The factory spec is [74 ft-lbs at the frame side and 55 ft-lbs](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/front-track-bar-torque-specs.12653/) at the axle bracket \u2014 these are lower than JK numbers because the TJ uses smaller hardware. Aftermarket bars with larger bolts will specify their own torque, often 100\u2013125 ft-lbs. Always follow the bar manufacturer's torque spec for the supplied hardware.\n\nLength adjustment on an adjustable bar happens through a threaded sleeve at the center of the tube, locked with a jam nut on each side. Set the starting length to match the old bar (or, on a fresh lift, to your measured centering numbers below) before you torque anything, then fine-tune once the Jeep is back on the ground. Don't skip the jam nuts \u2014 a track bar that backs out of adjustment under load is a steering failure waiting to happen, not a minor annoyance.\n\nA track bar alone will not cure death wobble if other steering components are also worn. Before condemning the track bar, rule out the tie rod ends, drag link ends, steering stabilizer, and ball joints \u2014 a worn part in any of those will reintroduce the wobble even with a brand-new track bar installed.","steps":["Park on level ground, set parking brake, chock rear wheels.","Loosen front lug nuts, lift front, set on jack stands under frame, remove wheels.","Measure frame rail to inner tire edge on both sides \u2014 record.","Support front axle on floor jack to take tension off the track bar.","Remove the axle-side bolt first (typically 18mm head).","Remove the frame-side bolt (21mm) \u2014 note tapered stud fitment if applicable.","Compare new bar to old at equal length as starting point.","Install frame side first, then axle side, finger-tight.","Reinstall wheels, lower to ride height.","Torque to manufacturer spec at ride height.","Re-measure axle centering \u2014 adjust bar length if off by more than 1/8\".","Tighten the jam nuts against the adjustment sleeve once centering is correct.","Drive 50 miles, re-check all torque."],"pros":["Cures TJ death wobble from worn factory bar","Adjustable bar re-centers axle after lift","HD bars resist bending on rocks","Bolt-in install if you choose a bar that keeps the tapered mount"],"cons":["Some bars require drilling the axle bracket \u2014 irreversible","Tapered-stud mount can seize and be hard to remove","Torque-at-ride-height step is critical or bushings wear early","Bar replacement alone won't fix death wobble if ball joints or tie rod are also worn","Lifts over ~4\" need a relocation bracket too, or the bar runs at a bind-inducing angle"]},{"id":"tj-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit \u2014 Coil Spacers and Entry Springs (\u2264$600)","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":129,"high":1100},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","suspension work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["lift","suspension","budget","coil-spacer","entry-level"],"parts":[{"name":"Daystar 2\" Coil Spacers KJ09123BK (front and rear, polyurethane)","vendor":"Daystar Products","price_estimate_usd":129},{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Budget Lift Kit TJ 674","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Skyjacker 3.5\" Suspension Lift Kit with Hydro Shocks TJ35K","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":599}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Budget Lift Discussion","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/budget-lift-tj-wrangler/"},{"title":"Rough Country TJ 2-Inch Lift Installation Guide","url":"https://roughcountry.com/installation-instructions"}],"alternatives":["tj-mid-lift","tj-shocks-upgrade"],"summary":"A budget lift (coil spacers or an entry spring kit) is a sound way to fit 31-inch tires and raise a TJ about 2 inches, but the sticker price is not the real price. Plan $450 to $1,000 once you add matched shocks, a track bar, and an alignment, because the cheapest kits skip all three.","description":"The TJ's coil-spring front and rear suspension makes entry lifts less invasive than on leaf-sprung Jeeps. Because the springs are held in their seats by axle droop and not by a compressor, a 2-inch spacer goes in during a Saturday afternoon with a floor jack and basic hand tools. Expect the Jeep to ride about 2 inches taller, clear 31x10.50R15 tires, and look more level once the spacers go on. What you do not get is more suspension travel, a corrected steering geometry, or better shock performance.\n\n**Spacers versus replacement springs.** A polyurethane coil spacer such as the Daystar 2-inch set in the parts list sits between the spring and its perch. It keeps the factory spring rate and the factory ride, which is fine if your springs are healthy. It also leaves the stock shocks in place, and a stock shock at 2 inches higher ride height runs closer to its fully extended length, which limits how far the axle can droop before the shock stops it. A replacement coil kit such as [the Rough Country 2-inch kit](https://roughcountry.com/installation-instructions) changes the spring itself, usually with a softer rate, and lets you tune front and rear height separately. Entry springs are the more complete answer; spacers are the cheapest one. At the top of this price range, the Skyjacker 3.5-inch kit adds more lift, and its included track-bar drop bracket matters because geometry errors scale with lift. Treat the included hydraulic shocks as entry-grade and plan to replace them before serious trail use (see the [shock upgrade guide](/db/tj/tj-shocks-upgrade/)).\n\n**Geometry at 2 inches.** Raising the body 2 inches moves the track bar and drag link away from their designed angles. The front axle shifts slightly sideways under suspension travel, and the steering linkage creates mild bump steer. Both effects are small at 2 inches and visible at 3. Symptoms are a steering wheel that is off-center after the install, vague feel at highway speed, and wandering over expansion joints. The corrective parts are an adjustable front track bar ($150 to $360, see the [track bar guide](/db/tj/tj-track-bar-tj/)) and, on higher kits, a drop bracket. If the Jeep has any history of death wobble, solve that first; a lift will not improve it.\n\n**Other things a lift changes.** Check these before you decide the Jeep is done.\n\n- **Sway bar end links:** The stock links become too short at full droop. Disconnecting the sway bar for trail use is common; for a daily driver, longer end links fix it.\n- **Brake hoses and the axle vent hose:** The factory hoses have enough slack for about 2 inches, but check at full droop with the Jeep on stands so nothing tightens before the axle stops.\n- **Bump stops:** A taller body leaves the bump stops farther from the axle, which allows more flex before contact. If the tires rub, extend them rather than trimming first.\n- **Driveshaft angle:** Two inches is within the tolerance of the factory shafts at the front. At about 2.5 inches and above, the rear shaft angle at the NP231 slip yoke begins to cause vibration, and the transfer case becomes the next item; the [NP231 guide](/db/tj/tj-np231-tj/) explains the options.\n- **Alignment:** Camber, caster, and toe shift. Budget $100 to $150 for a front alignment, and have the shop check the thrust angle.\n\n**Tire expectations.** A 2-inch lift runs 31x10.50R15 tires comfortably. A 32-inch tire will often rub at full flex and may need trimming or extended bump stops. A 33-inch tire needs more lift than this guide covers; see the [mid-lift guide](/db/tj/tj-mid-lift/). Do not pick the lift to justify the tire; pick the tire for your terrain and buy the lift that fits.\n\n**The real cost.** Spacers ($129) plus quality shocks ($200 to $450 for the set) plus an adjustable track bar ($150 to $360) plus alignment ($100 to $150) land between $580 and $1,100. An entry spring kit ($189) plus the same items sits in the same range. If your budget cannot cover that, install the spacers and keep the stock shocks only while the shocks are healthy, drive moderately, and add the track bar next. That order protects steering feel first.\n\n**Costs and sources.** Daystar spacers: about $129. Rough Country 2-inch kit: about $189. Skyjacker 3.5-inch kit with shocks: about $599. Matched shocks: $200 to $450. Adjustable track bar: $150 to $360. Alignment: $100 to $150. All-in: $450 to $1,100 depending on how much of the missing hardware you add. Compare against the [lift options guide](/db/tj/tj-lift-options/) before you commit, because moving from a budget kit to a 3 to 4 inch kit later means paying for the spacers twice.","steps":["Loosen the front lug nuts, raise the Jeep, and set it on jack stands under the frame rails, never under the axle you are working on. Remove the wheels.","Support the front axle with a floor jack. Disconnect the sway bar end links, the lower shock bolts, and the track bar at the axle bracket. Photograph or note the original track bar bolt orientation.","Lower the axle slowly with the jack until the coil springs are loose in their seats. Watch the brake hoses and vent hose while you do this. Do not use a spring compressor; the springs are free once the axle drops.","Remove the springs. Fit the spacer onto the spring perch, or install the replacement spring, making sure the spring seats in the isolator and the lower perch.","Raise the axle until the springs are held, then reconnect the shocks, the sway bar end links, and the track bar. Leave suspension bolts finger-tight.","Repeat at the rear: support the axle, disconnect the shocks and sway bar links, lower the axle, fit the spacer or spring, and reassemble.","Reinstall the wheels and torque the lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs in a star pattern, then lower the Jeep onto the ground.","With the Jeep at ride height and bounced to settle, torque the control arm and track bar bolts to the figures in the service manual or the kit instructions. Tighten rubber bushings only at ride height, or they will be preloaded and wear early.","Re-check front and rear for clearance by turning the wheel lock to lock and having a helper compress each corner.","Drive 100 miles, re-torque every suspension fastener, then get the alignment."],"pros":["The lowest price of entry into the lift world, and reversible if you change your mind.","Fits 31-inch tires without modifying fenders.","Installs with ordinary hand tools and a floor jack, with no spring compressor.","The factory ride is nearly retained with spacers, which suits a daily driver."],"cons":["No travel gain: spacers leave the stock shocks and limit droop.","Geometry is not corrected on cheap kits, so you pay in bump steer and a track bar you must add later.","At 2.5 inches and above, driveline vibration at the NP231 slip yoke becomes your next expense.","Entry-grade hydraulic shocks packaged with some kits are not trail-ready."]},{"id":"tj-front-coil-spring","title":"TJ Front Coil Spring Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","13mm socket","15mm socket","18mm socket","T55 Torx","torque wrench","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","suspension disassembly"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["coils","lift","ride-quality"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front coil TJ","part_number":"52087789","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":105},{"name":"Old Man Emu front coil 2\" lift (heavy)","part_number":"2937","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Currie 3\" front coil pair","vendor":"Currie","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Rubicon Express 3.5\" front coil set","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Steps for replacing front coil springs \u2014 TJ Forum","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/steps-for-replacing-front-coil-springs.13739/"},{"title":"2003 Jeep TJ Torque Specifications \u2014 Wandering Trail","url":"http://www.wanderingtrail.com/Mechanical/TJLJ/2003_Jeep_Torques.htm"},{"title":"Stock Spring Swap Walk-Thru \u2014 TJ Forum","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/stock-spring-swap-walk-thru-with-pics.12655/"}],"alternatives":["tj-rear-leaf-replacement","tj-shock-replacement","tj-lift-options"],"summary":"Front TJ coils come out the top of the spring perch once you drop the axle and disconnect the track bar \u2014 [no spring compressor needed](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/steps-for-replacing-front-coil-springs.13739/) for stock or up to 3.5\" coils.","description":"The TJ uses a coil-sprung four-link front suspension that descends directly from the XJ, but with shorter coil pockets and a panhard (track) bar that has to come loose before the axle can drop far enough to free the springs. Almost every TJ coil swap turns into a half-hour fight at the track bar because the bolt is captured in a tight bracket and the bushing is corroded onto the sleeve. Spray it with penetrant the night before and the job goes faster.\n\nPricing splits cleanly: OEM Mopar coils are around $100\u2013$120 each, used to refresh a sagging stock rig. Aftermarket 2\"\u20133\" lift coils run $200\u2013$350 a pair. Old Man Emu 2937 heavy-rate fronts are the daily-driver favorite for owners running a winch or steel front bumper; Currie and Rubicon Express are the mid-tier choices; Rock Krawler and Clayton sit above $400 with progressive-wound springs. For 4\"+ lifts you'd be doing this swap as part of a full kit with longer shocks and adjustable control arms.\n\nThe TJ's geometry gets touchy past 3.5\" because the factory control arms are short and the panhard angle steepens as the axle drops. Going beyond 3\" of coil lift without adjustable arms will leave the axle pulled to the passenger side and the caster down around 1\u00b0\u20132\u00b0 (factory spec is 7\u00b0). You'll feel the wandering on the freeway before you make it home from the install. Either keep the lift at 3\" or budget for adjustable control arms and a longer or adjustable track bar in the same project.\n\nDrop the axle slowly. The coils are captive in the lower spring pocket by a retainer clip but the upper pocket is an isolator. Once the axle drops about 5 inches the coil tension is gone and the spring will sit loose in the pocket. Don't let it fall out and bend the lower isolator. After install, the bumpstops should sit roughly 1\" off the axle pad with the rig on the ground \u2014 if they're touching, the coils are sagging or the wrong rate. If there's a 3\"+ gap, you may need bumpstop extensions to prevent the shock bottoming out before the bumpstop does.\n\nTorque specs that matter on this job: front shock at axle 21 ft-lb (this is correct, not a typo \u2014 it's a small bolt), spring retainer clip 16 ft-lb, sway bar end link 75 ft-lb, track bar at frame 40 ft-lb, track bar at axle 125 ft-lb, lower control arm at axle 130 ft-lb, lower control arm at frame 115 ft-lb.","steps":["Loosen front lug nuts. Spray track bar bolt and sway bar end link bolt with penetrant.","Lift the front of the Jeep, support on stands under the frame behind the front axle.","Remove front wheels. Place a floor jack under the differential.","Disconnect one sway bar end link at the axle (18mm, T55). One side is enough \u2014 keeps the axle from twisting.","Disconnect the track bar at the axle bracket (21mm).","Unbolt both front shocks at the axle (13mm long bolt, 10mm box end on shaft).","Slowly lower the floor jack to drop the axle.","Once coils lose tension (~5\" of drop), remove the spring retainer clip at the lower seat (13mm).","Tilt the coil and pull it out the top.","Install the new coil \u2014 seat it in the upper isolator and lower spring pad.","Reinstall the retainer clip (16 ft-lb).","Jack the axle back up, reconnect shocks, track bar, sway bar end link. Snug only.","Lower the Jeep to the ground.","Final-torque: shocks 21 ft-lb, track bar 125 ft-lb at axle and 40 ft-lb at frame, sway bar end link 75 ft-lb.","Get an alignment. Caster target: 5\u00b0\u20137\u00b0."],"pros":["Stock or 3\"-or-less coils swap with no spring compressor","Bolt-in with hand tools","Wide aftermarket \u2014 heavy-rate fronts available for built-up rigs","Doesn't require pulling brake lines"],"cons":["Track bar bolt is the bottleneck \u2014 penetrant the night before","3.5\"+ lifts pull caster out of spec; need adjustable arms","Bumpstop height after install needs to be checked","Always get an alignment after \u2014 caster shift is unavoidable"]},{"id":"tj-lift-options","title":"TJ Lift Kit Options \u2014 Coil vs. Long Arm","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":3500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","spring compressor (or coil pull method)","metric sockets","torque wrench","welder (long arm only)"],"skills_required":["suspension R&R","alignment basics","welding (long arm only)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["lift","geometry","build-planning"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" budget boost coil spacer kit","part_number":"65530","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Old Man Emu 2.5\" coil and shock kit","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":1200},{"name":"Rubicon Express 3.5\" Super-Flex short arm kit","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":1400},{"name":"Clayton Off Road long arm upgrade kit (4.5\")","vendor":"Clayton Off Road","price_estimate_usd":2800},{"name":"Metalcloak 3.5\" Game-Changer short arm kit","vendor":"Metalcloak","price_estimate_usd":1800}],"citations":[{"title":"Choosing the Right Jeep Lift Kit: Long vs. Short Arm Lift Kits","url":"https://dixie4wheeldrive.com/choosing_right_jeep_lift_kit_long_vs_short_arm_lift_kits/"},{"title":"Long arm vs adjustable short arm worth the $$$?","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/long-arm-vs-adjustable-short-arm-worth-the.334/"},{"title":"Metalcloak TJ Long Travel Short Arm Lift Kit","url":"https://metalcloak.com/tj-long-travel-short-arm-suspension-system-lift-kit.html"}],"alternatives":["tj-short-arm-vs-long-arm","tj-front-coil-spring"],"summary":"Up to 3.5 inches of lift on a TJ, [stay with a short arm kit and adjustable arms](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/long-arm-vs-adjustable-short-arm-worth-the.334/) \u2014 it's cheaper, bolts in, and rides fine. At 4 inches and beyond, [the stock control arm angles get steep enough](https://dixie4wheeldrive.com/choosing_right_jeep_lift_kit_long_vs_short_arm_lift_kits/) that a long arm upgrade is worth the welding and the money.","description":"The TJ runs a coil-sprung suspension at all four corners, which makes lift kits cleaner than the leaf-sprung XJ. The catch is the short factory control arms: when you lift the body, the axle pivots forward through an arc, caster drops, and the driveshaft angles steepen. Coil spacers ignore all of that and push the body up. New taller coils with matched shocks address ride height correctly. Adjustable control arms fix axle position and caster. Long arms fix the geometry by moving the control arm pivot rearward, which flattens the arm angle and brings the suspension closer to factory geometry at full lift height.\n\nTiers worth knowing about: a budget coil spacer kit from Rough Country runs about $250 and gives 2 to 2.5 inches \u2014 fine for a daily driver clearing 31s, terrible if you want to actually flex on a trail. A mid-range new-coil kit (Old Man Emu Heavy 2.5\", Rubicon Express Super-Flex 3.5\", [Metalcloak Game-Changer](https://metalcloak.com/tj-long-travel-short-arm-suspension-system-lift-kit.html) 3.5\") sits in the $1,200 to $1,800 range with shocks and adjustable arms \u2014 this is the sweet spot for the vast majority of TJ builds. A long arm upgrade from Clayton, Savvy, or Currie runs $2,500 to $3,500 plus install, requires welding new frame brackets onto the unibody, and only makes sense if you're building for 35-inch tires, 4.5+ inches of lift, or daily rock crawling. Rough Country's long arm kits are cheaper but cut corners on bushings and brackets \u2014 many TJ owners report squeaks and premature wear.\n\nThe single most important add-on at any lift height is the slip yoke eliminator (SYE) plus a CV rear driveshaft. At 3 inches and up, the stock NP231 transfer case output is at an angle the slip yoke can't handle, and you get vibration that no amount of u-joint cycling will fix. Budget another $700 to $1,000 for the SYE and driveshaft on top of the lift itself. A 3.5-inch short arm kit, slip yoke eliminator, adjustable track bars, and an alignment is the most common \"complete\" mid-range TJ build.\n\nDon't lift higher than you need. Each inch of lift pushes the center of gravity up, hurts on-road manners, and forces another bracket of compromises (adjustable track bar, longer brake lines, longer sway bar end links, possibly re-gearing). A 3-inch TJ on 33s is a more capable trail rig than a 6-inch TJ on 35s for most people.","steps":["Decide tire size first. Lift exists to clear the tires you want \u2014 not the other way around.","Pick a lift height: 2 to 2.5\" clears 31s and 32s, 3 to 3.5\" clears 33s, 4\"+ for 35s.","At 3\" and up, plan for: adjustable track bars (or relocation brackets), SYE and CV rear driveshaft, longer brake lines, longer sway bar links.","Pick coil spring rates appropriate to your weight \u2014 front winch bumper and rear tire carrier require heavy springs.","Install kit per manufacturer instructions. Torque all suspension bolts at ride height, not at droop.","Get a four-wheel alignment after install. Caster should land between 4 and 6 degrees.","Re-check torque after the first 100 miles on the trail."],"pros":["Coil suspension means lifts are usually bolt-in up to 3.5\"","Mid-range short arm kits give 95% of the long arm benefit at half the cost","TJ aftermarket support is deep \u2014 every brand makes a kit","Adjustable arms recover caster without welding"],"cons":["Cheap coil spacer kits ride harshly and don't add wheel travel","Long arm kits require welding and frame fabrication","Any lift over 3\" needs SYE plus CV driveshaft, adding $700\u2013$1,000","Lift adds rollover risk if you stack lift on lift on tires"]},{"id":"tj-long-arm-lift","title":"Long-Arm Lift System \u2014 4.5\"+ for Rock Crawling ($2,000+)","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":16,"high":24},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":3500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","angle grinder","welder (for some kits)","drill press","alignment tool","pry bar"],"skills_required":["suspension work","fabrication basics","torque specification knowledge","welding (kit-dependent)"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["lift","suspension","long-arm","articulation","rock-crawler","geometry"],"parts":[{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad 4.5\" Long-Arm Lift System TJ (bolt-on, LCG skid-mount)","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":2199},{"name":"Rock Krawler Max Travel 3.5\" 3-Link Front Suspension TJ","vendor":"Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":2499},{"name":"Rubicon Express 4.5\" Super-Flex LCG Long-Arm Kit TJ RE7130","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":2899}],"citations":[{"title":"Pirate4x4 \u2014 TJ Long-Arm vs Short-Arm Suspension Build Thread","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/forum/jeep-wrangler-tj/tj-long-arm-suspension/"},{"title":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Long-Arm Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/instructions"},{"title":"Rock Krawler TJ Max Travel System Overview","url":"https://www.rockkrawler.com/tj"}],"alternatives":["tj-mid-lift","tj-regear","tj-locker-rear"],"summary":"[Long arms fix what short arms can't](https://www.pirate4x4.com/forum/jeep-wrangler-tj/tj-long-arm-suspension/) \u2014 if the TJ is your dedicated rock rig, this is the upgrade that unlocks its full suspension geometry.","description":"Short-arm suspension \u2014 the factory layout \u2014 uses relatively short control arms that create a tight, responsive geometry at stock ride height. Raise the axle 3.5\"\u20134.5\" on short arms and the pinion angle, caster correction, and anti-squat geometry all degrade at full droop. The axle wants to rotate forward under compression and backward under droop, creating handling that's noticeably compromised on aggressive terrain. Long arms solve this by moving the front and rear pivot points forward (farther from the axle), which flattens the arc of axle travel and keeps the pinion angle stable through the full range of suspension travel.\n\nThe practical result is dramatic: a well-built TJ long-arm system delivers more axle droop, less pinion angle change through the suspension cycle, and far better on-trail predictability than a short-arm setup at the same lift height. On rock crawling terrain \u2014 ledges, V-ditches, side-hills \u2014 this translates directly to keeping tires on the ground when the terrain gets uneven. The trade-off is installation complexity and cost. Most long-arm systems require cutting factory crossmembers, welding in new mounts, or bolting in a new LCG (low center of gravity) skid crossmember that replaces the factory unit and provides the new long-arm pivot points.\n\nLow Center of Gravity (LCG) designs, used by Rubicon Express and [Iron Rock Offroad](https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/instructions), drop the front control arm mounting points as low as possible while running longer arms. This geometry lowers the TJ's center of gravity compared to a conventional high-mount long-arm, which meaningfully improves side-hill stability \u2014 relevant on any trail with off-camber sections. [Rock Krawler's 3-link front](https://www.rockkrawler.com/tj) is a different approach: a panhard bar replaces the track bar and a third upper link controls axle rotation, which allows even longer effective arm length without the geometry compromises of a standard 4-link.\n\nPlan for the full build to take an entire weekend for an experienced mechanic. Rear driveshaft replacement (SYE + rear CV shaft) is required at this lift height and should be bundled into the project. Wheel alignment is mandatory after installation.","steps":["Photograph all existing suspension geometry before disassembly \u2014 caster angle, pinion angle, and control arm orientation are reference points.","Raise the vehicle at the frame rails; support on jack stands at all four corners. Support both axles on separate floor jacks.","Drain transfer case if the skid crossmember must be removed for the LCG bracket.","Remove factory front and rear control arms, track bar, and (for LCG kits) factory crossmember.","Cut or remove factory control arm mounts per kit instructions \u2014 most kits specify which mounts to trim or remove entirely.","Install new LCG crossmember / frame brackets per kit; torque to specification.","Install long-arm control arms with new mounting hardware \u2014 do not fully torque until suspension is loaded.","Install new coil springs and shocks (included in kit or spec'd by kit documentation).","Reconnect sway bar end links and brake lines; check brake line length \u2014 extended lines are required at this lift height.","Lower the vehicle to the ground (suspension loaded) and torque all control arm bolts: front lower 130 ft-lbs, front upper 75 ft-lbs, rear lower 130 ft-lbs, rear upper 75 ft-lbs.","Set caster at 4\u00b0\u20137\u00b0 positive using adjustable upper control arms.","Verify pinion angle \u2014 should be 1\u00b0\u20133\u00b0 downward from horizontal at full droop.","Install SYE kit and rear CV driveshaft before driving.","Alignment shop visit is required \u2014 request a 4-wheel alignment with caster correction.","Drive 50 miles on pavement, re-torque all suspension fasteners."],"pros":["Dramatically improves axle articulation and droop compared to short-arm kits at the same lift height","Stable pinion angle through full suspension travel reduces U-joint wear and vibration","LCG designs lower the vehicle's center of gravity relative to conventional long-arm mounting","Rock Krawler 3-link eliminates track bar geometry limitations entirely","Best-in-class geometry for serious rock crawling builds"],"cons":["Installation requires partial frame modification on most kits \u2014 not fully reversible without significant effort","Welding skills may be required depending on kit and installer preference","Total project cost including SYE, rear driveshaft, extended brake lines, and alignment can reach $3,500\u2013$4,500","Long control arms can reduce departure angle and ground clearance at the frame if not paired with proper skid plates","Requires front-end alignment by a shop familiar with modified TJ geometry","Not appropriate for mild trail or mostly-street builds \u2014 this is a committed rock rig upgrade"]},{"id":"tj-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift Kit \u2014 3\"\u20134\" Spring Lift ($600\u2013$1,500)","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","ball joint press","alignment tool"],"skills_required":["suspension work","basic wrenching","torque specification knowledge"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["lift","suspension","mid-lift","coil-spring","geometry-correction"],"parts":[{"name":"OME 3\" Lift Kit with Nitrocharger Sport Shocks (TJ front/rear coils + shocks)","vendor":"Old Man Emu / ARB","price_estimate_usd":649},{"name":"Rubicon Express 3.5\" Super-Flex Suspension Lift Kit RE7000","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Rough Country 4\" Suspension Lift Kit with Premium N3 Shocks TJ 68230","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":699}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 OME vs Rubicon Express TJ Lift Comparison","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/ome-vs-rubicon-express-tj/"},{"title":"Rubicon Express TJ Super-Flex Installation Guide","url":"https://www.rubconexpress.com/instructions"},{"title":"4WD Hardware \u2014 TJ Suspension Lift Overview","url":"https://www.4wdh.com/TJ-Wrangler-Suspension-Lifts"}],"alternatives":["tj-budget-lift","tj-long-arm-lift","tj-shocks-upgrade"],"summary":"The 3\"\u20134\" lift is where the TJ starts performing like a trail rig \u2014 but it also introduces geometry problems that a quality kit must address to drive right on the road.","description":"At 3.5\" of lift the TJ genuinely transforms. You gain clearance for 33\" tires without fender trimming, meaningful flex improvement, and a ride height that holds its own on moderate trails. The problem this lift height introduces \u2014 and that distinguishes a good mid-tier kit from a budget one \u2014 is caster angle correction and track bar geometry. As the axle drops away from the body, the front axle's caster angle decreases, causing wandering and poor return-to-center steering. Every quality kit at this level includes adjustable upper control arms or cam bolts for caster correction.\n\nThe OME 3\" kit is the overland standard for a reason. Old Man Emu builds their coil rates around real-world load calculations, and the Nitrocharger Sport shock is a genuine upgrade over most stock units. The TJ OME setup delivers a controlled, comfortable ride on pavement that budget kits rarely match. It's not the most aggressive lift \u2014 3\" is the stated height \u2014 but OME spring rates are consistent and predictable over time, and they don't sag the way soft budget springs do after 30,000 miles.\n\nRubicon Express's 3.5\" Super-Flex kit leans toward articulation over on-road comfort. The Super-Flex mono-tube shocks and modified spring rates [produce noticeably more droop than the OME setup](https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/ome-vs-rubicon-express-tj/), which pays off on technical trails with uneven terrain. [The kit includes a track bar drop bracket](https://www.rubconexpress.com/instructions) and adjustable front upper control arms for caster correction. If you're building for moderate trail use over pavement comfort, this is the better choice. Rough Country's 4\" kit rounds out the tier with more lift height for less money \u2014 the N3 shocks are serviceable, but plan to upgrade them if you push the rig harder than light dirt roads.\n\nAt any lift over 2.5\", plan to add an SYE kit for the NP231 transfer case. Without it, the slip yoke output causes driveline vibration above 45 mph that gets worse with lift. It's not an emergency fix, but it's a known issue you'll want to budget for.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose before lifting the vehicle.","Raise the vehicle at the frame rails and support securely on jack stands at all four corners.","Support the front axle independently on a floor jack.","Disconnect sway bar end links and front track bar from the axle bracket.","Disconnect front upper and lower control arms from axle (if kit includes replacement control arms).","Lower the front axle carefully to release coil spring tension; remove old springs.","Install new coil springs; reconnect control arms hand-tight.","Install track bar drop bracket per kit instructions; reconnect track bar.","Repeat spring swap for rear coils (rear control arms may also need adjustment depending on kit).","Install new shocks \u2014 front and rear \u2014 per manufacturer instructions.","Torque all control arm bolts and track bar bolts with the suspension loaded (vehicle on ground). Front lower control arm: 130 ft-lbs. Track bar: 74 ft-lbs. Sway bar end links: 11 ft-lbs.","Set caster angle using adjustable upper control arms or cam bolts per kit spec (typically 4\u00b0\u20137\u00b0 positive caster on the TJ).","Drive 100 miles, re-torque all fasteners.","Schedule a full 4-wheel alignment immediately after install."],"pros":["Quality kits include geometry correction hardware (track bar drop bracket, adjustable upper control arms)","3.5\" clears 33\" tires without trimming; 35s are possible with minor flare trimming","OME kit provides one of the best on-road ride qualities in this price range","Rubicon Express Super-Flex provides trail articulation that approaches long-arm performance","Kit components are widely available and well-documented for DIY builds"],"cons":["Requires a professional front-end alignment after install","Lifts above 2.5\" require SYE kit + rear CV driveshaft to eliminate driveshaft vibration","At 4\", stock brake lines may be marginal \u2014 inspect and replace extended lines if needed","Budget shocks in lower-cost kits (Rough Country N3) are adequate but not trail-capable long-term","Adjustable upper control arms require periodic re-checking of caster after significant trail use"]},{"id":"tj-rear-leaf-springs","title":"Rear Leaf Spring Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","subcategory":"leaf_spring","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","breaker bar","torque wrench","pry bar","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["suspension disassembly","torque sequencing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["springs","lift","ride-quality","rear-suspension"],"parts":[{"name":"Old Man Emu CS049R Medium Load Rear Leaf (TJ)","part_number":"CS049R","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":285},{"name":"BDS 2\" Rear Leaf Spring (TJ)","vendor":"BDS Suspension","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Rubicon Express Standard Coil Rear Lift (TJ uses coils, not leaves \u2014 note: TJ is coil-rear)","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":0},{"name":"Rancho RS44113 TJ Rear Leaf (NOTE: TJ rear is coil \u2014 see body)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":0}],"citations":[{"title":"Old Man Emu YJ Rear Leaf CS036R reference","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/products/16090_5058.htm"},{"title":"Wrangler Forum \u2014 TJ vs YJ leaf spring discussion","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/jeep-leaf-springs.588145/"}],"alternatives":["tj-coil-spring-tj","tj-lift-options-tj"],"summary":"The TJ is a coil-sprung Wrangler \u2014 if you're searching for a TJ \"rear leaf spring,\" you've either got an LJ-to-leaf conversion build, or you're confusing the TJ with the older YJ Wrangler. This guide covers both situations.","description":"Stock TJ Wranglers (1997-2006) and LJ Unlimiteds (2004-2006) [use coil springs front and rear \u2014 not leaves](https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/jeep-leaf-springs.588145/). That's the headline. If you came into the garage expecting leaf-spring perches under the rear axle, you've got a YJ (1987-1995) or an aftermarket leaf-conversion kit. The coil-sprung rear is one of the TJ's biggest advantages over the YJ: better articulation, smoother ride, more manageable to lift.\n\nThat said, there are two real reasons to land on this page. First: you own a TJ with an aftermarket leaf-spring conversion (rare, usually shop-built for racing or specific suspension geometry). Second: you're cross-shopping a TJ with a YJ build and want to understand the differences. The TJ's coil-and-track-bar geometry is covered in detail in our [TJ Coil Spring Replacement guide](tj/suspension/coil-spring-tj.md). If your goal is rear lift, you want coil springs, not leaves.\n\nFor the rare TJ-with-leaves build (typically a Currie or shop-built setup): leaf packs are sized by load rating and arch. [Old Man Emu](https://www.quadratec.com/products/16090_5058.htm), BDS, and Skyjacker make spec-matched leaves for YJ that physically bolt to a TJ-to-leaf conversion frame bracket \u2014 but you need to confirm bushing eye diameter and arch height against your specific conversion kit. Mopar OE leaves do not exist for the TJ because the platform was never coil-rear from the factory.\n\nReplacement procedure (for the conversion build): support the frame, remove the U-bolts holding the axle to the spring pack, swing the spring forward off the front eye bolt, remove the rear shackle, replace the spring pack with the new one, torque U-bolts to 90-100 ft-lb in a cross pattern. Re-torque after 500 miles \u2014 leaf packs settle.\n\nIf you bought a TJ and want a 2-3\" rear lift, you're shopping coil springs. Skip this guide and go to the coil spring page.","steps":["Confirm your TJ actually has leaf springs in the rear (look for a multi-leaf pack between axle and frame). Stock TJ = coils, not leaves.","If coil-rear: stop. Go to the TJ coil spring guide.","If leaf-converted: chock front wheels; lift the rear; support frame on jack stands behind the axle.","Place a floor jack under the axle for support.","Spray penetrant on U-bolt nuts; let sit 10 minutes.","Loosen and remove U-bolt nuts; remove U-bolt plate and U-bolts.","Loosen the front eye bolt and rear shackle bolts.","Lower the axle to relieve tension on the spring pack.","Swing the spring forward off the front eye; remove the shackle; pull the spring out.","Install the new spring: front eye onto front mount bolt first, then rear into the shackle.","Center the axle on the spring perch (use the center pin alignment hole).","Reinstall U-bolts and plate; torque to 90-100 ft-lb in a cross pattern.","Torque front eye bolt to 80 ft-lb; shackle bolts to 55 ft-lb.","Drive 500 miles; re-torque U-bolts."],"pros":["Cheaper than coil-and-track-bar replacement (for conversion builds)","Leaf packs are tough and tolerant of bad geometry","Can add load capacity by adding leaves"],"cons":["TJ was not designed for leaves \u2014 converting from coils gives up articulation","Confusing platform mismatch: most TJ owners actually want the coil guide","Aftermarket conversion brackets are not common; parts harder to source","Rides stiffer than coil-rear unless tuned with proper shocks"]},{"id":"tj-rocker-arm-bushing","title":"TJ Control Arm Bushing Replacement \u2014 Press Them, or Replace the Arms","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","18mm and 15mm sockets and wrenches (21mm handy)","breaker bar","torque wrench (150 ft-lb range)","pry bar","penetrating oil","shop press or ball-joint press kit (bushing path only)","propane torch, drill, reciprocating saw (bushing path only)"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","press tool use"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["suspension","bushings","control-arms","alignment","clunk","johnny-joint"],"parts":[{"name":"Prothane front control arm bushing kit (poly)","part_number":"1-204","vendor":"Prothane","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Energy Suspension front control arm bushing kit (poly)","part_number":"2.3106","vendor":"Energy Suspension","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"MOOG lower control arm bushing (rubber, OE-style)","part_number":"K7420","vendor":"MOOG","price_estimate_usd":42},{"name":"OE-style fixed replacement control arm, stamped steel with rubber bushings (each)","vendor":"Dorman / MOOG / Crown","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Rubicon Express / Rough Country fixed tubular arm with poly or rubber bushings (each)","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Currie Johnny Joint control arms (per pair)","vendor":"Currie Enterprises","price_estimate_usd":300},{"name":"Metalcloak Duroflex or Clayton Off Road arms (per pair)","vendor":"Metalcloak / Clayton","price_estimate_usd":340}],"citations":[{"title":"Prothane 1-204 TJ control arm bushing kit","url":"https://www.prothanesuspensionparts.com/1204"},{"title":"Energy Suspension 2.3106 TJ control arm bushing kit","url":"https://www.energysuspensionparts.com/2.3106"},{"title":"TJ control arm torque specs from the factory service manual (JeepForum)","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f11/lower-upper-control-arm-torque-specs-255873/"},{"title":"Control arm bushing replacement discussion (WranglerTJForum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/control-arm-bushing-replacement.72483/"}],"alternatives":["tj-front-coil-spring","tj-tie-rods-tj","tj-track-bar-tj"],"summary":"Worn control arm bushings are the usual cause of the clunk on brake-and-turn, the wander that a new track bar didn't fix, and inner-edge tire wear on a TJ past 100,000 miles. You can press new bushings into the factory arms for $60\u2013$80 in parts and an afternoon of burning, drilling, and pressing \u2014 but for most owners the right answer is new arms with bushings already in them: $180\u2013$250 for a complete OE-style set, no press, two hours. Whichever path, [the bolts get torqued at ride height, not with the axle hanging](https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f11/lower-upper-control-arm-torque-specs-255873/), or the new bushings tear inside a year.","description":"The TJ front and rear suspensions are both 4-link coil setups: two lower arms and two upper arms per axle, eight arms total, sixteen bushings. The arms are stamped steel with a bonded rubber bushing pressed into each eye. Two more bushings live in the axle housing itself \u2014 the rear upper arms mount to a cast bracket on the axle that carries its own pressed-in bushing, and those don't come with new arms. Rubber lasts 80,000\u2013150,000 miles depending on how much mud, salt, and articulation the Jeep has seen; a lifted TJ with the arms running at an angle wears them faster because the bushings twist further through every cycle.\n\n**How to tell it's the bushings.** Front end on stands, axle supported: put a pry bar between the arm and the bracket and lever. A bushing that lets the sleeve shift visibly in the eye, or that shows cracked and separated rubber around the sleeve, is done. Grab each arm and try to rotate it about its axis \u2014 any rotational play at the eye is play in a bushing. Do the same at the rear. Don't stop at the arms: the [track bar](/db/tj/tj-track-bar-tj/) bushings and the sway bar end links make the same clunk, and the [tie rod ends](/db/tj/tj-tie-rods-tj/) make the same wander. Check all of it before buying anything, because half the \"control arm clunks\" on a TJ are a loose track bar bolt.\n\n**Path 1 \u2014 press new bushings into the existing arms ($60\u2013$80 parts).** The rubber is vulcanized to the steel sleeve and the sleeve is a tight press in the arm eye. The method that works: burn the rubber out with a torch (outside, away from the fuel lines, with the arm off the Jeep), drill the sleeve to weaken it, slot it lengthwise with a reciprocating saw, collapse it inward with a chisel, and drive it out. Then press the new bushing in with a shop press or a ball-joint press kit. Budget 45 minutes per eye once you've done one. Poly bushings ([Prothane 1-204](https://www.prothanesuspensionparts.com/1204), [Energy Suspension 2.3106](https://www.energysuspensionparts.com/2.3106)) are the usual replacement because they're two-piece and slide in; they last longer than rubber, transmit more road noise, and need grease at install and again every year or two or they squeak. Take this path if you enjoy the work or the arms are something you want to keep (long-arm brackets, custom lengths).\n\n**Path 2 \u2014 replace the whole arms with OE-style arms ($180\u2013$250 for eight).** Dorman, MOOG, Crown, and the house brands at the big Jeep retailers sell stamped or tubular fixed-length arms with the bushings already pressed in, $35\u2013$60 each. No press, no torch. This is the right call for a stock-height or 2 in-lift TJ with tired bushings, and it's what most shops do. The arms are the same length as factory, so the alignment comes back to where it was.\n\n**Path 3 \u2014 upgrade to adjustable arms with rebuildable joints ($280\u2013$400 per pair, $1,100\u2013$1,600 for all eight).** Currie Johnny Joints, Metalcloak Duroflex, Clayton, Rubicon Express Super-Flex, JKS. Rebuildable for the life of the Jeep, far stronger than stamped steel against rock hits, and adjustable \u2014 which is the point above a 3 in lift, where you need longer lowers to bring caster and the front driveshaft angle back. Below that lift height an adjustable arm is a nicer version of something you didn't need; the geometry is the argument, not the bushing. See [short-arm vs. long-arm](/db/tj/tj-short-arm-vs-long-arm/) before spending here.\n\nMixed approach that makes sense: Path 2 for the uppers, adjustable lowers (Path 3) if you're lifted. The lowers set caster; the uppers mostly follow.\n\n- **Torque at ride height** is the rule that everything else depends on. If the Jeep is on stands with the wheels off, the floor jack under the axle is what puts the suspension at ride height. There is no shortcut.\n- **Johnny Joints and Duroflex joints need periodic greasing** (Currie ships a zerk on most; grease every oil change) and the balls wear \u2014 a rebuild kit is $15\u2013$25 per joint and the reason these arms last forever.\n- **Poly bushings with no grease squeak** within weeks. Poly on the lowers and rubber on the uppers is a common compromise that keeps the noise down.\n- **Rear axle-bracket bushings.** If the rear uppers are worn, the bracket bushing on the axle usually is too. Order them with the arms; nothing is more annoying than a new arm mounted into a rotten bushing.\n- **Bolt heads facing the wrong way** on the front lower frame bracket can contact the sway bar link or a long-arm bracket. Match the factory orientation.\n- **Broken frame bracket welds** on the rear lower arm mounts are a known TJ failure and show up as a clunk that no bushing fixes. Inspect the bracket-to-frame welds and the frame rail around them while you're there \u2014 cracked welds get ground and re-welded (or a weld-on bracket from Rubicon Express or Poison Spyder), and a rusted-through rail is a frame repair, not a suspension job.\n\n| Path | Parts | Time | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Press bushings, all 8 arms | $60\u2013$130 (front + rear kits) | 6\u20138 hours | Needs press or press kit ($40 rental), torch, drill, saw |\n| OE-style replacement arms, all 8 | $180\u2013$250 | 2\u20133 hours | Dorman / MOOG / Crown; the default |\n| Fixed tubular aftermarket arms, all 8 | $300\u2013$480 | 2\u20133 hours | Rubicon Express, Rough Country; poly or rubber |\n| Adjustable Johnny Joint / Duroflex arms, 4 lowers | $550\u2013$700 | 3\u20134 hours | Buy if lifted 3 in or more, otherwise skip |\n| Shop labor (pressing) | $100\u2013$150 per arm | \u2014 | Most shops replace arms instead |\n| Alignment after | $80\u2013$130 | \u2014 | Required regardless of path |\n\nQuadratec, Northridge4x4, and RockAuto carry the OE-style arms; RockAuto is cheapest per arm and the fitment tool is reliable for the year splits. Currie, Metalcloak, and Clayton sell direct with the best instructions. If you're on the fence between Path 1 and Path 2: the press job is a good skill to have, and a bad way to spend a Saturday when eight arms full of fresh bushings cost $200.","steps":["**Park level, loosen the lugs, and get the front on stands under the frame.** Set a floor jack under the axle and raise it until the springs are at roughly their loaded length \u2014 you want the axle held at ride height so the arm bolts aren't loaded when you try to pull them.","**Soak every arm bolt** with penetrating oil the day before. They're 18 mm on the lowers and 15 mm on most uppers, with a nut on the back side. On a rust-belt TJ the sleeve rusts to the bolt inside the bushing and the bolt will not come out with a wrench; an air hammer on the bolt head, or a cutoff wheel through the bolt on both sides of the bracket, is the escalation. Buy new bolts if you cut any (grade 8, same thread \u2014 M12 on the lowers on most years).","**Do one arm at a time.** Pull the lower arm first: bolt at the axle bracket, bolt at the frame bracket, arm comes out. The axle stays located by the other three arms and the track bar. Never pull both lowers at once with the springs in \u2014 the axle rotates and the springs can unload.","**Path 1:** burn, drill, slot, collapse, drive out, press in. Grease poly bushings with the silicone grease that comes in the kit \u2014 not chassis grease, which swells poly. **Path 2 and 3:** compare the new arm against the old one eye-to-eye before installing; a fixed arm that's \u00bc in off is the wrong part.","**Install the arm** with both bolts hand-tight. Set the bolt heads facing the direction the factory had them (toward the outside on most brackets \u2014 matters for driveshaft and exhaust clearance).","**Repeat for the other lower, then the uppers.** On the rear, this is where you find out whether the axle-bracket bushings for the rear uppers are shot; they're a separate press job in the axle bracket (Prothane and Rubicon Express sell them) and the bracket is welded to the housing, so it's done in the Jeep with a C-clamp-style press.","**Lower the Jeep onto its tires** or, on stands, raise the axle with the jack until the springs are at their normal loaded height and the arms are at their working angle. Then torque. Factory spec: **front lower arms 85 ft-lb at the axle and 130 ft-lb at the frame; rear lower arms 130 ft-lb both ends; all upper arms 55 ft-lb.** Aftermarket arms with their own hardware use the manufacturer's spec, usually 80\u2013130 ft-lb. Torquing with the axle hanging clamps the bushing at full droop; the first time the suspension compresses the rubber is twisted past its limit and starts tearing.","**Adjustable arms:** set the lowers to the manufacturer's starting length for your lift, then check caster and pinion angle before final torque \u2014 see the [SYE and driveshaft guide](/db/tj/tj-sye-kit/) for the angle targets.","**Test drive slowly**, listen, and re-check torque after the first 50 miles. Get an alignment within the first couple hundred miles regardless of path; even fixed arms shift toe slightly."]},{"id":"tj-shocks-upgrade","title":"Shock Absorber Upgrade \u2014 OME, Bilstein, and Fox Options","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":380,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","open-end wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","suspension work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["shocks","suspension","dampers","ride-quality","trail-performance"],"parts":[{"name":"OME Nitrocharger Sport TJ Front and Rear Shock Set (90006 front / 90051 rear, for 3-3.5 inch lift)","vendor":"Old Man Emu / ARB","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 TJ Front and Rear Shock Set (33-187084 front / 33-187079 rear, for 0-2.5 inch lift)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series TJ Front and Rear Shock Set (for 2-3.5 inch lift)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":720}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Shock Upgrade Guide: OME vs Bilstein vs Fox","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/shocks-upgrade-tj-ome-bilstein-fox/"},{"title":"ARB OME TJ Shock Fitment Guide","url":"https://arbusa.com/ome-tj-shocks"},{"title":"Bilstein 5100 TJ Product Page","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/us/en/products/5100-series"}],"alternatives":["tj-budget-lift","tj-mid-lift","tj-long-arm-lift"],"summary":"Shock quality is where budget lift kits lose ground the fastest \u2014 replacing the included shocks with [a set of OME, Bilstein, or Fox units](https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/shocks-upgrade-tj-ome-bilstein-fox/) is one of the highest-return investments you can make on a TJ.","description":"Shock absorbers are the component most budget lift kits shortchange. A spring moves the vehicle; a shock controls how fast it moves and damps the oscillation. An undersized or overvalved shock on a TJ produces a ride that either bobs continuously on pavement or bottoms out hard on trail impacts. The TJ's coil-spring suspension responds extremely well to quality shocks because the coil springs themselves have relatively linear rates; the damper is doing the majority of the ride-quality work.\n\nOME's Nitrocharger Sport remains the value standard for TJ overland builds. The gas-charged monotube design provides consistent damping through extended use, and OME calibrates their valving specifically for loaded TJs \u2014 they account for the extra weight of armor, tools, and camping gear that's typically on an overland rig. The OME set at $380\u2013$450 is the pick if you want the best ride quality on pavement with competent trail performance. [OME shocks are sized for their own spring kits](https://arbusa.com/ome-tj-shocks), so verify fitment against your lift height before purchasing.\n\n[Bilstein's 5100 series](https://www.bilstein.com/us/en/products/5100-series) is a serious performance upgrade with a monotube design and zinc-plated body. Bilstein valves their shocks for consistent performance across the range from full bump to full droop \u2014 they feel firmer than OME on pavement but provide notably better control on rough trail surfaces where damper velocity is high. The 5100 is the choice for a TJ that's on technical trails regularly and needs a shock that can keep up with repeated high-frequency inputs.\n\nFox 2.0 Performance shocks are the upper tier in this category: larger 2.0\" diameter body, better heat dissipation on extended trail use, and adjustable rebound damping on some configurations. For builds at 3\"\u20134\" of lift with 35\" tires that see serious trail use, the Fox 2.0 is worth the premium. For anything milder, OME or Bilstein is the right answer.","steps":["Raise the vehicle at the frame rails and support on jack stands. Keep the axle supported on a separate floor jack to allow adjustment of suspension height.","Spray all shock hardware with penetrating oil and allow 10\u201315 minutes to soak on any vehicle over five years old.","Remove the top shock mount nut while holding the shock shaft with a second wrench to prevent spinning.","Remove the lower shock mount bolt.","Slide out the old shock; slide in the new shock. Orient the shock so the reservoir (if present) is positioned per manufacturer instructions.","Install lower mount bolt hand-tight. Install top mount nut hand-tight.","Load the suspension to ride height (lower the axle jack until the vehicle rests normally) before torquing fasteners.","Torque lower shock mount bolts to 40\u201350 ft-lbs; torque upper mount nuts to 22\u201326 ft-lbs.","Repeat for all four corners.","Drive 50 miles and check all fasteners."],"pros":["OME Nitrocharger Sport provides the best on-pavement ride quality in the under-$500 price range for TJ applications","Bilstein 5100 provides consistent trail performance across a wide range of input speeds","Fox 2.0 Performance handles heat and repeated high-velocity inputs better than twin-tube designs at this lift height","All three brands are TJ-specific fitments \u2014 correct lengths and mounting geometry are well-documented","Shock upgrade is independent of spring changes \u2014 can be done as a standalone upgrade on any existing lift kit"],"cons":["Shock length must match lift height \u2014 purchasing shocks without verifying your actual lift height results in either binding at full bump or falling out at full droop","OME shocks are valved for their own spring kits; mixing OME shocks with non-OME springs may produce suboptimal results at the extremes","Fox 2.0 rebound adjustment requires understanding of damping theory to set correctly \u2014 incorrect adjustment produces worse results than a non-adjustable shock","All shock upgrades are per-axle \u2014 mismatching front and rear shock quality produces inconsistent handling","Shocks should be replaced in sets of four \u2014 a single replacement changes the handling balance"]},{"id":"tj-short-arm-vs-long-arm","title":"Short-Arm vs Long-Arm: Which TJ Lift You Actually Need","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":3500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","spring compressor","breaker bar","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["wrenching","suspension geometry basics"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["suspension","lift","control-arms"],"parts":[{"name":"Short-arm lift kit (3-4 in)","vendor":"Rubicon Express/Rough Country/OME","price_estimate_usd":800},{"name":"Long-arm conversion kit","vendor":"Clayton/Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":2400},{"name":"Adjustable front track bar","vendor":"Currie/Synergy","price_estimate_usd":160}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ suspension reference \u2014 My Jeep TJ","url":"https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/"},{"title":"TJ factory specs","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-factory-specifications.71/"}],"alternatives":["tj-lift-options","tj-long-arm-lift","tj-track-bar-tj"],"summary":"Most TJ owners do not need a long-arm kit. A quality short-arm 3\u20134 inch lift clears 33s, corrects the geometry, and costs a fraction of a long-arm conversion. The long-arm earns its money only at the extremes \u2014 tall lift, 35s, and serious rock work \u2014 so match the kit to how you actually wheel, not a forum build thread.","description":"The TJ's short coil-spring control arms work well through about 3.5 inches. Past that the arms get steep, which shortens the wheelbase on compression and makes the ride harsh and the handling vague. A short-arm lift keeps the factory mounts and swaps springs, shocks, and ideally the arms; a long-arm relocates the lower mounts rearward and runs much longer arms for a flatter, smoother arc. Both are covered here with real kit names and prices; for the full menu of lift heights see [lift options](/db/tj/tj-lift-options/).\n\nThe factory TJ lower control arms are roughly 15\u201316 inches long, front and rear. As the suspension cycles, the axle swings on an arc whose radius is that arm length. Lift the Jeep 4 inches on stock-length arms and the arms now sit at a steep angle at ride height, so the first few inches of compression pull the axle rearward (front) or forward (rear) hard, which is the \"harsh over sharp bumps\" complaint. A long-arm kit doubles the arm length to 30-plus inches, so the same 4 inches of lift leaves the arms nearly flat and the axle's arc nearly vertical through its travel. Flatter arc means smoother ride, more usable flex, and less caster change as the suspension moves. That is the entire argument for long-arms. The counter-argument is that the flatter arc is bought with a crossmember or bracket set that hangs below the frame rails, with drilling or welding, and with $1,500\u20132,500 more money.\n\nAt 2.5 inches, Old Man Emu's TJ kit (springs, Nitrocharger shocks, around $900\u20131,100) is the ride-quality benchmark and clears 32s or a narrow 33 with mild trimming. At 3.5\u20134 inches for a proper 33, Rubicon Express's 3.5-inch Super-Flex ($1,300\u20131,600 with adjustable arms), Clayton's 4-inch short-arm ($1,200\u20131,500), and Metalcloak's 3.5-inch Game-Changer with Duroflex joints ($1,800\u20132,200) are the kits that ship with the geometry-correction parts already in the box. Rough Country's 4-inch X-Series ($700\u2013900) is the budget entry \u2014 the springs and shocks are fine, but budget for an adjustable front track bar and at least adjustable front lower arms on top of it, which closes most of the price gap. Whatever kit you choose, the parts list that keeps a 4-inch short-arm safe is: adjustable front lower arms to set caster, an adjustable front track bar to re-center the axle, adjustable rear uppers to set pinion angle, extended bump stops, and a transfer-case drop or SYE. A [budget lift](/db/tj/tj-budget-lift/) can skip some of that at 2 inches; at 4 inches none of it is optional.\n\nRubicon Express's 4.5-inch long-arm system ($2,800\u20133,300) is the most common TJ long-arm on the trail and bolts to a new crossmember with some drilling. Clayton's 4-inch and 6-inch long-arm kits ($2,900\u20133,600) use a frame-side bracket and are the fabricator's favorite for their weld-in strength. Rock Krawler's 3.5-inch X-Factor long-arm ($2,800\u20133,400) and Metalcloak's long-arm Game-Changer ($3,000\u20133,500) round out the field. The Currie/Savvy \"mid-arm\" is the alternative worth knowing: a belly-up skid plate raises the transfer case ~1.5 inches for breakover clearance while relocating the arm mounts for a longer-than-stock (but not full long) arm, so you gain geometry without a hanging crossmember. Expect a full weekend with a grinder: the factory lower mounts come off, the new crossmember goes in, and every kit needs the arms cycled and checked at full droop. The [long-arm install](/db/tj/tj-long-arm-lift/) guide has the step-by-step.\n\nFor 33s and mixed street-and-trail use, a 3\u20134 inch short-arm kit with adjustable control arms, an adjustable front track bar, and good shocks is the honest answer for the vast majority of TJs. It rides well, holds alignment, and handles real trails. It also keeps the factory belly line \u2014 the transfer-case skid stays where it was, and nothing new hangs below the frame to catch a ledge \u2014 and it comes apart with hand tools if you ever want to sell the Jeep stock.\n\nAt 4.5 inches and up, on 35s, for dedicated rock work, the long-arm's flatter geometry buys flex and ride quality a tall short-arm can't match. The cost is a bigger kit, drilling or welding new mounts, and reduced belly clearance from the crossmember on the kits that use one. It also makes sense on a TJ that already has a long-arm crossmember from a previous owner, or on an LJ Unlimited, whose 103.4-inch wheelbase tolerates the mounts better. If you are at 35s and still wondering whether the Dana 35 survives, it does not \u2014 settle the [axle question](/db/tj/tj-dana35-failures/) before the lift budget.\n\nWhichever kit you run, these are the targets after install. Front caster: 6\u20137 degrees positive on a lifted TJ (factory spec is around 7); below 4 degrees the Jeep wanders and death wobble becomes likely. Set caster with the adjustable front lower arms, then check the front pinion angle \u2014 on the TJ front axle, caster and pinion rotate together, so a compromise near 6 degrees caster is normal on a 4-inch lift with a CV front shaft. Rear pinion: 1\u20133 degrees below the driveshaft angle with a CV/SYE driveshaft, set with the adjustable rear uppers. Track bar: axle centered under the frame within 1/4 inch, measured tire-to-fender both sides. Toe: 1/16\u20131/8 inch toe-in. Torque control-arm bolts at ride height with the Jeep on its tires, never on jack stands, or the bushings take a permanent set at full droop and tear early.\n\nThe adjustable track bar and control arms are what keep the steering safe \u2014 they re-center the axle and restore caster. Never lift a TJ without correcting the geometry, or you invite death wobble. Above ~3.5 inches add a slip-yoke eliminator and CV driveshaft for the rear driveline angle; a transfer-case drop is the $40 stopgap that costs you belly clearance. Build short-arm to 33s for almost everyone; reserve the long-arm for the committed 35-inch crawler."},{"id":"tj-spring-over","title":"Spring-Over and Coil Conversions for Big TJ Lift","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":12,"high":30},"cost_usd":{"low":1500,"high":5000},"tools_required":["welder","grinder","floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["fabrication","suspension geometry","welding"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["suspension","lift","fabrication"],"parts":[{"name":"Long-arm or coilover conversion kit","vendor":"Clayton/Rock Krawler/EVO","price_estimate_usd":2800},{"name":"SYE + CV driveshaft","vendor":"JB Conversions/Tom Woods","price_estimate_usd":700},{"name":"High-steer / drop-pitman steering kit","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":400}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ suspension reference \u2014 My Jeep TJ","url":"https://myjeeptj.com/wrangler-write-ups/jeep-tj-general-drivetrain-specifications/"},{"title":"TJ factory specs","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-factory-specifications.71/"}],"alternatives":["tj-short-arm-vs-long-arm","tj-long-arm-lift","tj-sye-kit"],"summary":"The TJ is coil-sprung, so a true leaf \"spring-over\" does not apply the way it does to a YJ \u2014 the big-lift path here is a long-arm or coilover conversion, not flipping springs. If you are chasing 6 inches and 37s, that is a fabrication build with steering, driveline, and brake work, not a bolt-on. For most TJs, stop at a 4-inch short-arm and 33s; the guide below is for the owner who has decided not to.","description":"Because the TJ uses coils and control arms front and rear, gaining serious height means re-engineering the geometry, not relocating a leaf pack. The two real paths past ~4 inches are a long-arm kit (relocated mounts, longer arms) or a coilover conversion (the dedicated crawler route). Both are welding-and-fabrication jobs, and both drag the steering, driveline, and brakes along with them. If you searched \"spring over\" because a YJ friend told you about it, read the [short-arm vs long-arm](/db/tj/tj-short-arm-vs-long-arm/) breakdown first \u2014 the TJ equivalent of the YJ's big cheap lift does not exist.\n\nA YJ spring-over moves the leaf pack from under the axle to on top of it and nets 4\u20136 inches for the price of new perches and a weekend. It works because a leaf spring is both the spring and the locating link. The TJ separates those jobs: coils carry the weight, four control arms per axle locate it, and a track bar keeps it centered side to side. There is nothing to flip. Height on a TJ comes from taller coils, and every inch of taller coil steepens the control arms, pulls the axle rearward on compression, and tilts the pinion. Past ~4 inches the stock-length arms (roughly 15\u201316 inches) are steep enough that the ride turns harsh, the wheelbase shrinks noticeably under load, and the front axle's caster and pinion angle can no longer both be set right. That is the wall a long-arm or coilover kit is built to get past.\n\nA long-arm kit replaces the frame-side lower mounts with a new crossmember or bracket set further back, and runs arms roughly twice the factory length. The flatter arc restores ride quality and flex at 5\u20136 inches and keeps the axle from walking rearward on compression. Name-brand kits for the TJ run about $2,500\u20134,000 depending on shock and bushing choices: Rubicon Express's 4.5-inch long-arm system, Clayton's 4\u20136 inch long-arm kits, Rock Krawler's X-Factor long-arm, and Metalcloak's long-arm Game-Changer are the usual picks, and the Currie/Savvy \"mid-arm\" using a belly-up skid is the middle route that gains arm length without a hanging crossmember. Plan a full weekend with a welder and grinder even for kits marketed as bolt-in \u2014 the crossmember goes in with bolts, but every one of these ships with brackets that want tacking, and the old lower mounts have to come off. Read the [long-arm lift](/db/tj/tj-long-arm-lift/) guide for the install itself; this guide is about what surrounds it.\n\nA coilover conversion throws out the coil-and-shock pairing for 2.0- or 2.5-inch remote-reservoir coilovers on fabricated hoops, usually front only, with the rear either on a matching coilover setup or a long-travel coil-and-shock package. Rock Krawler, GenRight, and EVO sell TJ-specific front coilover kits in the $3,000\u20135,000 range before the rear, and a finished front-and-rear system with tuned springs and a quality long-arm underneath lands at $6,000\u20139,000 in parts. What you buy is adjustability \u2014 ride height, spring rate, and damping are all tunable \u2014 and wheel travel that a coil-and-shock setup cannot match. What you pay is fabrication: the hoops are welded to the frame, the fenders come off or get replaced with [highline flares](/db/tj/tj-fender-flares/), and the whole front end gets cycled with the springs out to find every contact point before it is final. This is not a driveway job unless the driveway has a welder and a lift.\n\nThree systems must be addressed together or the truck becomes unsafe, and each has a real cost.\n\nSteering. At 5\u20136 inches the drag link and track bar are no longer parallel, which is the geometry that produces bump-steer and feeds death wobble. The cheap fix is a drop pitman arm and a track-bar relocation bracket ($150\u2013250 combined); the correct fix at this height is high-steer \u2014 flipping the drag link to the top of the knuckle with a Currie or Reid high-steer knuckle and a new tie-rod/drag-link set, $700\u20131,200. Pair it with an adjustable [track bar](/db/tj/tj-track-bar-tj/) mounted on a raised axle-side bracket so both bars sit at the same angle. Hydro-assist is a separate conversation; big lift does not require it, 37s and a locked front do.\n\nDriveline. The TJ's rear driveshaft is short \u2014 around 16\u201318 inches [on a 93.4-inch wheelbase](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-factory-specifications.71/) \u2014 so even a 2-inch lift changes its operating angle noticeably, and at 5\u20136 inches the factory slip-yoke shaft will vibrate and eat u-joints. A slip-yoke eliminator on the NP231 plus a double-cardan CV driveshaft is mandatory ($600\u2013900 installed with a Tom Woods or JE Reel shaft); see the [SYE guide](/db/tj/tj-sye-kit/). 2003\u20132006 Rubicons already have the NV241OR's fixed yoke and skip the SYE but still need the CV shaft. Set the rear pinion 1\u20133 degrees below the driveshaft angle with the adjustable upper arms. The front driveshaft also gets long enough at 6 inches that some kits ship a new one; check yours at full droop for slip-yoke pull-out.\n\nBrake lines and bump stops. Factory rubber brake lines will pull taut and can tear at full droop on a 5-inch-plus setup \u2014 extended stainless lines run $80\u2013130 for a set. Bump stops must be extended by roughly the lift height so the tire does not meet the fender or the coil does not unseat under compression, and the coil retainers should be upgraded so a fully-drooped spring stays put. Longer sway-bar links or disconnects come with any kit; at this height most owners run quick-disconnects and leave the bar off on the trail.\n\nA 6-inch TJ on 37s is roughly 4 inches taller at the roofline and has moved its center of gravity up while the track width stayed at the factory 58.5 inches. That is a tippy combination on off-camber trail, which is why coilover builds almost always add width \u2014 1.25\u20131.5-inch wheel spacers or wheels with 3.5\u20133.75-inch backspacing \u2014 and why serious crawlers eventually swap in wider axles. The [35-inch tire fitment](/db/tj/tj-tire-fitment-35s/) guide covers the trimming, and 37s need highline flares or a hard cut of the front fenders on any lift under 6 inches. Regearing is not optional at this tire size: 4.88s for 35s, 5.13s for 37s on a 4.0L, and the Dana 35 rear has to be replaced before any of it \u2014 see the [Dana 35 guide](/db/tj/tj-dana35-failures/).\n\nA 6-inch, 37-inch TJ is a trailer-or-dedicated-crawler build. It will not drive like a stocker, and the higher center of gravity needs the wider track and careful tuning that come with a real coilover kit. If you mostly daily-drive and hit trails on weekends, the payoff is not there \u2014 a 4-inch short-arm on 33s with a locker will out-wheel a badly sorted 6-inch build every time, and it will still make the freeway drive home.\n\nA realistic all-in budget for the long-arm path at 5\u20136 inches: long-arm kit $2,500\u20134,000, SYE and CV driveshaft $600\u2013900, high-steer or drop-pitman steering $250\u20131,200, adjustable track bar and bracket $200\u2013300, extended brake lines and bump stops $150\u2013250, regear front and rear $1,200\u20131,800 at a shop, and the axle swap the Dana 35 forces at 35s-plus, $1,500\u20133,000. That is $6,500\u201311,000 before tires and wheels. The coilover path adds roughly $3,000\u20135,000 on top for the coilovers, hoops, and fabrication. Parts sources are the kit makers directly (Rubicon Express, Clayton, Rock Krawler, Metalcloak, GenRight, Currie/Savvy), Tom Woods or JE Reel for driveshafts, and a local shop with a proper gear-setup bench for the regear. Budget the fabrication labor honestly \u2014 a shop will bill 20\u201340 hours for a coilover front end that is cycled and tuned properly.\n\nRun a 4-inch short-arm on 33s and you have a TJ that crawls hard and still drives home comfortably. Reach for the long-arm or coilover conversion only when the trail genuinely demands 35s-plus and you are ready for the fabrication, the steering work, and the driveline cost that come with it \u2014 and do the steering, SYE, and brake lines in the same weekend as the lift, never \"later.\""},{"id":"tj-obd2-diagnostics","title":"TJ OBD-II Diagnostics \u2014 Reading Codes, Common Faults, and What They Mean","vehicle":"tj","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":80},"tools_required":["OBD-II scanner or code reader"],"skills_required":["basic electrical knowledge helpful but not required"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["diagnostic","OBD2","check-engine","PCM","tools_diagnostic"],"parts":[{"name":"Autel AL319 basic code reader","vendor":"Autel","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"BlueDriver Bluetooth OBD-II scanner (iOS/Android)","vendor":"BlueDriver","price_estimate_usd":100},{"name":"BAFX Products Bluetooth OBD-II adapter (Android)","vendor":"BAFX","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Innova 3100j code reader with live data","vendor":"Innova","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Wrangler TJ OBD-II Code List \u2014 Jeep Service Manual","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/obd-ii-codes-and-descriptions.html"},{"title":"TJ PCM Diagnostics Overview \u2014 QuadraJeep","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-tech/obd2-diagnostics"}],"alternatives":["tj-o2-sensor-replacement","tj-mass-airflow-sensor","tj-throttle-position-sensor"],"summary":"The TJ has been OBD-II compliant since 1997. Plug in a $20 code reader, pull the fault code, and you have the starting point for any check engine light diagnosis \u2014 don't pay a shop $80 to do that part.","description":"The TJ Wrangler uses Chrysler's OBD-II system through the PCM (powertrain control module). [The diagnostic port (DLC) is under the dash on the driver's side](https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-tech/obd2-diagnostics), near the steering column. Any generic OBD-II scanner reads it.\n\nA check engine light on a TJ means the PCM has stored a Diagnostic Trouble Code (DTC). Some codes are serious; most are not. Reading the code is step one \u2014 it tells you where to look, not necessarily what to replace.\n\n**Common TJ fault codes and their realistic meaning:**\n\n- **P0300\u2013P0306** (Random/cylinder misfire): Ignition system first \u2014 cap, rotor, plug wires, plugs. The 4.0L burns through plug wires every 60\u201380K miles. Cap and rotor failure is also common.\n- **P0171** (System lean, Bank 1): Check for vacuum leaks (cracked intake boot, cracked TPS-area hoses), a dirty MAF sensor, or a weak fuel pump. On high-mileage engines it can also be worn injectors.\n- **P0122/P0123** (Throttle Position Sensor range): TPS failure is common on the TJ 4.0L. Erratic idle, hesitation on acceleration, and transmission shift points that feel off are the symptoms. New TPS is a $15\u2013$30 part.\n- **P0351\u2013P0356** (Ignition coil circuit): The TJ 4.0L uses a distributor-based ignition. A failing ASD (automatic shutdown) relay can trigger these. Also check spark plug wires for shorts to ground.\n- **P0138/P0141** (O2 sensor downstream): The downstream (post-cat) O2 sensor on TJs fails regularly at 100,000+ miles. It monitors catalyst efficiency and this code often appears with no drivability symptoms \u2014 the light.\n- **P0340** (Camshaft position sensor circuit): The cam position sensor on the 4.0L is inside the distributor. If this code is active with hard starting or stalling, the distributor may need replacement.\n- **P1391** ([Intermittent loss of CMP or CKP](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/obd-ii-codes-and-descriptions.html)): This TJ-specific code is almost always the crankshaft position sensor or its wiring. Common failure mode on high-mileage 4.0Ls.\n\nA code reader (Autel AL319, $22) pulls stored and pending codes and clears the light. That's enough for most diagnostics. A scanner with live data (Innova 3100j, BlueDriver) lets you watch O2 sensor response, MAP pressure, coolant temp, and fuel trims in real time \u2014 worth the extra $30\u2013$50 if you're doing your own maintenance regularly.\n\nBluetooth adapters (BAFX, Veepeak) pair with free apps like Torque Pro on Android. They can't do everything a dedicated scanner does, but for code reading and live PIDs they're fully functional.\n\n- Clearing codes without fixing the underlying issue is pointless \u2014 the light will return within one drive cycle.\n- Multiple codes together tell a better story than one code in isolation. P0171 + P0174 together means fuel delivery or a large vacuum leak; P0171 alone could be one small vacuum leak or a lazy O2 sensor.\n- The Jeep has to complete a \"drive cycle\" for monitors to reset after clearing. Most monitors require highway and city driving, several cold starts, and some idle time. Inspection stations will fail the Jeep for an incomplete monitor even if no codes are present.\n\n- Autel AL319 (basic): $20\u2013$25 on Amazon\n- Innova 3100j (with live data): $50\u2013$60 on Amazon\n- BlueDriver (full diagnostics + repair reports): $100 on Amazon or Best Buy\n- Many auto parts stores (AutoZone, O'Reilly) will read codes for free with their loaner scanner","steps":["Locate the DLC port \u2014 driver's side, under the dash, near the steering column.","Plug in the scanner with the ignition OFF.","Turn key to ON (don't crank). The scanner will power on.","Select \"Read Codes\" on the scanner menu.","Note all stored codes (confirmed faults) and pending codes (one-trip faults that haven't reached confirmed threshold).","Research the specific code before replacing parts. The code points to a system or circuit, not always to the failed component.","After diagnosis and repair, clear codes and drive through one full drive cycle to confirm the light doesn't return."]},{"id":"tj-vacuum-leak-diagnosis","title":"TJ Vacuum Leak Diagnosis \u2014 Finding and Fixing Intake Leaks","vehicle":"tj","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":10,"high":120},"tools_required":["OBD-II scanner","propane torch (unlit)","carb cleaner spray","smoke machine (optional)","vacuum hand pump"],"skills_required":["basic diagnostic reading","intake system familiarity"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["tools_diagnostic","vacuum-leak","idle","diagnostic","drivability"],"parts":[{"name":"Dorman vacuum hose kit (universal assortment)","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":15},{"name":"3M intake manifold gasket set \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Fel-Pro","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Throttle body intake boot \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Dorman / OEM","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"How to Find a Vacuum Leak","url":"https://www.cars.com/articles/how-to-find-a-vacuum-leak/"},{"title":"TJ 4.0 idle surge and vacuum leak TSB","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/forum/"}],"alternatives":["tj-obd2-diagnostics","tj-fuel-filter-replacement","tj-throttle-body-cleaning"],"summary":"To find a vacuum leak on a TJ, start with the scan tool: long-term fuel trim above roughly +10 percent at idle that falls toward normal at 2,500 rpm points to unmetered air. Then [locate the leak with a smoke test](https://www.cars.com/articles/how-to-find-a-vacuum-leak/) (best), a propane or water-mist test (workable), or a hose-by-hose inspection. Most TJ leaks are cracked hoses that cost under $20; the expensive one is the intake manifold gasket.","description":"The 4.0L TJ is a speed-density system: the PCM calculates airflow from the MAP sensor, engine speed, and throttle position rather than measuring it with a mass airflow sensor. Any air that enters the intake after the throttle plate is unmetered, so the PCM adds too little fuel. It compensates with fuel trim, but a large leak exceeds what trim can correct. The result is a rough or surging idle, hesitation off idle, a high idle that will not settle, or lean codes.\n\nCodes that commonly accompany a leak include P0171 (system too lean, bank 1), P0300 (random misfire, from a mixture too lean to burn reliably), and idle-related codes such as P0506 and P0507. None of those codes proves a leak, because a weak fuel pump, a clogged fuel filter, and a failing oxygen sensor can produce the same lean readings. See [TJ OBD2 codes](/db/tj/tj-obd2-diagnostics/) for the full code list, and [TJ fuel filter replacement](/db/tj/tj-fuel-filter-replacement/) to eliminate the fuel side.\n\n**Where TJ leaks usually come from, in rough order of frequency:**\n\n- Brittle or split small-diameter vacuum hoses and their tees, particularly the ones near the intake manifold and firewall.\n- The PCV hose and its grommet at the valve cover, which cracks from heat and oil.\n- The large brake booster vacuum hose and its check valve at the booster.\n- The throttle body gasket and the idle air control (IAC) motor seal.\n- The intake manifold gasket on the 4.0, which hardens with age and leaks around the runners.\n- The oil filler cap, the dipstick tube seal, and, on some years, evaporative (EVAP) purge lines.\n\n- A scan tool that shows live short-term and long-term fuel trim (a $20 to $40 Bluetooth OBD2 dongle and a phone app works)\n- A smoke machine (rental from some parts stores, $150 to $250 to buy a basic one, or $50 to $100 at an independent shop for a test)\n- An unlit propane torch with the flame head removed, or a spray bottle of plain water, or brake-parts cleaner or carburetor cleaner as a last choice\n- A hand vacuum pump with gauge ($25 to $40) for testing the brake booster, check valve, and any vacuum-actuated parts\n- Vacuum hose in the sizes your Jeep uses, assorted clamps, and a few tees\n- Fel-Pro or equivalent intake gasket set, only if you confirm the manifold gasket is leaking\n\n1. **Read the fuel trims at idle, then at 2,500 rpm.** Warm the engine fully. A leak adds the same volume of air regardless of engine speed, so at idle (low airflow) the percentage error is large and at 2,500 rpm (high airflow) the same leak is a small percentage. Trims that are high at idle and near zero at higher rpm are the signature of a vacuum leak. Trims that are high at both speeds suggest a fuel delivery or sensor problem.\n\n2. **Do a visual inspection with the engine off.** Trace every hose from the intake manifold, throttle body, and brake booster. Look for cracks at bends, flattened or swollen sections, hoses pushed only partway onto nipples, and missing caps on unused ports. Squeeze each hose; a hard, brittle, or oily one needs replacing. Pull the PCV valve and check that its hose and grommet are intact. Check the large air intake tube between the air filter box and throttle body for splits at the corrugations.\n\n3. **Test the brake booster.** With the engine off, pump the brake pedal several times until it is firm. Hold moderate pressure on the pedal and start the engine. The pedal should sink slightly as the booster fills with vacuum. If it does not, or if the pedal is hard at idle, the booster hose, its check valve, or the booster itself is leaking. A leaking booster diaphragm is a brake issue, and also an idle issue: do not drive the Jeep on the road until you are confident the brakes work normally.\n\n4. **Smoke test.** Plug the intake inlet or the tube before the throttle body, connect the smoke machine to a vacuum port following the machine's instructions, and apply low pressure. Look for smoke at gasket seams, hose connections, and around the base of the throttle body. Smoke escaping at the oil cap or dipstick tube means those seals are leaking too. A test with a flashlight in a darkened garage shows even small wisps. Always follow the machine's maker on pressure; too much pressure can push out seals.\n\n5. **If no smoke machine, use the propane or water method.** With the engine idling, direct a very small amount of propane at suspect joints. If idle speed rises or smooths out when the gas reaches a point, air is entering there. Keep the propane away from the exhaust manifold and any ignition source, work outdoors or with the door open, and never ignite it. The water-mist version is slower but safer: a brief mist at a leak point can make the engine stumble or die as water is drawn in. Spray carburetor or brake cleaner only as a last choice; it is flammable, it attacks rubber and some plastics, and an over-spray on a hot manifold is a fire.\n\n6. **Fix the leak found.** Replace hoses with the same inside diameter and wall type (vacuum hose, not fuel hose). Replace the PCV grommet and hose if brittle. Replace the throttle body gasket when the body has been off, which also ties into [TJ throttle body cleaning](/db/tj/tj-throttle-body-cleaning/). If the evidence points to the intake manifold gasket, plan a full day: on the 4.0 the intake and exhaust manifolds share a gasket set and fastener pattern, and the job requires removing the throttle body, fuel rail components, and several hoses. Follow the service manual's torque sequence and specifications.\n\n7. **Clear the codes and drive a full cycle.** After repair, clear codes, then drive for 15 to 30 minutes of mixed city and highway driving. Re-read the long-term fuel trim. It should trend back toward zero over the next few days of driving. If trims remain high, there is a second leak, or the problem was not a leak.\n\n- **A second leak.** Old rubber fails in groups. When you find one cracked hose, inspect the rest before closing the hood. Replace the set if all are original, because the parts cost less than the time spent re-diagnosing.\n- **Swapping parts without data.** An oxygen sensor, MAP sensor, or IAC motor replaced on a guess is a $40 to $120 mistake. Read trims first.\n- **Lean codes that return after a good repair.** Check fuel pressure with a gauge on the Schrader port on the fuel rail (specification in the service manual) and inspect the fuel filter and pump. A vacuum leak and a weak fuel pump can coexist.\n- **Misdiagnosing a worn engine.** A cylinder with low compression or a burned valve produces rough idle that no hose will fix. A compression test settles it.\n- **Brake feel.** A hard pedal or a pedal that sinks with the engine running means a booster issue. Treat it as a safety repair.\n\n**Costs and sources.** OBD2 scan dongle: $20 to $40. Vacuum hose and fittings: $10 to $25. PCV valve and grommet: $8 to $20. Throttle body gasket: $5 to $15. Intake manifold gasket set: $40 to $90 depending on brand. Smoke test at a shop: $50 to $100. Hand vacuum pump: $25 to $40. A shop quote for an intake manifold gasket replacement is usually $450 to $900, so the DIY price is mostly time. For the diagnostic side, [TJ OBD2 codes](/db/tj/tj-obd2-diagnostics/) is the companion guide."},{"id":"tj-beadlock-wheels","title":"TJ Beadlock Wheels \u2014 What They Do and Whether You Need Them","vehicle":"tj","category":"wheels_tires","type":"reference","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":1000,"high":2800},"tools_required":["torque wrench (10-25 ft-lb range, inch-pound wrench ideal)","impact wrench (lug nuts only, never ring bolts)","floor jack and jack stands","tire lube or soapy water","bead-seating tank or air compressor with volume","pick or small screwdriver for cleaning ring bolt heads"],"skills_required":["wheel mounting","tire service"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["wheels","beadlock","tires","rock-crawler","airing-down","15-inch","17-inch"],"parts":[{"name":"Raceline Monster beadlock RT232, 15x8, 5x4.5, 3.75in BS (per wheel)","vendor":"Raceline","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Raceline Rock 8 beadlock RT81, 15x8, 5x4.5 (per wheel)","vendor":"Raceline","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Trail Ready HD15 beadlock, 15x8 or 15x9, 5x4.5 (per wheel)","vendor":"Trail Ready","price_estimate_usd":400},{"name":"Method 105 beadlock, 17x8.5, 5x4.5 (per wheel \u2014 Rubicon / 17in builds)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Hutchinson Rock Monster internal beadlock, 17x8.5, 5x4.5 \u2014 the one DOT-compliant option (per wheel)","vendor":"Hutchinson","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Counteract or Dyna Beads balancing beads (per set of 4)","vendor":"Counteract","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Method 105 beadlock \u2014 off-highway use only, not DOT approved","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com/products/105-beadlock-matte-black"},{"title":"Method beadlock installation and torque instructions (Summit Racing PDF)","url":"https://static.summitracing.com/global/images/instructions/mth-spc153136.pdf"},{"title":"15-inch beadlocks \u2014 who still makes them (WranglerTJForum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/15-inch-beadlocks-who-still-makes-them.82685/"},{"title":"DOT-approved beadlocks \u2014 Hutchinson vs. bolt-on rings (WranglerTJForum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/dot-approved-beadlocks.56626/"},{"title":"Raceline off-road beadlock wheels","url":"https://racelinewheels.com/collections/offroad-beadlock-wheels"}],"alternatives":["tj-wheels","tj-tire-fitment-35s"],"summary":"Beadlocks are the right wheel for a TJ that crawls at 8 psi or below \u2014 the bolt-on ring clamps the outer bead so a rock or a sidewall fold can't pop it off. Above 12 psi on an 8 in-wide wheel, a 33 or 35 stays seated without them and beadlocks add cost, weight, and a re-torque chore you don't need. And the \"DOT-approved beadlock\" you'll see advertised does not exist for bolt-on rings: [every ring-style beadlock, Method's 105 included, is sold as off-highway only](https://www.methodracewheels.com/products/105-beadlock-matte-black). [The single DOT-compliant option is Hutchinson's internal Rock Monster](https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/dot-approved-beadlocks.56626/), at roughly double the price.","description":"On a conventional wheel, the only thing holding the tire bead against the rim is air pressure pushing outward. Drop to 10 psi and there's not much pushing; drop to 5 psi and a hard side-load \u2014 a rock edge under the sidewall, a ledge you're sliding off sideways \u2014 can push the bead inward off its seat. The tire goes flat instantly and often walks off the wheel entirely. A beadlock wheel replaces the outer bead seat with a bolt-on ring: the tire's outer bead is sandwiched between the ring and the wheel by 24 to 32 bolts, and it can't move regardless of pressure. The inner bead seats the normal way with air, which is why a beadlock doesn't help a tire that's unseated on the inside \u2014 rare, but it happens on a wheel that's been badly bent.\n\n**Who needs them.** A TJ on 33s or 35s that runs technical rock at 6\u201310 psi, regularly, is the honest use case. Rubicon trail, Moab slickrock ledges, Johnson Valley, any club run where the group airs down into single digits. If your trail pressure floor is 12\u201315 psi \u2014 the range that gives most of the traction gain on an 8 in-wide 15 in wheel with a 33 \u2014 you don't need them. A wider wheel and a good compressor get you most of the benefit; see the [TJ wheel guide](/db/tj/tj-wheels/) for the width and backspacing that keep a low-pressure bead seated. A middle path exists: Coyote Enterprises' internal boltless beadlock (an inflatable inner tube that presses both beads outward, $140\u2013$180 per wheel) works on your existing wheels, needs no re-torquing, and is legal because the wheel isn't modified.\n\nThe TJ's 5-on-4.5 bolt pattern and its 15 in and 16 in factory wheels put it in the awkward spot of the beadlock market, which has moved to 17 in. What's still made:\n\n- **15x8 and 15x9, 5x4.5:** Raceline Monster (RT232) and Rock 8 (RT81), Trail Ready HD15, Walker Evans 15 in Legend series. Backspacing 3.5\u20133.75 in is the norm, which is right for 33s on a 2.5\u20134 in lift. 15 in wheels clear the Rubicon's Dana 44 front calipers on most designs but not all \u2014 check the wheel maker's caliper clearance note if you have an 03\u201306 Rubicon, and expect trouble with any big-brake kit.\n- **17x8.5 and 17x9, 5x4.5:** Method 105, Hutchinson Rock Monster, Raceline Monster. Going to 17 in on a TJ means 17 in tires, which are more expensive and available in fewer true rock-crawling sizes than 15 in \u2014 but the selection of wheels is far better and you're set for a Dana 44 or 60 big-brake swap later.\n- **What to avoid:** unbranded 15 in beadlocks from marketplace sellers with no published load rating or ring torque spec, and \"beadlock-style\" wheels with a decorative ring. The ring hardware is the part holding the tire on; a wheel with no spec sheet has no business at 6 psi.\n\nWeight: a 15x8 aluminum beadlock runs 28\u201336 lb bare, 6\u201312 lb more than an equivalent standard aluminum wheel and roughly the same as a steel wheel. The ring is most of the added mass and it's at the outer edge, which is why beadlocks balance so poorly. On a TJ, the [35 in tire fitment guide](/db/tj/tj-tire-fitment-35s/) covers what the extra unsprung weight does to a Dana 30 and a 35-spline argument you'll have anyway.\n\nMost tire shops won't touch a beadlock \u2014 liability \u2014 so plan on doing it yourself. It's not hard and it takes about 30 minutes a wheel once you've done one.\n\n1. **Lay the wheel face-up** on a clean tarp. Wipe the ring seat and the bead seat clean.\n2. **Lube the inner bead** and push the tire down onto the wheel. The inner bead goes over the rim like a normal tire; the outer bead sits on top of the wheel's outer lip, not inside it.\n3. **Seat the inner bead** with air through the valve stem \u2014 a bead-seating tank or a blast from a large-volume compressor pops it out to the inner bead seat. You'll hear it. Stop; don't try to inflate the tire yet.\n4. **Set the ring** on the outer bead, aligned to the bolt holes. Lube the bead face the ring touches.\n5. **Start every bolt by hand** two full turns. Cross-thread one and the wheel is scrap; use the wheel maker's bolts and anti-seize if the instructions call for it (most do \u2014 the bolts are steel in an aluminum wheel).\n6. **Tighten in a star pattern in stages**: 5 ft-lb all the way around, then 10, then the final spec. The ring draws the bead down as it tightens and the bolts you did first go loose again; keep going around until nothing moves at final torque. Final spec is the wheel maker's number \u2014 **Raceline 15 ft-lb, [Method 18 ft-lb](https://static.summitracing.com/global/images/instructions/mth-spc153136.pdf)**, Trail Ready per their sheet \u2014 and it's low. An impact wrench on ring bolts strips the wheel.\n7. **Inflate to 30\u201335 psi** and check the ring gap is even all the way around. A ring that's tight on one side and gapped on the other has a bead caught under it \u2014 deflate, loosen, reseat.\n8. **Balance** with 4\u20136 oz of balancing beads through the valve stem, or find the one shop in town that will spin a beadlock. Expect some vibration above 65 mph either way.\n9. **Re-torque the rings after the first drive**, then after every trail day for the first few, then per the maker's interval \u2014 every 200 miles for Method, every trip for the others. Bolts loosen from ring flex; a loose ring is exactly the failure a beadlock is supposed to prevent.\n\n- **Street legality.** Bolt-on beadlocks are off-highway wheels by the manufacturer's own labeling, and several states treat them as equipment violations on a registered vehicle. Enforcement ranges from nothing to a fix-it ticket; the bigger exposure is an insurer looking for a reason after a crash. Hutchinson's internal beadlock is the answer if that matters to you; so is a second set of street wheels.\n- **Mud and sand pack the ring bolt heads** and stop a torque wrench from seating. Carry a pick and clean each head before you check torque.\n- **Bent or cracked rings** from rock hits are a per-ring replacement ($60\u2013$120), not a wheel replacement \u2014 one of the few maintenance advantages. Inspect after any hit that gouged the ring.\n- **Lug nuts and ring bolts are different jobs.** Lugs to the [factory spec](/db/tj/tj-wheels/) with a torque wrench; ring bolts to their own low spec. Nobody's ever stripped a beadlock with a hand wrench.\n- **Tire pressure monitoring** gets awkward on a beadlock with a short stem; use a 90\u00b0 stem or a stem-mounted gauge that doesn't hang past the ring.\n\n| Wheel | Size | Per wheel | Set of 4 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Raceline Rock 8 / Monster | 15x8, 5x4.5 | $250\u2013$300 | $1,000\u2013$1,200 |\n| Trail Ready HD15 | 15x8 / 15x9, 5x4.5 | $370\u2013$450 | $1,500\u2013$1,800 |\n| Method 105 | 17x8.5, 5x4.5 | $360\u2013$420 | $1,450\u2013$1,700 |\n| Hutchinson Rock Monster (DOT) | 17x8.5, 5x4.5 | $600\u2013$700 | $2,400\u2013$2,800 |\n| Coyote internal beadlock (on your wheels) | 15/16/17 in | $140\u2013$180 | $560\u2013$720 |\n\nAdd $40 for balancing beads, and if you're going to 17 in, the tires. Northridge4x4, Quadratec, and the wheel makers direct all stock the 5x4.5 sizes; Raceline and Trail Ready are the two that still take 15 in seriously. Buy replacement ring bolts (a bag of 40) with the wheels \u2014 you will lose a few to rounding and mud, and the correct bolt is not at the hardware store."},{"id":"tj-tire-fitment-35s","title":"Running 35\" Tires on the TJ \u2014 Lift and Fitment Requirements","vehicle":"tj","category":"wheels_tires","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":1100,"high":1700},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel and tire mounting"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["tires","fitment","35-inch","lift-requirement","clearance"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich Mud-Terrain T/A KM3 35x12.50R15 LT (E-rated)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A KO2 35x12.50R15 LT (C-rated)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":270},{"name":"Nitto Trail Grappler M/T 35x12.50R15 (E-rated)","vendor":"Nitto Tire","price_estimate_usd":265},{"name":"Cooper STT Pro 35x12.50R15 LT (E-rated)","vendor":"Cooper Tires","price_estimate_usd":245}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 35s on a TJ: Lift Requirements and Trimming Guide","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/35-tires-tj-wrangler-fitment/"},{"title":"BFGoodrich KM3 Product Page","url":"https://www.bfgoodrich.com/tires/mud-terrain-ta-km3"},{"title":"4WD Hardware \u2014 TJ Tire Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.4wdh.com/TJ-Tire-Fitment"}],"alternatives":["tj-budget-lift","tj-mid-lift","tj-wheels","tj-regear"],"summary":"35-inch tires on a TJ are a platform decision rather than a tire swap. [Plan on 4 to 5 inches of lift, fender and bumper trimming](https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/35-tires-tj-wrangler-fitment/), new gears at both axles (4.88 for a manual, 5.13 for an automatic), and a close look at the Dana 30 and Dana 35, because the tire itself is only about a quarter of the real bill.","description":"The TJ was designed around tires near 29 to 31 inches: the Sport rolled on 225/75R15 (about 28.9 inches) and the Rubicon on 31x10.50R15. Moving to 35x12.50R15 adds about 6 inches of diameter, roughly 21% over stock, and more than a third in weight per corner. Every other component feels it: the engine sees a taller effective gear ratio, the axle shafts and U-joints see higher loads, the steering and brakes work harder, and the fenders have to make room. None of this is a reason to avoid 35s. It is the honest picture before you spend money.\n\n**Lift height and fitment.** A 35x12.50 will not clear a TJ at 2 inches, and 2.5 inches only works with heavy trimming and a rig that is not flexing much. For a Jeep that sees trail use, the realistic target is 4 to 5 inches of lift, plus cutting the front and rear fender flares back, trimming the front bumper corners and the bump-stop pockets, and adjusting the steering stops so the tire stays off the control arms and fender at full lock. Trail-use builds that retain stock-length bump stops, set the front end up with long control arms ([long-arm lift guide](/db/tj/tj-long-arm-lift/)), and often trim the rear tub corner. If you do not want to cut metal, [33x12.50R15 is the largest size that clears at 3 to 3.5 inches](https://www.4wdh.com/TJ-Tire-Fitment) with little or no trimming.\n\n**Wheel choice.** The TJ's 5x4.5-inch bolt pattern (5x114.3) and 15-inch wheel size are the most common trail setup, so 35x12.50R15 tires are widely stocked and the wheel catalog is deep. For a 15x8-inch wheel, 3.75 to 4 inches of backspacing keeps the tire off the control arms and the steering linkage at lock. Less backspacing pushes the tire outward and improves clearance on the inside, but it also pushes the tread beyond the fender, and some states regulate tire coverage. The [wheel guide](/db/tj/tj-wheels/) goes through offsets, and the [beadlock guide](/db/tj/tj-beadlock-wheels/) covers the heavier option for low-pressure rocks.\n\n**Tire choice.** The listed tires are typical of what Jeep owners run. Mud-terrains such as the [BFGoodrich KM3](https://www.bfgoodrich.com/tires/mud-terrain-ta-km3) and Nitto Trail Grappler give the most bite on loose or wet trails and are loud on pavement. An all-terrain like the BFGoodrich KO2 is quieter, wears better, and holds up well in the rocks with a thick sidewall. The Cooper STT Pro is a deeper, more aggressive mud pattern. A 35x12.50R15 weighs very roughly 75 to 90 pounds per tire depending on the model and load range, so each wheel and tire assembly is a heavy lift, and five assemblies including a spare add serious mass to a bumper-mounted carrier (check your spare carrier rating and the [rear bumper guide](/db/tj/tj-rear-bumper-guide/)).\n\n**Gearing.** At stock ratios of 3.07 or 3.73, depending on engine and options, a 35-inch tire pulls the effective gearing so tall that a 4.0L engine labors in top gear and lacks low-end power. The standard answer is 4.88 for a manual transmission and 5.13 for an automatic. The [regear guide](/db/tj/tj-regear/) has the process and the cost: roughly $1,200 to $2,500 for both axles with a shop doing the work. Skipping the regear means a sluggish Jeep, worse fuel economy, and a transmission that hunts between gears, and many owners end up doing it six months later.\n\n**Speedometer.** A 35-inch tire alone makes the speedometer read about 20% slow, which means the Jeep is going far faster than the dial shows. Regearing at the same time pulls this in the other direction: going from 3.73 to 4.88 with 35s makes the speedometer read about 8% fast. In both cases the number on the dial will be wrong, and you will need a calibration programmer or a speedometer corrector. Check with a GPS app before you trust it for speeding tickets or mileage.\n\n**Axles and drivetrain.** The Dana 30 front with stock shafts and the Dana 35 rear are the limiting parts. A 35 with a locker or on a hard trail loads the Dana 35's C-clip axles and the Dana 30's inner shafts beyond what they were designed for. Budget for chromoly shafts ([chromoly shaft guide](/db/tj/tj-chromoly-shafts/)) at minimum, and consider a rear Dana 44 swap ([Dana 44 rear guide](/db/tj/tj-dana44-rear/)) if you wheel hard. A Dana 35 failure on a trail is a tow and a $1,000 repair. See [Dana 35 failures](/db/tj/tj-dana35-failures/) for what goes first.\n\n**Steering and brakes.** Taller, heavier tires put more load on the tie rods, the drag link, and the steering box. Plan on a heavier tie rod and drag link, and a good steering stabilizer. The stock brakes are marginal with 35s; the [front pad guide](/db/tj/tj-front-pads/) covers a pad upgrade, and a larger-diameter caliper or a four-wheel-disc swap is the next step if you drive in mountains.\n\n**Real total cost.** Five tires mounted and balanced: $1,100 to $1,700. Lift kit: $1,000 to $2,500. Regear: $1,200 to $2,500. Wheels: $600 to $1,200 for a set of five. Axle shafts: $500 to $1,200. A conservative total is $4,500 to $9,000, and the tires are about one-fifth of it. That is the number to plan around when someone says the plan is only to move up to 35s.\n\n**Costs and sources.** Tires: $245 to $280 each (pricing in the parts list), $1,100 to $1,700 for five mounted. Wheels: $600 to $1,200. Lift: $1,000 to $2,500. Regear: $1,200 to $2,500. Shafts: $500 to $1,200. Budget the whole system, not the tire, before you start. If the budget is tight, 33-inch tires with the [mid-lift](/db/tj/tj-mid-lift/) fit with far less modification and are a more forgiving first step.","steps":["Confirm lift height. Plan on 4 inches or more; with less, expect rubbing at full flex and decide whether to trim before you buy tires.","Check your wheels for backspacing. 3.75 to 4 inches on a 15x8 wheel is the working range for 35s.","Mount and balance the tires on a bead breaker and balancer rated for the load range; a 35x12.50 on a standard machine can cause balance issues, so ask the shop.","Install the wheels with the Jeep on stands and torque the lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs in a star pattern.","Lower the Jeep and turn the steering lock to lock, then have a helper compress each corner by hand or with a jack. Mark every contact point with chalk.","Trim the contact points with a utility knife, nibbler, or cut-off wheel on plastic liner and flare edges; use a bump-stop extension before cutting metal.","Adjust the steering stops if the tire contacts the control arm or frame at full lock.","Re-torque the lug nuts after 50 miles.","Schedule the regear, calibrate the speedometer, and check the axle shafts, the steering, and the brakes before taking the Jeep on a trail."],"pros":["35 inches is the most common serious trail size for a TJ, so fitment advice and parts are plentiful.","15-inch wheels are stock fitment, so there is no adapter or special wheel size.","Meaningful ground clearance and approach-angle gain over 31s and 33s.","BFG KM3 and KO2 both have long trail records and broad availability."],"cons":["Gears at both axles are non-negotiable, adding $1,200 to $2,500.","35s overload the Dana 35 rear and the Dana 30 front shafts; plan on chromoly shafts or an axle swap.","Fuel economy drops 2 to 4 mpg and acceleration suffers, especially on the 4.0L automatic.","A 35 will not clear without cutting at lift under 4 inches, and the cuts are permanent.","The speedometer will be wrong until you correct it."]},{"id":"tj-wheels","title":"Wheel Selection for the TJ \u2014 Steel, Alloy, and Beadlock","vehicle":"tj","category":"wheels_tires","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":1000},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["wheels","rims","backspacing","beadlock","bolt-pattern"],"parts":[{"name":"Pro Comp Series 51 Steel Wheel 15x8\" 3.75\" BS (5x4.5\" bolt pattern)","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Method Race Wheels 105 Beadlock 15x8\" 3.5\" BS (5x4.5\")","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"Raceline Monster 8 15x8\" 3.75\" BS (5x4.5\")","vendor":"Raceline Wheels","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"KMC XD Rockstar 2 15x8\" 3.625\" BS (5x4.5\")","vendor":"KMC Wheels","price_estimate_usd":135}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Wheel Backspacing and Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/tj-wheel-backspacing-fitment-guide/"},{"title":"Pro Comp 51 Series Steel Wheel Specs","url":"https://procompusa.com/wheels/series-51"},{"title":"Method Race Wheels 105 Beadlock Overview","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com/105-beadlock"}],"alternatives":["tj-tire-fitment-35s","tj-budget-lift"],"summary":"Run 15x8 steel or alloy wheels with [about 3.75 inches of backspacing for 33s and 35s](https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/tj-wheel-backspacing-fitment-guide/) on a lifted TJ; move to a true beadlock only if you routinely air down below 12 PSI on rock. Budget $300 to $1,000 for a set of five, including the spare.","description":"The TJ uses a 5x114.3mm (5x4.5 inch) bolt pattern with a hub bore commonly listed at 71.5mm (about 2.81 inches), and factory studs take 1/2-20 lug nuts. Stock wheels are 15 inch on Sport and SE trims and 16 inch on Sahara and Rubicon. The bolt pattern is one of the most widely produced in the 4x4 world, so the real decision is not what fits but which trade-offs you want: weight, strength, bead retention, cost, and how the wheel sits under the fender.\n\n**Backspacing and offset.** Backspacing is the distance from the wheel's mounting face to its inner lip. Offset is the same idea measured from the wheel's centerline. For a given width, offset is backspacing minus half the width, so a 15x8 with 3.75 inches of backspacing has an offset of about minus 0.25 inch (roughly minus 6mm), meaning the wheel sits slightly outboard of the hub. That is the working fitment for most lifted TJs: it keeps the tire clear of the control arms and steering linkage at full lock while leaving a little more track width for stability. Drop to 3.5 inches or less and the tire pokes out past stock flares, which draws fender-coverage tickets in some states. Go up toward 4.5 inches and the inside of the tire gets close to the control arms, the track bar, and the sway-bar links, especially once a lift adds droop. If you are running 35s, check the tire-fitment notes in [35-inch tire fitment on the TJ](/db/tj/tj-tire-fitment-35s/) first, because a 35 needs lift, trimming, and gearing that the wheel choice cannot fix.\n\n**Steel versus alloy.** A steel wheel such as the [Pro Comp Series 51](https://procompusa.com/wheels/series-51) is heavy, cheap, and can be bent back after a rock strike, which is why it is still the trail default. A cast alloy wheel is lighter, usually looks better, and costs more; it also cracks rather than bends if it takes a hard hit, and a cracked alloy on the trail is a spare-tire day. On a TJ, unsprung weight matters most for ride quality and steering feel, and a 35-inch tire already adds far more weight than any wheel choice saves. Buy steel if the Jeep lives on rocks and you want the cheapest reliable set; buy alloy if it is a daily driver that sees trails on weekends.\n\n**Beadlocks, honestly.** A true beadlock clamps the tire bead to the wheel with a ring and bolts, so the tire stays seated at pressures that would otherwise let it break the bead, in the neighborhood of 8 to 12 PSI. That is a rock-crawling tool. On dirt roads, dunes, and most trails, a standard wheel at 15 PSI or more is fine, and a beadlock adds weight, cost, and maintenance. The bolt ring needs a periodic torque check (the maker's interval and torque pattern govern; do not rely on a remembered number), and street use of a true beadlock can leak slowly at the ring. Many wheels sold as beadlock-style are only a decorative ring bolted to a standard wheel; they do not hold the bead. Read the product description before paying for the look. Related reading: [TJ beadlock wheels](/db/tj/tj-beadlock-wheels/).\n\n**The spare and the carrier.** A heavier wheel and tire changes the tailgate load. A 33 on a steel wheel is manageable on the stock carrier; a 35 on steel can sag the tailgate and stress its hinges, so budget for a reinforced carrier or a bumper-mounted carrier if you move up.","steps":["Confirm the specs before you order: 5x4.5 inch pattern, hub bore of 71.5mm or larger (a larger bore is fine with hub-centric rings; a smaller one will not fit), and backspacing in the 3.75 to 4 inch range for a lifted TJ. Ask the seller to confirm fitment for a 1997 to 2006 Wrangler TJ in writing.","Have the tires mounted and balanced at a shop. Ask for the wheel's rated load to be at or above the tire's, and have them check that the tire's bead seats cleanly all the way around.","Loosen the lug nuts on the old wheel about a quarter turn while the Jeep is on the ground. Raise the Jeep and support it on jack stands under the axle or frame; never rely on the jack alone.","Remove the wheel and clean the hub face of rust and debris with a wire brush. Rust or paint on the mating surface causes lug nuts to loosen as it compacts.","Mount the new wheel and thread the lug nuts by hand. The TJ takes 1/2-20 nuts; confirm your aftermarket wheel's seat type (conical is typical) matches the nut, because a wrong seat can loosen under load.","Snug in a star pattern, lower the Jeep, and torque to the factory figure in the service manual. Several references and earlier TrailManual guides cite 95 ft-lb; verify against your manual before relying on it.","Drive 25 to 50 miles and re-torque. Aluminum and painted wheels seat in during the first drive.","At full lock in both directions, check the tire against the sway-bar links, brake hoses, control arms, and the inside of the fender. Do the same at full compression with a helper bouncing the corner, or by stuffing the bump stop if your suspension allows it."],"pros":["The 5x4.5 pattern gives the widest selection and price range of any common 4x4, from $75 steel to premium forged beadlocks.","Steel wheels survive rock strikes that crack cast alloy and can be bent back on the trail.","A 15x8 at about 3.75 inches of backspacing clears the TJ's steering and control arms with a modest lift and gives 33s a stable stance.","True beadlocks let you air down to the pressures that make rock crawling controllable, with far less risk of losing a bead."],"cons":["Steel wheels are heavier than comparable alloys, and the extra rotating mass is felt in steering and acceleration.","Backspacing below 3.5 inches pushes the tire past stock flares, and wheels near 4.5 inches can contact control arms after a lift.","True beadlocks need periodic bolt maintenance and can seep air; beadlock-look wheels cost the same as beadlocks and do not do the same job.","Wheel spacers or adapters as a fix for bad fitment add stress to the studs and another failure point; buy the correct backspacing instead.","Moving to heavier wheels and tires can load the tailgate hinges and stock tire carrier beyond their design.","**Costs and sources.** Pro Comp Series 51 steel 15x8: about $75 to $100 each, or $375 to $500 for five. Method 105 beadlock 15x8: about $145 to $250 each depending on finish and vendor, which puts a set of five at $700 to $1,250 before tires. Mid-price alloys (Raceline Monster, KMC Rockstar): $110 to $190 each. Mounting and balancing: $25 to $35 per tire for LT-rated off-road tires. Prices drift; confirm at checkout. Related: [Budget lift for the TJ](/db/tj/tj-budget-lift/)."]}];